window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("training-adaptation", {"topics":[{"key":"training-adaptation/pre-training","sources":["Radford et al., 2018 — Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training","Brown et al., 2020 — Language Models are Few-Shot Learners","Kaplan et al., 2020 — Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models"],"why":"- **Per-task models starved for data.** Before pre-training, each NLP task got its own model trained from\n  scratch. A sentiment classifier learned English from a few thousand labelled reviews, far too little\n  for grammar, world knowledge and idiom.\n- **Labels are expensive.** The quantity of labelled data needed to learn language from zero would be\n  impossible to annotate.\n- **Next-token prediction has free labels.** Hide the token after position *t* and predict it from tokens\n  ≤ *t*. Every web document becomes training data without a single annotator. To predict well the model\n  must learn syntax, semantics, facts (the capital of France is ...) and procedures (how a proof or a\n  function body continues). The objective is simple; the data makes it hard.\n- **Training the big model on your task fails.** No downstream dataset is large enough to set billions of\n  parameters without overfitting, and most of what the task needs is general competence shared across\n  tasks.\n- **Pay once, adapt many times.** Learning that competence once is cheaper than per task. Pre-training is\n  the one-time payment; fine-tuning, RAG and prompting are all ways to spend its result.","how":"### The objective\nFor a token sequence x₁…x_T, the model with parameters θ maximises the log-likelihood of each token given\nits prefix:\n\n```\nL(θ) = − Σ_t log p_θ(x_t | x_<t)\n```\n\n- **It is cross-entropy** between the one-hot next token and the softmax output, averaged over every\n  position in every sequence.\n- **One pass scores every position.** The causal mask lets a forward pass score all T positions at once,\n  so a sequence of 8k tokens yields 8k training signals.\n- **Progress is reported as loss or perplexity**, not accuracy, because perplexity is just exp of this\n  loss.\n\n| Anchor | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Starting loss of a random model | ≈ ln(V), about 11.9 nats for a 152k-token vocabulary |\n| Starting perplexity | equal to the vocabulary size (probability spread evenly) |\n| Each nat below ln(V) | information extracted about how text continues |\n| Reading perplexity | \"the number of tokens the model is still choosing between, on average\" |\n\n### Why causal, not masked, prediction\n| | Masked LM (BERT-style) | Causal next-token |\n|---|---|---|\n| What is supervised | only the ~15% of tokens hidden | *every* position in one pass |\n| Context used | both sides | left only |\n| Produces a generator? | no, nothing learns to write left to right | yes, the trained form is a sampler |\n| Kept for | embedding and classification encoders | LLMs: completion, chat, tool calls after post-training |\n\nThat is why decoder-only causal pre-training became the default for LLMs.\n\n### The data\n1. **Train the tokenizer first**, on a sample of the same corpus, then freeze it for the model's life.\n   Its vocabulary sets how many tokens a text costs, which is why a model can be several times more\n   expensive per word in a language its tokenizer under-represents.\n2. **Build a curated mixture**: web crawl, code, books, reference text, academic papers, and increasingly\n   multilingual and synthetic data.\n3. **Run the pipeline**, which matters as much as the model:\n   - language identification;\n   - quality filtering (classifiers or heuristics);\n   - near-duplicate removal, because duplicated text makes the model memorise rather than generalise;\n   - decontamination against evaluation sets.\n4. **Choose mixture weights** (how much code versus web). They shape what the base model is good at,\n   because the model allocates capacity to what it sees most.\n\n### The compute budget\n```\ntraining FLOPs ≈ 6 · N · D      (N = parameters, D = tokens)\n               = 2·N·D forward + ~4·N·D backward\n```\n\n- **Kaplan et al.:** loss falls smoothly as a power law in compute.\n- **Hoffmann et al. (Chinchilla):** earlier models were undertrained. For compute-optimal training N and\n  D should grow together, roughly 20 tokens per parameter as a rule of thumb.\n- **Later open models train far past that ratio** because they optimise for *inference* cost: a smaller\n  model trained on more tokens is cheaper to serve for the same quality.\n\n### The schedule\n| Ingredient | Typical choice | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| Optimiser | AdamW | standard for transformers |\n| Warmup | linear | Adam's second-moment estimates are unreliable for the first steps, and a full LR on random weights tends to diverge |\n| Decay | cosine or warmup-stable-decay | a high rate keeps weights bouncing around a basin; loss drops visibly in the final decay |\n| Batch | millions of tokens | stable gradient estimates at scale |\n| Precision | bf16 mixed precision, gradient clipping | throughput and stability |\n\nTraining runs in stages:\n1. **Main run** on short sequences.\n2. **Long-context stage**, shorter, that extends the context window.\n3. **Annealing** (often) on high-quality data with the LR decayed to near zero, which measurably lifts\n   benchmark scores.\n4. **Throughout:** keep mid-training checkpoints, so a loss spike or divergence can be rolled back to a\n   point before it and resumed, often with the offending data batches skipped.\n\n### What the model learns, and why prompting works\n- **General machinery is the cheapest route to low loss** on trillions of varied tokens: representations\n  of entities and relations, syntax, and the structure of code.\n- **In-context learning is a by-product.** Web documents often repeat patterns (lists of examples, Q&A\n  pages, tables). Predicting the next item requires inferring the rule from earlier items.\n- **So a base model already does few-shot learning** from the prompt, the latent ability instruction\n  tuning later makes reliable.\n\n### Why the result is a base model\n- **Nothing in the objective says \"be helpful\".** Asked a question, a base model may answer, continue with\n  more questions, or write a forum-style thread, because all are plausible continuations of web text.\n- **Post-training adds behaviour.** Instruction following, refusals and chat formatting come later.\n- **The base carries knowledge and latent skills**; it is the asset every downstream stage starts from.","example":"### One step of pre-training in code\nThe labels are the input ids shifted left by one; the loss is cross-entropy at every position. This is\nexactly what `AutoModelForCausalLM` does internally when you pass `labels=input_ids`.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"               # a small base model, not an -Instruct one\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16).cuda()\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-5, weight_decay=0.1)\n\ntext = \"Pre-training predicts the next token. The loss is cross-entropy over the vocabulary.\"\nids = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()        # [1, T]\n\nlogits = model(input_ids=ids).logits                          # [1, T, vocab]\npred, target = logits[:, :-1, :], ids[:, 1:]                  # position t predicts token t+1\nloss = F.cross_entropy(pred.reshape(-1, pred.size(-1)).float(), target.reshape(-1))\nprint(f\"loss={loss.item():.3f}  perplexity={loss.exp().item():.1f}\")\n\nloss.backward()                                               # one optimiser step of \"pre-training\"\ntorch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), 1.0)\nopt.step(); opt.zero_grad()\n# Scale this loop to ~10^13 tokens, thousands of GPUs and a cosine LR schedule and you have a base model.\n```\n\n- **Read the loss against the anchor:** a trained base model sits far below the ~11.9 nats a random model\n  would score on this sentence.\n- **The `[:, :-1]` / `[:, 1:]` shift is the entire supervision mechanism.** The sentence's tokens are\n  both input and label, which is the \"self-supervised\" claim made concrete.\n\n### Worked budget: GPU-days for a 7B base\nBack-of-envelope, as an illustrative order of magnitude:\n\n| Step | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Model and data | 7B parameters, 1 trillion tokens |\n| FLOPs | 6 × 7e9 × 1e12 ≈ 4e22 |\n| Achieved throughput | 4e14 FLOP/s per GPU (roughly 40% utilisation of a modern accelerator's bf16 peak) |\n| GPU-seconds | 1e8 |\n| GPU-days | about 1,200 |\n\nPre-training is the one stage where the arithmetic is in GPU-years, not GPU-hours. The same sum as a\nfunction you can rerun with your own numbers:\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef pretrain_budget(params, tokens, flops_per_gpu=4e14, gpus=1024, vocab=152_000):\n    flops = 6 * params * tokens\n    gpu_days = flops / flops_per_gpu / 86_400\n    return {\n        \"flops\": f\"{flops:.1e}\",\n        \"gpu_days\": round(gpu_days),\n        \"wall_clock_days\": round(gpu_days / gpus, 1),\n        \"chinchilla_tokens\": f\"{20 * params:.1e}\",       # ~20 tokens per parameter\n        \"tokens_per_param\": round(tokens / params),\n        \"initial_loss_nats\": round(math.log(vocab), 1),  # random model: ln(V)\n    }\n\nprint(pretrain_budget(7e9, 1e12))\n# gpu_days ~1215, wall_clock ~1.2 days on 1,024 GPUs, chinchilla_tokens 1.4e+11,\n# tokens_per_param 143 (well past compute-optimal, as inference-minded models are), initial loss 11.9\n```","patterns":["**Measure tokenizer cost before choosing a base.** When shortlisting checkpoints, tokenise a sample of your documents with each tokenizer and compute tokens per word. Monitor it per language, because it sets your API bill and how much fits in the context window.","**Compare bases on bits per byte, not raw loss.** Score held-out domain text with each candidate, divide total negative log-likelihood (in bits) by the text's byte count, and add few-shot or log-likelihood tasks you care about. Monitor rank agreement with downstream evals.","**Replay general data in continued pre-training.** When adapting to a large domain corpus, mix general text back into every batch rather than training on domain text alone. Monitor loss on a general held-out set alongside domain loss; a rise means forgetting.","**Checkpoint often and roll back on spikes.** Save checkpoints at a fixed step interval and alert on loss and gradient-norm jumps. On a spike that does not recover, restore the last good checkpoint, skip the offending batches, and lower the LR or tighten clipping.","**Decontaminate before you trust a benchmark.** Run n-gram overlap checks between training data and every eval set you report, and drop matching documents. Monitor the gap between public-benchmark scores and a private held-out set of the same kind.","**Track utilisation against the 6·N·D estimate.** Divide 6·N·(tokens per second) by the cluster's peak FLOP/s to get model FLOPs utilisation. A falling figure flags data-loader stalls, communication bottlenecks or a slow node."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Raw corpus: web, code, books\"] --> P[\"Filter, dedup, decontaminate\"]\n    P --> M[\"Weighted data mixture\"]\n    S[\"Corpus sample\"] --> TK[\"Train and freeze tokenizer\"]\n    TK -->|\"tokenize\"| M\n    M --> MR[\"Main run: short sequences\"]\n    MR --> LC[\"Long-context stage\"]\n    LC --> AN[\"Anneal: high-quality data, LR to 0\"]\n    AN --> B[\"Base model: completes text\"]\n    MR -->|\"save at fixed intervals\"| CK[(\"Checkpoints\")]\n    CK -.->|\"roll back on spike, skip batches\"| MR\n    B --> PT[\"Post-training\"]","caption":"Follow the data from raw text to a base model. The pipeline (language ID, quality filter, dedup,\ndecontamination) and the frozen tokenizer come first; the main run uses short sequences with AdamW and\nwarmup then decay; checkpoints are the rollback path when loss spikes."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is pre-training called self-supervised, and why did that unlock scale?","a":"- **The text is its own label.** At each position the target is the next token already in the document,\n  so the loss is cross-entropy against data that needs no annotator.\n- **So the corpus can be the web.** Trillions of tokens become usable, whereas no labelled downstream\n  dataset is large enough to set billions of parameters without overfitting.\n- **Causal beats masked for generation** because every position is supervised in one pass and the trained\n  model is already a left-to-right sampler."},{"q":"Your pre-training loss spikes at step 40k and does not recover. What do you do?","a":"- **Stop training through it.** A spike that never recovers, or recovers to a worse curve, usually means\n  numerical instability or a bad data shard.\n- **Roll back** to the last good checkpoint before the spike and resume with the offending batches skipped.\n- **Then reduce risk:** lower the LR or tighten gradient clipping, and check that bf16 mixed precision and\n  clipping are configured as intended.\n- **Monitor** loss and gradient norm with alerts on jumps so the next one is caught early."},{"q":"Chinchilla says about 20 tokens per parameter. Why do open models train far past that?","a":"- **Chinchilla optimises training compute.** For a fixed budget, N and D should grow together.\n- **Product teams optimise total cost.** A smaller model trained on more tokens is cheaper to serve for the\n  same quality, so over-training wins when inference spend will exceed training spend.\n- **It flips** for a model trained once and queried rarely, where the extra training compute is wasted."},{"q":"Estimate the compute to pre-train a 7B model on 1 trillion tokens.","a":"- **Use 6·N·D:** 6 × 7e9 × 1e12 ≈ 4e22 FLOPs (2·N·D forward plus about 4·N·D backward).\n- **Divide by achieved throughput:** at 4e14 FLOP/s per GPU that is about 1e8 GPU-seconds, roughly 1,200\n  GPU-days, or a little over a day of wall clock on 1,024 GPUs.\n- **Sanity-check the ratio:** 1e12 / 7e9 is about 143 tokens per parameter, well past compute-optimal."},{"q":"How would you compare two candidate base models for a domain product?","a":"- **Not by chatting with them**, because a base model was never trained to answer.\n- **Not by raw loss either**, because loss is per token and tokenizers differ; normalise to bits per byte\n  on the same held-out domain text.\n- **Add** tokens per word on your documents (cost and context budget) and few-shot or log-likelihood\n  scores on a small task set, then check licence and data provenance."}],"tradeoffs":["**More tokens per parameter vs training compute.** Quality rises and serving cost falls for a fixed model size, but past the compute-optimal point each extra point costs disproportionately more training compute. Over-training wins when inference spend will exceed training spend; it loses for a model trained once and queried rarely.","**Broad mixture vs domain strength.** A broad mixture gives general competence; over-weighting one domain (code, a language) improves it at the expense of others. The right mix depends on what the model will be served for.","**Long sequences vs quadratic attention.** They teach long-range structure but cost quadratic attention compute. At a few thousand tokens the MLP and projection matmuls dominate, so the quadratic term bites only at long contexts, which is why context extension is a short late stage.","**Aggressive filtering vs robustness.** It raises per-token value but can remove dialects, formats and messy text the model meets in production. It pays while unique high-quality data is plentiful and stops paying once it forces the same documents to repeat for many epochs."],"pitfalls":["**Judging a base model by chatting with it.** It was never trained to answer, so a rambling or repetitive reply says nothing about its knowledge. Fix: evaluate with few-shot prompts or log-likelihood tasks.","**Letting eval data leak into the corpus.** Symptom: benchmark scores that do not transfer to held-out tasks of the same kind. Fix: decontamination is part of the pipeline.","**Comparing models by raw pre-training loss.** Loss is per token, so a coarser tokenizer looks worse per token while being better per character. Symptom: a perplexity ranking that disagrees with downstream evals. Fix: normalise to bits per byte (or per character) on the same corpus.","**Training through loss spikes.** A jump that does not recover, or recovers to a worse curve, usually means numerical instability or a bad data shard. Fix: roll back to an earlier checkpoint, skip the batches, and lower the LR or tighten clipping.","**Treating continued pre-training as cheap.** Without general data mixed in at a sensible ratio the model forgets general skills. Symptom: loss jumps on everything outside the domain."],"when":"Almost no application team pre-trains from scratch: the cost is in GPU-years and the data engineering is\na product in itself.\n- **Decide which base to build on.** Its token budget and data mix predict its strengths; its tokenizer,\n  context window, licence and training-data provenance are all fixed at this stage.\n- **Measure on your data:** tokens per word on a sample of your documents (cost and context budget), bits\n  per byte on held-out domain text, and few-shot or log-likelihood scores on a small task set you care\n  about.\n- **Use continued pre-training when** you have a large domain corpus (tens of millions of tokens or more,\n  such as legal or biomedical text). It is the one case where an application team runs this objective,\n  usually on a smaller model.\n- **Avoid when** the goal is \"know our facts\": retrieval or fine-tuning on a pre-trained base is cheaper\n  and keeps answers current.\n- **Interviews:** explain the objective, why it is self-supervised, what scaling laws say about N versus\n  D, and why a base model differs from a chat model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Chooses a base checkpoint by its training tokens, data mix and context length, and runs continued pre-training only with a general-data replay mix to avoid forgetting.","architect":"Owns the build-versus-adopt decision on foundation models, weighing training cost, data provenance and licence terms against the control a custom base would give.","fde":"Explains to a customer why \"train a model on our data\" usually means fine-tuning or RAG on a pre-trained base, not pre-training, and what a corpus of their size can realistically teach."},"check":{"q":"A customer with 200 million tokens of proprietary manuals asks you to \"pre-train a model on our\ndata so it knows our products\". How do you respond, and what would you actually propose?","a":"200M tokens is four orders of magnitude short of a pre-training corpus, so training from scratch\nwould produce a model that cannot even write fluent English. The knowledge they want lives in two\nplaces: facts, which are best served by retrieval over the manuals so answers stay current and citable,\nand style or terminology, which a fine-tune on a pre-trained base can absorb. If the domain vocabulary is\ngenuinely far from web text, continued pre-training of an open-weight base on the 200M tokens, mixed with\ngeneral data to prevent forgetting, is a defensible middle option. I would propose RAG first, measure the\nfailure cases, and only then decide whether adaptation of the weights is justified."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can pre-training use the raw internet while earlier NLP models needed labelled datasets?","options":["Transformers are robust to label noise, so unlabelled text is acceptable","Next-token prediction uses the text itself as the label, so no annotation is needed","Web text is pre-labelled by its HTML structure","Pre-training uses clustering, which is unsupervised"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The target at each position is simply the next token already present in the document. That makes the objective self-supervised: supervision exists, but it comes for free from the data rather than from annotators."},{"q":"According to the Chinchilla result, what was wrong with earlier large models like GPT-3?","options":["They had too few parameters for their training data","They were trained on too few tokens for their parameter count","They used the wrong tokenizer","They were trained with too high a learning rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hoffmann et al. showed that for a fixed compute budget, parameters and tokens should scale together, roughly 20 tokens per parameter. GPT-3-era models had far fewer tokens than that, so a smaller model trained longer would have matched them for the same compute."},{"q":"A freshly pre-trained base model is asked \"What is the capital of France?\" and replies with three more geography questions. What does this show?","options":["The model failed to learn the fact during pre-training","The tokenizer split the question badly","The base model is continuing text in a plausible style; answering is a behaviour added in post-training","The learning rate was too low in the annealing phase"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A list of questions is a perfectly likely continuation of a question in web text. The fact may well be encoded; eliciting it as an answer rather than a continuation is what instruction tuning and preference training provide."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/post-training","sources":["Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models","Lambert et al., 2024 — Tülu 3: Pushing Frontiers in Open Language Model Post-Training"],"why":"- **A base model is a very good autocomplete.** Asked a question, it may continue with more questions, a\n  forum thread, or an answer in the wrong register, because all are likely continuations of web text.\n- **The knowledge is there; the behaviour is not.** It has no notion of \"user\" and \"assistant\", no reason\n  to be concise, no reason to decline a harmful request, and no habit of showing its reasoning.\n- **Prompting helps but is fragile.** Few-shot Q&A examples work surprisingly well (evidence the skills are\n  latent), but they eat context and cannot teach what is not an obvious pattern: a consistent refusal\n  policy, a house JSON format, calling a tool when unsure.\n- **Next-token loss cannot express preference.** \"This answer is better than that one\" has no place in it.\n- **So post-training adds layers, each past the previous one's limit.** Demonstrations teach format and\n  basic behaviour. Comparisons teach quality, because most prompts have no single correct answer, only\n  better and worse ones. Checkable-reward RL teaches behaviours that need many attempts to discover, such\n  as multi-step reasoning.","how":"### The three stages at a glance\n| Stage | Signal | Teaches | Limit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. SFT | demonstrations | chat template, persona, \"answer the question\" | can only imitate |\n| 2. Preference optimisation | comparisons | quality, tone, refusals | only reranks answers the SFT policy already produces; cannot discover behaviours that need many attempts |\n| 3. RL on verifiable rewards | programmatic checks | multi-step reasoning, exact formats | only for checkable tasks |\n\nThe stages are ordered because each one needs the behaviour the previous one installed.\n\n### Stage 1: supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations\n1. Humans (or a stronger model) write prompt-response pairs in the target format: system prompt, user\n   turn, assistant turn.\n2. Train with the same next-token loss as pre-training, but only on the assistant tokens.\n3. Result: the chat template, the assistant persona and basic answering behaviour.\n\n- **It comes first because it is cheap and stable**, and later stages need a policy that already\n  produces plausible candidate answers.\n- **Its limit:** it cannot tell the model that one of two fluent answers is wrong. See *supervised\n  fine-tuning* and *instruction tuning* for mechanics and data design.\n\n### Stage 2: preference optimisation on comparisons\n- **Data:** raters see two or more responses to the same prompt and rank them. Cheaper than\n  demonstrations, and it captures judgements people find easy to make but hard to write down.\n- **RLHF:** train a reward model on the comparisons, then optimise the policy against it with PPO and a\n  KL penalty that stops it drifting too far from the SFT model.\n- **DPO:** skip the reward model; optimise the policy directly on the pairs with a classification-style\n  loss derived from the same KL-constrained objective.\n- **Choosing is an engineering call.** RLHF is on-policy and can keep improving as the policy changes; DPO\n  is simpler, cheaper and offline.\n- **Effect:** both raise the quality of answers the SFT model already produces; refusal behaviour and tone\n  are mostly shaped here.\n\n### Stage 3: reinforcement learning on verifiable rewards\n1. Pick tasks with a checkable answer: maths with a known result, code with unit tests, output that must\n   parse as JSON.\n2. Compute the reward with a program rather than learning it from humans.\n3. Sample many attempts per prompt, reward the correct ones, push toward whatever reasoning got there.\n\n- **GRPO is the common algorithm** in open recipes because it needs no value model.\n- **This stage is credited with long chain-of-thought** in reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1.\n- **It is usually the most expensive post-training stage**, because generating many long samples per\n  prompt dominates the cost, though it remains orders of magnitude below pre-training.\n\n### Where the signal comes from\nThe mix of sources is the main cost lever:\n\n| Source | Examples | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **People** | annotators, domain experts | highest trust; the only source that can define \"good\" for a new domain | slowest |\n| **Stronger models** | teacher writes SFT responses (distillation through data); judge ranks pairs (RL from AI feedback, LLM-as-a-judge) | cheap, scalable | student inherits teacher's blind spots and judge's biases (e.g. preferring longer answers), so keep a human-labelled slice to calibrate |\n| **Programs** | verifiers, schema validators, unit tests | free per sample, impossible to flatter | only for checkable tasks; reward exactly what they check, gaps included |\n\n**Rejection sampling bridges the stages.** Sample several responses from the current model, keep the ones\na reward model, judge or verifier scores highest, and SFT on them. It reuses cheap SFT machinery while\nlearning from a quality signal, which is why many recipes run it before or instead of full RL.\n\n### Recurring design constraints\n- **A reference model and a KL leash.** Nearly every stage after SFT penalises distance from the previous\n  checkpoint, because an unconstrained optimiser finds degenerate outputs that score well on the reward\n  and badly with users. The leash strength is the central hyperparameter.\n- **Iteration.** Production recipes run several rounds: train, sample from the new model, collect new\n  comparisons on those samples, train again. Off-policy data from an old model loses value as the policy\n  moves.\n- **Evaluation per stage.** Forgetting after SFT, verbosity after preference training and reward hacking\n  after RL each get their own gates: capability benchmarks (the loss is the *alignment tax*), preference\n  win rates, task-specific checks.\n- **Safety and policy.** Refusals, sensitive topics and tool-use permissions are trained in with targeted\n  SFT data and preference pairs, then red-teamed, because a helpful but unsafe model cannot ship.\n\n### What post-training costs\n| Stage | Compute |\n|---|---|\n| Pre-training (for scale) | GPU-years |\n| SFT on tens of thousands of examples | GPU-hours on one node |\n| DPO on a comparable set | similar to SFT |\n| RLHF or GRPO | more, because sampling dominates; still orders of magnitude below pre-training |\n\nThe expensive part is the data: demonstrations and comparisons written or reviewed by people, plus the\nevaluation sets that tell you whether a round helped.","example":"### Worked scenario: post-training a support assistant\nA team post-trains an open-weight 8B base model as a support assistant for a software product. All sizes\nare illustrative.\n\n1. **SFT.** 6,000 demonstrations: real support tickets paired with answers by senior agents, rendered\n   through a chat template with a fixed system prompt. After two epochs it answers in the right voice and\n   format.\n   - Held-out eval: fluent, but invents feature names about one time in eight.\n   - It answers \"how do I delete my account\" with a wall of text where policy is a short, specific\n     procedure.\n2. **Preference optimisation.** For 2,000 held-out tickets, sample four responses each from the SFT model;\n   agents rank them with a rubric that penalises invented features and rewards concision.\n   - DPO on the pairs (top-ranked against each of the other three, 6,000 in all) cuts hallucinated\n     feature names by more than half on the held-out set and shortens answers.\n   - A capability benchmark before and after confirms general question-answering has not regressed.\n3. **Verifiable rewards.** Some answers must emit a JSON block that opens a help-desk ticket. Reward: +1 if\n   the JSON parses and matches the schema, +1 if the category is correct per a lookup table, 0 otherwise.\n   - A few hundred GRPO steps with 8 samples per prompt raise schema validity from 91% to above 99%.\n   - SFT alone never got there because the few malformed examples were drowned out.\n4. **Iteration.** Collect fresh rankings on the *new* model's outputs, because the old pairs no longer\n   describe its mistakes, and run one more DPO round before release.\n\n| Cost line | Size |\n|---|---|\n| Compute | a few hundred GPU-hours |\n| People | several weeks of agent time, the line that dominates the budget |\n\n### The data plumbing behind steps 2 and 3\nRankings become DPO pairs, and the JSON check becomes a reward function. Pure Python, runnable as is.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\n# 1) Turn agent rankings into DPO pairs: best response vs each of the others.\ndef rank_to_pairs(prompt, ranked):              # ranked: best first\n    best, *rest = ranked\n    return [{\"prompt\": prompt, \"chosen\": best, \"rejected\": r} for r in rest]\n\npairs = rank_to_pairs(\"How do I reset my password?\", [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"])\nprint(len(pairs), \"pairs per ticket ->\", 2000 * len(pairs), \"pairs for 2,000 tickets\")\n\n# 2) Verifiable reward for the ticket-opening JSON block (0, 1 or 2).\nCATEGORY = {\"login\": \"account\", \"invoice\": \"billing\", \"crash\": \"bug\"}   # lookup table\nREQUIRED = {\"title\": str, \"category\": str, \"priority\": int}\n\ndef ticket_reward(completion: str, keyword: str) -> int:\n    try:\n        obj = json.loads(completion)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        return 0\n    if not isinstance(obj, dict) or any(not isinstance(obj.get(k), t) for k, t in REQUIRED.items()):\n        return 0                                  # does not match the schema\n    return 1 + int(obj[\"category\"] == CATEGORY.get(keyword))\n\nsamples = ['{\"title\": \"Cannot log in\", \"category\": \"account\", \"priority\": 2}',\n           '{\"title\": \"Cannot log in\", \"category\": \"billing\", \"priority\": 2}',\n           '{\"title\": \"Cannot log in\", \"category\": \"account\"}',\n           'Sure! Here is your ticket: {...}']\nprint([ticket_reward(s, \"login\") for s in samples])   # [2, 1, 0, 0]\n```\n\nThe `{\"prompt\", \"chosen\", \"rejected\"}` record is the shape common preference trainers (for example TRL's\nDPO trainer) consume.","patterns":["**Gate every round on a fixed eval suite.** Before promoting a checkpoint, run format or schema compliance, win rate against the previous checkpoint on held-out prompts, refusal rate on harmful and benign borderline sets, mean response length and a general capability suite. Block promotion when the capability delta is negative, whatever the stage's own metric says.","**Refresh preference data on-policy each round.** Sample the next batch of comparison prompts from the current checkpoint, not the one that produced the last dataset. Monitor the gap between training-set win rate and real-user complaint rate; a widening gap means the pairs have gone stale.","**Use rejection sampling before full RL.** Sample several responses per prompt, score them with a verifier, judge or reward model, keep the best, and run an ordinary SFT pass on them. Monitor the pass rate of kept samples per round; when it plateaus, on-policy RL is the next step.","**Calibrate AI judges against a human slice.** Keep a few hundred human-ranked pairs and measure judge agreement on them. Swap response order between calls to cancel position bias, and track judge preference versus length. Re-audit whenever the domain or teacher model changes.","**Ship the chat template with the checkpoint.** Store the template in the tokenizer config saved next to the weights and render training and serving data with the same `apply_chat_template` call. Monitor format-compliance rate in production, which drops first when they diverge."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Base model\"] --> SFT[\"Stage 1: SFT\"]\n    H[\"People and teacher models\"] -->|\"demonstrations\"| SFT\n    SFT --> PO[\"Stage 2: RLHF or DPO\"]\n    J[\"Raters, reward model, AI judge\"] -->|\"ranked comparisons\"| PO\n    PO --> RL[\"Stage 3: RL, e.g. GRPO\"]\n    V[\"Programs: tests, schema checks\"] -->|\"verifiable reward\"| RL\n    RL --> G{\"Eval gate\"}\n    G -->|\"pass\"| S[\"Ship assistant\"]\n    G -->|\"fail or next round\"| N[\"Sample new model, rank again\"]\n    N -->|\"on-policy pairs\"| PO","caption":"Read it top to bottom: each stage consumes a different signal, and each round ends at an eval gate\n(format, win rate, refusals on harmful and benign sets, length, capability delta). A failed or\nunfinished round samples the new model and feeds fresh on-policy comparisons back into stage 2, with a\nKL leash to the reference model."},"interview":[{"q":"What does post-training add that pre-training cannot, and why are there three stages?","a":"- **Pre-training gives knowledge, not behaviour.** A base model has no notion of user and assistant and\n  no reason to be concise or to refuse.\n- **Each stage covers the previous one's limit.** SFT imitates demonstrations; preference optimisation\n  learns \"better than\" from comparisons; verifiable-reward RL discovers behaviours that need many\n  attempts, such as multi-step reasoning.\n- **The order is not arbitrary**, because each stage needs a policy that already produces what the\n  previous stage installed."},{"q":"After a DPO round, win rate on your training distribution is up but user complaints are flat. Why?","a":"- **Stale pairs are the first suspect.** The comparisons describe an older model's mistakes, so the\n  current model learns to beat errors it no longer makes.\n- **Fix:** sample the next comparison set from the current checkpoint, collect fresh rankings and rerun.\n- **Also check the rubric**, because raters or a judge rewarding length and confidence can raise win rate\n  without making answers more correct."},{"q":"When would you choose RLHF with PPO over DPO, and when not?","a":"- **RLHF is on-policy**, so it keeps improving as the policy changes and can exploit a strong reward model.\n- **DPO is offline, simpler and cheaper**: no reward model, no sampling loop, fewer hyperparameters.\n- **Pick DPO** when you have good pairs and a modest budget; pick on-policy RL when the target is far\n  from the SFT model and you can afford the sampling loop and the debugging."},{"q":"Roughly what does post-training an 8B assistant cost, and what dominates?","a":"- **Compute is small:** SFT on tens of thousands of examples is GPU-hours on one node; a full recipe like\n  the support-assistant example is a few hundred GPU-hours.\n- **RL costs most of the compute**, because generating many long samples per prompt dominates.\n- **People dominate the budget:** demonstrations, rankings and eval sets took several weeks of agent time\n  in the worked example."},{"q":"What would you put on the eval gate between post-training rounds?","a":"- **Stage metrics:** format or schema compliance and win rate against the previous checkpoint.\n- **Side effects:** refusal rate on harmful and benign borderline sets, mean response length, and the\n  delta on a general capability suite (the alignment tax).\n- **Rule:** a negative capability delta blocks promotion, whatever the stage's own metric says."}],"tradeoffs":["**Demonstrations vs comparisons.** Demonstrations are precise but expensive and only teach imitation. Comparisons are cheaper and express quality, but a poorly designed rubric teaches length and confidence instead of correctness.","**Offline vs on-policy.** SFT and DPO are simple and reproducible. On-policy RL keeps learning as the model changes, at the price of a sampling loop, more hyperparameters and harder debugging.","**Strong vs loose KL leash.** A strong leash preserves capabilities and prevents reward hacking but caps how far behaviour moves. Loosen it when the target is far from the SFT model and evaluation can catch drift.","**Trained-in safety vs over-refusal.** Training refusals in makes the model safer by default but raises over-refusal on benign edge cases. The balance depends on the audience and whether external guardrails exist.","**AI-generated data vs quality ceiling.** Model-written data and AI judges cut labelling cost dramatically but cap the student near the teacher. The saving becomes a liability where the teacher is weak, which only a human-labelled audit slice reveals."],"pitfalls":["**Preference training before SFT into the target format.** Both responses in every pair are off-distribution, so the loss moves without the behaviour improving. Fix: SFT first.","**Reusing stale comparison data across rounds.** The pairs describe an old model's errors, so training-distribution win rates rise while real-user complaints do not change. Fix: sample each round's comparisons from the current checkpoint.","**Measuring only the preference metric.** A model can win more comparisons while losing points on reasoning or coding benchmarks: catastrophic forgetting hiding behind a good-looking number. Fix: gate on a general capability suite too.","**Optimising the reward that is easy to compute rather than the one that matters.** Symptom: outputs that satisfy the schema or the judge yet are useless to the person reading them. Fix: audit a sample of high-reward outputs by hand each round.","**Measuring refusals only on harmful prompts.** Symptom: the model passes the safety set yet declines ordinary requests that merely mention a sensitive word. Fix: track refusal rate on a benign borderline set too."],"when":"- **Use when** prompting and retrieval cannot give the behaviour: a house format that must hold on every\n  response, a reliable refusal or escalation policy, a domain style prompting keeps drifting from, or a\n  multi-step skill that needs reward-driven practice.\n- **Order:** start with SFT (cheap and diagnostic); add preference optimisation when the failure is\n  \"fluent but worse than it should be\"; add verifiable-reward RL only when you can write a trustworthy\n  reward function.\n- **Measure before and after every stage:** format or schema compliance, win rate against the previous\n  checkpoint on held-out prompts, refusal rate on harmful and benign borderline sets, mean response\n  length, and the delta on a general capability suite.\n- **A stage that wins its own metric but moves the capability suite down is not a win.**\n- **Avoid when** an existing instruct model plus prompting already passes these gates; post-training it\n  yourself is then a maintenance liability, not an asset.\n- **Interviews:** name the three stages, say what signal each consumes, and explain why the ordering is\n  not arbitrary.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the SFT and DPO pipelines, writes the reward functions and chat templates, and owns the before/after evaluation harness that gates each round.","architect":"Decides whether the product needs post-training at all versus prompting and RAG, and sets the data, evaluation and safety gates a post-trained model must pass to ship.","fde":"Turns a customer's ticket history and reviewer time into demonstrations and comparisons, and manages the expectation that the data effort, not the GPUs, is the schedule risk."},"check":{"q":"A colleague proposes skipping SFT and running DPO directly on the base model using the\npreference pairs you already have, to save a stage. What goes wrong, and when could it work?","a":"DPO adjusts the relative log-probabilities of chosen versus rejected responses against a\nreference, and it assumes the policy already assigns reasonable probability to responses in that\nformat. A base model puts almost no mass on chat-formatted assistant turns, so the pairs are far\noff-distribution: the loss can fall by making both responses even less likely in ways that generalise\npoorly, and the model still does not reliably answer in the format. It could work if the pairs are in\nplain completion format the base model already produces, or if the \"base\" is actually an\ninstruction-tuned checkpoint. In practice, a short SFT on the chosen responses first is cheap and makes\nthe DPO stage stable, so the saving is not worth it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does post-training use comparisons between responses rather than only more demonstrations?","options":["Comparisons are the only data format PPO can consume","For most prompts there is no single correct answer, and ranking captures quality that imitation cannot express","Demonstrations cause catastrophic forgetting while comparisons do not","Comparisons require no human labour"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A demonstration says \"produce this\"; it cannot say \"this is better than that\". Preference data encodes the quality dimension directly, and people find ranking easier and cheaper than authoring ideal answers."},{"q":"What role does the KL penalty against a reference model play in RLHF, DPO and GRPO?","options":["It speeds up convergence by reducing gradient variance","It replaces the need for a separate evaluation set","It limits how far the policy can drift, preventing degenerate outputs that score well on the reward but read badly","It ensures the model forgets the base model's unsafe behaviours"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An unconstrained optimiser will exploit any gap between the reward and true quality. Penalising divergence from the previous checkpoint keeps the policy in the region where the reward model or preference data is trustworthy and preserves learned capabilities."},{"q":"In the support-assistant example, why did the JSON-validity problem need verifiable-reward RL rather than more SFT data?","options":["SFT cannot train on structured output","The rare malformed cases were drowned out in imitation data, while a programmatic reward targets exactly the failure","DPO cannot be run after SFT","RL is always cheaper than collecting demonstrations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Imitation learning improves the average behaviour; a 9% failure rate persists because nothing in the loss singles it out. A reward computed by a schema check pushes directly on the failure, and sampling many attempts finds the behaviour that fixes it."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","sources":["Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Zhou et al., 2023 — LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment","Official docs: Hugging Face TRL"],"why":"- **Prompting is not enough for strict behaviour.** The prompt is re-sent every call, drifts on edge\n  cases, and cannot install what the base does not already do. \"Open with a one-line summary, cite a\n  source id, never exceed 120 words\": a prompt gets most of the way most of the time; a fine-tune gets\n  there every time with a shorter prompt.\n- **Continued pre-training teaches the wrong thing.** It learns the *distribution* of your documents, not\n  how to answer about them: continued-pre-trained on support tickets, a model produces more support\n  tickets.\n- **You want a conditional.** Given this prompt, produce this response. SFT frames data as (prompt,\n  response) pairs and masks the loss so the prompt is context rather than target.\n- **Quality beats quantity.** LIMA showed about a thousand carefully written demonstrations can turn a\n  strong base into a capable assistant; quality and diversity matter more than count. SFT chooses which\n  existing behaviours to surface, and noisy data surfaces noisy behaviour.","how":"### Data format and the chat template\n- **Each example is a conversation:** optional system message, one or more user turns, and the assistant\n  turns you want produced.\n- **A chat template renders it** into one token sequence with special tokens marking each role, for\n  example `<|im_start|>assistant ... <|im_end|>`.\n- **The template is part of the model's contract.** Train with one and serve with another and the model\n  will not recognise where it is supposed to speak.\n\n### Loss masking\n1. Score the sequence with next-token cross-entropy exactly as in pre-training.\n2. Set labels for prompt and system tokens to an ignore value (−100 in PyTorch), so gradients flow only\n   from assistant tokens.\n3. In multi-turn data, every assistant turn is a target and every earlier turn is context.\n\n- **Why mask:** the model should not spend capacity predicting user questions, and in multi-turn data user\n  turns are often noisy or adversarial.\n- **Keep the end-of-turn token supervised.** `<|im_end|>` above (or the model's EOS) must sit *inside*\n  the supervised span. It is the only thing that teaches the model to stop; mask it with the prompt and\n  the model rambles past its answer into an invented next turn.\n\n### How the loss is averaged\nMasked examples contribute very different numbers of supervised tokens, say a 20-token and a 600-token\nanswer in one batch.\n\n| Rule | Weights each | Effect when lengths are skewed |\n|---|---|---|\n| Average over all supervised tokens (default in most trainers) | *token* equally | long responses dominate; over-trains the long-form style |\n| Average per example first | *conversation* equally | short answers get equal say |\n\n- **Neither is wrong, but the choice changes what the model learns.**\n- **Gradient accumulation can silently mix the two:** code that averages per micro-batch, then across\n  micro-batches, is neither rule. Check what your trainer does before comparing runs.\n\n### Where demonstrations come from\n- **Human-written:** the gold standard, but scarce.\n- **Distilled:** a stronger model drafts responses, humans or filters reject the bad ones, the student\n  trains on the survivors. Most practical SFT sets are partly this.\n- **Rejection-sampling fine-tuning:** sample several responses from the model itself and keep those a\n  verifier or reward model scores best.\n- **All three are still SFT;** only target provenance changes, and the student inherits whatever errors\n  survive the filter.\n\n### Full fine-tuning versus parameter-efficient\n| | Full SFT | LoRA / QLoRA |\n|---|---|---|\n| What trains | every weight | small adapters on a frozen base |\n| Memory | about 16 bytes per parameter (weights, gradients, AdamW state, mixed precision): a 7B model needs over 100 GB before activations | fits a single GPU |\n| Prefer when | target behaviour is far from the base, or data is large | many variants or a small budget |\n| Cost | hardware | some limit on how far behaviour can move |\n\n### Hyperparameters that actually matter\n- **Learning rate.** Far smaller than pre-training: roughly 1e-5 to 2e-5 for full fine-tuning of a\n  7B-class model, around ten times higher for LoRA adapters. Too high and the model forgets general\n  capability within a few hundred steps.\n- **Epochs.** Usually one to three. Small sets are easy to memorise; rising validation loss after epoch\n  two is the usual signal to stop.\n- **Sequence packing.** Concatenate short examples to fill the context window so no compute is wasted on\n  padding. Either reset position ids and mask attention across example boundaries, or accept small\n  cross-contamination; naive packing lets one example attend to its neighbour.\n- **Mixing in general data.** A slice of broad instruction data alongside domain examples prevents the SFT\n  form of catastrophic forgetting.\n\n### What SFT can and cannot teach\n- **Strong at:** format, register, length, persona, well-specified procedures, domain terminology.\n- **Weak where the demonstration is one of many acceptable answers.** It never sees a worse answer, so it\n  cannot learn that a confident, fluent, wrong answer is worse than a hedged right one.\n- **It can teach hallucination.** Demonstrations containing facts the base does not know teach it to\n  produce facts in that style whether or not it knows them.\n- **So keep demonstrations consistent with the base's knowledge**, or grounded in context provided in the\n  prompt.","example":"### One masked SFT step\nRender with the chat template, mask the prompt tokens, compute cross-entropy on assistant tokens only.\nTrainers such as TRL's `SFTTrainer` wrap exactly this with batching, packing and checkpointing.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16).cuda()\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-5, weight_decay=0.0)\n\nexample = [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a support assistant. Answer in at most two sentences.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"How do I rotate my API key?\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Open Settings > API keys, click Rotate next to the key, and \"\n                                     \"update any clients within 24 hours; the old key expires then.\"},\n]\nprompt = tok.apply_chat_template(example[:-1], tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\nfull = tok.apply_chat_template(example, tokenize=False)\nids = tok(full, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()            # [1, T]\nn_prompt = len(tok(prompt).input_ids)   # valid because the prompt ends at a template boundary,\n                                        # so its tokens are an exact prefix of the full sequence\n\nlabels = ids.clone()\nlabels[:, :n_prompt] = -100                                      # prompt tokens are context, not targets\nlogits = model(input_ids=ids).logits\nloss = F.cross_entropy(logits[:, :-1].reshape(-1, logits.size(-1)).float(),\n                       labels[:, 1:].reshape(-1), ignore_index=-100)\nloss.backward(); torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), 1.0)\nopt.step(); opt.zero_grad()\nprint(f\"trained on {(labels != -100).sum().item()} of {ids.numel()} tokens, loss {loss.item():.3f}\")\n```\n\n- **What it achieves:** with a few thousand such examples, two epochs and a cosine schedule, the model\n  learns the two-sentence support voice.\n- **Read the printed count critically.** System prompt, question and role markers (a few dozen tokens)\n  are context; only the answer plus its end-of-turn marker are targets, so expect roughly half the\n  sequence.\n- **Count equals full length:** masking is off.\n- **Count is zero:** the prompt length came from a different template and every label is −100, which\n  trains nothing useful while the loop runs without error. In plain PyTorch the mean over zero targets\n  is NaN, so a `nan` loss is the tell; do not let that step update the weights.\n- **One-minute test for both:** decode `ids[0][labels[0] != -100]` back to text and eyeball it.\n\n### Worked numbers: loss averaging and memory\nThe two silent budget questions before a run, as pure Python you can rerun:\n\n```python\ndef batch_weights(lengths):\n    \"\"\"Share of the gradient each example gets under the two averaging rules.\"\"\"\n    total = sum(lengths)\n    token_avg = [n / total for n in lengths]          # every supervised token counts once\n    example_avg = [1 / len(lengths)] * len(lengths)   # every conversation counts once\n    return token_avg, example_avg\n\ntok, ex = batch_weights([20, 600])                    # a short and a long answer\nprint([f\"{w:.1%}\" for w in tok], [f\"{w:.1%}\" for w in ex])\n# ['3.2%', '96.8%'] ['50.0%', '50.0%']\n\ndef full_ft_gb(params, bytes_per_param=16):          # weights + grads + AdamW state, mixed precision\n    return params * bytes_per_param / 1e9\n\nprint(full_ft_gb(7e9))                                # 112.0 GB before activations\n```\n\n| Question | Answer | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who drives the gradient in a batch of a 20- and a 600-token answer? | the long one, ~97% under token averaging | a set with many long answers pulls the model toward long-form style |\n| Does full SFT of a 7B model fit one 80 GB GPU? | no, ~112 GB before activations | shard across GPUs, or use LoRA/QLoRA |","patterns":["**Assert the mask on every run.** At data-load time, decode the supervised span of a few examples and log the supervised-token count per batch. Fail the job if any batch has zero supervised tokens or a count equal to its full length, and check that the end-of-turn token is inside the span.","**Let the trainer do assistant-only loss and packing.** In recent TRL versions, set `SFTConfig(assistant_only_loss=True, packing=True)` for conversational data rather than hand-rolling masks. Assistant-only loss needs a chat template that marks assistant spans with generation tags, so confirm yours does. Monitor the same supervised-token count, because a template change can silently break span detection.","**Keep the best checkpoint by validation loss.** Evaluate every epoch (or every few hundred steps) on held-out prompts and use `load_best_model_at_end=True` with `metric_for_best_model=\"eval_loss\"` in Hugging Face `TrainingArguments`, with matching eval and save strategies. Monitor validation loss for the upturn that signals memorisation.","**Replay general instruction data.** Mix a slice of broad instruction data into every epoch alongside domain examples. Monitor a general capability benchmark before and after each round; a drop means the replay slice is too small.","**Split held-out data by topic, not row.** Group examples by product area or intent and hold out whole groups, so the eval measures generalisation rather than paraphrase recall. Gate releases on a held-out task metric, never on training loss."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Demonstration: system, user, assistant turns\"] --> T[\"Render with the chat template\"]\n    T --> K[\"Tokenize into one sequence\"]\n    K --> M{\"Which role produced this token?\"}\n    M -->|\"system or user\"| C[\"Label minus 100: context only\"]\n    M -->|\"assistant, including end-of-turn\"| S[\"Label kept: supervised target\"]\n    C --> F[\"Forward pass, next-token cross-entropy\"]\n    S --> F\n    F --> A[\"Average: per token or per example\"]\n    A --> U[\"Backward, clip, AdamW step at about 1e-5\"]\n    U --> E{\"Validation loss rising after epoch 2?\"}\n    E -->|\"no\"| D\n    E -->|\"yes\"| B[\"Stop, keep best checkpoint\"]","caption":"Trace one demonstration through a training step: the chat template and the mask decide which tokens\nthe loss sees, and the end-of-turn token must land on the supervised side."},"interview":[{"q":"What is SFT, and how does it differ from continued pre-training on the same data?","a":"- **SFT learns a conditional:** given this prompt, produce this response, with loss on response tokens\n  only because the prompt is masked as context.\n- **Continued pre-training learns the distribution** of your documents: trained on support tickets, a\n  model produces more support tickets rather than answers to them.\n- **So pick SFT** when you want a format, persona or procedure, and continued pre-training when the\n  domain vocabulary itself is unfamiliar."},{"q":"Offline evals looked great, but in production the fine-tuned model ignores the system prompt. What do you check?","a":"- **Template parity first.** If the serving stack renders a different chat template from training (or a\n  client builds raw strings), the model does not recognise where the system turn is.\n- **Compare token ids, not text:** render one training example with the saved tokenizer's\n  `apply_chat_template` and diff it against a logged production request.\n- **Then check the data:** if every demonstration used the same fixed system prompt, the model never\n  learned that changing it should change behaviour; vary system prompts in the training set."},{"q":"When would you choose LoRA over full fine-tuning, and when not?","a":"- **LoRA when** budget is one GPU or you need many swappable variants on one base.\n- **Full fine-tuning when** the target behaviour is far from the base or the dataset is large, because\n  adapters limit how far behaviour can move.\n- **Memory decides fast:** full SFT needs about 16 bytes per parameter, so a 7B model needs about 112 GB\n  before activations, more than one 80 GB GPU."},{"q":"Your batch mixes 20-token and 600-token answers. Why does that matter?","a":"- **Under token averaging** (the default in most trainers) the long answer gets about 97% of the gradient.\n- **The model drifts toward long-form style** if long answers are common in the set.\n- **Per-example averaging** gives each conversation an equal 50% say; pick deliberately and check that\n  gradient accumulation does not silently mix the two rules."},{"q":"How much data and what hyperparameters would you start with for a style fine-tune of a 7B model?","a":"- **Data:** LIMA showed about a thousand carefully written demonstrations can be enough; quality and\n  diversity matter more than count.\n- **Learning rate:** roughly 1e-5 to 2e-5 for full fine-tuning, around ten times higher for LoRA.\n- **Epochs:** one to three, stopping when validation loss turns up, with a general-data slice mixed in to\n  prevent forgetting."}],"tradeoffs":["**Precise control vs data cost.** Demonstrations control format and style precisely, but are the most expensive data per example in post-training. That flips in your favour only when a strong model can draft them for human review.","**Full fine-tuning vs LoRA.** Full moves behaviour furthest and handles large datasets; LoRA fits one GPU and gives swappable adapters, but loses when the target distribution is far from the base.","**More epochs vs overfitting.** More epochs fit the demonstrations better but overfit sooner; the gain reverses once validation loss turns upward, typically between epochs two and three on small sets.","**Assistant-only loss vs signal per example.** It focuses capacity on the wanted behaviour, but very short responses give few supervised tokens per example, so you need more examples or longer responses."],"pitfalls":["**Serving with a different chat template from training.** Symptom: the model ignores the system prompt, answers as the user, or never emits the end-of-turn token.","**Forgetting to mask the prompt.** Symptom: suspiciously low training loss (it learns to predict easy prompt text) and a model that generates user questions.","**Fine-tuning on facts the base does not know.** Symptom: confident hallucination in the same style on unrelated questions, because it learned \"state facts fluently\" rather than the facts.","**Skipping a general-data mix.** Symptom: excellent on the domain eval, measurably worse on general reasoning or coding benchmarks after a single round.","**Judging success by training loss.** Low loss on memorised examples says nothing about generalisation; hold out prompts by topic, not just by row.","**Masking the end-of-turn token with the prompt.** Symptom: the model answers correctly, then keeps going, generating a fake user turn or repeating itself until the token limit."],"when":"- **Use when** the behaviour is consistent and describable by examples: a fixed output schema, a tone, a\n  domain vocabulary, a procedure, or a tool-calling convention that prompting keeps getting slightly\n  wrong.\n- **Avoid when** injecting knowledge that changes weekly; retrieval does that better and stays auditable.\n- **Baseline first:** measure how far prompting plus a few-shot examples gets you; the fine-tune must beat\n  that net of its maintenance cost.\n- **Measure during the work:** validation loss, a held-out task metric and a general capability benchmark,\n  together.\n- **Interviews:** explain loss masking, why the learning rate is tiny, and why SFT alone cannot fix\n  \"fluent but wrong\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the data pipeline that renders conversations through the chat template, masks prompts, packs sequences and tracks the supervised-token count per batch as a sanity check.","architect":"Decides whether a fine-tuned model is worth owning versus a prompt, including who re-trains it when the base model or the format changes.","fde":"Turns a customer's best human responses into demonstrations, negotiates the review process that keeps them consistent, and sets expectations that a few thousand good examples beat a hundred thousand scraped ones."},"check":{"q":"After SFT on 20,000 customer-service transcripts, the model's training loss is very low but it\nfrequently makes up product features that do not exist, and it sometimes continues the conversation by\nwriting the customer's next message. What went wrong, and how would you fix each symptom?","a":"Two separate bugs. Writing the customer's next turn means the loss was not masked to assistant\ntokens, so the model learned to generate user turns too; fix by setting prompt and user labels to the\nignore index and checking the supervised-token count per batch. Invented features point to data\ncontent: transcripts contain facts the base model never saw, so SFT taught it to assert product facts\nfluently without grounding. Fix by grounding responses in retrieved documentation placed in the prompt\nso the demonstrations show \"answer from the provided context\", filtering transcripts with unverifiable\nclaims, and following up with preference pairs that penalise invented details. The low training loss is\nconsistent with both problems, which is why a held-out task metric, not loss, should gate the release."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are the prompt tokens masked out of the SFT loss?","options":["To reduce the memory used by the backward pass","So the model learns to produce the response conditioned on the prompt rather than to predict the prompt itself","Because prompt tokens are already encoded in the KV cache","To prevent the model from seeing the system prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The prompt is context, not a target. Training on it wastes capacity on predicting user text and, in multi-turn data, teaches the model to generate user turns, which shows up as the model continuing the conversation on the user's behalf."},{"q":"Which learning rate is a sensible starting point for full fine-tuning a 7B model with SFT?","options":["3e-4, the usual pre-training peak","1e-2, because the dataset is small and needs fast learning","About 1e-5 to 2e-5, far below pre-training","1e-7, to avoid any change to the base weights"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fine-tuning starts from a good solution and must not destroy it. Learning rates near pre-training levels cause rapid forgetting of general capability; rates that are too low fail to move the behaviour in the few thousand steps available."},{"q":"A team wants the model to stop giving confident wrong answers and prefer hedged correct ones. Why is SFT the wrong tool on its own?","options":["SFT cannot change the tone of responses","SFT requires more data than preference methods","SFT only ever sees the demonstrated answer, so it cannot learn that one fluent answer is worse than another","Hedging requires a larger context window"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Imitation learning has no notion of a worse alternative. Expressing \"A is better than B\" needs comparison data consumed by RLHF or DPO; SFT can set the format, but the quality ranking comes from preference optimisation."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","sources":["Wei et al., 2021 — Finetuned Language Models Are Zero-Shot Learners","Sanh et al., 2021 — Multitask Prompted Training Enables Zero-Shot Task Generalization","Wang et al., 2022 — Self-Instruct: Aligning Language Models with Self-Generated Instructions","Chung et al., 2022 — Scaling Instruction-Finetuned Language Models"],"why":"- **The capability exists; the interface does not.** A base model does many tasks given few-shot\n  examples, because such patterns are common in its training data. But users write \"summarise this in two\n  lines\" or \"translate to French, formal register\", and a base model may echo that, answer something\n  adjacent, or continue the document.\n- **One task at a time does not scale.** A summarisation model and a translation model each worked on its\n  task and not at all on the next, and the number of tasks users invent is unbounded.\n- **Dozens of instruction-phrased tasks transfer.** FLAN and T0 showed that fine-tuning on many tasks\n  teaches instruction following on unseen ones, and the effect grows with distinct tasks and model size.\n  The model learns the mapping from instruction text to behaviour, which transfers.\n- **One huge single-task dataset fails** because the model overfits to that phrasing and task, and there\n  is nothing to generalise across. Instruction tuning works precisely because the data is wide and\n  shallow.","how":"### The data unit\n- **Instruction, optional input, output:** \"Classify the sentiment of the following review. Review: ...\n  Answer: positive\".\n- **Templates convert academic datasets.** A sentiment set becomes instruction data through several\n  phrasings (\"Is this review positive or negative?\", \"What is the sentiment of this text?\") and often\n  inversion (\"Write a negative review about a laptop\").\n- **Multiple templates per task matter** because the model must not key on exact wording; it has to learn\n  the intent.\n\n### Why diversity beats depth\n- **The finding (FLAN line):** more *tasks* improve held-out performance more than more examples of the\n  same tasks, and per-task gains saturate quickly.\n- **The mechanism:** each task is one more point in \"instruction space\"; generalisation is interpolation\n  between those points.\n- **The rule of thumb:** past a modest number of examples per task, a new task is worth more than more\n  rows of an old one.\n- **Balance the mixture.** Sample tasks roughly in proportion to size but cap the large ones\n  (examples-proportional mixing with a cap, as the FLAN work did), so a giant dataset does not dominate\n  the gradient.\n\n### Mixing prompt formats, not just tasks\n| Format | Prompt contains |\n|---|---|\n| Zero-shot | instruction only |\n| Few-shot | instruction plus worked examples |\n| Chain-of-thought | an answer with reasoning steps |\n\nLater FLAN work found that training on a mix of these improved both zero-shot and few-shot use versus a\nsingle format, for the same reason as templates: learn the intent, not one surface layout.\n\n### Where the instructions come from\n| Source | How | Strength | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Converted benchmarks** | re-template public NLP datasets | cheap, broad | academic in style |\n| **Human-written** | annotators write realistic requests and answers | most useful per example; the only way to get genuine user-style prompts | expensive |\n| **Model-generated (Self-Instruct)** | seed tasks → strong model invents and answers new instructions → filter for quality and diversity → repeat | how most open instruction datasets scaled: a few hundred seeds become tens of thousands of examples | student inherits teacher's errors and style; instructions cluster around what the teacher finds easy |\n\n### Reasoning and format data\n- **Keep chain-of-thought in the mixture.** It preserves and strengthens multi-step reasoning. Mixtures\n  without any reasoning data were found to *hurt* reasoning benchmarks, because the model learns to answer\n  tersely.\n- **Whatever is absent drifts away:** code, long-form answers, refusals, tool calls. If it is not in the\n  mixture, the model drifts from it.\n\n### How it relates to SFT and chat tuning\n- **Mechanically it *is* SFT:** next-token loss on output tokens, instruction masked.\n- **The difference is intent and data.** SFT covers any demonstration-based fine-tuning, including narrow\n  single-task adaptation; instruction tuning targets broad instruction following.\n- **Modern chat models** are instruction-tuned with multi-turn conversational data through a chat\n  template; the single-turn instruction/input/output format is its ancestor.\n\n### Scale dependence\n- **The original FLAN finding:** the benefit appeared only above a certain model size; small models got\n  *worse* on held-out tasks.\n- **The usual reading:** small models lack spare capacity for both the tasks and the meta-skill, so they\n  overfit the seen tasks.\n- **Later work with better mixtures narrowed the gap**, but the lesson stands: instruction tuning surfaces\n  latent ability, it does not create it.","example":"### A miniature FLAN-style mixture\nTwo task families, several templates each, balanced with a per-task cap. FLAN-style pipelines do this at\na scale of hundreds of tasks.\n\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(0)\n\nsentiment = [(\"The battery lasts two days, love it.\", \"positive\"),\n             (\"Screen cracked in a week.\", \"negative\")]\nsummar = [(\"Q3 revenue rose 12% on cloud demand while hardware fell 3%.\",\n           \"Cloud growth drove a 12% Q3 revenue rise despite weaker hardware.\")]\n\ntemplates = {\n    \"sentiment\": [\"Classify the sentiment of this review as positive or negative.\\nReview: {x}\",\n                  \"Is the following text positive or negative?\\n{x}\",\n                  \"What sentiment does this review express?\\n{x}\"],\n    \"summarize\": [\"Summarise in one sentence:\\n{x}\",\n                  \"Give a one-line summary of the text below.\\n{x}\"],\n}\nraw = {\"sentiment\": sentiment, \"summarize\": summar}\n\ndef build(cap=1000):\n    out = []\n    for task, rows in raw.items():\n        rows = rows[:cap]                         # cap large tasks so no task dominates\n        for x, y in rows:\n            for t in random.sample(templates[task], k=min(2, len(templates[task]))):\n                out.append({\"instruction\": t.format(x=x), \"output\": y, \"task\": task})\n    random.shuffle(out)\n    return out\n\ndata = build()\nfor ex in data[:3]:\n    print(ex[\"task\"], \"|\", ex[\"instruction\"].split(\"\\n\")[0], \"->\", ex[\"output\"])\n# Each example is then rendered through the chat template and trained with the SFT loss\n# (instruction masked, output supervised). Hold out whole tasks, not rows, to measure generalisation.\n```\n\n- **Hold out entire tasks for evaluation.** If the model handles a task family it never saw, the skill\n  transferred; if it only handles seen tasks, you have a multi-task model, not an instruction-following\n  one.\n\n### Worked numbers: why the cap matters\nSuppose the real mixture has three tasks with 500,000, 20,000 and 2,000 examples.\n\n| Mixing rule | Task A (500,000) | Task B (20,000) | Task C (2,000) | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Proportional to size | ~96% | ~4% | ~0.4% | effectively single-task |\n| Cap each at 10,000 | ~45% | ~45% | ~9% | small task becomes visible |\n\nThe large task loses examples it did not need. The cap is a knob you tune by watching per-task held-out\nscores, not a constant. The same arithmetic as a function:\n\n```python\ndef mixing_rates(sizes, cap=None):\n    \"\"\"Examples-proportional mixing, optionally capping each task's size first.\"\"\"\n    eff = {t: min(n, cap) if cap else n for t, n in sizes.items()}\n    total = sum(eff.values())\n    return {t: round(100 * n / total, 1) for t, n in eff.items()}\n\nsizes = {\"qa\": 500_000, \"extraction\": 20_000, \"rewrite\": 2_000}\nprint(mixing_rates(sizes))              # {'qa': 95.8, 'extraction': 3.8, 'rewrite': 0.4}\nprint(mixing_rates(sizes, cap=10_000))  # {'qa': 45.5, 'extraction': 45.5, 'rewrite': 9.1}\n```","patterns":["**Extend an instruct checkpoint, do not start from scratch.** Most teams take an already instruction-tuned open model and add their tasks to a general instruction mixture to keep breadth. Monitor a general benchmark suite before and after; a drop means the general share is too small.","**Hold out task families, not rows.** Tag every example with a task id and reserve whole families (and a sample of real user requests) for evaluation. Monitor the gap between seen-task and unseen-task scores; a large gap means a multi-task model, not an instruction follower.","**Write several templates per task, including inversions.** Generate at least two or three phrasings per task and evaluate on paraphrases never used in training. Monitor score variance across phrasings of the same request; high variance means the model keys on wording.","**Filter generated instructions for diversity.** When bootstrapping with Self-Instruct, drop a new instruction that is too similar to existing ones (the paper used a ROUGE-L overlap threshold of 0.7), and have humans review a sample. Monitor the task-type distribution so it does not collapse toward what the teacher finds easy.","**Keep reasoning, long-form and refusal data in every mix.** Reserve a fixed slice for chain-of-thought, code, long answers and refusals. Monitor multi-step reasoning benchmarks, mean output length and refusal rate, which fall first when these slices are dropped."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    BM[\"Public benchmarks\"] -->|\"several templates and inversions\"| P[\"Instruction pool\"]\n    HW[\"Human-written requests\"] --> P\n    SD[\"Seed tasks\"] --> SI[\"Self-Instruct: teacher generates\"]\n    SI -->|\"filter quality and diversity\"| P\n    P --> MX[\"Mix prompt formats\"]\n    MX --> CAP[\"Proportional mixing, per-task cap\"]\n    CAP --> SFT[\"SFT, instruction masked\"]\n    SFT --> IT[\"Instruction-tuned model\"]\n    HO[\"Held-out task families\"] --> EV{\"Works on unseen tasks?\"}\n    IT --> EV\n    EV -->|\"no: add tasks and phrasings\"| P\n    EV -->|\"yes\"| NX[\"Preference and safety training\"]","caption":"Follow how many narrow sources become one wide, balanced mixture: formats are mixed (zero-shot,\nfew-shot, chain-of-thought), large tasks are capped before proportional mixing, and the SFT loss masks\nthe instruction. Evaluation holds out whole task families and real user requests, not rows."},"interview":[{"q":"What is instruction tuning, and how is it different from plain SFT?","a":"- **Mechanically it is SFT:** next-token loss on the output, with the instruction masked.\n- **The difference is the data and the goal.** It trains on many tasks phrased as natural-language\n  instructions, so the model learns the mapping from instruction to behaviour and follows unseen ones\n  zero-shot.\n- **Plain SFT** also covers narrow single-task adaptation, which does not generalise to new requests."},{"q":"You have one 500,000-example task and two small ones. How do you build the mixture?","a":"- **Proportional mixing** gives the big task about 96% of samples, so the result is effectively\n  single-task.\n- **Cap each task** (for example at 10,000) before proportional mixing; the small tasks then become\n  visible at about 45% and 9%.\n- **Tune the cap** by watching per-task held-out scores, because it is a knob, not a constant."},{"q":"When would you not instruction-tune, and do a narrow fine-tune instead?","a":"- **When the product has one fixed task**, because a narrow SFT on that task is simpler and wins on it.\n- **When an existing instruct model already passes your evals** with prompting, since owning a tuned\n  model is a maintenance cost.\n- **Instruction tuning pays** when requests are open-ended and users will word them in ways you cannot\n  enumerate."},{"q":"In the original FLAN work, small models got worse on held-out tasks after instruction tuning. Why, and what does it imply?","a":"- **The usual reading is capacity:** small models lack room to learn both the seen tasks and the\n  meta-skill of following instructions, so they overfit the seen tasks.\n- **Instruction tuning surfaces latent ability; it does not create it.** If the base cannot do a task\n  few-shot, tuning on instructions will not make it appear.\n- **Implication:** check the base's few-shot ability on your task families before tuning, and expect\n  better mixtures to narrow, not erase, the gap for small models."},{"q":"What are the risks of scaling instruction data with Self-Instruct, and how do you control them?","a":"- **The student inherits the teacher's errors and style**, and generated tasks cluster around what the\n  teacher finds easy.\n- **Controls:** filter new instructions that are too similar to existing ones (the paper used a ROUGE-L\n  threshold of 0.7), have humans review a sample, and keep human-written seeds.\n- **Monitor** the task-type distribution across bootstrap rounds so diversity does not collapse."}],"tradeoffs":["**Broad and shallow vs narrow and deep.** Broad mixtures generalise to unseen instructions; narrow ones excel at the seen task. When you only ever need one task, the narrow fine-tune wins on that task.","**Model-generated scale vs teacher limits.** Generated instructions scale cheaply and widen coverage but import the teacher's errors and style; the saving reverses if the teacher is weaker than your target or your domain is unusual.","**Templated benchmarks vs real requests.** Templating public benchmarks gives thousands of tasks for free, but academic phrasings differ from real user requests, so a slice of genuine human-written prompts is worth more per example.","**Reasoning and long-form data vs cost.** Including them keeps those abilities but lengthens average outputs and training cost; omit them only when the deployment never needs them."],"pitfalls":["**One template per task.** Symptom: the model follows training phrasings exactly and fails when a user words the same request differently.","**Only zero-shot prompts.** Symptom: confusion, or copying the examples' format, when a user pastes a few demonstrations into the prompt.","**One huge dataset dominating the mixture.** Symptom: excellent at that task, quietly regressed on everything else, visible only if you evaluate per task family.","**Held-out rows of seen tasks called generalisation.** The number looks great and says nothing about unseen instructions, which is what users send.","**No chain-of-thought or long answers in the data.** Symptom: terse replies and a drop on multi-step reasoning benchmarks after tuning.","**Treating \"instruction tuned\" as \"aligned\".** An instruction-tuned model follows harmful instructions as readily as benign ones until preference training and safety data add a refusal policy."],"when":"- **Use when** you need a model that follows a wide range of requests it has not seen verbatim: a general\n  assistant, an internal copilot fielding open-ended tasks, or a base for later preference training.\n- **Avoid when** the product has one fixed task; a narrow SFT on that task is simpler and better.\n- **In practice** start from an already instruction-tuned open model and add your tasks to a general\n  mixture to keep breadth, rather than building the mixture from scratch.\n- **Measure** with held-out *task families* and a general benchmark suite.\n- **Interviews:** explain why diversity beats depth, how Self-Instruct bootstraps data, and how\n  instruction tuning differs from plain SFT and from preference optimisation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the template and mixing pipeline, caps task sizes, holds out task families, and verifies the chat template renders every example the way serving will.","architect":"Chooses between adopting an instruction-tuned open model and extending it with domain tasks versus narrow tuning, based on how open-ended the product's requests really are.","fde":"Collects the real phrasings a customer's users send and converts them into instruction data, because academic templates rarely match how a procurement analyst actually asks."},"check":{"q":"Your instruction-tuned model scores well on held-out examples from all 40 training tasks, but\nusers complain it misunderstands simple requests. What is the likely gap in your evaluation and data,\nand what would you change?","a":"Held-out rows from seen tasks only measure multi-task performance; they never test whether the\nmodel generalises to new instructions, which is what users send. The likely causes are too few\ndistinct tasks and too few phrasings per task, so the model keys on training wording. I would first\nre-evaluate on entirely held-out task families and on a sample of real user requests to confirm the\ngap, then widen the mixture: add tasks, write several templates per task including inverted forms,\nand add a slice of genuine user-style instructions, possibly bootstrapped with Self-Instruct and\nfiltered by humans. I would also check that the mixture is balanced, since one large task can crowd out\nthe generalisation signal, and confirm the model is large enough to benefit, because small models\ntend to overfit seen tasks."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding more distinct tasks help instruction tuning more than adding more examples of existing tasks?","options":["Larger datasets cause overfitting regardless of task count","Each new task adds a point in instruction space, and generalisation comes from covering that space, not from depth on one task","More tasks reduce the learning rate needed","Examples of the same task are usually duplicates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The target skill is \"map an instruction to a behaviour\" in general. Depth on one task refines that task; breadth across tasks teaches the mapping itself, which is what transfers to unseen instructions."},{"q":"What is the main risk of building an instruction dataset with Self-Instruct from a strong teacher model?","options":["The resulting data cannot be used with a chat template","It produces too few examples to be useful","The student inherits the teacher's mistakes and style, and generated tasks cluster around what the teacher finds easy","The loss must be computed on instruction tokens as well"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bootstrapping scales data cheaply, but every error and bias in the teacher's answers becomes a training target, and diversity narrows toward the teacher's comfort zone unless filtering and human-written seeds counteract it."},{"q":"After instruction tuning, a model answers multi-step maths questions worse than the base model did with few-shot prompts. What most likely happened?","options":["The learning rate was too low to change behaviour","The mixture contained no chain-of-thought or long-form data, so the model learned to answer tersely","Instruction tuning always reduces reasoning ability","The chat template removed the ability to use few-shot prompts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Whatever is absent from the mixture drifts away. Without reasoning traces in the data the model learns that outputs are short answers, which suppresses the step-by-step behaviour the base model would show when prompted."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/rlhf","sources":["Christiano et al., 2017 — Deep Reinforcement Learning from Human Preferences","Stiennon et al., 2020 — Learning to Summarize from Human Feedback","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Gao et al., 2022 — Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization"],"why":"- **SFT cannot rank.** After SFT a model answers in the right format, but the demonstration it imitated\n  is one of many acceptable answers; it cannot know a confident wrong answer is worse than a careful\n  right one.\n- **Comparing is easier than writing.** Perfect demonstrations are slow, and annotators' own answers are\n  often worse than the model's. People agree with each other on comparisons much more than on what the\n  ideal response is.\n- **A comparison is not a next-token target**, only a scalar notion of goodness. That is an RL problem:\n  the model is a *policy* emitting a response (an action sequence), the environment returns a *reward*,\n  the objective is expected reward.\n- **The recipe has a lineage.** Christiano et al. showed it in 2017 for control tasks: fit a reward model\n  to comparisons, optimise the policy, keep collecting comparisons on the new policy. Stiennon et al. and\n  InstructGPT applied it to language: the jump from \"completes text\" to \"helps\".\n- **The reward model amortises human labels.** Optimising directly against human ratings is hopeless at\n  RL's data appetite: PPO scores hundreds of thousands of samples or more per run, far beyond what raters\n  could label. Tens of thousands of comparisons train a model that scores\n  every sample for free. The price: it is wrong in places, and the policy will find them.","how":"### Step 1: collect comparisons\n1. Sample prompts from the target distribution.\n2. The current policy generates two or more responses per prompt.\n3. Raters pick the better one, or rank several; rankings are decomposed into pairs.\n\n- **Rater engineering is a large part of the work:** guidelines, calibration sessions and agreement\n  measurement, because a reward model trained on inconsistent labels learns noise.\n\n### Step 2: train the reward model\n- **Architecture:** r_φ(x, y) is often the SFT model (or a smaller sibling, as in InstructGPT) with its\n  output head replaced by a scalar head; it reads prompt and response and emits one number.\n- **Loss:** Bradley–Terry pairwise, which says the probability y_w beats y_l is the sigmoid of their score\n  difference:\n\n```\nL_RM(φ) = − E_(x, y_w, y_l) [ log σ( r_φ(x, y_w) − r_φ(x, y_l) ) ]\n```\n\n- **Only differences matter**, so the reward scale is arbitrary; implementations often normalise it.\n- **Why this loss:** it models the data you have (comparisons) rather than absolute scores nobody gave\n  you.\n- **Why start from SFT:** the reward model must judge the kind of text the policy produces, and a model\n  already representing that distribution learns the judgement from far fewer comparisons.\n- **Measure it** as accuracy on held-out comparison pairs, read against inter-annotator agreement on the\n  same pairs: a reward model cannot meaningfully beat the rate at which humans agree with each other.\n\n### Step 3: optimise the policy with PPO\nExpected reward with a KL penalty to the reference policy π_ref (the SFT model):\n\n```\nJ(θ) = E_x E_y~π_θ [ r_φ(x, y) − β · KL( π_θ(·|x) ‖ π_ref(·|x) ) ]\n```\n\n**Reward shaping.** The KL term is applied per token as a shaped reward\n(−β·[log π_θ(y_t|·) − log π_ref(y_t|·)]); the reward model's score is added at the final token, because\nit judges the finished response and says nothing about individual tokens.\n\n**Credit assignment.** Which of 300 tokens earned the score? PPO uses a learned *value model* V(s_t)\nthat predicts the eventual return from each prefix:\n1. TD error `δ_t = r_t + V(s_{t+1}) − V(s_t)`: did token t make the outcome look better or worse than\n   expected?\n2. Generalised advantage estimation sums these with a decay: `A_t = Σ_k (γλ)^k · δ_{t+k}`. γ is usually 1\n   for text; λ trades bias against variance.\n\n**The PPO loop:**\n1. Sample responses from the current policy.\n2. Compute rewards, then advantages.\n3. Update the policy with the clipped surrogate `min(ρ·A, clip(ρ, 1−ε, 1+ε)·A)`, where ρ is the ratio of\n   new to old token probabilities.\n\n- **Why clip:** policy-gradient steps on sampled text are high-variance; the clip stops any one batch\n  moving the policy too far.\n- **PPO-ptx:** InstructGPT also mixed in pre-training gradients to limit the capability loss they called\n  the alignment tax.\n\n### Why the KL penalty is not optional\n- **The reward model is accurate near its training data** and increasingly wrong far from it.\n- **An unconstrained policy climbs into high-score, low-quality regions:** longer and longer answers,\n  flattery, repeated keywords, an authoritative tone regardless of correctness, or gibberish that happens\n  to score well.\n- **Gao et al. measured this *overoptimisation*:** as KL from the reference grows, proxy reward keeps\n  rising while true reward peaks and then falls.\n- **β sets how far you let it go.** Tune it against held-out human evaluation, not against the reward\n  model.\n\n### What is running during training\n| Network | State | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Policy | trained | generates responses |\n| Reference | frozen copy | KL penalty |\n| Reward model | frozen | scores finished responses |\n| Value model | trained | per-prefix return estimate for advantages |\n\n- **Memory and orchestration are the burden:** several copies of a large policy's weights, optimiser\n  state for two of them, and a fast generation loop, because sampling dominates wall-clock time.\n- **This cost is the main reason DPO and GRPO exist.**\n\n### Iteration and rejection sampling\n- **Iterate because the reward model is trusted only near its data:** after a PPO round, sample from the\n  new policy, collect new comparisons, retrain the reward model, repeat.\n- **Rejection sampling fine-tuning is the cheaper cousin:** sample k responses, keep the top-scored, SFT\n  on those. Same reward model, no RL machinery, and often the first round before PPO.\n\n### Where the comparisons come from\n- **\"Human feedback\" is increasingly partly AI feedback.** In RLAIF a strong model ranks pairs against\n  written principles (the idea behind Constitutional AI); humans label a smaller slice to audit the\n  judge.\n- **The rest of the pipeline is unchanged**, but the judge's biases (for length, for its own style)\n  become the reward model's biases, so the audit slice is not optional.","example":"### Reward-model loss and PPO reward shaping\nThe two numerical pieces that make RLHF concrete. Shapes are for one prompt with two candidate responses.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\n\n# --- Reward model training step: Bradley-Terry loss on one (chosen, rejected) pair ---\ndef rm_loss(score_chosen, score_rejected):\n    # score_* : [batch] scalars from the reward head at the final token\n    return -F.logsigmoid(score_chosen - score_rejected).mean()\n\ns_w, s_l = torch.tensor([1.3]), torch.tensor([0.4])\nprint(\"RM loss:\", rm_loss(s_w, s_l).item())          # 0.341 ; falls as the gap widens\n\n# --- PPO reward shaping for one sampled response of T tokens ---\nbeta = 0.05\nlogp_policy = torch.tensor([-2.1, -0.4, -1.7, -0.9, -0.3])   # log pi_theta(y_t | x, y_<t)\nlogp_ref    = torch.tensor([-2.0, -0.6, -1.5, -0.9, -2.1])   # log pi_ref(y_t | x, y_<t)\nrm_score    = 0.8                                            # r_phi(x, y) for the whole response\n\nper_token_kl = logp_policy - logp_ref                        # positive where policy drifted up\nrewards = -beta * per_token_kl                               # KL penalty at every token ...\nrewards[-1] += rm_score                                      # ... plus the RM score at the end\nprint(\"shaped rewards:\", rewards.tolist())                   # [0.005, -0.01, 0.01, 0.0, 0.71]\nprint(\"sequence KL estimate:\", per_token_kl.sum().item())    # 1.7 ; watched as a drift gauge\n# PPO then computes advantages from these rewards with a value model and applies the clipped\n# surrogate objective to the policy's token log-probs. If the KL estimate climbs while held-out\n# human win-rate stalls, beta is too low: the policy is optimising the reward model's mistakes.\n```\n\n- **The last token carries a large KL penalty.** Its log-ratio is +1.8: the policy puts about six times\n  (e^1.8) more mass on it than the reference, so −β·1.8 ≈ −0.09 is subtracted from the 0.8 reward-model\n  score at that position.\n- **A few tokens drifted far up is the exploitation pattern**, which is why per-token KL, not just the\n  final score, is logged during training.\n- **Single-sample log-ratios can be negative** on individual tokens (tokens 1 and 3 above, where the\n  policy moved down). Only the sum or expectation over a sequence estimates the KL, so the sequence-level\n  number is the one watched.\n\n### From shaped rewards to advantages (GAE)\nThe next step PPO takes with those rewards, in pure Python. The value estimates are illustrative.\n\n```python\ndef gae(rewards, values, gamma=1.0, lam=0.95):\n    \"\"\"Advantages A_t = sum_k (gamma*lam)^k * delta_{t+k}, computed backwards.\"\"\"\n    adv, running = [0.0] * len(rewards), 0.0\n    for t in reversed(range(len(rewards))):\n        next_v = values[t + 1] if t + 1 < len(values) else 0.0   # terminal state is worth 0\n        delta = rewards[t] + gamma * next_v - values[t]           # TD error for token t\n        running = delta + gamma * lam * running\n        adv[t] = running\n    return adv\n\nrewards = [0.005, -0.01, 0.01, 0.0, 0.71]   # shaped rewards from the example above\nvalues  = [0.60, 0.62, 0.61, 0.64, 0.66]    # value model's guess of the return at each prefix\nprint([round(a, 3) for a in gae(rewards, values)])\n# [0.1, 0.079, 0.104, 0.067, 0.05]  every token gets credit for the final score,\n# scaled by how much better than expected the outcome turned out from that prefix\n```\n\n- **The final score reaches early tokens** only through the value model: a bad value model gives noisy\n  advantages, which is one reason PPO for LLMs is fragile and why GRPO drops it.","patterns":["**Early-stop on human win rate, not reward.** Run a held-out human or strong-judge win-rate eval at fixed step intervals and plot it against sequence KL from the reference. Keep the checkpoint at the KL where win rate peaks; alert when proxy reward rises while win rate is flat.","**Gate the reward model before PPO.** Measure accuracy on held-out comparison pairs and on fresh samples from the current policy, and compare it with inter-annotator agreement on the same pairs. Do not start RL when accuracy on the new policy's outputs is far below the in-distribution number.","**Watch length as an exploit signal.** Log mean response length and reward-versus-length correlation every round. If length creeps up, add a length penalty to the reward or use length-controlled win rates, and re-check rater guidelines for a verbosity preference.","**Run best-of-n rejection sampling as round one.** Sample several responses per prompt, keep the top reward-model score, and SFT on them before setting up PPO. Monitor the reward gap between best and median sample; when it shrinks, the cheap round has done its work.","**Use an established RL stack.** Libraries such as Hugging Face TRL (`RewardTrainer`, PPO trainer) or OpenRLHF provide the four-model orchestration and generation loop. Still log per-token KL, value loss, clip fraction and reward per step, because default hyperparameters do not transfer between models."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    SFT[\"SFT model\"] -->|\"frozen copy\"| REF[\"Reference policy\"]\n    SFT -->|\"initialise\"| POL[\"Policy\"]\n    POL -->|\"two or more responses\"| RAT[\"Raters or AI judge rank\"]\n    RAT -->|\"chosen and rejected pairs\"| RM[\"Reward model, Bradley-Terry\"]\n    POL -->|\"sample responses\"| SC[\"Shaped reward: RM score, KL penalty\"]\n    RM -->|\"score at final token\"| SC\n    REF -->|\"per-token log-probs\"| SC\n    SC --> VM[\"Value model, GAE advantages\"]\n    VM --> PPO[\"PPO clipped update\"]\n    PPO --> POL\n    PPO -.->|\"proxy up, win rate down\"| CHK{\"Overoptimised?\"}\n    CHK -.->|\"raise beta, recollect pairs\"| RAT","caption":"The three steps as one loop: comparisons train the reward model, PPO optimises the policy against it\nunder a KL leash, and fresh comparisons on the new policy restart the cycle."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through RLHF end to end. Why does each piece exist?","a":"- **Comparisons:** raters rank responses, because comparing is easier and more consistent than writing\n  ideal answers.\n- **Reward model:** trained with the Bradley–Terry loss, it amortises tens of thousands of labels over\n  every sample PPO generates.\n- **PPO with a KL penalty:** maximises reward while staying near the SFT reference, because the reward\n  model is only trustworthy near its training data."},{"q":"Why does PPO for LLMs need a value model, and what goes wrong when it is poor?","a":"- **Credit assignment:** the reward arrives once at the final token, so a value model V(s_t) estimates the\n  return from each prefix to compute TD errors and GAE advantages.\n- **A poor value model gives noisy advantages**, so updates push on the wrong tokens and training becomes\n  fragile.\n- **That cost and fragility** is one reason GRPO drops the value model and uses group-relative rewards."},{"q":"When would you not run full PPO, even though you have comparison data?","a":"- **When the dataset is fixed and the budget small:** DPO on the same pairs avoids four models in memory\n  and a sampling loop.\n- **When a cheaper round suffices:** best-of-n rejection sampling plus SFT uses the same reward model\n  without RL machinery.\n- **PPO pays** when on-policy iteration clearly helps, you can keep collecting comparisons, and the team\n  can monitor an RL loop."},{"q":"Your answers get longer every RLHF round. Diagnose and fix it.","a":"- **It is a reward exploit:** raters and reward models often prefer longer answers, so the policy learns\n  verbosity.\n- **Confirm** by logging mean length and the correlation between reward and length per round.\n- **Fix** with a length penalty or length-controlled win rates, tighten rater guidelines, and raise β if\n  KL from the reference is also climbing."},{"q":"How do you know the reward model is good enough to start PPO?","a":"- **Held-out pair accuracy** compared with inter-annotator agreement on the same pairs, because it cannot\n  meaningfully beat how often humans agree.\n- **Accuracy on fresh samples from the current policy**, since that is where PPO will query it.\n- **Do not start** if accuracy on the new policy's outputs is far below the in-distribution number."}],"tradeoffs":["**Learned reward vs off-distribution error.** It lets you optimise against millions of samples from tens of thousands of human labels, but is wrong off-distribution, so the gain reverses once the policy drifts past its data.","**On-policy PPO vs cost.** PPO adapts as the policy improves and can reach behaviours no demonstration contains, at the cost of four models in memory, a sampling loop and fragile hyperparameters. DPO wins when the dataset is fixed and the budget small.","**Low vs high KL coefficient.** Low allows bigger behaviour changes and faster reward gains; high preserves capability. The right value flips with how trustworthy the reward model is far from its data.","**Separate vs single reward models.** Separate helpfulness and safety models allow explicit trade-offs but need a combination rule and twice the comparison data. A single model is simpler and wins when the goals rarely conflict on your prompts."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting the reward model's score as success.** Symptom: rising proxy reward with flat or falling human win rate, the overoptimisation signature. Fix: select checkpoints by held-out human or judge win rate.","**Length and sycophancy bias in comparisons.** Raters prefer longer, more agreeable answers, so the policy learns verbosity and flattery, visible as answer length creeping up round over round.","**Noisy or inconsistent labels.** Low inter-annotator agreement yields a reward model that cannot separate good from bad, and PPO then optimises noise.","**Reusing a reward model after the policy moved.** Trained on an old policy's comparisons, it is confidently wrong on the new policy's typical outputs. Fix: collect comparisons on the current policy and retrain it each round.","**Treating PPO hyperparameters as defaults.** A too-large clip range or learning rate shows up as sudden KL spikes and a collapse into repetitive text within a few hundred steps."],"when":"- **Use when** demonstrations cannot express what you want, you can collect reliable comparisons on your\n  own prompt distribution, and you have the engineering capacity to run and monitor an RL loop.\n- **Prefer for most application teams:** DPO on the same comparison data, or rejection sampling with a\n  reward model; reserve full PPO for cases where on-policy iteration clearly pays.\n- **Measure** with human or strong-judge win rates on held-out prompts, track KL from the reference, and\n  run a general capability suite to catch the alignment tax.\n- **Interviews:** draw the three stages, write the Bradley–Terry loss, explain why the KL penalty exists,\n  and describe reward overoptimisation and how you would detect it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements comparison collection, trains and validates the reward model on held-out pairs, and watches KL, reward and win-rate curves together during PPO.","architect":"Decides whether on-policy RL is justified over DPO or rejection sampling given the data, compute and evaluation budget, and owns the safety-versus-helpfulness reward policy.","fde":"Runs the rater calibration with a customer's domain experts, measures agreement, and explains why the reward model, not the GPUs, is the component that decides what the model becomes."},"check":{"q":"During PPO, the reward model's average score on sampled responses rises steadily for a thousand\nsteps, but a weekly human evaluation shows win rate against the SFT model peaked at step 300 and has\nsince fallen. What is happening, how do you confirm it, and what do you change?","a":"This is reward overoptimisation: the policy has moved into regions where the reward model\noverestimates quality, so the proxy rises while real quality falls. I would confirm by plotting the KL\nfrom the reference over steps and checking that the human win rate peaks at a particular KL and\ndeclines beyond it, and by inspecting samples for the usual exploits, such as growing length,\nflattery, or repeated phrasing. Remedies: raise the KL coefficient or stop at the KL where human\nevaluation peaked; collect fresh comparisons on the current policy's outputs and retrain the reward\nmodel so it is accurate where the policy now lives; consider a reward ensemble or a length penalty if\nthe exploit is identifiable. The deeper fix is institutional: never gate releases on the reward model's\nown score."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does RLHF train a reward model instead of optimising the policy directly on human ratings?","options":["Human ratings are not differentiable, but a neural reward model is","RL scores far more sampled responses than raters could label; the reward model amortises tens of thousands of human comparisons over all of them","PPO can only consume scalar rewards from a network of the same size as the policy","Humans cannot compare responses reliably"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Policy-gradient methods are sample-hungry. A reward model turns a modest number of human comparisons into a function that can score every sampled response for free, which is the only way the RL loop is affordable. Differentiability is irrelevant: PPO does not backpropagate through the reward."},{"q":"What does the Bradley-Terry loss −log σ(r(x,y_w) − r(x,y_l)) assume about the data?","options":["That each response has an absolute quality score from 0 to 1","That rewards are normally distributed across prompts","That the probability one response beats another is a sigmoid of their score difference, so only differences matter","That the chosen response is always correct"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The loss models comparisons, not absolute scores, which matches the data raters actually produce. Because only differences enter, the reward scale is arbitrary and implementations normalise it."},{"q":"A team sets the KL coefficient β to zero to \"let the model improve freely\". What is the most likely result?","options":["Training diverges immediately because the gradients explode","The model stops changing, because there is no penalty to push against","Proxy reward rises while real quality degrades into verbose, repetitive or sycophantic outputs that exploit the reward model","The reward model is retrained automatically to compensate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without the leash the policy climbs the reward model's surface into regions far from its training data where its scores are unreliable. The classic exploits are length, flattery and degenerate text that happen to score well."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/dpo","sources":["Rafailov et al., 2023 — Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Official docs: Hugging Face TRL"],"why":"- **PPO's machinery is heavy.** Four models resident, two training; a generation loop dominating\n  wall-clock time; interacting hyperparameters (clip range, value loss weight, KL coefficient, learning\n  rate schedule) that take experienced teams weeks to stabilise; and a reward model to train, validate\n  and keep current.\n- **The tool outsizes the problem.** For a team with ten thousand preference pairs and one node of GPUs,\n  that is a mismatch.\n- **SFT on chosen responses wastes half the signal.** The model learns what good looks like but nothing\n  about bad, and cannot push probability *away* from rejected answers.\n- **A naive contrastive loss has no leash.** Raising chosen and lowering rejected log-probability can be\n  satisfied by driving the rejected sequence to zero probability, trashing everything nearby.\n- **DPO combines leash and comparison analytically.** Invert the exact optimum of \"reward minus β·KL\",\n  substitute into Bradley–Terry, and you get a policy-only loss for the same optimisation problem, with\n  no reward model and no sampling. The leash is inside the formula.","how":"### The derivation in four lines\n1. The KL-regularised objective `max_π E_y~π[r(x,y)] − β·KL(π ‖ π_ref)` is maximised by\n   `π*(y|x) = π_ref(y|x)·exp(r(x,y)/β) / Z(x)`, where Z(x) normalises over all responses.\n2. Solve for the reward: `r(x,y) = β·log(π*(y|x)/π_ref(y|x)) + β·log Z(x)`.\n3. Put that into the Bradley–Terry probability that y_w beats y_l. The `β·log Z(x)` term is the same for\n   both responses to the same prompt, so it cancels in the difference.\n4. The resulting loss over the policy θ is:\n\n```\nL_DPO(θ) = − E [ log σ( β·log π_θ(y_w|x)/π_ref(y_w|x)  −  β·log π_θ(y_l|x)/π_ref(y_l|x) ) ]\n```\n\n- **Implicit reward:** each log-ratio term is `r̂(x,y) = β·log(π_θ(y|x)/π_ref(y|x))`, how much more the\n  policy likes this response than the reference does, scaled by β.\n- **Goal:** make the implicit reward margin between chosen and rejected large and positive.\n\n### What the gradient does\nDifferentiating the loss gives `−β·σ(r̂_l − r̂_w)·[∇log π_θ(y_w|x) − ∇log π_θ(y_l|x)]`.\n\n- **The bracket** raises the chosen sequence's likelihood and lowers the rejected one's.\n- **The weight in front** is the sigmoid of how wrong the current implicit ranking is.\n- **So correctly ranked, well-separated pairs contribute almost nothing; backwards pairs contribute most.**\n  That weighting is what the naive contrastive loss lacks, and it stops DPO endlessly pushing\n  already-separated pairs.\n\n### The role of β\n- **Same KL coefficient as RLHF, read the other way round:** it scales how large a log-ratio is needed for\n  a given preference probability.\n- **Small β (TRL's default is 0.1)** lets the policy move far from the reference to separate pairs;\n  **large β** keeps it close.\n- **No explicit KL term**, but reference log-probabilities appear in every pair, so drift is penalised\n  implicitly.\n\n### Sequence log-probabilities\n- **Each term is a sum** of per-token log-probabilities over response tokens only, prompt masked.\n- **Cache the reference.** It is frozen, so its log-probs for the dataset can be computed once. Training\n  memory drops to one trainable model plus stored numbers, versus RLHF's four networks.\n- **LoRA trick:** disable the adapter for the reference forward pass instead of loading a second copy.\n\n### Why \"offline\" matters\n- **DPO uses whichever pairs you give it.** Pairs from a different model compare sequences the policy\n  would rarely generate, so the gradient's effect on its actual outputs is indirect: the\n  distribution-shift cost of dropping the sampling loop.\n- **The reference should sit near the data.** The derivation treats π_ref as the distribution the pairs\n  live near; with only someone else's pairs, the DPO paper advises first SFT-ing the reference on the\n  chosen responses.\n- **The better fix: go on-policy in rounds.** Generate candidates with the policy (or its SFT ancestor),\n  label those, and iterate (often called iterative or online DPO). This recovers much of on-policy RLHF's\n  advantage with the simple loss.\n\n### Known failure: likelihood displacement\n- **The loss only sees the *difference* in log-ratios.** The margin can grow while both chosen and\n  rejected probabilities fall, as long as rejected falls faster.\n- **In practice chosen log-probability often decreases** during DPO, and the mass goes somewhere\n  unobserved, sometimes to worse responses.\n- **Detect** by monitoring chosen log-probability, not just the margin. **Mitigate** by mixing in a small\n  SFT term on chosen responses, or by switching to a variant.\n\n### The variants, and what each one changes\n| Variant | Change | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| **IPO** | squared loss pulling the margin toward a fixed target, not infinity | with near-deterministic preferences DPO keeps rewarding ever-larger margins and overfits |\n| **KTO** | no pairs: each response labelled desirable or undesirable | fits thumbs-up / thumbs-down product feedback that never comes as matched comparisons |\n| **ORPO** | no reference; odds-ratio penalty added to an ordinary SFT loss | folds SFT and preference training into one stage |\n| **SimPO** | no reference; *length-normalised* average log-prob as implicit reward, plus a target margin | directly attacks the length bias of summed log-probs |\n\nAll four keep DPO's shape: an offline loss on preference data, no reward model, no sampling. Which one\nwins is data-dependent, so treat them as ablations to run, not upgrades to assume.","example":"### The DPO loss from sequence log-probs\nReference log-probs are precomputed. Shapes are `[batch]` after summing per-token log-probs over\nresponse tokens; the helper shows the masked sum.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef seq_logprob(logits, ids, response_mask):\n    \"\"\"Sum of log p(token) over response tokens. logits [B,T,V], ids [B,T], mask [B,T] (1 on response).\"\"\"\n    lp = torch.log_softmax(logits[:, :-1].float(), -1).gather(-1, ids[:, 1:, None])[..., 0]\n    return (lp * response_mask[:, 1:]).sum(-1)                          # [B]\n\ndef dpo_loss(pol_w, pol_l, ref_w, ref_l, beta=0.1):\n    \"\"\"pol_*/ref_*: sequence log-probs of chosen (w) and rejected (l) under policy / reference.\"\"\"\n    margin = beta * ((pol_w - ref_w) - (pol_l - ref_l))                 # implicit reward margin\n    loss = -F.logsigmoid(margin).mean()\n    reward_acc = (margin > 0).float().mean()                            # fraction of pairs ranked right\n    return loss, margin, reward_acc\n\n# One pair, numbers illustrative. Policy has drifted toward chosen and away from rejected.\npol_w, ref_w = torch.tensor([-40.0]), torch.tensor([-42.0])            # chosen: +2 nats vs reference\npol_l, ref_l = torch.tensor([-35.0]), torch.tensor([-33.0])            # rejected: -2 nats vs reference\nloss, margin, acc = dpo_loss(pol_w, pol_l, ref_w, ref_l)\nprint(f\"margin={margin.item():.2f} loss={loss.item():.3f} reward_acc={acc.item():.0%}\")\n# margin = 0.1 * (2 - (-2)) = 0.40 ; loss = -log sigma(0.40) = 0.513 ; accuracy 100%\n\n# Watch during training: loss falling, reward_acc rising toward ~0.7-0.9 on a held-out set,\n# and chosen log-prob (pol_w) NOT falling steadily. If pol_w drops while margin grows,\n# probability mass is leaving both responses: likelihood displacement.\n```\n\n- **Note the scale.** A 4-nat total swing across responses of dozens of tokens is a small per-token\n  change, yet with β = 0.1 it already yields a 60/40 preference.\n- **β turns modest log-probability shifts into decisive preferences**; lowering it lets the model move\n  further.\n\n### Worked numbers: what β does to the same 4-nat swing\nPreference probability is σ(β × log-ratio margin). Computed for the 4-nat swing above:\n\n| β | Margin β×4 | P(chosen beats rejected) | Loss | Log-ratio margin needed for P = 0.9 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.05 | 0.2 | 0.55 | 0.598 | ~44 nats |\n| 0.1 | 0.4 | 0.60 | 0.513 | ~22 nats |\n| 0.5 | 2.0 | 0.88 | 0.127 | ~4.4 nats |\n\n- **Small β means the loss keeps pulling** until the policy has moved tens of nats from the reference on\n  each pair: big behaviour change, more drift risk.\n- **Large β is satisfied after small moves:** the policy stays close to the reference, which is what you\n  want when pairs are noisy or off-policy.\n\n### Production run with TRL\nA typical first round on the SFT checkpoint. Argument names follow recent TRL releases; check your\ninstalled version.\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nfrom trl import DPOConfig, DPOTrainer\n\nname = \"my-org/support-sft\"                      # the SFT checkpoint: policy init and reference\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name)\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\npairs = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files=\"pairs.jsonl\", split=\"train\")   # prompt / chosen / rejected\nsplit = pairs.train_test_split(test_size=0.05, seed=0)\n\nargs = DPOConfig(\n    output_dir=\"dpo-round1\",\n    beta=0.1,                        # the KL leash\n    num_train_epochs=1,              # small pair sets overfit fast\n    learning_rate=5e-7,              # far below SFT rates; tune per model\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4,\n    precompute_ref_log_probs=True,   # reference scored once, not every step\n    eval_strategy=\"steps\", eval_steps=100,\n)\ntrainer = DPOTrainer(model=model, ref_model=None,   # None: TRL builds the frozen reference itself\n                     args=args, train_dataset=split[\"train\"], eval_dataset=split[\"test\"],\n                     processing_class=tok)\ntrainer.train()\n```","patterns":["**Cache or share the reference.** Precompute reference log-probs once (`precompute_ref_log_probs=True` in TRL), or with LoRA pass no separate reference so the adapter is disabled for the reference pass. Monitor GPU memory and step time; a second full copy is the usual reason a run does not fit.","**Plot chosen log-prob next to the margin.** TRL logs implicit rewards, margins, accuracies and chosen and rejected log-probs at each logging step. Alert when chosen log-prob falls steadily while margins grow, the likelihood-displacement signature, and respond with larger β or an SFT term on chosen.","**One epoch, early stop on held-out reward accuracy.** Hold out a few percent of pairs and evaluate every few hundred steps. Stop when held-out accuracy plateaus, because training accuracy near 100% with flat held-out accuracy means the pairs are being memorised.","**Regenerate pairs on-policy each round.** Sample candidates from the current checkpoint, have the judge or experts label them, and run the next DPO round with that checkpoint as the new reference. Monitor win rate against the previous round's model on a fixed prompt set.","**Audit length before training.** Compute the chosen-to-rejected length ratio over the dataset and the share of pairs where chosen is longer. If it is heavily skewed, rebalance pairs or try SimPO, and monitor mean output length every round."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[(\"Pairs: prompt, chosen, rejected\")] --> REF[\"Frozen reference, the SFT model\"]\n    REF -->|\"score once\"| C[(\"Cached reference log-probs\")]\n    D --> POL[\"Policy: response log-probs\"]\n    POL -->|\"policy log-probs\"| M[\"Implicit reward margin\"]\n    C -->|\"reference log-probs\"| M\n    M --> L[\"Loss: minus log sigmoid\"]\n    L -->|\"weighted by how wrong the ranking is\"| G[\"Raise chosen, lower rejected\"]\n    G --> POL\n    POL -.->|\"monitor\"| W{\"Chosen log-prob falling?\"}\n    W -.->|\"yes: likelihood displacement\"| FX[\"Raise beta or add SFT term\"]","caption":"One DPO step: the frozen reference is scored once and cached, the policy is scored every step, and the\nloss pushes the implicit reward margin up, weighted by how wrong the current ranking is."},"interview":[{"q":"How does DPO get rid of the reward model and the RL loop?","a":"- **The KL-regularised objective has a closed-form optimum**, π* proportional to π_ref·exp(r/β), so the\n  reward can be written as β·log(π/π_ref) plus a prompt-only term.\n- **Substituted into Bradley–Terry**, the prompt-only β·log Z(x) term cancels between chosen and\n  rejected, leaving a loss over the policy alone.\n- **The leash is inside the formula**, because reference log-probs appear in every pair."},{"q":"Your product only collects thumbs-up and thumbs-down on single responses. Can you still do DPO?","a":"- **Not directly:** DPO needs a chosen and a rejected response to the *same* prompt, and unpaired\n  feedback rarely gives you that.\n- **KTO fits the data as it comes:** each response is labelled desirable or undesirable, with no matched\n  pairs required.\n- **Or build pairs on purpose:** sample two responses per prompt from the current model and ask raters or\n  a calibrated judge to compare them, which also makes the data on-policy."},{"q":"When would you choose PPO or GRPO over DPO?","a":"- **When you need on-policy exploration**, because DPO only learns from the pairs you give it.\n- **When you want a reusable scorer** (a reward model) for rejection sampling, filtering and monitoring\n  across many rounds.\n- **When the reward is programmatic**, such as unit tests or a schema check, which GRPO uses directly."},{"q":"What does β = 0.1 versus β = 0.5 mean in numbers?","a":"- **Preference probability is σ(β × log-ratio margin).** A 4-nat swing gives 0.60 at β = 0.1 and 0.88 at\n  β = 0.5.\n- **To reach P = 0.9** the policy needs about 22 nats of margin at β = 0.1 but only about 4.4 at β = 0.5.\n- **So small β permits more drift** from the reference; prefer larger β when pairs are noisy or\n  off-policy."},{"q":"Your pairs come from a public dataset generated by a different, stronger model. Is that a problem?","a":"- **Yes, it is off-policy:** the pairs compare sequences your policy rarely produces, so the loss moves\n  while outputs barely change.\n- **Mitigate** by first SFT-ing the reference on the chosen responses, as the DPO paper advises.\n- **Better:** generate candidates with your own SFT model, label those, and iterate in rounds."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheap and stable vs offline.** One trainable model and a cached reference instead of four networks makes DPO cheap and stable, but its advantage shrinks when the pairs came from a model far from the one you are training.","**Small vs large β.** Small β separates pairs faster and allows larger behaviour changes; large β preserves the reference. The right value flips toward large β when pairs are noisy or off-policy.","**No reward model vs no reusable scorer.** Dropping it removes an artefact to maintain, but also a scorer for rejection sampling, data filtering and monitoring, which matters once you run many rounds.","**Reference-free variants vs leash.** ORPO and SimPO save memory and can reduce length bias, but give up the explicit leash and need their own margin or length terms to stay stable."],"pitfalls":["**Several epochs on a small pair set.** Symptom: reward accuracy near 100% on training pairs, falling held-out win rate, and verbose or strangely confident outputs.","**Ignoring chosen log-probability.** Margin and loss look healthy while the likelihood of good responses falls and mass migrates to unobserved, often degenerate, outputs.","**Treating pairs from a stronger, different model as on-policy.** The loss moves but the trained model's outputs barely change, because it never produced sequences like those.","**Length-confounded pairs** where chosen is usually longer. The model learns length: answers grow every round with no quality gain.","**Mismatched tokenisation or chat template** between stored reference log-probs and policy inputs. The implicit rewards are noise; training appears to work while nothing improves."],"when":"- **Use as the default first preference method** for an application team: a few thousand to a few hundred\n  thousand pairs, one node, and a need for reproducible runs.\n- **Run it after SFT**, on pairs generated by the SFT model or by the previous round's policy, and iterate\n  in rounds if you can afford fresh labelling.\n- **Prefer RLHF with PPO or GRPO when** you need on-policy exploration, a reusable reward model, or a\n  programmatic reward, and have the engineering to run the loop.\n- **Measure** held-out reward accuracy, chosen log-probability, judge or human win rate against the SFT\n  model, and a general capability suite.\n- **Interviews:** sketch the derivation, name the implicit reward, and explain why DPO is offline and what\n  that costs.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Precomputes reference log-probs, verifies the response mask and chat template match, and plots margin, chosen log-prob and held-out reward accuracy together each run.","architect":"Picks DPO over PPO when pair data is fixed and budgets are modest, and sets the iteration policy: how often fresh on-policy pairs are collected before the next round.","fde":"Sets up pairwise review with a customer's experts on the model's own outputs, since pairs about the model's actual mistakes are worth far more than imported preference datasets."},"check":{"q":"A team runs DPO for three epochs on 8,000 pairs. Training loss falls to near zero and\nreward accuracy is 99%, but a held-out judge prefers the SFT model and outputs have become long and\noddly phrased. Diagnose and propose fixes.","a":"The run overfit the pairs: three epochs on 8k pairs lets the model separate every training pair\nby memorising surface features, and length is the easiest such feature if chosen responses tend to be\nlonger. The near-zero loss means pairs are separated by huge margins, which with β = 0.1 implies large\nlog-ratio shifts from the reference, and that drift is what the held-out judge is penalising. I would\ncheck chosen log-probability over training for likelihood displacement and check the length ratio of\nchosen to rejected in the data. Fixes: one epoch with early stopping on held-out reward accuracy,\nlarger β or an added SFT term on chosen responses, balancing or length-normalising the pairs, and\nregenerating pairs on-policy so they describe this model's real errors rather than the dataset's\nartefacts."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the partition function Z(x) disappear from the DPO loss?","options":["It is assumed to equal one for a well-normalised language model","It depends only on the prompt, so it is identical for the chosen and rejected responses and cancels in their difference","It is absorbed into the learning rate","The reference model is trained to make it constant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Z(x) sums over all possible responses to the prompt, which is intractable, but Bradley–Terry only uses the difference of rewards for two responses to the same prompt, and the same Z(x) appears in both."},{"q":"What is the \"implicit reward\" in DPO?","options":["The output of a frozen reward model used to label the pairs","β times the log-ratio of policy to reference probability for a response","The number of pairs the policy ranks correctly","The negative of the KL divergence between policy and reference"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inverting the optimal policy of the KL-regularised objective gives r(x,y) = β·log(π/π_ref) up to a prompt-only constant. DPO optimises the margin of this quantity between chosen and rejected responses."},{"q":"Which observation most directly indicates likelihood displacement during DPO?","options":["Reward accuracy on training pairs stays below 60%","The margin between chosen and rejected stops increasing","The chosen response's log-probability falls steadily even as the margin grows","The reference model's log-probabilities change between epochs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The loss rewards a growing difference, which can be achieved by lowering both responses' probability as long as the rejected one falls faster. Falling chosen log-probability means mass is moving to sequences the loss never sees."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/grpo","sources":["Shao et al., 2024 — DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models","DeepSeek-AI, 2025 — DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning","Schulman et al., 2017 — Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms"],"why":"- **PPO's critic is costly and unstable for text.** PPO judges each token against a *value model's*\n  prediction of expected reward, usually another copy of the policy with a scalar head, trained alongside\n  it. That doubles trainable memory.\n- **Its estimates are poor.** The reward arrives only at the end of a long response, the value model must\n  guess from partial text, and its errors feed straight into the advantages. Teams spent as much effort\n  stabilising the critic as training the policy.\n- **Verifiable rewards changed the economics.** For maths with a known answer, code with unit tests, or\n  checkable formats, a *program* scores the response: no reward model, no raters, no overoptimisation of a\n  learned proxy. Cheap rewards make many samples per prompt affordable.\n- **So the group is the baseline.** With eight attempts at one problem, the natural question is \"was this\n  better than the other seven\", not \"better than a critic's guess\".\n- **Raw REINFORCE has enormous variance**: every correct answer gets the same push whether the prompt was\n  trivial or hard. Group-relative advantages fix this: always-solved prompts give zero advantage (no\n  wasted gradient); a prompt solved one time in eight gives its single success a large positive push.","how":"### Sampling the group\n1. For each prompt q, the current (\"old\") policy samples G responses o₁ … o_G.\n2. G is commonly between 8 and 16 in open recipes, and as high as 64 in DeepSeekMath; larger groups suit\n   harder tasks.\n3. Each response gets a scalar reward r_i: a rule-based verifier, a reward model, or a sum of terms\n   (accuracy plus format, for instance).\n\n### Group-relative advantage\n```\nÂ_i = ( r_i − mean(r₁…r_G) ) / std(r₁…r_G)\n```\n\n- **Every token of response i gets the same Â_i**, because the reward is for the whole outcome and nothing\n  finer-grained is known.\n- **Two numbers per prompt replace the value model.** Cheap and nearly unbiased: the group mean includes\n  the sample's own reward, a small bias that a leave-one-out baseline such as RLOO removes.\n- **Difficulty-adaptive:** the signal concentrates on prompts the model sometimes, but not always, solves.\n- **Dividing by std is a separate choice.** For a 0/1 reward the spread is smallest when the pass rate is\n  near 0 or near 1, so the division up-weights almost-always-failed and almost-always-solved prompts\n  relative to half-solved ones. Whether that is desirable is debated (see below).\n- **All-equal groups contribute nothing** (zero variance). Implementations add a small epsilon to the\n  denominator and often filter such prompts out of the batch.\n\n### The clipped surrogate\n```\nratio_t = π_θ(o_t | q, o_<t) / π_old(o_t | q, o_<t)\nL_clip  = mean over tokens of  min( ratio_t · Â_i ,  clip(ratio_t, 1−ε, 1+ε) · Â_i )\n```\n\n- **The ratio** compares the policy being updated with the one that generated the samples.\n- **The clip** removes the incentive to move any token's ratio beyond about 1±ε in one update, so a batch\n  of samples can be reused for a few gradient steps without the policy running away from the data. That\n  is the point of PPO-style methods over plain policy gradient.\n- **Edge case:** with exactly one gradient step per batch, π_θ equals π_old, every ratio is 1, the clip\n  never binds, and GRPO reduces to REINFORCE with a group-mean baseline (plus the KL term). The clip\n  only earns its keep when samples are reused.\n\n### Outcome versus process rewards\n| | Outcome supervision (default) | Process supervision |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reward | one per response, spread to all its tokens | a process reward model scores each reasoning step |\n| Token advantage | the response's Â_i | normalised sum of step rewards from that point on |\n| Credit assignment | coarse: every token shares the outcome | more precise, per reasoning step |\n| Cost | needs only a final-answer check | needs a step-level scorer: expensive and itself hackable |\n| Used by | most open reasoning recipes since DeepSeekMath | described in DeepSeekMath |\n\n### The KL term\n- **Added to the loss, not the reward:** `L = −L_clip + β · KL(π_θ ‖ π_ref)`.\n- **Per-token estimator:** `π_ref/π_θ − log(π_ref/π_θ) − 1`, low-variance and always non-negative.\n- **Reference:** the SFT or base checkpoint the run started from.\n- **Why separate:** advantages then reflect only task success, and drift is controlled on its own knob.\n- **Some later recipes drop the KL entirely** when the reward is fully verifiable and the run is\n  monitored, trading safety for faster movement.\n\n### Reward design\n- **Results depend on the reward more than on the algorithm.**\n- **DeepSeek-R1 used rule-based rewards:** an accuracy reward checking the final answer against ground\n  truth, and a format reward checking that reasoning appears inside designated tags.\n- **Rules cannot be flattered** the way a learned reward model can, **but they can be gamed** by format\n  tricks, answers that match a lax checker, or incomplete test suites.\n- **Without a verifier** you use a reward model, and GRPO inherits RLHF's overoptimisation problem.\n\n### Why long reasoning emerges\n- **Outcome rewards plus many samples select for checking, backtracking and trying alternatives**,\n  because those responses succeed more often on hard problems.\n- **Length grows during training** as a result: a feature when it buys accuracy, a cost when it does not.\n- **Length normalisation interacts with this.** Averaging the loss per response over its own length (the\n  original formulation) penalises wrong long responses less per token than wrong short ones, biasing\n  toward verbosity on failures.\n\n| Variant | What it changes |\n|---|---|\n| **Dr. GRPO** | removes both the per-response length normalisation and the std division |\n| **DAPO** | keeps the group baseline; token-level average across the whole group; higher upper clip bound so rare useful tokens can grow; dynamic sampling that drops all-correct and all-wrong groups and refills the batch; overlong-response penalty |\n\nWhich normalisation and clipping you use is a real design choice, not a detail: comparing GRPO runs that\ndiffer in it is comparing different algorithms.","example":"### Group advantages and the GRPO loss\nOne prompt, eight sampled responses, verifier rewards with three of eight correct. Token log-probs are\nillustrative placeholders.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\nG, eps_clip, beta = 8, 0.2, 0.04\nrewards = torch.tensor([1., 0., 0., 1., 0., 0., 0., 1.])         # verifier: 3 of 8 correct\nadv = (rewards - rewards.mean()) / (rewards.std() + 1e-4)        # group-relative advantage, one per response\nprint(\"advantages:\", [round(a, 2) for a in adv.tolist()])\n# mean 0.375 -> correct responses get ~ +1.21, wrong ones ~ -0.72 (sample std); a 0/8 or 8/8\n# group would get all zeros and teach nothing.\n\ndef grpo_loss(logp_new, logp_old, logp_ref, adv, mask):\n    \"\"\"logp_* : [G, T] per-token log-probs; mask: [G, T] 1 on response tokens; adv: [G].\"\"\"\n    ratio = torch.exp(logp_new - logp_old)                          # pi_theta / pi_old, per token\n    a = adv[:, None]                                                # same advantage on every token\n    surr = torch.min(ratio * a, torch.clamp(ratio, 1 - eps_clip, 1 + eps_clip) * a)\n    lr = logp_ref - logp_new                                        # log(pi_ref / pi_theta)\n    kl = torch.exp(lr) - lr - 1                                     # non-negative per-token estimator\n    per_tok = -(surr - beta * kl)\n    return (per_tok * mask).sum() / mask.sum()   # token-mean over the group (DAPO-style); the original\n                                                 # averages per response first, then over responses\n\nT = 12\nlogp_old = -torch.rand(G, T) * 3\nlogp_new = logp_old + 0.05 * torch.randn(G, T)                     # a small policy change\nlogp_ref = logp_old + 0.1 * torch.randn(G, T)\nmask = torch.ones(G, T)\nprint(\"loss:\", grpo_loss(logp_new, logp_old, logp_ref, adv, mask).item())\n# In a real step: sample G responses per prompt with vLLM, score them, compute logp_old once,\n# then take 1-4 gradient steps on this loss before sampling again.\n```\n\n- **Three of eight right:** each success gets about +1.2, each failure about −0.7.\n- **Seven of eight right:** the single failure gets a large negative push (about −2.5) and each success a\n  small positive one (about +0.35), exactly where the gradient should go.\n\n### Worked numbers: advantages across pass rates\nHow the advantage of one correct and one wrong response changes with pass rate, with and without the std\ndivision (G = 8, pure Python):\n\n```python\nfrom statistics import mean, stdev\n\ndef group_advantages(k, G=8, scale=True):\n    \"\"\"Advantage of a correct and a wrong response when k of G pass a 0/1 verifier.\"\"\"\n    r = [1.0] * k + [0.0] * (G - k)\n    m, s = mean(r), stdev(r)                      # sample std, as torch.std\n    f = (lambda x: (x - m) / (s + 1e-4)) if scale else (lambda x: x - m)\n    return round(f(1.0), 2), round(f(0.0), 2)\n\nfor k in (1, 2, 4, 6, 7):\n    print(f\"{k}/8\", group_advantages(k), group_advantages(k, scale=False))\n```\n\n| Pass rate | Correct, with std (GRPO) | Wrong, with std | Correct, no std (Dr. GRPO) | Wrong, no std |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1/8 | +2.47 | −0.35 | +0.88 | −0.12 |\n| 2/8 | +1.62 | −0.54 | +0.75 | −0.25 |\n| 4/8 | +0.94 | −0.94 | +0.50 | −0.50 |\n| 6/8 | +0.54 | −1.62 | +0.25 | −0.75 |\n| 7/8 | +0.35 | −2.47 | +0.12 | −0.88 |\n\n- **The std division multiplies extreme groups more:** about ×2.8 at 1/8 versus ×1.9 at 4/8. That is the\n  up-weighting of near-always-failed and near-always-solved prompts described above.\n- **Without it (Dr. GRPO)** half-solved prompts carry relatively more of the gradient.","patterns":["**Curate prompts by pass rate before training.** Sample G responses per prompt from the starting checkpoint, score them, and drop prompts solved 0/G or G/G. Monitor the share of zero-advantage groups per step and re-curate as the policy improves, because solved prompts accumulate.","**Generate rollouts with a fast inference engine.** Trainers such as TRL's `GRPOTrainer` (with `GRPOConfig(num_generations=..., use_vllm=True)`) or OpenRLHF hand sampling to vLLM. If π_old comes from the sampler's log-probs, monitor the ratio at the first gradient step on fresh samples; values far from 1 mean the sampler and trainer disagree.","**Unit-test the reward function like production code.** Keep a suite of known hacks (empty reasoning tags, hard-coded test outputs, answers that fool a lax parser) and assert they score zero. Log each reward component separately per step so a format term cannot hide a falling accuracy term.","**Score against an independent held-out verifier.** Evaluate every few dozen steps on prompts and tests the reward never sees. Stop or roll back when training reward rises and held-out accuracy does not: the reward-hacking signature.","**Cap and watch length.** Set a maximum completion length and, if needed, an overlong penalty. Monitor mean response length, truncation rate and accuracy together; length rising with flat accuracy means you pay inference cost for nothing."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Prompt q\"] --> OLD[\"Old policy samples G responses\"]\n    OLD --> VR[\"Verifier scores each response\"]\n    VR --> Z{\"All rewards equal?\"}\n    Z -->|\"yes: zero advantage\"| DROP[\"Filter group, no gradient\"]\n    Z -->|\"no\"| ADV[\"Group-standardised advantage\"]\n    ADV -->|\"same advantage on every token\"| CL[\"Clipped surrogate loss\"]\n    REF[\"Frozen reference\"] -->|\"per-token KL in the loss\"| CL\n    CL --> UP[\"1 to 4 gradient steps\"]\n    UP --> NEW[\"Updated policy\"]\n    NEW -->|\"becomes old policy\"| OLD\n    NEW -.->|\"every few dozen steps\"| HO{\"Held-out accuracy rising too?\"}\n    HO -.->|\"no: reward hacking\"| RW[\"Fix reward, roll back\"]","caption":"One GRPO iteration: the old policy samples a group of G responses (often 8 to 16), and the group of\nsamples replaces the critic. Rewards are standardised within the group (minus the group mean, divided\nby the group std) before the clipped update on new-over-old token ratios."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem does GRPO solve relative to PPO, and how?","a":"- **PPO needs a value model**, usually another policy-sized network trained alongside, whose estimates\n  are poor because the reward only arrives at the end of a long response.\n- **GRPO uses the group as the baseline:** sample G responses per prompt and standardise each reward by\n  the group mean and std.\n- **The result:** about half the trainable memory, no critic instability, and a signal that concentrates\n  on prompts the model sometimes but not always solves."},{"q":"Half your batches have zero advantage. What is going on and what do you do?","a":"- **Those groups are all-correct or all-wrong**, so every reward equals the group mean.\n- **Curate by pass rate:** drop prompts solved 0/G or G/G at the start, and re-curate as the policy\n  improves and solved prompts accumulate.\n- **Or use dynamic sampling** as DAPO does: drop such groups and refill the batch, so GPU time is not\n  burned on samples with no gradient."},{"q":"Should you divide the advantage by the group std?","a":"- **With it (original GRPO)** extreme groups are up-weighted: at G = 8, a lone success at 1/8 gets +2.47\n  versus +0.94 at 4/8.\n- **Without it (Dr. GRPO)** half-solved prompts carry relatively more of the gradient.\n- **It is debated, not a detail:** runs that differ in normalisation are different algorithms, so ablate\n  on your task rather than assume."},{"q":"When would you not use GRPO?","a":"- **When you have no reward you trust:** with a learned reward model GRPO overoptimises just as PPO does.\n- **When a few thousand demonstrations or preference pairs already solve it**, because the sampling loop\n  is the most expensive post-training stage after pre-training.\n- **When the starting policy almost never succeeds:** every group is all-zero, so there is no gradient;\n  SFT on demonstrations or an easier curriculum has to come first."},{"q":"Training reward climbs and mean response length doubles, but held-out accuracy is flat. Diagnose.","a":"- **Paying for length with nothing to show:** outcome rewards select for long reasoning only when it\n  buys accuracy.\n- **Check normalisation:** per-response length averaging penalises wrong long responses less per token,\n  biasing toward verbosity on failures.\n- **Check the reward for hacks** such as format terms or a lax checker, then set a maximum completion\n  length or an overlong penalty and watch truncation rate."}],"tradeoffs":["**No value model vs more sampling.** Removing it halves trainable memory and eliminates critic instability, but the group baseline needs several samples per prompt, so generation cost per update rises with G.","**Verifiable rewards vs coverage.** They cannot be flattered and need no raters, yet exist only for tasks with a checker, and a lax checker becomes what the model optimises instead of the task.","**Large vs small groups.** Larger groups give lower-variance advantages and more signal on hard prompts; small groups are cheaper. The balance shifts toward larger G as pass rate per prompt falls.","**KL term vs speed.** Keeping it protects capability and prevents collapse but slows movement where the reward is trustworthy; dropping it is defensible only with close monitoring of general evals."],"pitfalls":["**Prompts the model always or never solves.** Symptom: many zero-advantage groups and GPU time burned on samples that produce no gradient. Fix: curate difficulty and filter such groups.","**Reward hacking on format or verifier gaps.** Symptom: reward rising while accuracy on an independent held-out checker stays flat, or outputs that satisfy tags with empty reasoning.","**Sampler and trainer probabilities disagree** (different kernels or precision). Symptom: ratios far from 1 at the first gradient step and erratic training.","**Unchecked response length.** Symptom: rising mean length with flat accuracy and exploding inference cost, often the per-length normalisation bias or a weak length cap.","**Treating the reward-model case like the verifiable case.** With a learned reward GRPO overoptimises exactly as PPO does, so the same KL and human-evaluation discipline applies."],"when":"- **Use when** you can write a reward function you trust, the task benefits from many attempts, and you\n  want on-policy RL without PPO's critic: maths and code reasoning, structured-output compliance,\n  tool-call correctness checked by execution, agentic tasks with verifiable end states.\n- **Avoid when** a few thousand demonstrations or preference pairs already solve the problem, because the\n  sampling loop is the most expensive post-training stage after pre-training.\n- **Measure** accuracy on an independent held-out verifier, response length, KL from the reference, and a\n  general capability suite.\n- **Interviews:** explain why the group replaces the critic, write the advantage formula, and describe how\n  reward hacking shows up with rule-based rewards.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes and tests the reward function, wires a fast sampler to the trainer, filters zero-variance groups, and tracks reward, length and KL per step.","architect":"Decides which product behaviours have a reward trustworthy enough to optimise with RL, and budgets the sampling compute against the expected gain over SFT and DPO.","fde":"Turns a customer's acceptance checks (schema validators, test suites, business rules) into reward functions, and audits them for the gaps a model will find and exploit."},"check":{"q":"You train with GRPO on a code task where the reward is \"all provided unit tests pass\". After a\nfew hundred steps, training reward is near 1.0 but the model's solutions fail on a hidden test set\nand often contain special-casing for the visible test inputs. What happened and what do you change?","a":"The reward was the visible tests, so the policy optimised them directly and discovered that\nhard-coding the expected outputs for the visible inputs is a cheaper path to full reward than solving\nthe problem. This is reward hacking on a lax verifier, and the hidden-set gap is the diagnostic.\nChanges: hold out tests from the reward so the model cannot see every case it is scored on, and\nrandomise or generate additional inputs per episode; add a reward term or a filter that penalises\ncode referencing literal test inputs; evaluate on a held-out verifier every few steps and stop when\nit diverges from training reward; and keep a KL term and general coding benchmarks in the loop so the\npolicy cannot drift into a degenerate style. The algorithm did its job; the reward defined the wrong\ntask."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does GRPO use instead of PPO's value model to compute advantages?","options":["The reward model's score for the prompt alone","The mean and standard deviation of the rewards of a group of responses sampled for the same prompt","The KL divergence from the reference policy","The length-normalised log-probability of each response"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each response's advantage is its reward standardised against its siblings' rewards. This removes the critic network entirely and makes the signal concentrate on prompts the model sometimes but not always solves."},{"q":"Why does a group in which all eight responses are correct contribute no gradient?","options":["The clip range zeroes the ratio when all rewards are equal","The KL term dominates when responses agree","All rewards equal the group mean, so every advantage is zero","The verifier returns no reward for repeated answers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The group-relative advantage subtracts the group mean; with identical rewards every difference is zero. This is intentional: the model has nothing to learn from a prompt it always solves, so the compute is better spent on harder prompts."},{"q":"Compared with RLHF using a learned reward model, what is the main reason rule-based rewards reduce reward hacking?","options":["Rule-based rewards are differentiable","A verifier cannot be flattered or drifted away from by stylistic changes, so the policy can only raise reward by actually satisfying the check","Rule-based rewards remove the need for a KL term entirely","Rule-based rewards are always dense per token"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A learned reward model has errors far from its training data that a policy can exploit with length or tone. A verifier has no such surface, though it has its own gaps (lax checks, visible test cases) that the policy will find instead."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/peft","sources":["Hu et al., 2021 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","Dettmers et al., 2023 — QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs","Aghajanyan et al., 2020 — Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning","Official docs: Hugging Face PEFT"],"why":"- **Full fine-tuning does not fit.** Updating every weight of a 7B model needs on the order of 100 GB of\n  GPU memory for weights, gradients and optimizer state.\n- **AdamW in mixed precision costs ~16 bytes per parameter.** The bf16 weight (2 bytes), its gradient\n  (2 bytes), and an fp32 master copy plus the two Adam moments (12 bytes), before activations. For 7B that\n  is roughly 112 GB, which does not fit on one 80 GB GPU.\n- **\"Just fine-tune it\" is expensive twice.** It means multi-GPU sharding, a bigger bill, and a 14 GB\n  checkpoint for every task or customer. You cannot keep ten copies resident on one server, cannot A/B two\n  variants cheaply, and rollback means moving tens of gigabytes.\n- **The update has low intrinsic rank.** The *change* a fine-tune makes to a pretrained matrix lives\n  mostly in a small subspace, so you can parametrize ΔW directly in that subspace and train only it.\n- **LoRA beats earlier PEFT on serving cost.** Earlier methods work, but adapter layers between blocks\n  add inference latency and learned \"virtual tokens\" (prefix and prompt tuning) eat context. LoRA's update has exactly the shape of\n  the original matrix, so it folds back in and inference cost is unchanged, while keeping most of full\n  fine-tuning's quality.","how":"### LoRA: the update as a low-rank product\nFor a frozen weight W₀ of shape d×k, the fine-tuned weight is W₀ + ΔW with ΔW = B·A:\n- A is r×k, B is d×r, and the rank r is small (8–64 is typical, versus d = 4,096).\n- Only A and B receive gradients. The forward pass becomes:\n\n```\nh = W₀x + (α / r) · B(Ax)\n```\n\nThree design choices, each with its reason:\n- **B starts at zero, A at small random values.** ΔW = BA is exactly zero at step 0, so training begins\n  at the pretrained model's function; the first steps cannot wreck what pretraining built.\n- **The α/r scale.** Keeps the update's magnitude roughly independent of r, so you can change rank\n  without retuning the learning rate. (rsLoRA argues α/√r is better when r is large.)\n- **Which matrices get an adapter.** The original paper adapted only attention query and value. Later\n  evidence, including the QLoRA paper's ablations, showed adapters on *all* linear layers, MLP included,\n  matter more for quality than raising r. Knowledge-heavy tasks especially need the MLP capacity.\n\n### Parameter arithmetic (Llama-2-7B-style, r = 16)\nLayer shape: d = k = 4,096, MLP width 11,008, 32 layers.\n\n| Item | Count |\n|---|---|\n| One 4,096×4,096 attention matrix | 16.8M weights |\n| Its adapter: 16 × (4,096 + 4,096) | ≈ 131k (under 1%) |\n| 32 layers × 4 attention matrices | 16.8M adapter params |\n| Plus 3 MLP matrices: 16 × (4,096 + 11,008) | ≈ 242k each |\n| Total trainable | about 40M, roughly 0.6% of 6.7B |\n| fp32 Adam state | 40M × 16 bytes ≈ 640 MB instead of ~107 GB |\n\n### What LoRA does *not* save\n- **The frozen base stays in memory.** ≈13.4 GB in bf16 for 7B.\n- **Compute per step drops only modestly.** Backprop still flows through every layer to reach the\n  adapters. Only the weight-gradient matmuls for frozen matrices are skipped: about half of the backward\n  FLOPs, or a third of the forward-plus-backward total.\n- **Activation memory is unchanged.** Every frozen layer's activations are needed to backpropagate to the\n  adapters below it. At long sequence lengths activations, not weights, OOM, which is why LoRA recipes\n  almost always switch on gradient checkpointing.\n- **The win:** optimizer state, master weights and weight gradients, plus a checkpoint of tens to a few\n  hundred megabytes (40M adapter parameters are about 80 MB in bf16 or 160 MB in fp32, against 13.4 GB).\n\n### Why rank 16 suffices\n- A gradient step on W₀ for a narrow task mostly pushes in a few consistent directions.\n- LoRA bets the full ΔW is well approximated by its top r singular directions.\n- The bet holds for style, format and behaviour shifts and weakens as the task demands genuinely new\n  information. That condition decides LoRA versus full fine-tuning.\n\n### Export: merge or keep separate\n- **Merge:** W = W₀ + (α/r)BA, a dense matrix of the original shape, zero extra latency.\n- **Keep separate:** one base and many adapters on a server, batching requests for different adapters\n  together; vLLM, SGLang and similar engines support this.\n\n### QLoRA: shrinking the frozen base\nLoRA leaves one large term: 2 bytes per parameter for the frozen weights. QLoRA attacks it with three\npieces:\n1. **4-bit NormalFloat (NF4).** Pretrained weights are roughly normal, so NF4 puts its 16 levels at\n   quantiles of a standard normal instead of evenly spaced. Each bin holds about equal probability mass,\n   minimizing expected rounding error. Quantization is block-wise (blocks of 64 weights, one absmax scale\n   each).\n2. **Double quantization.** The per-block scales are quantized to 8-bit with a second-level scale,\n   because at block size 64 the fp32 scales alone cost 0.5 bits per weight. Saves a few tenths of a bit\n   per parameter on average.\n3. **Paged optimizers.** Optimizer state lives in CUDA unified memory and pages to CPU RAM during the\n   memory spikes long sequences cause, turning a hard OOM into a slowdown.\n\nDuring training:\n- Each 4-bit block is dequantized to bf16 on the fly and multiplied as usual; LoRA matrices stay in bf16\n  with full-precision gradients.\n- The base is never updated, so its quantization error is fixed and the adapter learns around it.\n- **Headline:** fine-tuning a 65B model on a single 48 GB GPU, matching 16-bit fine-tuning on the paper's\n  benchmarks.\n- **Costs:** a slower step (dequantization on every matmul), and a coupling: the adapter was trained\n  against *this* quantized base, so merging into a bf16 copy gives a slightly different model.\n\n### Other PEFT methods\n| Method | Idea | Catch |\n|---|---|---|\n| Adapter layers | bottleneck MLPs inserted into each block | extra latency |\n| Prefix / prompt tuning | learned virtual tokens | consume context; weak on small models |\n| (IA)³ | learned per-channel rescaling vectors | small capacity |\n| DoRA | split W into magnitude and direction, LoRA on direction | often tracks full FT closer at same rank |\n\nLoRA remains the default because it merges away.","example":"### QLoRA setup with adapters on all linear layers\nLoad in NF4, attach LoRA to all linear projections, inspect. On an 8B Llama-3-style model with r = 16\nthis yields on the order of 42M trainable parameters (about 0.5%).\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, BitsAndBytesConfig\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, prepare_model_for_kbit_training\n\nname = \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B\"                       # any causal LM; ~6 GB VRAM for the base in 4-bit\nbnb = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",       # NormalFloat4 for the frozen base\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True,                      # quantize the quantization constants too\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16)               # dequantize to bf16 for the matmuls\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, quantization_config=bnb, device_map=\"auto\")\nmodel = prepare_model_for_kbit_training(model)          # fp32 norms, gradient-checkpointing hooks\n\ncfg = LoraConfig(\n    r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05, bias=\"none\", task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\", \"gate_proj\", \"up_proj\", \"down_proj\"])\nmodel = get_peft_model(model, cfg)\nmodel.print_trainable_parameters()                      # trainable ~0.5% of total\n\nq = model.base_model.model.model.layers[0].self_attn.q_proj\nprint(q.lora_A[\"default\"].weight.shape, q.lora_B[\"default\"].weight.shape)   # [16, 4096], [4096, 16]\nprint(float(q.lora_B[\"default\"].weight.abs().max()))     # 0.0: training starts at the base model\n\n# ... train with TRL's SFTTrainer or a plain loop; LoRA LRs of 1e-4 to 2e-4 are typical ...\nmodel.save_pretrained(\"adapter/\")                        # only A and B: ~85 MB bf16 / ~170 MB fp32, not 16 GB\n# To ship without the peft runtime: load the base in bf16, attach the adapter, then\n# merged = model.merge_and_unload(); merged.save_pretrained(\"merged/\")  -> re-evaluate after merging\n```\n\n### Memory calculator: full FT vs LoRA vs QLoRA\nPure Python, runnable anywhere. Static memory only; activations come on top.\n\n```python\ndef ft_memory_gb(params_b, method, trainable_frac=0.006):\n    p = params_b * 1e9\n    if method == \"full\":                     # bf16 weight + grad, fp32 master + 2 Adam moments\n        return p * 16 / 1e9\n    base = p * (2 if method == \"lora\" else 0.5)   # bf16 vs ~4-bit NF4 frozen base\n    adapter = p * trainable_frac * 16             # adapters keep the full 16 bytes/param\n    return (base + adapter) / 1e9\n\nfor size in (7, 8, 70):\n    row = {m: round(ft_memory_gb(size, m), 1) for m in (\"full\", \"lora\", \"qlora\")}\n    print(size, \"B:\", row, \"GB before activations\")\n# 7 B: {'full': 112.0, 'lora': 14.7, 'qlora': 4.2} GB before activations\n# 8 B: {'full': 128.0, 'lora': 16.8, 'qlora': 4.8} GB before activations\n# 70 B: {'full': 1120.0, 'lora': 146.7, 'qlora': 41.7} GB before activations\n```\n\nReading the output (the 0.5 bytes per weight ignores scale overhead, so real NF4 bases run a little\nlarger):\n\n| Model | Fits one 24 GB card? | Fits one 80 GB card? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8B full FT | no | no |\n| 8B bf16 LoRA | tight, with gradient checkpointing | yes |\n| 8B QLoRA | yes, with room for activations | yes |\n| 70B QLoRA | no | yes |","patterns":["**One base, many adapters.** When tenants or tasks share a base, serve unmerged adapters: vLLM's `--enable-lora` with `--max-loras` and `--max-lora-rank` sized to your adapters. Monitor per-adapter latency and adapter load/evict churn.","**Pin the base revision with the adapter.** Load the base with `revision=\"<commit sha>\"` and store that sha next to the adapter directory, because an adapter is a delta against exact weights. Alert in CI if the serving base differs from the training base.","**Merge-then-eval release gate.** Before shipping a merged or re-quantized model, re-run the task eval and a small general benchmark on the final artifact and block release if it drops below the unmerged adapter's numbers. Track task metric, general metric and the merged-versus-unmerged gap.","**Small rank sweep, all linear layers.** Fix `target_modules` to all projections and sweep r over 8, 16 and 32 with the LR held near 1e-4 to 2e-4; keep the smallest r where held-out loss stops improving. Watch train-versus-eval loss for overfitting.","**Gradient checkpointing by default for long sequences.** Set `gradient_checkpointing=True` in the trainer arguments, because activations, not adapters, are what OOM. Monitor peak memory and step time."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Input x\"] --> W[\"Frozen W0: bf16 in LoRA, NF4 in QLoRA\"]\n    X --> A[\"A: r x k, trainable, small random init\"]\n    A --> B[\"B: d x r, trainable, starts at zero\"]\n    W --> S[\"h = W0x + (alpha / r) B(Ax)\"]\n    B -->|\"scale by alpha / r\"| S\n    S --> L[\"Loss: gradients flow only to A and B\"]\n    L -.->|\"update\"| A\n    L -.->|\"update\"| B\n    B -->|\"after training\"| M[\"Merge: W = W0 + (alpha / r)BA, zero extra latency\"]\n    B -->|\"after training\"| K[\"Keep separate: one base serves many adapters\"]","caption":"The input feeds two paths: the frozen base matrix and the trainable low-rank pair A then B. Only A and B\nget gradients; after training the update is either merged into W₀ or kept as a swappable adapter."},"interview":[{"q":"What is LoRA, and why does a rank-16 update work at all?","a":"- **Definition:** LoRA freezes W₀ and learns the update as ΔW = B·A, with A of shape r×k and B of shape\n  d×r, so only a tiny fraction of parameters train and the update can be merged back into W₀.\n- **Why it works:** the change a fine-tune makes to a pretrained matrix has low intrinsic rank, so the\n  top r directions capture most of it for style, format and behaviour shifts.\n- **Init detail:** B starts at zero, so training begins exactly at the base model's function."},{"q":"Your LoRA run's training loss barely moves after an epoch. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Learning rate first:** a full fine-tuning LR near 1e-5 is too small for LoRA; move to the typical\n  1e-4 to 2e-4 range.\n- **Check gradients reach the adapters:** with a k-bit base and gradient checkpointing, a missing\n  `prepare_model_for_kbit_training` call or input-gradient hook leaves the adapter's loss flat.\n- **Check capacity:** if only `q_proj` and `v_proj` are targeted, add the MLP projections before raising r."},{"q":"When would you not use LoRA, and when would you pick bf16 LoRA over QLoRA?","a":"- **Skip LoRA for large shifts:** a new language or heavy code pretraining is where full fine-tuning or a\n  much higher rank on all layers pulls ahead, so use it when your eval shows LoRA underfitting.\n- **Prefer bf16 LoRA when it fits:** it is simpler and faster per step, with no fixed quantization error.\n- **Reach for QLoRA** only when the bf16 base plus activations does not fit the GPU you have."},{"q":"Roughly how many parameters does LoRA train on a 7B model at r = 16 on all linear layers?","a":"- **Count:** about 40M trainable parameters, roughly 0.6% of 6.7B, from 16.8M on attention plus about\n  242k per MLP matrix per layer.\n- **Optimizer state:** 40M × 16 bytes ≈ 640 MB instead of ~107 GB for full fine-tuning.\n- **Checkpoint:** about 80 MB in bf16 against 13.4 GB for the base, which is what makes per-tenant\n  adapters cheap to store and roll back."},{"q":"You must serve fine-tuned variants for 50 tenants on a shared base. How do you design it?","a":"- **Serve unmerged adapters:** one base resident on the GPUs, adapters loaded on demand, with an engine\n  that batches requests across adapters (vLLM's `--enable-lora`, `--max-loras`, `--max-lora-rank`).\n- **Pin the base revision:** every adapter is a delta against exact weights, so store the base commit\n  sha with each adapter and fail deploys on a mismatch.\n- **Watch the costs:** each request pays a small extra matmul, and adapter load/evict churn shows up as\n  latency; merge only the few highest-traffic tenants if that overhead matters."}],"tradeoffs":["**Memory vs speed.** Memory falls by an order of magnitude and checkpoints by about two, but per-step compute barely drops because backprop still traverses the frozen base; PEFT makes fine-tuning *fit*, not fast.","**Learns less, forgets less.** On narrow tasks that is a feature (base capabilities survive). For large shifts such as a new language or heavy code pretraining, full fine-tuning or a much higher rank on all layers pulls ahead.","**QLoRA vs bf16 LoRA.** QLoRA cuts base-model memory roughly 3–4× relative to bf16, at the cost of slower steps and a fixed quantization error. When bf16 LoRA fits, it is simpler and faster.","**Unmerged vs merged adapters.** Unmerged lets one base serve many tenants, but each request pays a small extra matmul and the engine must support multi-adapter batching. Merged gives zero overhead and one model per adapter.","**Higher rank vs diminishing returns.** Past the point where training loss stops improving with r, extra rank only adds memory and overfitting risk."],"pitfalls":["**Copying a full fine-tuning learning rate (≈1e-5).** LoRA parameters are tiny and need a larger LR. Symptom: training loss barely moves across epochs. Fix: start in the 1e-4 to 2e-4 range.","**Adapting only `q_proj` and `v_proj` because an old tutorial did.** Tasks needing new knowledge or formats underfit. Symptom: good style, wrong facts. Fix: target the MLP projections too.","**Merging a QLoRA adapter into a bf16 base without re-evaluation.** The adapter compensated for a specific quantized base, so the merged model drifts slightly. Re-run evals after merging, or after re-quantizing the merged model to GPTQ/AWQ for serving.","**Training with one chat template, serving with another.** The adapter learned token patterns around the template. Symptom: rambling, never emitting the end token, or ignoring the system prompt.","**Not pinning the exact base model revision.** Adapters are deltas against specific weights, so a silently updated base produces a quietly different model.","**Forgetting `prepare_model_for_kbit_training` or input-gradient hooks with gradient checkpointing on a frozen base.** Symptom: \"element 0 of tensors does not require grad\", or an adapter whose loss never changes."],"when":"- **Use LoRA when:** fine-tuning a model larger than a few hundred million parameters for a style,\n  format, domain-vocabulary or behaviour shift: support-ticket tone, structured output, a house-specific\n  classifier, tool-call formatting.\n- **Use QLoRA when:** the bf16 base does not fit your GPU alongside activations; accept the slower step.\n- **Use full fine-tuning when:** you have the hardware and the task is a large shift that LoRA\n  demonstrably underfits on your eval set.\n- **Measure:** task metric versus the base model, a held-out general benchmark to catch forgetting, and\n  the merged model's metric versus the unmerged one.\n- **Day to day:** PEFT shows up as a `LoraConfig` block, an adapter directory in object storage, and a\n  serving decision about merging.\n- **Interviews:** derive the 16-bytes-per-parameter figure, explain why B is zero-initialized, and say\n  where QLoRA's savings come from and what they cost.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `LoraConfig`, picks rank and target modules, sets a LoRA-appropriate learning rate, and verifies the merged checkpoint matches the adapter's eval numbers before handing it to serving.","architect":"Decides between one merged model per task and one base with hot-swapped adapters, based on tenant count, GPU budget and latency, and owns the base-model version pinning policy.","fde":"Fine-tunes on a customer's data inside their VPC on whatever GPU they have, often a single 24–48 GB card, which is precisely the regime QLoRA was built for."},"check":{"q":"You have one 24 GB GPU and 20k support transcripts, and you need to adapt an 8B model to the\ncustomer's tone and ticket format. Walk through what fits in memory and what you would choose.","a":"Full fine-tuning is out: 8B × 16 bytes ≈ 128 GB for weights, gradients and Adam state. bf16 LoRA\nneeds the frozen base at ≈16 GB plus adapter state (tens of MB) plus activations; with gradient\ncheckpointing and short sequences it might squeeze into 24 GB but it is tight. QLoRA stores the base in\nNF4 at roughly 5–6 GB, leaving ample room for activations at longer sequence lengths, so that is the\npractical choice. I would target all linear projections at r = 16, LR around 1e-4 to 2e-4, train one to\nthree epochs while watching a held-out slice of transcripts and a small general benchmark for forgetting,\nthen merge into a bf16 base for serving and re-run the evals on the merged model, because the adapter was\ntrained against the quantized base."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is LoRA's B matrix initialized to zero while A is random?","options":["Zero initialization keeps the adapter sparse so it can be stored compactly","So the update BA is zero at step 0 and training starts from the exact pretrained function","Because gradients cannot flow through two randomly initialized matrices","To make the rank r adjustable without changing α"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With B = 0 the product BA vanishes, so the model behaves identically to the base model before training and early updates cannot damage it. Random A still gives B a non-degenerate gradient, so learning proceeds."},{"q":"Where does QLoRA's memory saving over plain LoRA come from?","options":["It trains fewer adapter parameters by using a smaller rank","It drops the optimizer state entirely","It stores the frozen base weights in 4-bit NF4 and dequantizes them on the fly during matmuls","It offloads activations to CPU memory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"LoRA already removed optimizer state and gradients for the base; what remained was the 2-byte-per-weight frozen model. QLoRA shrinks that term to about half a byte per weight; paged optimizers only handle transient spikes."},{"q":"What happens to inference latency after a LoRA adapter is merged into the base weights?","options":["It increases slightly because of the extra rank-r projection","It decreases because the merged matrix is lower rank","It is unchanged, because W₀ + BA is a dense matrix of the original shape","It depends on whether the base was quantized during training"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Merging adds BA into W₀, producing one dense d×k matrix, so the forward pass performs exactly the same matmul as the base model. Only an *unmerged* adapter adds the extra low-rank computation."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","sources":["Hinton, Vinyals & Dean, 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network","Kim & Rush, 2016 — Sequence-Level Knowledge Distillation","Sanh et al., 2019 — DistilBERT, a distilled version of BERT","Agarwal et al., 2023 — On-Policy Distillation of Language Models: Learning from Self-Generated Mistakes"],"why":"- **The best model is the one you cannot afford to serve.** A 70B-class model costs roughly ten times the\n  GPU time per token of a 7B one. \"Train a 7B model on the same data\" loses much of that accuracy, partly\n  from capacity and, surprisingly, partly from the training signal.\n- **One-hot labels throw away knowledge.** A label says \"the next token is ` Paris`\" and nothing else.\n  The large model says ` Paris` 0.9, ` the` 0.05, ` located` 0.02, ` Berlin` 0.0001. That ranking of wrong\n  answers is knowledge about the world and the language; Hinton called it *dark knowledge*.\n- **The teacher works on any input.** The student reproduces the teacher's function, which is smoother,\n  lower-variance and available for unlabelled prompts. You can run it on millions of prompts from real\n  traffic that you could never pay humans to label.\n- **It changes architecture, unlike compression.** Quantization and pruning keep the architecture and\n  shave a constant factor. Distillation can go to fewer layers, narrower width or a different family, and\n  combines with both: prune a big model, then distil from the original to repair it.","how":"### Classic logit distillation\nTeacher and student each produce logits **z** over the vocabulary (or classes). Soften both with a\ntemperature *T*:\n\n```\np_i(T) = exp(z_i / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T)\n```\n\nWhy temperature matters, for teacher logits [6, 2, 1]:\n\n| *T* | Distribution | Mass on the two wrong answers | p₂/p₃ = e^(1/T) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [0.976, 0.018, 0.007] | about 2.5%: almost no gradient | about 2.7 |\n| 4 | [0.605, 0.222, 0.173] | 40%: the student learns the ranking | 1.3 |\n\n- **At *T* = 1** a confident teacher is almost one-hot, so information in small probabilities is invisible.\n- **Raising *T*** flattens the distribution and amplifies the ratios between wrong answers. The ranking\n  is compressed but never inverts.\n- **Too high a *T*** approaches uniform, washing out which alternative is *more* plausible. That is why\n  small values (roughly 1–4 for LLMs) are the usual search range, not a rule.\n\nThe loss mixes two terms:\n\n```\nL = α · T² · KL( p_teacher(T) ‖ p_student(T) )  +  (1 − α) · CE( y, p_student(1) )\n```\n\n- **The *T²* factor:** the soft-term gradient scales roughly as 1/*T²*; multiplying back keeps the soft\n  and hard terms' relative weight stable when you change *T*.\n- **The hard-label term:** kept because the teacher is sometimes wrong, and ground truth, where you have\n  it, corrects that.\n- **High-*T* limit:** Hinton showed matching softened distributions becomes matching logits directly (an\n  MSE on **z**), which is why \"logit distillation\" and \"soft-target distillation\" are used interchangeably.\n\n### Three regimes for language models\n| Regime | Needs | Signal | Main cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| White-box, token-level | shared tokenizer, logits | full distribution per token | teacher pass over all data |\n| Black-box, sequence-level | teacher text only | one sampled sequence | teacher generation |\n| On-policy | live teacher scoring | teacher's view of student's own text | generation inside training |\n\n1. **White-box, token-level.**\n   - Teacher and student share a tokenizer; at every position the student minimises a divergence to the\n     teacher's next-token distribution.\n   - Per token the signal is a distribution over 100k+ entries instead of one index.\n   - Teams precompute the teacher's top-*k* logits (say 64 per position, renormalised), because full\n     distributions would be hundreds of kilobytes per token.\n   - Several recent small models were pretrained with a larger sibling's logits as targets, not only hard\n     tokens.\n2. **Black-box, sequence-level.**\n   - Generate teacher responses for many prompts, then run ordinary SFT on (prompt, teacher response).\n     This is \"distilled\" in most product announcements; technically it is SFT on synthetic data.\n   - The framing changes what you optimise: prompt coverage of the deployment distribution, samples per\n     prompt, and verifier filtering (rejection sampling) so the student learns correct reasoning, not the\n     average output.\n   - Reasoning-model distillates released in 2025 fine-tuned open 1.5B–70B checkpoints on hundreds of\n     thousands of teacher-generated traces.\n3. **On-policy.**\n   - Both regimes above train on prefixes the *teacher* wrote. At inference the student sees its own\n     prefixes and mistakes and never learned to recover (exposure bias).\n   - On-policy distillation samples from the student, has the teacher score every token, and trains toward\n     the teacher on the student's own trajectories.\n   - It costs generation in the loop and a live teacher, but attacks exactly that train/inference mismatch.\n\n### Which divergence\n- **Forward KL(teacher ‖ student) is mode-covering.** It punishes near-zero mass on anything the teacher\n  finds plausible, so a small student spreads mass thinly; samples look hedged and low quality.\n- **Reverse KL(student ‖ teacher) is mode-seeking.** The student concentrates on the teacher's dominant\n  modes, which suits a student with less capacity. MiniLLM uses it.\n- **Generalised-KD** interpolates between the two with a Jensen–Shannon style divergence.\n- **Treat it as a tunable.** Whichever you choose, the teacher is the student's ceiling on the covered\n  distribution.\n\n### Feature (intermediate-layer) distillation\n- **What:** losses pulling the student's hidden states (via a learned projection if widths differ) or\n  attention maps toward the teacher's at chosen layers. DistilBERT added a cosine loss on hidden states to\n  its soft-target loss.\n- **Why:** every layer gets a direct target instead of only the last, which speeds recovery.\n- **Catch:** needs white-box access to both models and a layer mapping (student layer *i* ↔ teacher layer\n  *2i*, say), so it is used mostly when the student was built by shrinking the teacher.\n\n### Why it pairs with pruning\n- A randomly initialised student must relearn everything.\n- A student made by structurally pruning the teacher (dropping layers, heads or MLP width) already sits\n  near a good solution; distillation adds a dense correction signal.\n- The pair recovers most of the teacher's quality with a small fraction of pretraining's tokens. That is\n  the recipe behind several open small-model families, and why the two topics sit together here.","example":"### Token-level distillation loss in PyTorch\nBoth models must use the same tokenizer so vocabulary positions line up; the teacher is frozen. The `T²`\nscaling and mixed hard/soft objective follow Hinton; padded positions are masked out of the soft term.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef kd_loss(student_logits, teacher_logits, labels, T=2.0, alpha=0.5, ignore_index=-100):\n    \"\"\"student/teacher logits: [batch, seq, vocab]; labels: [batch, seq], a copy of input_ids with\n    ignore_index at masked positions (HF convention: unshifted, the shift happens below).\"\"\"\n    s = student_logits[:, :-1].float()          # position t predicts token t+1\n    t = teacher_logits[:, :-1].float()\n    y = labels[:, 1:]\n    mask = (y != ignore_index).float()\n    # soft term: KL(teacher || student) at temperature T, scaled by T^2\n    log_p_s = F.log_softmax(s / T, dim=-1)\n    p_t = F.softmax(t / T, dim=-1)\n    soft = F.kl_div(log_p_s, p_t, reduction=\"none\").sum(-1)      # [batch, seq-1]\n    soft = (soft * mask).sum() / mask.sum() * (T * T)\n    # hard term: ordinary next-token cross-entropy against the ground-truth tokens\n    hard = F.cross_entropy(s.reshape(-1, s.size(-1)), y.reshape(-1), ignore_index=ignore_index)\n    return alpha * soft + (1 - alpha) * hard\n\n# one training step; `teacher` is in eval mode with requires_grad disabled\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    t_out = teacher(input_ids=batch[\"input_ids\"], attention_mask=batch[\"attention_mask\"])\ns_out = student(input_ids=batch[\"input_ids\"], attention_mask=batch[\"attention_mask\"])\nloss = kd_loss(s_out.logits, t_out.logits, batch[\"labels\"], T=2.0, alpha=0.7)\nloss.backward(); optimizer.step(); optimizer.zero_grad()\n# For sequence-level distillation there is no kd_loss at all: generate teacher responses offline,\n# filter them with a verifier, and run plain SFT on the survivors.\n```\n\n### See temperature expose dark knowledge\nPure Python; runs anywhere. Same teacher logits as the table above.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef softmax(z, T=1.0):\n    e = [math.exp(x / T) for x in z]\n    s = sum(e)\n    return [x / s for x in e]\n\nteacher = [6, 2, 1]\nfor T in (1, 2, 4, 20):\n    p = softmax(teacher, T)\n    tail = p[1] + p[2]\n    print(f\"T={T:>2}: p={[round(x, 3) for x in p]}  tail mass={tail:.1%}  p2/p3={p[1]/p[2]:.2f}\")\n# T= 1: p=[0.976, 0.018, 0.007]  tail mass=2.4%  p2/p3=2.72\n# T= 2: p=[0.821, 0.111, 0.067]  tail mass=17.9%  p2/p3=1.65\n# T= 4: p=[0.604, 0.222, 0.173]  tail mass=39.6%  p2/p3=1.28   (0.6045 before rounding)\n# T=20: p=[0.385, 0.315, 0.3]  tail mass=61.5%  p2/p3=1.05    <- near uniform, ranking washes out\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: why teams cache top-k logits\nStorage for teacher targets over 100M training tokens with a 128k-entry vocabulary (illustrative sizing):\n\n| Option | Bytes per token | Total for 100M tokens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Full distribution, fp16 | 128,000 × 2 = 256 KB | ~25.6 TB |\n| Top-64: int32 index + fp16 value | 64 × 6 = 384 bytes | ~38.4 GB |\n\nTop-64 is about 670× smaller and fits on one disk, at the cost of dropping the long tail beyond rank 64,\nwhich is why the kept entries are renormalised.","patterns":["**Offline teacher pass with cached targets.** When the tokenizer is shared, run the teacher once over the prompt corpus and write top-*k* indices and log-probs to sharded files keyed by example id, so student runs never wait on the teacher. Monitor the probability mass the top-*k* captures per position.","**Rejection-sample before sequence-level SFT.** Generate several teacher samples per prompt and keep only those that pass a verifier (unit tests, schema check, ground-truth label match). Track pass rate per prompt category so thin categories are visible.","**Student-first cascade with teacher fallback.** Serve the student and escalate to the teacher when a confidence signal or a validator fails. Monitor escalation rate and task metric on escalated versus non-escalated traffic.","**Mix general data into the student's training set.** Keep a slice of general instruction data so the narrow capability does not erode other skills. Run the same general benchmark before and after every distillation run.","**Teacher licence register.** Record for every teacher model or API whether its terms permit training other models on its outputs, and block pipelines that use unapproved teachers."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P[(\"Prompts from real traffic\")] --> Q[\"Shared tokenizer and teacher logits?\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| WB[\"White-box: teacher pass, cache top-k logits\"]\n    Q -->|\"no, text only\"| BB[\"Black-box: generate teacher responses\"]\n    WB --> KD[\"Train student: alpha T^2 KL plus (1 - alpha) CE\"]\n    BB --> F[\"Verifier filter or rejection sampling\"]\n    F --> SFT[\"Plain SFT on surviving responses\"]\n    KD --> OP[\"Optional on-policy pass: teacher scores student samples\"]\n    OP --> E[\"Eval on held-out real traffic plus a general benchmark\"]\n    KD -->|\"skip on-policy\"| E\n    SFT --> E","caption":"The decision path for a distillation pipeline: tokenizer and logit access pick the regime, and every\nregime ends in the same held-out evaluation on real traffic. The on-policy pass hangs off the white-box\nbranch because the teacher must score every token the student writes."},"interview":[{"q":"What is \"dark knowledge\", and why does distillation raise the softmax temperature?","a":"- **Definition:** the teacher's ranking of wrong answers (` the` 0.05, ` Berlin` 0.0001) carries\n  information about language and the world that a one-hot label throws away.\n- **Why temperature:** at *T* = 1 a confident teacher is almost one-hot, so the tail gives almost no\n  gradient; raising *T* flattens it and exposes the ranking without inverting it.\n- **The correction:** the soft term is multiplied by *T²* because its gradient shrinks roughly as 1/*T²*."},{"q":"Your distilled student scores exactly the same as a plain SFT baseline. What do you check?","a":"- **Temperature and mixing weight:** with *T* = 1 and a high α the soft targets are nearly one-hot, so\n  the student learns nothing beyond SFT; try *T* in roughly 1–4.\n- **Tokenizer alignment:** a naive logit loss across different tokenizers is meaningless, so confirm both\n  models index the same vocabulary or switch to sequence-level distillation.\n- **The pipeline:** log the soft and hard terms separately to confirm the soft term is non-zero and\n  weighted in, and that only padded positions are masked out of it."},{"q":"Forward or reverse KL for a small student: which, and why?","a":"- **Forward KL(teacher ‖ student)** is mode-covering: it punishes near-zero mass anywhere the teacher is\n  plausible, so a low-capacity student spreads thin and samples look hedged.\n- **Reverse KL(student ‖ teacher)** is mode-seeking: the student commits to the teacher's dominant modes,\n  which usually suits a much smaller model (MiniLLM's choice).\n- **Treat it as a tunable:** generalised-KD interpolates between them, and either way the teacher caps\n  the student on the covered distribution."},{"q":"Why do teams cache only the teacher's top-k logits? Give rough numbers.","a":"- **Full distributions are huge:** a 128k-entry vocabulary in fp16 is 256 KB per token, about 25.6 TB for\n  100M training tokens.\n- **Top-64 is about 670× smaller:** 64 × (int32 index + fp16 value) = 384 bytes per token, about 38.4 GB.\n- **The cost:** the tail beyond rank 64 is dropped, so the kept entries are renormalised; track how much\n  probability mass the top-*k* captures."},{"q":"A bigger teacher gave you a worse student. Why might that happen, and when would you not distil?","a":"- **Capacity gap:** a student far smaller than its teacher can learn less from it than from a mid-sized\n  intermediate, the teacher-assistant effect; try distilling through a middle model.\n- **Avoid distilling** when the product needs the teacher's full breadth, because students degrade faster\n  outside the covered prompt distribution.\n- **Also avoid** when you cannot evaluate on held-out real data, or the teacher's terms forbid training on\n  its outputs."}],"tradeoffs":["**Accuracy per parameter vs teacher compute.** You pay a teacher forward pass over the whole training set, plus storage if you cache logits; for sequence-level distillation the bill is teacher generation, the expensive direction.","**Token-level vs black-box.** Token-level carries far more signal per example but needs a shared tokenizer and logits. Black-box works across families and APIs but transfers less per example and copies the teacher's mistakes verbatim.","**Off-policy vs on-policy.** Off-policy sequence distillation is simple and embarrassingly parallel. On-policy fixes exposure bias but needs generation in the loop and a teacher at training time, roughly turning a data-processing job into an RL-shaped one.","**Narrow strength vs breadth.** A student is strong on the covered prompt distribution and degrades faster than the teacher outside it: fine for a narrow product, a problem for a general assistant.","**API teachers vs terms of service.** Distilling from a third-party API may be forbidden by its terms for training competing models; check before building a pipeline around it."],"pitfalls":["**Leaving *T* = 1 and α high.** Soft targets are nearly one-hot, so the student learns nothing beyond SFT. Symptom: the distilled model matches the plain fine-tuning baseline exactly.","**Distilling across tokenizers with a naive logit loss.** Vocabularies do not align, so the KL is meaningless. Symptom: loss plateaus high or goes NaN. Fix: align tokens or use sequence-level distillation.","**Training on unfiltered teacher generations.** The student inherits hallucinations and verbosity. Symptom: confidently wrong in exactly the teacher's way. Fix: verifier or rejection sampling first.","**Measuring agreement with the teacher on training prompts.** Use task metrics on held-out real traffic instead. Symptom: a high \"fidelity\" number and unhappy users.","**Choosing the biggest teacher regardless of the gap.** A student far smaller than its teacher can learn *less* from it than from a mid-sized intermediate (the teacher-assistant effect). Symptom: a larger teacher produces a worse student.","**Forgetting general data in the mix.** Distilling a narrow capability into a small instruct model erodes other skills. Symptom: a drop on a general benchmark run before and after."],"when":"- **Use when:** a strong model is too slow or costly for deployment, you have many prompts (labelled or\n  not) representing real usage, and the target surface is narrower than the teacher's.\n- **Right tool for:** turning a frontier model's behaviour on *your* task into a small model on one GPU,\n  and building reasoning-capable small models from a reasoning-capable large one.\n- **Avoid when:** the product needs the teacher's full breadth, or you cannot evaluate the student on\n  real held-out data.\n- **Measure:** student versus teacher on your task metric, latency and cost per thousand requests, and a\n  general benchmark for collateral damage.\n- **Interviews:** explain dark knowledge and the *T²* factor, say when sequence-level distillation is\n  just SFT on synthetic data, and argue why on-policy distillation exists.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the pipeline that runs the teacher over prompt logs, stores top-*k* logits or filtered generations, and trains the student with a temperature-scaled loss, verifying gains on held-out tasks.","architect":"Decides which capabilities justify a frontier model and which should be distilled into a small hosted one, and owns the policy on which teachers are legally and contractually usable.","fde":"Distils a customer's expensive prompt-chain into a small model trained on their own traffic inside their environment, so the solution survives their cost and data-residency constraints."},"check":{"q":"A team runs a 70B model for ticket triage plus a short drafted reply, at a cost they cannot sustain.\nThey want an 8B replacement. Lay out how you would distil it and what could go wrong.","a":"Collect several hundred thousand real tickets from logs, deduplicated and scrubbed of PII, and run\nthe 70B teacher over them to produce triage labels and drafts. Filter: drop drafts failing a format check\nand, where ground-truth triage labels exist from agents, drop teacher outputs that disagree. If both models\nshare a tokenizer, store the teacher's top-64 logits per position and train the 8B student with a\ntemperature-scaled KL plus cross-entropy; otherwise run SFT on the filtered teacher text. Evaluate on a\nheld-out month of tickets against the teacher's metrics, not against the teacher's outputs. Risks: the\nstudent copies teacher hallucinations if filtering is weak; it degrades on ticket types rare in the sample;\nand on-policy problems appear in long drafts, which argues for a final on-policy distillation pass or at\nleast rejection sampling with the teacher as judge."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Hinton's distillation loss multiply the soft-target term by T²?","options":["To penalise the student more heavily for confident mistakes","Because the soft-term gradient shrinks roughly as 1/T², so T² keeps its weight stable across temperatures","To convert the KL divergence into a cross-entropy","Because the teacher's logits are stored at temperature T"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Softening by T scales the logit gradients down by about 1/T². Without the correction, raising T to expose dark knowledge would also silently shrink the soft term relative to the hard-label term."},{"q":"Which distillation regime requires the teacher and student to share a tokenizer?","options":["Sequence-level distillation on generated text","Token-level distillation that matches next-token distributions","Rejection-sampled SFT on teacher outputs","Any distillation from an API-only teacher"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Matching distributions position by position only makes sense if the vocabulary indices mean the same thing in both models. Text-based regimes transfer through tokens, so any tokenizer pair works."},{"q":"What problem does on-policy distillation solve that sequence-level distillation leaves open?","options":["The teacher's outputs are too long to fit the student's context","The student cannot see the teacher's logits","The student only ever trains on teacher-written prefixes and never learns to recover from its own errors","The teacher must be re-run every epoch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Training on teacher trajectories creates exposure bias: at inference the student conditions on its own imperfect prefixes. Sampling from the student and scoring with the teacher trains on exactly that situation."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/quantization","sources":["Dettmers et al., 2022 — LLM.int8(): 8-bit Matrix Multiplication for Transformers at Scale","Frantar et al., 2022 — GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","Lin et al., 2023 — AWQ: Activation-aware Weight Quantization for LLM Compression and Acceleration","Official docs: llama.cpp (GGUF)"],"why":"- **Memory.** A 70B-parameter model in bf16 is 140 GB of weights and does not fit one 80 GB GPU; at 4 bits\n  it is about 35–40 GB and does. A 7B model goes from 14 GB to about 4 GB: data-centre card to laptop.\n- **Speed.** During decode the GPU reads every weight once per token, with tiny arithmetic per weight (one\n  multiply-add per token at batch size 1). Time per token ≈ weight bytes ÷ memory bandwidth, so halving\n  the bytes nearly doubles small-batch decode speed. It is a bandwidth optimisation wearing a compression\n  costume.\n- **\"Train a smaller model instead\" loses more quality.** At a fixed memory budget, a 4-bit model with\n  roughly four times the parameters generally beats the 16-bit model filling the same bytes. That is the\n  core finding of the k-bit inference scaling-law work (Dettmers & Zettlemoyer), and why 4-bit became the\n  default for local models.\n- **\"Just cast to int8 with one scale per tensor\" fails on outliers.** Transformers past a few billion\n  parameters develop a handful of activation channels tens of times larger than the rest. One such value\n  sets the whole tensor's scale, so everything else rounds to zero or one level.\n- **Every modern method attacks that range problem:** finer-grained scales, outlier-aware scaling, or\n  error compensation.","how":"### The arithmetic of a quantizer\nSymmetric *b*-bit quantization maps a real *x* to an integer *q* with one scale *s*:\n\n```\ns = max|x| / (2^(b−1) − 1)        q = clamp(round(x / s), −2^(b−1), 2^(b−1) − 1)        x̂ = s · q\n```\n\n- **Levels:** INT8 gives 127 levels on each side of zero; INT4 gives only −8…7.\n- **Asymmetric (affine):** adds a zero-point *z* so the grid covers [min, max] exactly. Matters for skewed\n  distributions; the usual choice for 4-bit weights.\n- **Error:** roughly uniform in [−*s*/2, *s*/2] with mean-squared error *s*²/12, so error grows with the\n  range the scale must cover. That one fact explains the granularity ladder.\n\n### The granularity ladder\n| Granularity | Scales | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Per-tensor | one | cheapest; ruined by a single outlier |\n| Per-channel | one per output row | standard for INT8 |\n| Per-group | one (plus zero-point) per *g* consecutive input weights, *g* = 128 or 32 | an outlier only degrades its own group |\n\n- **Per-group overhead:** INT4 with *g* = 128, an fp16 scale and a 4-bit zero-point costs 4 + 20/128 ≈\n  4.16 bits per weight; *g* = 32 costs about 4.6.\n- **\"4-bit\" models are never exactly 4 bits.**\n\n### Weights only, or weights and activations\n- **W4A16 / W8A16 (weight-only).**\n  - Low-bit storage, dequantized to fp16/bf16 inside the matmul kernel. GPTQ and AWQ live here.\n  - Memory and bandwidth drop; compute does not, because the multiply still runs in 16-bit.\n  - Helps decode (bandwidth-bound), does nothing for prefill (compute-bound). At large batch sizes the\n    dequantization overhead can make W4A16 slower than the fp16 original.\n- **W8A8 (weights and activations).**\n  - The matmul runs on INT8 (or FP8) tensor cores at roughly twice fp16 throughput.\n  - Activations are harder: outlier channels, and they change with every input.\n  - LLM.int8() pulls the few outlier columns into an fp16 side-matmul. SmoothQuant migrates activation\n    range into the weights with a per-channel scale before quantizing both.\n- **FP8 (E4M3) on GPUs with native support.** A float format has a built-in exponent and copes with\n  dynamic range, so FP8 weights+activations+KV cache became the default \"almost free\" setting for\n  throughput serving on that hardware.\n- **KV cache.** Quantizing it (8-bit is common) doubles concurrent sequences at long context; see the KV\n  cache lesson for why that is often the binding constraint rather than weights.\n\n### PTQ versus QAT\n| | Post-training (PTQ) | Quantization-aware training (QAT) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Input | trained model + calibration set (a few hundred sequences) | a fine-tuning run |\n| Time | minutes to hours | a training run |\n| Mechanism | fit quantized values to calibration data | simulate quantization in forward, straight-through estimator in backward, so the weights learn to live on the grid |\n| Best fit | the common 4–8 bit settings, where it is cheap and usually good enough | extreme bit-widths (2–3 bits) or small models, where it is more accurate |\n| Examples | GPTQ, AWQ | QAT fine-tunes |\n\nQLoRA sits in between: a frozen 4-bit base with trainable adapters that learn around its rounding error\n(see the PEFT lesson).\n\n### GPTQ: compensate the error you just made\n1. **Objective.** Round-to-nearest treats weights independently. GPTQ minimises the layer's reconstruction\n   error ‖**WX** − **ŴX**‖² over calibration inputs **X**, using Optimal Brain Surgeon's second-order\n   machinery.\n2. **Hessian.** **H** = 2**XXᵀ**, which depends on the inputs, not on **W**.\n3. **Column by column.** Round column *j*, compute the error, and update all still-unquantized columns by a\n   multiple of the matching row of **H⁻¹** so they absorb it.\n4. **Made practical.** A fixed column order, a Cholesky factor of **H⁻¹** and lazy batched updates made it\n   stable and fast enough to quantize a 175B model in a few GPU-hours.\n\n- **Result:** 4-bit (and usable 3-bit) weights with a small perplexity increase.\n- **Caveat:** it fits **W** to the calibration inputs, so it is somewhat sensitive to calibration domain.\n\n### AWQ: protect the weights that matter\n- **Saliency comes from activations.** A small weight on a channel that always carries large activations\n  contributes a lot to the output, so its rounding error hurts. A weight's own magnitude is not the signal.\n- **Keeping ~1% of channels in fp16 works** but yields a mixed-precision layout hardware dislikes.\n- **The free trick.** Multiply a salient weight channel by *s* > 1 and divide the matching activation\n  channel by *s*. The product is unchanged, but the weight is larger relative to the rounding step, so its\n  relative error falls.\n- **Choosing scales.** A small grid search (*s* = mean activation magnitude^α, α ∈ [0, 1]) minimising\n  output error on calibration data.\n- **Why it generalises.** No gradient and no weight reconstruction, so AWQ runs fast, overfits calibration\n  less than GPTQ, and transfers well across domains and modalities.\n- **Kernels.** Both ship with fast INT4 GPU kernels (Marlin, ExLlama) in vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM.\n\n### GGUF: a container, not a method\n- **What it is.** The single-file format of llama.cpp (it replaced the older GGML format in 2023). One\n  file holds tensors, per-tensor quantization types and all metadata: architecture hyperparameters,\n  tokenizer vocabulary and chat template, laid out for memory-mapping.\n- **Why it exists.** llama.cpp targets CPUs, Apple silicon and mixed CPU/GPU machines that offload layer\n  by layer; block layouts are designed for SIMD dequantization there.\n- **On a GPU server** GPTQ/AWQ/FP8 through a batching engine usually out-throughputs it.\n\n| GGUF type | Scheme | Bits per weight |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Q8_0`, `Q4_0` | blocks of 32, one fp16 scale | about 8.5 and 4.5 |\n| K-quants: `Q4_K_M`, `Q5_K_M`, `Q6_K` | 256-weight super-blocks, compact sub-block scales | varies |\n| `S`/`M`/`L` suffix | mixing recipe: sensitive tensors (attention value/output, parts of MLP) kept higher | varies |\n| I-quants: `IQ2_XXS`…`IQ4_XS` | non-uniform codebooks, optional importance matrix from calibration | below 4 |","example":"### Why the granularity ladder matters (NumPy)\nA Gaussian weight matrix gets one outlier input channel, as real transformer weights and (more severely)\nactivations do. Relative output error on random activations shows per-tensor INT4 collapsing, per-channel\nbarely helping, and per-group scales rescuing it.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nW = rng.normal(0, 0.02, size=(4096, 4096)).astype(np.float32)   # [out, in]\nW[:, 7] *= 40                                                   # one outlier input channel\n\ndef quant_sym(x, bits, axis=None):\n    \"\"\"Symmetric round-to-nearest with one scale per slice along `axis` (None = per-tensor).\"\"\"\n    qmax = 2 ** (bits - 1) - 1\n    s = np.max(np.abs(x), axis=axis, keepdims=True) / qmax\n    q = np.clip(np.round(x / s), -qmax - 1, qmax)\n    return (q * s).astype(np.float32)\n\ndef quant_grouped(x, bits, g):\n    rows, cols = x.shape\n    return quant_sym(x.reshape(rows, cols // g, g), bits, axis=2).reshape(rows, cols)\n\nX = rng.normal(size=(512, 4096)).astype(np.float32)             # calibration activations\nref = X @ W.T\nfor name, W_hat in [(\"int8 per-tensor\", quant_sym(W, 8)),\n                    (\"int4 per-tensor\", quant_sym(W, 4)),\n                    (\"int4 per-channel\", quant_sym(W, 4, axis=1)),\n                    (\"int4 group=128\", quant_grouped(W, 4, 128)),\n                    (\"int4 group=32\", quant_grouped(W, 4, 32))]:\n    err = np.linalg.norm(X @ W_hat.T - ref) / np.linalg.norm(ref)\n    print(f\"{name:17s} relative output error = {err:.3f}\")\n# Prints roughly 0.32 / 0.85 / 0.68 / 0.16 / 0.10 (int8 per-tensor already hurts with a 40x outlier).\n# Per-channel still fails here because every output row contains the outlier column, so every\n# row's scale is set by it; grouping isolates the damage to 1/32 (g=128) or 1/128 (g=32) of the weights.\n# GPTQ would additionally push each group's rounding error into its neighbours; AWQ would rescale\n# channel 7 before rounding. Bits per weight with an fp16 scale + 4-bit zero-point per group, as in\n# the ladder above: 8 / 4 / 4 / ~4.16 / ~4.6 respectively (this symmetric toy stores no zero-point).\n```\n\n### Sizing calculator: real bits per weight\nPure Python. Shows why \"4-bit\" 70B lands at 35–40 GB, not 35 GB flat.\n\n```python\ndef bits_per_weight(bits, group=None, scale_bits=16, zero_bits=0):\n    return bits if group is None else bits + (scale_bits + zero_bits) / group\n\ndef weights_gb(params_b, bpw):\n    return params_b * 1e9 * bpw / 8 / 1e9\n\nschemes = {\n    \"bf16\":              bits_per_weight(16),\n    \"int8 per-channel\":  bits_per_weight(8),            # per-row scales: negligible overhead\n    \"int4 g=128 (+zp)\":  bits_per_weight(4, 128, 16, 4),\n    \"int4 g=32 (+zp)\":   bits_per_weight(4, 32, 16, 4),\n    \"Q4_0 (block 32)\":   bits_per_weight(4, 32, 16),\n}\nfor name, bpw in schemes.items():\n    print(f\"{name:18s} {bpw:5.2f} bits/w   7B: {weights_gb(7, bpw):5.1f} GB   70B: {weights_gb(70, bpw):6.1f} GB\")\n# bf16               16.00 bits/w   7B:  14.0 GB   70B:  140.0 GB\n# int8 per-channel    8.00 bits/w   7B:   7.0 GB   70B:   70.0 GB\n# int4 g=128 (+zp)    4.16 bits/w   7B:   3.6 GB   70B:   36.4 GB\n# int4 g=32 (+zp)     4.62 bits/w   7B:   4.0 GB   70B:   40.5 GB\n# Q4_0 (block 32)     4.50 bits/w   7B:   3.9 GB   70B:   39.4 GB\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what INT4 does to decode and prefill\nA 7B model on a GPU with about 2 TB/s of bandwidth (bandwidth floor only; rough orders of magnitude, not a\nbenchmark):\n\n| Workload | bf16 (14 GB) | INT4 g=128 (3.6 GB) | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Decode, batch 1 | ~7 ms/token floor | ~1.8 ms/token floor | bytes read per token fall ~4× |\n| Prefill of a long RAG prompt | compute-bound | about the same, or slower | weight-only keeps 16-bit FLOPs, adds dequant |\n| High-batch serving | baseline | can lose to fp16 or FP8 W8A8 | dequant overhead dominates |\n\nReal decode gains are smaller than the floor suggests, because KV-cache reads and kernel overhead do not\nshrink.","patterns":["**FP8 first on hardware that supports it.** For throughput serving, enable FP8 weights and KV cache in the engine (in vLLM, `--quantization fp8` and `--kv-cache-dtype fp8`), then run task evals. Monitor eval deltas, time to first token and tokens per second at real batch size.","**Serve pre-quantized INT4 checkpoints for single-GPU fits.** Quantize once with AWQ or GPTQ offline on in-domain calibration data, publish the checkpoint, and point the engine at it (vLLM detects the format from the checkpoint config). Confirm a fast kernel (Marlin, ExLlama) is actually selected in the engine logs.","**Quantize from the highest-precision source.** Keep the bf16 master in the model registry and derive every format (AWQ, GPTQ, each GGUF type) from it, never from another quantized file, because errors compound.","**GGUF build pipeline for edge targets.** Convert with llama.cpp's `convert_hf_to_gguf.py`, then quantize with `llama-quantize` to `Q4_K_M` or `Q5_K_M`; for I-quants, build an importance matrix from in-domain text first. Check that the chat template and end-of-turn token survive by running a scripted conversation.","**Eval-delta release gate.** No quantized model ships without task evals versus its full-precision parent, covering maths, code and minority languages if you serve them. Block on regressions above your agreed threshold, not on perplexity alone."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[(\"bf16 master checkpoint\")] --> T[\"Where does it run?\"]\n    T -->|\"GPU with native FP8\"| F[\"FP8 weights, activations and KV cache\"]\n    T -->|\"must fit a smaller or single GPU\"| C[\"Calibrate on in-domain data\"]\n    C --> G[\"GPTQ: compensate rounding error column by column\"]\n    C --> A[\"AWQ: rescale salient channels, then round\"]\n    G --> I4[\"INT4 per-group, about 4.16 bits per weight\"]\n    A --> I4\n    T -->|\"laptop, CPU or edge\"| U[\"Convert to GGUF, Q4_K_M or Q5_K_M\"]\n    F --> E[\"Task evals vs bf16 parent, tokens per second at real batch\"]\n    I4 --> E\n    U --> E","caption":"Read it as the decision path from deployment target to format; every branch ends at the same eval gate\nagainst the bf16 parent."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does weight-only INT4 speed up decode but not prefill?","a":"- **Decode is bandwidth-bound:** each token reads every weight once with about one multiply-add per\n  weight at batch 1, so time per token ≈ weight bytes ÷ bandwidth and about 4× fewer bytes lowers that floor about 4×\n  (real gains are smaller, because KV-cache reads and kernel overhead do not shrink).\n- **Prefill is compute-bound:** W4A16 dequantizes to 16-bit inside the kernel, so FLOPs do not fall and\n  time-to-first-token stays the same or gets worse.\n- **To cut compute** you need W8A8 or FP8 on tensor cores that run it at roughly twice fp16 throughput."},{"q":"Why does per-tensor INT8 break on large transformers, and how do the main methods fix it?","a":"- **The cause:** past a few billion parameters a handful of activation channels grow tens of times larger\n  than the rest, and one outlier sets the whole tensor's scale, so other values round to zero or one level.\n- **Finer scales:** per-channel or per-group (*g* = 128 or 32) confine an outlier's damage to its slice.\n- **Outlier handling:** LLM.int8() routes outlier columns to an fp16 side-matmul; SmoothQuant moves\n  activation range into the weights; AWQ scales salient weight channels up before rounding."},{"q":"GPTQ or AWQ for a code model when your calibration set is small? Defend the choice.","a":"- **GPTQ** minimises layer reconstruction error on the calibration inputs with second-order updates, so it\n  is somewhat sensitive to calibration domain and can overfit a small or mismatched set.\n- **AWQ** uses activation statistics only to pick per-channel scales by a small grid search, with no\n  weight reconstruction, so it overfits calibration less and transfers better across domains.\n- **Either way:** calibrate on in-domain code, and decide on task evals, not perplexity."},{"q":"How much memory do a 7B model's INT4 weights need with group 128? Show the arithmetic.","a":"- **Bits per weight:** 4 + (16-bit scale + 4-bit zero-point) / 128 = 4 + 20/128 ≈ 4.16.\n- **Weights:** 7 × 10⁹ × 4.16 / 8 ≈ 3.6 GB, against 14 GB in bf16; at *g* = 32 it is about 4.6 bits and\n  4.0 GB.\n- **Then add** the KV cache at your context length and concurrency, because sizing at a flat 4.0 bits and\n  forgetting the cache is a classic OOM."},{"q":"Your quantized model passed a perplexity check, but maths and Spanish users complain. What happened?","a":"- **Perplexity hides it:** it can move by 0.1 while maths, instruction following or a minority language\n  regress sharply, so the gate measured the wrong thing.\n- **Likely causes:** calibration on English web text instead of in-domain data, or a checkpoint\n  re-quantized from another quantized file, which compounds errors.\n- **Fix:** re-quantize from the bf16 master with in-domain calibration, add maths and per-language task\n  evals to the release gate, and step up to group 32, 8-bit or FP8 if 4-bit still regresses."}],"tradeoffs":["**Memory and speed vs quality.** Gains are roughly proportional to bits saved. Loss is small at 8-bit and usually modest at 4-bit for 7B and up; it grows for small models and precision-sensitive tasks (maths, code, long multi-step reasoning), where it must be measured.","**Weight-only INT4 vs W8A8.** INT4 speeds small-batch decode but not prefill, and at high batch its dequantization overhead can lose to fp16 or FP8 W8A8. The choice flips with batch size and with prompt-heavy (RAG) versus generation-heavy (chat) workloads.","**Smaller groups vs overhead.** They cut error but add bits per weight and kernel complexity; *g* = 128 is the usual compromise, and *g* = 32 pays off mainly below 4 bits or on small models.","**PTQ vs QAT.** PTQ is cheap and fast but depends on calibration data matching deployment. QAT is more robust at extreme bit-widths but costs a training run and a pipeline most teams lack.","**GGUF portability vs GPU throughput.** GGUF runs nearly anywhere with partial GPU offload and one portable file; a batching GPU engine with AWQ/GPTQ/FP8 kernels gives far higher throughput per dollar for a multi-user service."],"pitfalls":["**Evaluating only perplexity on generic text.** Perplexity can move by 0.1 while instruction following, maths or a minority language regress sharply. Symptom: a clean report and a stream of complaints. Run your task evals before and after.","**Calibrating a code or multilingual model on English web text.** Quality looks fine on general prompts and degrades exactly in the deployment domain; GPTQ is more sensitive than AWQ here.","**Expecting INT4 to cut time-to-first-token on long prompts.** Prefill is compute-bound and weight-only quantization does not reduce FLOPs. Symptom: unchanged or worse TTFT.","**Sizing hardware at 4.0 bits per weight.** Scales and zero-points make it 4.2–5.5 depending on scheme, and the KV cache is extra. Symptom: OOM on a card that \"should have fit\".","**Re-quantizing an already quantized checkpoint.** GPTQ → GGUF, or a merged QLoRA model, compounds errors. Symptom: noticeably worse than the same scheme applied to the bf16 original.","**Losing tokenizer or chat-template metadata in GGUF conversion.** Symptom: the model rambles, never emits the end-of-turn token, or ignores the system prompt despite correct weights."],"when":"- **GPU servers:** start with FP8 if the hardware supports it: near-zero quality cost, faster prefill and\n  decode.\n- **INT4 AWQ or GPTQ:** when a model must fit a smaller or single GPU, or decode latency at low batch\n  dominates; verify on your evals.\n- **GGUF:** for a laptop, CPU box, edge device or developer machine; `Q4_K_M` or `Q5_K_M` are sensible\n  defaults, moving up when quality evals demand it.\n- **QLoRA, not a serving format:** when the goal is fine-tuning on a small GPU.\n- **Measure every time:** task evals against the unquantized model, tokens per second at your real batch\n  size, and total memory including the KV cache at your context length.\n- **Interviews:** derive bits per weight for a grouped scheme, explain why outliers break per-tensor\n  scaling, and contrast how GPTQ and AWQ decide what to protect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the quantization scheme per deployment target, runs the calibration and the task evals, and checks the serving engine actually has a fast kernel for that format before shipping.","architect":"Owns the fit between model size, precision, GPU class and concurrency targets, and sets the rule that no quantized model ships without an eval delta against its full-precision parent.","fde":"Gets a customer's chosen model running on the hardware they already own, often a single 24–48 GB card or a CPU-only box, by choosing AWQ/GPTQ or a GGUF quant and proving quality on their data."},"check":{"q":"You must serve a 70B-class model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128) on a single 80 GB GPU with 8k\ncontext. Which precision do you pick, how much room is left for the KV cache, and what else do you check?","a":"bf16 needs 140 GB, so it is out; INT8 needs about 70 GB, leaving almost nothing for activations and\ncache; INT4 with group 128 is about 4.2 bits per weight, roughly 37 GB, leaving around 40 GB. The KV cache\nper token in fp16 is 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 ≈ 0.33 MB, so an 8k sequence costs about 2.6 GB and about 15\nconcurrent sequences fit, more with an 8-bit cache. I would use AWQ (or GPTQ with in-domain calibration),\nrun the task evals against the bf16 model on a second machine, check that the engine has a fast INT4 kernel\nfor this architecture, and confirm the workload is decode-heavy, because INT4 does nothing for prefill. If\nquality drops too far, the honest options are two GPUs with FP8 or a smaller model."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does weight-only INT4 quantization speed up decoding but not prefill?","options":["Prefill runs on the CPU in most engines","Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, so reading fewer weight bytes helps; prefill is compute-bound and the matmul still runs in 16-bit","The KV cache is written in fp16 during prefill","INT4 kernels only support batch size 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Weight-only schemes dequantize to 16-bit inside the kernel, so FLOPs are unchanged. Decode reads all weights per token and benefits from fewer bytes; prefill is dominated by arithmetic over many tokens."},{"q":"How does AWQ decide which weights deserve protection?","options":["It keeps the largest-magnitude weights in fp16","It uses the Hessian of the reconstruction loss to rank weights","It looks at activation magnitudes per input channel and scales up the channels that carry large activations before rounding","It trains the weights with a straight-through estimator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"AWQ's observation is that saliency comes from the activations a weight multiplies, not the weight's own size. Rescaling those channels is output-preserving and shrinks their relative rounding error."},{"q":"What exactly is GGUF, and what does a GGUF file contain?","options":["A 4-bit quantization algorithm that replaces GPTQ","A single-file container used by llama.cpp holding per-tensor quantized weights plus tokenizer and architecture metadata","NVIDIA's FP8 format for Hopper GPUs","A calibration dataset for post-training quantization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GGUF is a file format: it bundles tensors in block-quantized types such as Q4_K_M with everything needed to run them. The quantization recipes are chosen when converting into it, not defined by it."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/pruning","sources":["Han et al., 2015 — Learning both Weights and Connections for Efficient Neural Networks","Frantar & Alistarh, 2023 — SparseGPT: Massive Language Models Can Be Accurately Pruned in One-Shot","Sun et al., 2023 — A Simple and Effective Pruning Approach for Large Language Models (Wanda)","Muralidharan et al., 2024 — Compact Language Models via Pruning and Knowledge Distillation"],"why":"- **Large models are heavily over-parameterised.** They are trained big because width and depth make\n  optimisation easy and sample-efficient, not because every parameter is needed at inference. Much\n  inference compute moves the output very little.\n- **The slack is well documented.** Many attention heads can be removed after training with little loss;\n  a sizeable fraction of middle layers barely change the residual stream they receive; half the individual\n  weights of a large model can be zeroed in one shot with a modest perplexity increase. You pay for that\n  slack on every token served.\n- **Training small from scratch is the expensive route.** It costs the full pretraining budget, trillions\n  of tokens, and the result was never as good as the big one. A pruned-and-repaired model starts from the\n  parent's knowledge; recovery has been reported to need tens of times fewer tokens.\n- **Quantization alone hits a floor.** It shaves a constant factor on bytes but leaves parameter count and\n  FLOPs unchanged, and bottoms out around 4 bits. Pruning attacks the count and composes with\n  quantization: fewer weights at fewer bits multiply.\n- **Hardware decides what counts.** GPUs multiply dense matrices; a matrix 50% zeros in random places\n  costs the same unless the kernel knows the pattern. The real question is \"which *shape* of removal can\n  the hardware cash in\", not \"which weights are unimportant\".","how":"### What you remove: the granularity ladder\n| Granularity | Removes | Accuracy at a given sparsity | GPU speedup |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unstructured | individual weights anywhere | best | almost none |\n| Semi-structured 2:4 | two of every four consecutive weights | middle | up to 2× matmul on Ampere+ |\n| Structured | heads, neurons, channels, layers | largest hit per parameter | everywhere, no special support |\n\n- **Unstructured.**\n  - Most freedom, so best accuracy, but an irregular sparse matrix: dense kernels gain nothing.\n  - Sparse storage needs column indices whose overhead eats most of the saving at 50%; general sparse\n    kernels only beat dense at very high sparsity (around 90% and up).\n  - Useful for CPU runtimes with sparse support and research, rarely for GPU serving.\n- **Semi-structured N:M, in practice 2:4.**\n  - NVIDIA GPUs from Ampere onward have sparse tensor cores that run this pattern at up to twice the dense\n    matmul rate.\n  - Storage: the two survivors plus 2-bit position indices. Per four 16-bit weights, 2 × 16 + 2 × 2 = 36\n    bits instead of 64, about 56% of dense.\n  - Sparsity is fixed at 50% and the constraint is local, so quality drops more than free unstructured\n    pruning at the same ratio.\n- **Structured.**\n  - Attention heads: the matching slices of W_Q, W_K, W_V and W_O.\n  - MLP neurons: columns of the up/gate projections and the matching rows of the down projection.\n  - Hidden channels: hard, because the residual stream touches every layer.\n  - Entire layers: depth pruning.\n  - The output is a smaller *dense* model, so every kernel on every device gets faster. The coarsest\n    constraint costs the most quality, which is why recovery training almost always follows.\n\n### How you decide: importance scores\n- **Magnitude (|w|).** Classic, cheap, and surprisingly good after training with weight decay. On large\n  LLMs it fails in one shot at 50% because of outlier features: a small weight on an input channel that\n  always carries huge activations is important, and magnitude cannot see that.\n- **Activation-aware (Wanda).**\n  - Score each weight by |W_ij| · ‖X_j‖₂: magnitude times the norm of its input feature over a few\n    calibration sequences.\n  - Prune the lowest scores *within each output row*, so every output keeps the same number of inputs.\n  - No gradients, no retraining, seconds per layer. It matches far more expensive methods at 50% on large\n    models precisely because it fixes the outlier blind spot.\n- **Second-order (SparseGPT).**\n  - The Optimal Brain Surgeon machinery GPTQ uses. Prune the weight whose removal least increases\n    ‖WX − ŴX‖², then update the row's remaining weights with **H⁻¹** = (XXᵀ)⁻¹ to compensate.\n  - Column-wise with shared Hessian updates, it prunes 100B+ models to 50–60% unstructured or 2:4 in a few\n    hours on one GPU, more accurately than Wanda at the cost of the Hessian work.\n- **Gradient / Taylor (|w · ∂L/∂w| and group versions).** Estimate the loss change from removing a unit;\n  the usual choice for heads and neurons.\n- **Layer influence by cosine similarity.** Compare a layer's input and output hidden states on\n  calibration data. Similarity near 1 means a near-identity block that can be dropped first. The first and\n  last few layers almost never qualify.\n\n### When you prune and how you recover\n- **One-shot (Wanda, SparseGPT).** Prune a finished model and stop: the right tool for a quick 50% result.\n- **Iterative.** Alternate prune → fine-tune → prune with a gradually ramping sparsity schedule, because\n  the network needs time to reroute around each cut.\n- **Lottery tickets.** Iterative magnitude pruning of small vision networks found sparse subnetworks that,\n  rewound to early-training weights, train to full accuracy alone. Useful intuition for *why*\n  over-parameterised training works, but not a practical LLM recipe: finding the ticket costs more than\n  training the dense model.\n- **Structured LLM recipe: prune, then distil from the unpruned parent.** The pruned model is an excellent\n  initialisation; token-level distillation gives a dense repair signal at a small fraction of a\n  pretraining budget.\n\nReported findings from that line of work:\n\n| Choice | Quality at equal parameter count | Latency win |\n|---|---|---|\n| Width pruning (heads, MLP, embedding channels) | better preserved | smaller |\n| Depth pruning (layers) | worse | bigger: fewer sequential layers, fewer kernel launches per token |\n\n**Contrast mixture-of-experts.** MoE is structured sparsity designed in and *learned during pretraining*\n(each token activates a few experts), not carved out afterwards. At frontier scale it has been the more\nwidely adopted way to cut FLOPs per token; see that lesson.","example":"### Wanda vs magnitude pruning on one layer\nPyTorch, unstructured (per-row 50%) and 2:4. The calibration activations include a few outlier channels,\nexactly where magnitude scores go wrong and activation-aware scores do not.\n\n```python\nimport torch\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\nd_in, d_out, n_tok = 1024, 1024, 4096\nW = torch.randn(d_out, d_in) * 0.02                # [out, in]\nX = torch.randn(n_tok, d_in)\nX[:, torch.randperm(d_in)[:8]] *= 30                # a few scattered outlier input channels, as in real LLMs\n\ndef prune_rows(W, score, sparsity=0.5):\n    \"\"\"Zero the lowest-scoring fraction of weights within each output row (Wanda's comparison group).\"\"\"\n    k = int(W.shape[1] * sparsity)\n    idx = score.argsort(dim=1)[:, :k]\n    mask = torch.ones_like(W, dtype=torch.bool).scatter_(1, idx, False)\n    return W * mask\n\ndef prune_2of4(W, score):\n    \"\"\"Keep the two highest-scoring weights in every block of four along the input dimension.\"\"\"\n    s = score.view(W.shape[0], -1, 4)\n    keep = torch.zeros_like(s, dtype=torch.bool).scatter_(2, s.topk(2, dim=2).indices, True)\n    return W * keep.view_as(W)\n\nref = X @ W.T\nmagnitude = W.abs()                                 # |W_ij|\nwanda = W.abs() * X.norm(dim=0)                     # |W_ij| * ||X_j||_2\nfor name, Wp in [(\"magnitude 50%\", prune_rows(W, magnitude)), (\"wanda 50%\", prune_rows(W, wanda)),\n                 (\"magnitude 2:4\", prune_2of4(W, magnitude)), (\"wanda 2:4\", prune_2of4(W, wanda))]:\n    density = (Wp != 0).float().mean().item()\n    err = ((X @ Wp.T - ref).norm() / ref.norm()).item()\n    print(f\"{name:14s} density={density:.2f} relative output error={err:.3f}\")\n# Expect wanda to beat magnitude clearly: magnitude happily deletes small weights on the outlier\n# channels, which carry most of the output. Same density, very different damage.\n# In a real model you would score every linear layer with a few hundred calibration sequences,\n# then measure perplexity and task evals; for structured cuts, follow with distillation from W.\n```\n\n### Rank layers for depth pruning by block influence\nHugging Face Transformers. `hidden_states` holds the embedding output plus each layer's output, so entries\n*i* and *i+1* are layer *i*'s input and output. Use production prompts as calibration text.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"                                   # any causal LM\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float32).eval()\ntexts = [\"Summarise this support ticket: printer offline after update.\",\n         \"Write a SQL query that counts orders per customer.\"]   # use a few hundred real prompts\n\nsims = None\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for t in texts:\n        hs = model(**tok(t, return_tensors=\"pt\"), output_hidden_states=True).hidden_states\n        s = torch.stack([F.cosine_similarity(hs[i], hs[i + 1], dim=-1).mean() for i in range(len(hs) - 1)])\n        sims = s if sims is None else sims + s\nsims /= len(texts)\norder = sims.argsort(descending=True)                        # most identity-like first\nprint(\"drop candidates:\", [int(i) for i in order[:4]])       # skip the first and last few layers\n# Note: in many HF models the final entry of hidden_states is taken after the last norm, so the\n# last layer's score is not comparable to the others; another reason to exclude it.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what each cut buys an 8B model on an A100\nIllustrative planning numbers for a 32-layer model; confirm every row with your own benchmark.\n\n| Option | Weight bytes | Decode effect | Recovery needed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 50% unstructured | unchanged in a dense engine | none | optional |\n| 2:4 sparse | about 56% of dense | only if the kernel reads the compressed form | usually |\n| Drop 8 of 32 layers | about 75% of the layer weights | about a quarter less sequential work | distil from parent |\n| INT4 weights, no pruning | about 26% of bf16 | largest at low batch | none |\n\nThis is why quantization comes first: it hits the memory target with no training.","patterns":["**Quantize first, prune second.** When the target is bytes or decode latency, try INT8/INT4 before any pruning; prune only when parameter count or sequential depth is the constraint. Track tokens per second at deployment batch size after each step.","**Prune → distil → quantize, with evals at every stage.** Keep the dense parent as the distillation teacher, recover the pruned model, and quantize last, because errors compound. Log task, safety and general-benchmark scores at each stage so you know where quality went.","**Calibrate on production prompts.** Importance scores are activation-dependent, so sample a few hundred real requests (code, chat, languages in proportion) for Wanda, SparseGPT or block-influence scoring. Watch per-domain eval deltas, not just aggregate perplexity.","**Verify 2:4 end to end in the engine.** In PyTorch, `torch.sparse.to_sparse_semi_structured` converts a 2:4-masked weight for sparse kernels; in a serving engine, confirm the sparse kernel path is actually selected. Benchmark real tokens per second against the dense model before claiming a win.","**Safety suite as a release gate.** Re-run refusal and safety evals on every pruned candidate, because safety behaviour can be disproportionately fragile under pruning."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P[(\"Dense parent model\")] --> Q[\"Try quantization first: does it hit the target?\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Ship quantized model after evals\"]\n    Q -->|\"no: depth or parameter count is the limit\"| C[\"Score importance on production prompts\"]\n    C --> U[\"Unstructured: best accuracy, no GPU speedup\"]\n    C --> T[\"2:4: up to 2x matmul on Ampere and later\"]\n    C --> R[\"Structured: heads, neurons, layers, smaller dense model\"]\n    T --> D[\"Recover: distil from the dense parent\"]\n    R --> D\n    D --> QZ[\"Quantize last\"]\n    QZ --> E[\"Task, safety and tokens per second evals\"]\n    P -.->|\"teacher\"| D","caption":"The production pipeline: quantization is tried first, the granularity choice decides whether hardware\ncan cash in the cut, and recovery plus evals sit between every stage."},"interview":[{"q":"What does 2:4 semi-structured sparsity buy you, concretely?","a":"- **The pattern:** two of every four consecutive weights are zero, which sparse tensor cores on NVIDIA\n  GPUs from Ampere onward run at up to twice the dense matmul rate.\n- **Storage:** per four 16-bit weights, 2 × 16 + 2 × 2 = 36 bits instead of 64, about 56% of dense.\n- **The catch:** sparsity is fixed at 50%, quality drops more than free unstructured pruning, recovery is\n  usually needed, and decode only benefits if the kernel reads the compressed form."},{"q":"One-shot magnitude pruning at 50% wrecked your LLM. Why, and what would you use instead?","a":"- **Outlier features:** a small weight on an input channel that always carries huge activations matters a\n  lot, and |w| alone cannot see it, so magnitude deletes exactly those weights.\n- **Wanda** fixes the blind spot by scoring |W_ij| · ‖X_j‖₂ per output row, with no gradients and seconds\n  per layer.\n- **SparseGPT** is more accurate still, using the inverse Hessian to compensate the remaining weights, at\n  the cost of the Hessian work."},{"q":"Width pruning or depth pruning for a latency-bound service? What do you give up?","a":"- **Depth pruning** drops whole layers: fewer sequential layers and kernel launches per token, so the\n  bigger latency win and the simplest implementation.\n- **Width pruning** removes heads, MLP neurons or embedding channels: better quality at equal parameter\n  count, smaller latency win.\n- **Either way** recovery by distillation from the parent follows, and for depth pruning you measure block\n  influence and never cut the first or last few layers."},{"q":"A pruned 8B model passes perplexity checks but now answers requests its parent refused. What went wrong?","a":"- **Perplexity and behaviour diverge after pruning:** next-token loss can stay intact while instruction\n  following, reasoning or refusals collapse.\n- **Safety behaviour is fragile:** it can be disproportionately damaged by pruning, so a capability-only\n  eval misses it.\n- **Fix:** add the refusal and safety suite to the release gate for every pruned candidate, and recover\n  with distillation from the parent before re-checking."},{"q":"When would you not prune at all?","a":"- **When bytes are the constraint:** quantization is cheaper, needs no training and usually reaches the\n  memory target alone; INT4 weights are about 26% of bf16.\n- **When the GPU lacks the pattern:** 2:4 on hardware without sparse tensor cores is pure quality loss,\n  and unstructured sparsity gives no speedup in a dense engine.\n- **When you have no recovery budget or strong parent:** a different, smaller model family may be the\n  cheaper route."}],"tradeoffs":["**Unstructured accuracy vs no GPU speedup.** Best accuracy per removed weight, but it only pays on sparse-aware CPU runtimes or at extreme sparsity; for GPU serving it is a reporting exercise, not a cost saving.","**2:4 speed vs rigidity.** Matmuls can run at up to twice the dense rate on supporting GPUs (realised gains are smaller and kernel-dependent) and weight bytes drop to about 56%. But it is a fixed 50%, usually needs recovery, and helps decode only if the kernel reads the compressed form. On hardware without sparse tensor cores it is pure quality loss.","**Structured speed vs quality.** Faster everywhere, but more quality lost per parameter and the most recovery compute. Depth pruning is cheapest to implement and best for latency; width pruning preserves quality better at the same size.","**Prune-then-distil efficiency vs inheritance.** A small fraction of a fresh model's tokens, but the result inherits the parent's architecture, tokenizer and biases, and its ceiling is the parent.","**Stacking with quantization.** Savings multiply but errors compound, so prune and recover first, quantize last, and evaluate at every stage."],"pitfalls":["**Reporting sparsity as speedup.** A 50% unstructured-sparse model runs at identical tokens per second in a dense engine. Symptom: a proud \"50% smaller\" slide and an unchanged GPU bill.","**Calibrating on data unlike deployment traffic.** Scores are activation-dependent. Symptom: fine perplexity on web text, broken behaviour on code or chat.","**Removing layers by position rules of thumb.** Without measuring block influence, and especially cutting first or last layers. Symptom: degenerate, repetitive output even at a small depth cut.","**Skipping recovery because perplexity looked fine.** Perplexity and downstream ability diverge after pruning. Symptom: intact next-token loss, collapsed instruction following or reasoning on your evals.","**Width-pruning one side of a paired matrix.** Dropping MLP up-projection columns without the matching down-projection rows, or query heads without accounting for grouped KV heads. Symptom: a shape error if lucky, silent garbage if not.","**Assuming pruning is behaviour-neutral.** Safety and refusal behaviour can be disproportionately fragile. Symptom: a smaller model that complies with requests its parent refused. Re-run the safety suite, not just capability evals."],"when":"- **Use when:** you need a size that does not exist in your standard family, you have a strong parent and\n  recovery budget, or the hardware has 2:4 support and the workload is matmul-heavy.\n- **Prefer quantization first:** it is cheaper and usually reaches the memory target alone. Prune only\n  when latency or parameter count, not bytes, is the constraint.\n- **Measure:** tokens per second and task evals at deployment batch size, never sparsity percentages.\n  Keep the KV cache in view: pruning query heads does not shrink it, pruning KV heads or layers does.\n- **Day to day:** a model-shrinking project with a distillation phase attached.\n- **Interviews:** explain why unstructured sparsity does not speed up a GPU, how Wanda differs from\n  magnitude pruning, and what 2:4 actually buys.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs one-shot pruning with calibration data from production prompts, wires the recovery distillation run, and benchmarks real tokens per second against the dense parent before claiming a win.","architect":"Decides whether the size gap is better closed by pruning a known-good parent or by adopting a different model, weighing recovery compute against the operational cost of a new family.","fde":"Rarely prunes in the field, but must recognise a vendor's \"50% sparse\" claim for what it is and ask which hardware and kernel path turns that into latency the customer will feel."},"check":{"q":"A product needs decode latency about 30% lower than its 8B model delivers on A100s, with no budget\nfor a different GPU. Which pruning options are realistic, and in what order would you try them?","a":"Decode is bandwidth-bound, so the target is fewer bytes read per token, and the first option is not\npruning at all: INT4 or INT8 weights cut bytes more than 30% with no training, so quantize first and\nmeasure. If latency is still short, depth pruning is the direct lever, since dropping a quarter of the\nlayers removes a quarter of the sequential work and the bytes; measure block influence by input/output\ncosine similarity, remove the least influential middle layers, and distil from the unpruned 8B to recover.\n2:4 sparsity is tempting on A100s, but its decode benefit depends on the engine reading the compressed\nformat and it needs recovery training too, so I would try it only after depth pruning. Throughout, I would\ntrack task evals and safety evals, not perplexity, and verify the KV cache budget is unchanged."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does 50% unstructured sparsity usually not make a GPU model faster?","options":["Zero weights still consume registers during attention","Dense kernels multiply the zeros anyway, and sparse formats need index overhead and specialised kernels that only pay off at much higher sparsity","The KV cache grows to compensate for the missing weights","Sparse models cannot be quantized"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPUs execute dense matmuls regardless of zero values. Cashing in sparsity needs either hardware-known patterns such as 2:4 or structural removal that yields a smaller dense matrix."},{"q":"What does Wanda's importance score multiply together?","options":["Weight magnitude and the weight's gradient","Weight magnitude and the inverse Hessian diagonal","Weight magnitude and the L2 norm of the input activation feature it multiplies","Output activation variance and layer depth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wanda scores |W_ij| · ‖X_j‖₂ so that small weights on outlier input channels are protected, which is the case where pure magnitude pruning does the most damage in large language models."},{"q":"After structurally pruning a large model, what is the most token-efficient way to recover quality?","options":["Retrain the pruned architecture from random initialisation on the original corpus","Distil from the unpruned parent, using the pruned weights as the initialisation","Quantize it to 4-bit so the remaining weights are used more efficiently","Increase the learning rate and run one more epoch of SFT"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pruned model already sits near a good solution, and token-level distillation gives a dense signal, so recovery reportedly needs a small fraction of the tokens a fresh small model would require."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/model-merging","sources":["Wortsman et al., 2022 — Model soups: averaging weights of multiple fine-tuned models improves accuracy without increasing inference time","Ilharco et al., 2023 — Editing Models with Task Arithmetic","Yadav et al., 2023 — TIES-Merging: Resolving Interference When Merging Models","Yu et al., 2023 — Language Models are Super Mario: Absorbing Abilities from Homologous Models as a Free Lunch"],"why":"- **Serving several fine-tunes is costly.** Three fine-tunes of one 8B base (support domain, tool-call\n  formats, a community instruct tune with better conversation) mean three copies of the weights, a router,\n  and no way for one answer to use two skills at once.\n- **Multi-task training is often impossible.** You need all three datasets (the community one is not yours\n  to have), must rebuild the mix whenever one changes, and pay a training run per iteration. Ensembling\n  costs three forward passes per token.\n- **Merging is a fourth option.** Every model is `base + small delta`, so you add the deltas and get one\n  model in minutes on a CPU.\n- **Why it is not absurd.** Fine-tunes from the same pretrained weights stay in the same loss basin, so the\n  straight line between them has no loss barrier (linear mode connectivity). Averaging two good solutions\n  tends to land on a third, often better than either.\n- **The evidence.** Model soups: averaging fine-tunes with different hyperparameters improved accuracy and\n  out-of-distribution robustness at zero inference cost. Task arithmetic: deltas behave like vectors you\n  can add, scale and even negate to remove a behaviour.\n- **Plain averaging breaks on disagreement.** One tune pushes a weight up, another down, and the average\n  cancels both. Later methods all answer that interference problem.","how":"### Task vectors\n- **Definition:** τ_i = θ_i − θ_base, computed tensor by tensor for base θ_base and fine-tune θ_i.\n- **Uses:**\n  - multi-task merging: θ = θ_base + λ Σ τ_i;\n  - forgetting: θ_base − λ τ (negating a toxicity fine-tune reduces toxicity);\n  - even analogies.\n- **λ** is one scalar (or one per task) tuned on a validation set. Deltas are additive approximations, so\n  λ around 0.3–1.0 in total usually works; larger sums push activations out of the range the base was\n  trained in.\n\n### Linear averaging and soups\n- **Formula:** θ = Σ w_i θ_i with Σ w_i = 1.\n- **Uniform soups** average everything; **greedy soups** add a model only if the validation metric improves.\n- **Right tool when** models learned the *same* thing differently (same data, different seeds or learning\n  rates). Checkpoint averaging at the end of a long run is the same idea along one trajectory.\n- **Wrong tool when** the models learned different things.\n\n### SLERP\nSpherical linear interpolation treats each tensor (or the whole flattened model) as a vector and moves\nalong the arc between two, with cos Ω = a·b / (‖a‖‖b‖) and\n\n```\nslerp(a, b, t) = sin((1 − t)Ω) / sin Ω · a  +  sin(tΩ) / sin Ω · b\n```\n\n- **Why: norm preservation.** A straight-line midpoint between two vectors at an angle is *shorter* than\n  either, shrinking activation scales layer by layer; SLERP keeps the magnitude.\n- **Limit:** two models at a time. The usual choice for blending two closely related instruct tunes.\n\n### TIES-Merging: trim, elect sign, disjoint merge\nTIES names two sources of interference:\n- **Noise:** most entries of a task vector are tiny but still perturb other tasks when summed.\n- **Sign conflicts:** when τ_A and τ_B disagree on direction, averaging cancels useful movement.\n\nThree steps, in order, each removing one failure mode:\n1. **Trim.** Keep only the top *k*% of each τ_i by magnitude (20% is common); zero the rest. Fine-tuning\n   changes concentrate in a few parameters, so this removes noise without losing much.\n2. **Elect.** Per parameter, pick the sign carrying more total magnitude: sign(Σ_i τ_i) after trimming.\n   This decides which way the merged model moves.\n3. **Disjoint merge.** Average only task vectors agreeing with the elected sign there, ignoring dissenters\n   instead of letting them cancel the majority. Then θ = θ_base + λ τ_merged.\n\nFour-parameter walkthrough (trim to top 50%, two entries each):\n\n| | p1 | p2 | p3 | p4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| τ_A | +0.30 | −0.02 | +0.10 | −0.40 |\n| τ_B | −0.25 | +0.01 | +0.12 | −0.05 |\n| τ_C | +0.05 | 0.00 | −0.08 | −0.30 |\n| Plain mean | +0.03 | 0.00 | +0.05 | −0.25 |\n| Trimmed A / B / C | +0.30 / −0.25 / 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 / +0.12 / −0.08 | −0.40 / 0 / −0.30 |\n| Elected sign | + | 0 | + | − |\n| τ_merged | +0.30 | 0 | +0.12 | −0.35 |\n\n- **Agreement survives at full strength.** It is not divided by the number of tasks, which is why λ for\n  TIES sits near 1 rather than 1/N.\n- **Conflicts are decided, not resolved.** On p1, B loses outright because A's magnitude was larger, so\n  B's skill pays wherever it depended on that weight.\n\n### DARE: drop and rescale\n- **Observation:** SFT deltas are extremely redundant. Zero a random 90% (or more, on large models) of a\n  task vector's entries and multiply survivors by 1/(1 − p): behaviour barely changes.\n- **Why:** the rescaling keeps the expected delta identical, and the redundancy covers dropped entries.\n- **Use:** sparsified deltas from different tasks collide far less, so DARE is preprocessing before\n  summing or before TIES (\"dare_ties\").\n- **Limit:** works when deltas are small relative to the base; fails on continued-pretraining deltas,\n  which are large and not redundant.\n\n### Passthrough and frankenmerges\n- Stacking layers from different models, or repeating a block to make a deeper model (depth up-scaling),\n  is merging without arithmetic.\n- The result is broken until healed with continued training, so this is model building, not a free\n  combination step.\n\n### Constraints that follow from the mechanism\n- **Same architecture, shapes and lineage.** Everything is elementwise arithmetic, so it needs identical\n  tensor shapes (hence the same tokenizer and vocabulary size) and the same base.\n- **Different base checkpoints:** deltas measured from different origins.\n- **Different random initialisations:** related by hidden permutations of neurons; they average into\n  noise unless aligned first.\n- **LoRA adapters are task vectors** in a low-rank subspace, so adapter merging (a weighted sum of B_iA_i\n  products, with the same TIES/DARE options) has the same interference problems.\n- **Nothing is trained, so nothing is verified.** Every merge needs the full eval suite, including chat\n  template, end-of-turn behaviour and safety checks, before anyone calls it a model.","example":"### TIES merge over Hugging Face state dicts\nPyTorch. All checkpoints must share the base model and tensor shapes. It works tensor by tensor, so peak\nmemory is one tensor times the number of models, and copies non-float buffers unchanged.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef ties_merge(base, finetunes, k=0.2, lam=1.0):\n    \"\"\"base, finetunes: state dicts (name -> tensor) of the same architecture and base lineage.\n    Returns base + lam * TIES-merged task vector.\"\"\"\n    merged = {}\n    for name, w0 in base.items():\n        if not w0.is_floating_point():                       # token ids, buffers: pass through\n            merged[name] = w0.clone(); continue\n        taus = torch.stack([ft[name].float() - w0.float() for ft in finetunes])   # [n_tasks, ...]\n        # 1. Trim: keep the top-k fraction of each task vector by magnitude\n        flat = taus.abs().flatten(1)\n        n_keep = max(1, int(k * flat.shape[1]))\n        thresh = flat.kthvalue(flat.shape[1] - n_keep + 1, dim=1).values  # per-task cutoff\n        taus = taus * (taus.abs() >= thresh.view(-1, *([1] * (taus.dim() - 1))))\n        # 2. Elect: the sign with more total magnitude at each parameter wins\n        sign = torch.sign(taus.sum(0))\n        # 3. Disjoint merge: average only the entries that agree with the elected sign\n        agree = (torch.sign(taus) == sign) & (taus != 0)\n        tau = (taus * agree).sum(0) / agree.sum(0).clamp(min=1)\n        merged[name] = (w0.float() + lam * tau).to(w0.dtype)\n    return merged\n\n# usage sketch (every path must be a fine-tune of exactly the same base revision):\n# from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\n# base = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"org/base-8b\").state_dict()\n# fts = [AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(p).state_dict() for p in (\"org/support-sft\", \"org/tools-sft\")]\n# model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"org/base-8b\"); model.load_state_dict(ties_merge(base, fts))\n# Then: run the per-task evals, a general benchmark, the chat-template smoke test and the safety suite.\n# mergekit expresses the same recipe as a YAML config (merge_method: ties, density: 0.2, weight: ...).\n```\n\n### Reproduce the four-parameter walkthrough\nPure Python; runs anywhere and prints the table's last row.\n\n```python\ntau = {\"A\": [+0.30, -0.02, +0.10, -0.40],\n       \"B\": [-0.25, +0.01, +0.12, -0.05],\n       \"C\": [+0.05, 0.00, -0.08, -0.30]}\n\ndef trim(v, keep=2):                       # keep the top-`keep` entries by magnitude\n    top = sorted(range(len(v)), key=lambda i: abs(v[i]), reverse=True)[:keep]\n    return [x if i in top else 0.0 for i, x in enumerate(v)]\n\nsign = lambda x: (x > 0) - (x < 0)\nmean = [round(sum(c) / 3, 2) for c in zip(*tau.values())]\ntrimmed = [trim(v) for v in tau.values()]\nmerged = []\nfor col in zip(*trimmed):\n    s = sign(sum(col))                                   # elect\n    agree = [x for x in col if x != 0 and sign(x) == s]  # disjoint merge\n    merged.append(round(sum(agree) / len(agree), 2) if agree else 0.0)\nprint(\"plain mean:\", mean)      # [0.03, -0.0, 0.05, -0.25]\nprint(\"TIES      :\", merged)    # [0.3, 0.0, 0.12, -0.35]\n```\n\n### The same merge as a mergekit config\nThe declarative form teams usually commit to version control; run it with `mergekit-yaml config.yml\n./merged`.\n\n```yaml\nmerge_method: ties\nbase_model: org/base-8b\nmodels:\n  - model: org/support-sft\n    parameters:\n      density: 0.2      # TIES trim: keep the top 20% of each task vector\n      weight: 0.5\n  - model: org/instruct-tune\n    parameters:\n      density: 0.2\n      weight: 0.5\nparameters:\n  normalize: true\ndtype: bfloat16\n```","patterns":["**Merge recipes as code.** Keep each merge as a versioned config (method, parent revisions, density, weights, λ) next to its eval report, so any registered model can be rebuilt exactly. Monitor that parent revisions in the config match the registry.","**Lineage check before merging.** Verify every parent was fine-tuned from the same base revision and shares tokenizer and vocabulary size; refuse the merge on any shape mismatch instead of padding tensors.","**Small hyperparameter grid on a mixed validation set.** Sweep density (for example 0.2 to 0.5) and weights or λ, plus a SLERP candidate for two-model merges, scoring every parent's task. Pick the point where no parent's metric drops beyond tolerance.","**Full release gate, like a trained model.** Per-task evals against each parent, a general benchmark, a chat-template smoke test (end-of-turn, role tags, system prompt) and the safety suite. Track refusal rate on a fixed red-team set across every merge.","**Routing fallback.** If the merge cannot hold every parent's quality, keep the specialists behind a router and treat the merge as an experiment. Compare cost per request and quality across both paths."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[(\"Shared base weights\")] --> T[\"Task vectors: tau_i = fine-tune_i minus base\"]\n    F[(\"Fine-tunes of the same base revision\")] --> T\n    T --> D[\"Optional DARE: drop random p, rescale by 1 / (1 - p)\"]\n    D --> TR[\"Trim: keep top k% of each tau by magnitude\"]\n    TR --> EL[\"Elect: per-parameter sign with more total magnitude\"]\n    EL --> DM[\"Disjoint merge: average only agreeing entries\"]\n    DM --> M[\"theta = base + lambda times tau_merged\"]\n    B --> M\n    M --> G[\"Per-task evals, template smoke test, safety suite\"]\n    G -->|\"fails\"| R[\"Fall back to routed specialists\"]","caption":"Follow one tensor through a TIES or dare_ties merge: deltas are taken against the shared base, sparsified,\nsign-voted, averaged among agreeing tasks, then added back and gated by evals."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a task vector, and why can you add fine-tunes together at all?","a":"- **Definition:** τ_i = θ_i − θ_base, computed tensor by tensor; merging is θ_base + λ Σ τ_i, and\n  θ_base − λ τ removes a behaviour.\n- **Why it works:** fine-tunes from the same pretrained weights stay in one loss basin, so the line\n  between them has no loss barrier (linear mode connectivity) and the deltas behave like additive vectors.\n- **Tuning:** λ around 0.3–1.0 in total usually works; bigger sums push activations out of range."},{"q":"Your merged model produces repetitive, degenerate text. Walk through the debugging.","a":"- **Check the weights first:** merge weights summing well above one, or λ too high, push activations out\n  of the range the base expects.\n- **Check lineage:** parents fine-tuned from different base revisions, or deltas taken against the wrong\n  origin, give incoherent output.\n- **Check the template:** a mixture of parents' chat templates shows up as leaked role tags and never\n  emitting end-of-turn."},{"q":"SLERP or TIES: when would you pick each?","a":"- **SLERP** for exactly two closely related tunes: it moves along the arc between them, preserving norms\n  that a straight-line midpoint would shrink.\n- **TIES** for three or more parents or conflicting deltas: trimming removes noise and sign election stops\n  disagreeing updates from cancelling.\n- **Plain averaging or soups** when the models learned the same thing differently, such as different seeds\n  or learning rates on the same data."},{"q":"When would you not merge, and what would you do instead?","a":"- **When you own the data and compute:** multi-task SFT is more reliable, because merging has no\n  training signal to fix interference.\n- **When there are many tasks:** past a handful, per-task quality usually slips below the specialists, so\n  keep them behind a router.\n- **When parents differ in lineage or vocabulary:** different initialisations average into noise unless\n  aligned, and shape mismatches should block the merge rather than be padded."},{"q":"A merge tops a public leaderboard. Why might you still refuse to ship it?","a":"- **Contamination accumulates:** each parent may have seen benchmark data, so the merge can top a board\n  and still underperform its parents on your tasks.\n- **Safety can silently erode:** merging can undo refusal training, so run the red-team set and track\n  refusal rate across merges.\n- **Ship on your own gate:** per-task evals against each parent, a general benchmark and a template smoke\n  test, with any parent's drop counted as the merge's cost."}],"tradeoffs":["**No training compute vs no training signal.** No datasets or GPUs needed, but nothing guarantees the merge works, so evaluation effort replaces training effort. When you own the data and compute, multi-task SFT is more reliable.","**One model vs interference.** One model for several skills saves GPUs and routing, but interference grows with task count and delta size; past a handful of tasks, per-task quality usually slips below the specialists.","**Averaging vs TIES/DARE.** Plain averaging improves robustness for near-duplicates but cancels conflicting updates; TIES and DARE fix that at the cost of hyperparameters (k, p, λ) tuned on a validation set.","**SLERP vs N-way methods.** SLERP preserves norms and often beats linear interpolation for two models, but does not extend to many; linear and TIES scale to N at the cost of norm drift.","**Negation power vs governance.** Task-vector negation removes unwanted behaviour cheaply, but the same mechanism can strip safety tuning, deliberately or by accident, making merges a governance problem too."],"pitfalls":["**Wrong origin for deltas.** Merging fine-tunes of different base revisions, or treating an instruct model as the base for deltas computed against the pretrained one. Symptom: incoherent or garbage output.","**Vocabulary mismatch from added special tokens.** Embedding and output-head shapes differ. Symptom: a shape error, or, if someone pads the tensors, wrong tokens at generation time.","**Weights summing well above one, or λ too high.** Activations leave the range the base expects. Symptom: repetitive, degenerate text and logits that blow up.","**Mixed chat templates.** Each parent learned its own, and the merge gets a mixture. Symptom: leaked role tags, never emitting end-of-turn, or ignoring the system prompt.","**Trusting leaderboard scores for merges.** Benchmark contamination accumulates across parents. Symptom: tops a public board, underperforms its parents on your tasks.","**Skipping the safety regression.** Merging can quietly undo refusal training. Symptom: complies with requests every parent refused, discovered by a user rather than a test."],"when":"- **Use when:** you have several fine-tunes of the same base and need one artifact; you want a community\n  tune's conversational quality for your domain model without its data; you are averaging checkpoints or\n  seeds from your own run; or you want a fast experiment before a multi-task training job.\n- **Avoid when:** you own the data, so training is the better substitute.\n- **Never ship** a merge without per-task evals, a general benchmark, a template smoke test and the\n  safety suite.\n- **Measure:** each parent's task metric before and after; treat any drop as the merge's cost, not noise.\n- **Interviews:** explain task vectors, why TIES elects a sign, why DARE's rescaling preserves behaviour,\n  and why two models from different initialisations cannot simply be averaged.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes task vectors, tunes density and λ on a validation set, and runs the full eval and safety suite on the merged checkpoint before registering it like any trained model.","architect":"Decides whether a merged generalist or routed specialists better fit the serving budget and risk posture, and sets provenance rules for which parent checkpoints may be merged into production.","fde":"Combines a customer's domain fine-tune with a stronger instruct tune when retraining is not an option on site, and proves the result against the customer's own regression suite."},"check":{"q":"A team has a support-domain SFT of an 8B base and wants the conversational quality of a newer\ncommunity instruct tune built on the same base. What are their options, and how would you merge safely?","a":"Options are routing between the two models, retraining on the instruct data (unavailable), or\nmerging. For merging, first confirm both checkpoints derive from the exact same base revision and share\nthe tokenizer and vocabulary size. Compute both task vectors against the base, then try TIES with density\naround 0.2 and λ near 1, and SLERP between the two as a second candidate. Tune on a validation set holding\nsupport tickets and general chat prompts. Evaluate per-task metrics against each parent, a general\nbenchmark for collateral damage, a chat-template smoke test for end-of-turn behaviour, and the safety\nsuite, because merging can erode refusals. Ship only if support quality holds within tolerance and the\nconversational gain is measurable; otherwise fall back to routing."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does TIES-Merging elect a sign per parameter before averaging?","options":["To make the merged weights sparse enough for fast kernels","Because task vectors that disagree in direction cancel under plain averaging, so only the majority direction is kept","Because negative deltas are usually noise","To normalise each task vector to unit length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sign conflicts are a main source of interference: averaging +0.3 and −0.3 yields zero and loses both tasks' progress. Electing the dominant sign and averaging only agreeing entries preserves movement."},{"q":"DARE drops 90% of a task vector's entries and rescales the rest by 10×. Why does the model still work?","options":["Because 90% of fine-tuned weights are exactly zero already","Because the base model ignores small deltas","Because SFT deltas are highly redundant and rescaling keeps the expected delta unchanged","Because dropped entries are recovered during the first inference pass"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Random dropping with 1/(1 − p) rescaling is unbiased, and small fine-tuning deltas carry redundant information, so the behaviour survives; the method fails on large, non-redundant continued-pretraining deltas."},{"q":"Which pair can be safely merged by weight averaging?","options":["Two 7B models of the same architecture trained from different random initialisations","Two fine-tunes that started from the identical pretrained checkpoint","A 7B and a 13B model from the same family","Any two models that share a tokenizer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Linear mode connectivity holds for models sharing an initialisation. Independently trained models have permuted hidden units and different basins, and different sizes do not even have matching tensor shapes."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/data-curation","sources":["Lee et al., 2022 — Deduplicating Training Data Makes Language Models Better","Zhou et al., 2023 — LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment","Rae et al., 2021 — Scaling Language Models: Methods, Analysis & Insights from Training Gopher","Penedo et al., 2024 — The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at Scale"],"why":"- **A model is a compressed copy of its training distribution.** Gradient descent learns boilerplate,\n  duplicates, leaked test sets and labelling noise with equal diligence, and no architecture or\n  hyperparameter choice recovers what bad data teaches.\n- **Raw data is full of junk.** A crawl or log export holds navigation menus repeated across ten thousand\n  pages, the same licence header in every file, cookie banners, SEO spam, half-translated listings,\n  benchmark test questions, customer emails with phone numbers, and transcripts where the agent's\n  \"resolution\" was wrong.\n- **Duplicates are the quietest problem.** A passage repeated a thousand times is a thousand gradient steps\n  toward memorising it, not \"more evidence\". Deduplicating a web corpus gave better perplexity and an\n  order-of-magnitude drop in verbatim regurgitation.\n- **\"Get more data\" and \"the model will figure it out\" both fail.** More data means more of the same\n  distribution, more compute on repeats, and no fix for contamination. Every published ablation shows the\n  same architecture on a filtered subset beating the whole corpus on knowledge and reasoning, by margins\n  that would otherwise cost a much larger model or far more tokens.\n- **For fine-tuning it is sharper still.** LIMA fine-tuned a 65B model on about a thousand curated\n  prompt–response pairs and was competitive with models trained on orders of magnitude more. Alignment\n  mostly teaches format and style; a small diverse, uniformly excellent set teaches it cleanly, a huge noisy\n  set teaches the average of its noise. Curation decides what the model becomes; architecture decides how\n  well it can become it.","how":"A pipeline is a sequence of filters, each cheap enough to run on everything surviving the previous one,\nwith expensive judgement calls last. Every stage also loses good data, so measure drop rates per stage and\nsample the rejects by hand.\n\n| # | Stage | Main tool | Why here |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Provenance and permission | metadata, content hash, PII scrub | needed for deletion and audit |\n| 2 | Extraction and normalisation | HTML extraction, language ID, one template | everything later assumes clean text |\n| 3 | Heuristic filters | Gopher/C4 rules | cheap, high precision |\n| 4 | Deduplication | hash, MinHash + LSH, suffix arrays | stops memorisation |\n| 5 | Decontamination | n-gram and embedding overlap | keeps evals valid |\n| 6 | Model-based scoring | quality classifier | judges content, costly |\n| 7 | Mixing and curriculum | per-source weights | sets what the model sees most |\n| 8 | Fine-tuning specifics | verification, coverage sampling, reading | quality of each example |\n\n### 1. Provenance and permission\n- **Record first:** source, licence, collection date and a content hash for every document, because you\n  will need to delete by source (a takedown, a customer leaving), reweight by source, and explain where a\n  behaviour came from.\n- **Scrub PII here:** pattern rules plus a named-entity model. A model trained on real emails will emit\n  real emails.\n\n### 2. Extraction and format normalisation\n- Pull main text out of HTML, fix encodings, and identify language with a fast classifier, keeping the\n  ones you intend to serve.\n- **Fine-tuning data:** parse every example into one fixed structure (system, user, assistant turns) and\n  one template, and drop anything that does not parse, because the model learns the template as rigidly as\n  the content.\n\n### 3. Heuristic quality filters\nCheap, high-precision rules that remove boilerplate (the Gopher and C4 style rules):\n- document length bounds;\n- mean word length in a sane range;\n- a cap on the symbol-to-word ratio;\n- a cap on the fraction of lines that are bullets or end in an ellipsis;\n- common function words must appear;\n- lines must end in terminal punctuation.\n\nThey work because machine-generated junk and navigation chrome fail them while prose passes. **Caveat:**\nthey are deliberately blunt; some rules (bad-word lists, punctuation requirements) over-remove dialects and\ninformal text, a bias you must know you are introducing.\n\n### 4. Deduplication\n1. **Exact duplicates:** a hash of normalised text.\n2. **Near-duplicates with MinHash:** represent each document as its set of word 5-gram shingles, apply *k*\n   hash functions (128 is typical), and keep each function's minimum. Each signature position matches with\n   probability equal to the Jaccard similarity of the shingle sets.\n3. **LSH banding:** all-pairs comparison is O(n²), impossible at billions of documents. Split the signature\n   into *b* bands of *r* rows and compare only documents colliding in at least one band.\n4. **Within-document repeats** (boilerplate pasted into otherwise unique pages) need a suffix-array pass.\n5. **Fine-tuning sets:** deduplicate *prompts*, not just whole examples, and add an embedding pass to\n   catch paraphrases.\n\nThe candidate probability for similarity *J* is an S-curve:\n\n```\nP(candidate) = 1 − (1 − Jʳ)ᵇ        threshold ≈ (1/b)^(1/r)\n```\n\nWith *r* = 8, *b* = 16: about 0.95 at *J* = 0.8 and 0.06 at *J* = 0.5, threshold near\n(1/*b*)^(1/*r*) ≈ 0.71.\n\n### 5. Decontamination\n- **Index every eval set you will ever report** and remove training documents sharing long n-grams with\n  any of them. 13-gram overlap was the GPT-3 rule; 8–13 remains common.\n- **Include internal golden sets**, not just public benchmarks: the goal is a valid measurement, not a\n  clean public record.\n- **Add an embedding pass.** N-gram overlap misses paraphrases and translations, so a similarity search\n  against eval embeddings is a worthwhile second pass.\n\n### 6. Model-based quality scoring\n- **Why:** heuristics cannot judge content.\n- **How:** train a small classifier on \"good\" versus \"random\" (early LLM corpora used reference text as the\n  positive class), or have a strong LLM score a sample for educational value and distil that into a fast\n  classifier run over everything, as FineWeb-Edu did. Keeping only high scores gave large gains on\n  knowledge benchmarks.\n- **Cost:** the labeller's taste is imported at scale. A classifier tuned for textbook prose quietly\n  down-weights code, forums and dialects unless you score those domains separately.\n\n### 7. Mixing and curriculum\n- **Weights per source** (web, code, books, maths, your domain). Up-sample small high-quality sources, but\n  repeating data beyond a few epochs returns little.\n- **Tuning:** learned weighting with a proxy model exists, but most teams tune mixes empirically on a small\n  run.\n- **Annealing:** pretraining runs commonly anneal onto the highest-quality slice at the end.\n- **Fine-tuning:** balance task types and response lengths to match production, because the model learns\n  the length distribution as surely as the content.\n\n### 8. Fine-tuning specifics\n- **Verify** responses where you can (execute code, check answers).\n- **Remove** hedging or refusals you do not want; **enforce** one style.\n- **Check** inter-annotator agreement on a sample.\n- **Select for coverage as well as quality:** embed prompts, cluster them, and sample across clusters,\n  because a quality-only filter collapses onto the most common, easiest request types and leaves the long\n  tail untaught.\n- **Look at the data:** a few hundred examples read by a human expose systematic problems no metric\n  reports.\n- **Version** the dataset, pin it to the model it produced, and feed eval error analysis into the next\n  version.","example":"### Compact curation pass\nGopher-style heuristics, exact deduplication, MinHash/LSH near-deduplication via `datasketch`, and\n13-gram decontamination against eval sets. Each stage returns early so you can count what it dropped.\n\n```python\nimport re, hashlib\nfrom datasketch import MinHash, MinHashLSH\n\ndef shingles(text, n=5):\n    toks = re.findall(r\"\\w+\", text.lower())\n    return {\" \".join(toks[i:i + n]) for i in range(max(1, len(toks) - n + 1))}\n\ndef passes_heuristics(text):\n    w = re.findall(r\"\\w+\", text)\n    if not 50 <= len(w) <= 100_000: return False                   # length bounds\n    if not 3 <= sum(map(len, w)) / len(w) <= 10: return False      # mean word length\n    lines = [l for l in text.splitlines() if l.strip()]\n    if sum(l.lstrip().startswith((\"-\", \"*\", \"•\")) for l in lines) > 0.9 * len(lines): return False\n    if sum(l.rstrip().endswith(\"...\") for l in lines) > 0.3 * len(lines): return False\n    return True\n\ndef curate(docs, eval_texts, num_perm=128, threshold=0.7):\n    eval_grams = set().union(*(shingles(t, 13) for t in eval_texts))   # decontamination index\n    seen, lsh, kept, dropped = set(), MinHashLSH(threshold=threshold, num_perm=num_perm), [], {}\n    for i, text in enumerate(docs):\n        if not passes_heuristics(text): dropped[\"heuristic\"] = dropped.get(\"heuristic\", 0) + 1; continue\n        h = hashlib.sha1(\" \".join(text.lower().split()).encode()).hexdigest()\n        if h in seen: dropped[\"exact\"] = dropped.get(\"exact\", 0) + 1; continue\n        seen.add(h)\n        if shingles(text, 13) & eval_grams: dropped[\"contaminated\"] = dropped.get(\"contaminated\", 0) + 1; continue\n        m = MinHash(num_perm=num_perm)\n        for g in shingles(text): m.update(g.encode())\n        if lsh.query(m): dropped[\"near_dup\"] = dropped.get(\"near_dup\", 0) + 1; continue\n        lsh.insert(str(i), m)\n        kept.append(text)\n    return kept, dropped\n\n# kept, dropped = curate(raw_docs, eval_texts); print(len(kept), dropped)\n# Then: read 100 random rejects per stage and 100 random keeps, run a quality classifier on the keeps,\n# and only afterwards split train/validation, so near-duplicates cannot straddle the split.\n```\n\n### Choose LSH bands for your duplicate threshold\nPure Python. Same 128 hash functions, three band layouts: the S-curve's threshold moves with *r* and *b*.\n\n```python\ndef p_candidate(J, r, b):\n    \"\"\"Probability two docs with Jaccard J share at least one LSH band of r rows (b bands).\"\"\"\n    return 1 - (1 - J ** r) ** b\n\nfor r, b in [(8, 16), (16, 8), (4, 32)]:\n    thresh = (1 / b) ** (1 / r)\n    row = \"  \".join(f\"J={J:.1f}:{p_candidate(J, r, b):.2f}\" for J in (0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9))\n    print(f\"r={r:2d} b={b:2d} (k={r * b}) threshold~{thresh:.2f}  {row}\")\n# r= 8 b=16 (k=128) threshold~0.71  J=0.5:0.06  J=0.6:0.24  J=0.7:0.61  J=0.8:0.95  J=0.9:1.00\n# r=16 b= 8 (k=128) threshold~0.88  J=0.5:0.00  J=0.6:0.00  J=0.7:0.03  J=0.8:0.20  J=0.9:0.81\n# r= 4 b=32 (k=128) threshold~0.42  J=0.5:0.87  J=0.6:0.99  J=0.7:1.00  J=0.8:1.00  J=0.9:1.00\n```\n\n- **r = 16, b = 8** keeps templated contracts and forms that differ in a few fields.\n- **r = 4, b = 32** catches loose paraphrases but floods you with candidates to verify.\n\n### Worked scenario: a support-log funnel\nAn illustrative stage report for 200,000 support conversations (the numbers show the shape to expect, not\na benchmark):\n\n| Stage | Dropped | Remaining | What a reject sample showed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Parse into one template | 6,000 | 194,000 | truncated exports, mixed formats |\n| Heuristics (empty, one-line, auto-replies) | 38,000 | 156,000 | macros and \"ticket closed\" bots |\n| Exact dedup | 21,000 | 135,000 | copy-pasted macro replies |\n| MinHash near-dedup | 47,000 | 88,000 | the same macro with a name swapped |\n| Decontamination vs eval set | 400 | 87,600 | eval tickets pulled from the same logs |\n| Outcome filter (rated or resolved) | 52,000 | 35,600 | unresolved or low-rated threads |\n\nThe lesson: macros dominate support logs, so near-dedup and outcome filtering remove far more than\nexpected, and the eval set leaked because it was sampled from the same export.","patterns":["**Per-stage drop dashboard plus reject sampling.** Emit counts per stage and source on every run and alert when a stage's drop rate shifts sharply between versions. Store each reject with its reason so thresholds can be tuned and audited.","**Provenance manifest with deletion by source.** Keep a table of document hash, source, licence and collection date, so a takedown or a departing customer becomes a filter and a rebuild, not a forensic search. Monitor how many rows lack provenance.","**Dedup before split, then split by group.** Deduplicate first, then split by conversation, customer or near-duplicate cluster (for example scikit-learn's `GroupShuffleSplit`) so near-copies cannot straddle train and validation.","**Protected eval registry.** Every eval set, public or internal, is registered once and every training build is checked against it with n-gram and embedding overlap; the build fails on hits above a threshold. Track hits per eval set over time.","**Datasets versioned like code.** Record a content hash of the final JSONL, the pipeline commit and the stage counts in the model's registry entry, so every model points to the exact data that made it.","**Scale with existing tooling.** For web-scale corpora use a pipeline library built for it, such as Hugging Face's `datatrove`, which was used for FineWeb, instead of hand-rolled loops."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[(\"Raw crawl, logs or exports\")] --> P[\"1. Provenance, licence, hash, PII scrub\"]\n    P --> X[\"2. Extract, language ID, one template\"]\n    X --> H[\"3. Heuristic filters\"]\n    H --> D[\"4. Dedup: exact hash, then MinHash and LSH\"]\n    D --> C[\"5. Decontaminate vs every eval set\"]\n    C --> M[\"6. Model-based quality scoring\"]\n    M --> MX[\"7. Mix and balance per source\"]\n    MX --> V[(\"8. Versioned dataset pinned to its model\")]\n    H -.->|\"rejects with reason\"| J[(\"Reject store, sampled by hand\")]\n    D -.->|\"rejects with reason\"| J\n    C -.->|\"rejects with reason\"| J\n    V -->|\"eval error analysis\"| P","caption":"Cheap, high-precision filters run first and costly judgement last; every stage logs its rejects with a\nreason, and eval error analysis feeds the next dataset version."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does deduplication make a model better, not just a dataset smaller?","a":"- **Repeats are not evidence:** a passage seen a thousand times is a thousand gradient steps toward\n  memorising it, and compute spent on repeats is compute not spent on new content.\n- **Measured effect:** deduplicating a web corpus gave better perplexity and an order-of-magnitude drop\n  in verbatim regurgitation.\n- **It also protects measurement:** near-duplicates straddling train and validation inflate validation\n  scores."},{"q":"Validation loss looks excellent but production quality is poor. Where do you look in the data?","a":"- **Split order:** if you split before deduplicating, near-copies sit on both sides; deduplicate first,\n  then split by conversation, customer or cluster.\n- **Contamination:** check internal golden sets as well as public benchmarks, with n-gram and embedding\n  overlap, because evals sampled from the same export leak.\n- **Distribution mismatch:** compare template, task-type and response-length mix with production, since\n  the model learns all three."},{"q":"How aggressively should you filter with a model-based quality classifier? When would you hold back?","a":"- **The gain:** it judges content, which heuristics cannot; keeping only high educational-value scores\n  gave FineWeb-Edu large knowledge-benchmark gains.\n- **The cost:** it imports the labeller's taste at scale, quietly down-weighting code, forums and dialects\n  and narrowing diversity.\n- **Hold back** when those domains matter to your product: score them separately or set per-source\n  thresholds, and compare downstream evals across filter levels."},{"q":"You run MinHash with 128 permutations. How do you set bands and rows for a 0.7 similarity cut?","a":"- **The S-curve:** P(candidate) = 1 − (1 − Jʳ)ᵇ, with threshold ≈ (1/b)^(1/r).\n- **r = 8, b = 16** puts the threshold near 0.71: about 0.95 at J = 0.8 and 0.06 at J = 0.5.\n- **Tune per source:** r = 16, b = 8 (threshold about 0.88) keeps templated contracts and forms, while\n  r = 4, b = 32 (about 0.42) catches loose paraphrases but floods you with candidates to verify."},{"q":"A customer leaves and asks that their data be removed from future models. What does your pipeline need?","a":"- **Provenance from stage one:** source, licence, collection date and content hash per document, so the\n  request becomes a filter and a rebuild, not a forensic search.\n- **Versioned datasets pinned to models:** you can say which models contained the data and rebuild the\n  dataset without it.\n- **Monitor coverage:** track rows lacking provenance, because those are the ones you cannot delete."}],"tradeoffs":["**Quality vs volume and diversity.** Aggressive filtering raises average quality but shrinks volume and narrows diversity (dialects, code, niche domains). When data-constrained, a cleaner smaller set repeated a few times beats a larger noisy one; beyond a few repeats, fresh data wins again.","**Dedup threshold: templates vs memorisation.** A low threshold removes legitimate templated text such as contracts and forms you may want learned; a high one keeps near-copies that drive memorisation. Tune per source.","**Model scoring vs heuristics.** Far better at judging content, but it imports the labeller's preferences at scale, costs a classifier pass over everything, and can over-select benchmark-like prose.","**Decontamination vs useful data.** It protects eval validity but deletes useful documents quoted in benchmarks, and cannot catch paraphrased leaks without an embedding pass.","**Small curated SFT sets vs knowledge.** Cheap and effective for format, tone and task structure, but they cannot inject much new knowledge; for that, volume and pretraining-style data come back into play."],"pitfalls":["**Deduplicating only exact matches.** Near-duplicate boilerplate survives. Symptom: the model regurgitates licence headers, cookie notices and canned openings, and validation scores are inflated.","**Splitting train/validation before deduplicating.** Near-duplicates straddle the split. Symptom: excellent validation loss that production performance does not match.","**Selecting by length or surface polish.** The model learns to be long and ornate. Symptom: responses grew after fine-tuning with no gain on task metrics.","**Mixing templates.** Some examples with system prompts, some without, or two turn formats. Symptom: instruction adherence varies with the prompt's shape.","**Decontaminating against public benchmarks only.** The internal golden set leaks in, so your own numbers are the ones you cannot trust.","**Training on logs without PII scrubbing.** Symptom: the model emits real names, emails or account numbers, found by a customer or an auditor.","**Discarding rejects without the reason.** You cannot audit a missing behaviour, tune a threshold, or explain to a reviewer what was excluded."],"when":"Curation is not optional; what varies is the effort.\n- **One-off fine-tune on a few thousand examples:** a human read of all of them, a dedup pass and template\n  enforcement.\n- **Production pipeline regenerating sets from logs:** the full staged pipeline with per-stage drop\n  metrics, contamination checks against every eval set, PII scrubbing and dataset versioning.\n- **Measure:** drop rate per stage, duplicate rate, contamination hits, inter-annotator agreement, and the\n  downstream eval delta between dataset versions, the only number that matters in the end.\n- **Day to day:** scripts over JSONL, a dashboard of stage counts and a habit of reading samples.\n- **Interviews:** explain MinHash and LSH, why deduplication reduces memorisation, what LIMA showed, and\n  how you would decontaminate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the filter, dedup and decontamination code, versions each dataset with its stage counts, and reads samples of what every stage rejected before trusting the pipeline.","architect":"Sets the data policy: licensing and PII rules, which eval sets are protected by decontamination, retention and deletion paths, and the versioning contract between datasets and models.","fde":"Turns a customer's messy export (tickets, documents, logs) into a clean, deduplicated, de-identified training set inside their environment and documents exactly what was removed and why."},"check":{"q":"You inherit 200,000 customer support conversations to fine-tune a drafting model. What do you do\nbefore you train, and what do you measure to know the data is ready?","a":"First provenance and PII: confirm the data may be used, then scrub names, emails, phone and account\nnumbers. Parse every conversation into one turn template and drop the unparseable. Filter by heuristics\n(empty or one-line replies, macros, auto-responses) and by outcome where available, keeping conversations\nwith good customer ratings or verified resolutions. Deduplicate exactly, then near-deduplicate with\nMinHash on both whole conversations and customer prompts, since macros produce thousands of near-copies.\nDecontaminate against the evaluation set built with the support leads. Read a few hundred examples with\nan SME and label a sample for quality to check agreement. Balance ticket categories and response lengths\nto the production distribution, then version the set. I would measure per-stage drop counts, duplicate\nrate, contamination hits, agreement on the quality sample, and finally the eval delta versus the base\nmodel, which decides whether the data is ready."},"quiz":[{"q":"What problem do MinHash and locality-sensitive hashing solve together?","options":["Removing PII from documents at scale","Finding near-duplicate documents without comparing every pair","Scoring documents for educational quality","Measuring n-gram overlap with evaluation sets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MinHash signatures estimate Jaccard similarity, and LSH banding only compares documents that collide in a band, turning an O(n²) pairwise search into a near-linear candidate-generation step."},{"q":"Why can 13-gram overlap decontamination still miss contamination?","options":["Because 13 is too short to match any real sentence","Because paraphrased or translated copies of a test item share no long exact n-gram with it","Because evaluation sets are never public","Because n-gram checks only run on the validation split"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Exact n-gram matching catches verbatim leaks. A benchmark question rewritten in different words, or translated, carries the same answer with no shared 13-gram, so an embedding-similarity pass is needed."},{"q":"What did LIMA demonstrate about fine-tuning data?","options":["That more instruction data always improves alignment","That synthetic data outperforms human-written data","That around a thousand diverse, uniformly high-quality examples can teach format and style competitively","That pretraining data should be deduplicated before fine-tuning"],"answer":2,"explanation":"LIMA's claim is that alignment mostly teaches format and style, so a small set with diverse prompts and excellent responses suffices; large noisy sets teach the average of their noise."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","sources":["Wang et al., 2022 — Self-Instruct: Aligning Language Models with Self-Generated Instructions","Zelikman et al., 2022 — STaR: Bootstrapping Reasoning With Reasoning","Xu et al., 2023 — WizardLM: Empowering Large Language Models to Follow Complex Instructions","Shumailov et al., 2024 — AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data"],"why":"- **Real data is scarce where you need it most.** The long tail of odd inputs that break a product\n  appears once a month in logs and never in a scraped corpus.\n- **Real data is private.** A customer's internal forms, ticket formats and tool outputs exist nowhere\n  public, and often cannot leave their environment.\n- **Real data lacks the behaviours you want.** Humans do not write out step-by-step reasoning, preference\n  comparisons between two drafts, or tool-call sequences, so modern post-training has no natural corpus.\n- **Naive generation fails three ways.** Generation is cheap (a hundred thousand examples overnight), but\n  \"prompt the big model to write a dataset\":\n  - collapses onto the generator's favourite phrasings, names and structures, so the model learns a\n    narrow template and fails on real inputs;\n  - turns the generator's mistakes into the student's ground truth;\n  - can contain your evaluation questions, making scores meaningless.\n- **The scarce resource is verification, not generation.** Self-Instruct bootstrapped from 175\n  human-written seed tasks and filtered for novelty; STaR kept only traces reaching the correct answer;\n  code pipelines keep programs that pass tests; reasoning distillates keep traces a checker accepts. With\n  strong verification a model can exceed its own data; without it, you get a slow copy of the generator\n  with the tails cut off.","how":"### The loop\n```\nseed → specify → generate → verify → deduplicate and balance → train\n     → evaluate on REAL held-out data → inspect failures → adjust the specification\n```\nThe last three steps are the ones teams skip, and they are why the loop converges rather than drifts.\n\n### Sources of diversity\nMode collapse is the default behaviour of a sampled generator, so diversity must be engineered in:\n- **Seeds and in-context examples.** Self-Instruct sampled a handful of tasks from a seed pool (human\n  written at first, then including accepted generations) per prompt, and rejected candidates whose ROUGE-L\n  overlap with existing tasks was too high, so each round pushed outward.\n- **Evolution.** Evol-Instruct rewrites instructions along explicit axes (add a constraint, deepen,\n  concretise, require more reasoning, or mutate to a new topic), so difficulty grows instead of regressing\n  to easy prompts.\n- **Personas.** Conditioning on who is asking (a billion-persona pool was built for exactly this) spreads\n  vocabulary, intent and style far more than temperature does.\n- **Grounding in real artefacts.** Generate questions *from* real documents, records or code. Inputs then\n  follow the real distribution, and often the label comes for free.\n- **Sampling.** Several samples per prompt at moderate temperature, then dedup by n-gram overlap and\n  embedding similarity, because duplicates are far denser in synthetic sets than in scraped ones.\n\n### Verification, strongest to weakest\n| # | Verifier | How | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Execution | code vs unit tests, SQL vs a database, maths vs a known answer or symbolic solver | exact, cheap | only some domains |\n| 2 | Construction | decide the label first, generate the input | correct by construction | needs a structured label |\n| 3 | Consistency | majority vote (self-consistency) or two generators agreeing | filters one-off errors | shared errors survive |\n| 4 | LLM-as-judge | rubric-driven judge | scales to anything | length, position, self-preference bias |\n| 5 | Humans | domain experts on a stratified sample | gold standard | the bottleneck |\n\n- **Execution** is why code and maths are where synthetic data has done best.\n- **Construction:** sample a structured record and have the model write the email containing it; sample\n  a tool-call sequence and generate the request it answers. Add a round-trip check (an independent pass\n  must recover the label) to catch generations that quietly dropped a fact.\n- **Consistency:** STaR's \"rationalisation\" regenerates a rationale with the answer as a hint, recovering\n  training signal from failures.\n- **LLM judges** must be calibrated against human labels before you trust their filter rate.\n- **Humans:** spend them on calibrating the other verifiers, not on everything.\n\n### Where it is used\n- **Instruction tuning:** prompt–response pairs.\n- **Preference data for DPO and RLHF:** AI feedback over pairs of candidates, with a judge or a rubric.\n- **Pretraining-scale corpora:** synthetic textbooks and rephrased web text proved unusually\n  token-efficient for small models.\n- **Evaluation and red-team sets:** with the caveat that a model must not be judged on data its own\n  generator produced.\n- **Privacy-preserving stand-ins:** a programmatic generator with synthetic identities lets work proceed\n  outside a restricted environment.\n- **Distillation:** the same machinery with a specific aim; see the knowledge distillation lesson.\n\n### Model collapse and how to avoid it\n**Mechanism:** training on its own (or a predecessor's) unfiltered output, generation after generation,\nshrinks the tails first, then the whole distribution. Rare phrasings vanish, variance falls, errors\ncompound, and each generation is a blurrier copy. The defences follow from the mechanism:\n- **Keep real data in the mix**, accumulating rather than replacing it.\n- **Verify**, so errors do not compound.\n- **Track diversity directly** across dataset versions: distinct n-gram ratios, embedding dispersion,\n  length and topic distributions.\n- **Prefer a different or stronger generator** to the model being trained.\n- **Decontaminate against every eval**, because a generator that has seen the benchmark will reproduce it.\n\n### Provenance and permission\n- **Per example, record** the generator model and version, prompt template, seed, every verifier's\n  verdict and the sampling settings, because you will need to trace a bad behaviour to the batch that\n  taught it.\n- **Check the generator's terms.** Some API providers prohibit using outputs to train competing models,\n  and some open-weight licences attach conditions to derived models.","example":"### Construction-first generation with a round-trip check\nAn extraction task. A program samples the record, so the label is correct before any model is involved;\nthe model only writes the realistic email. `llm` is a stub for whichever provider you use.\n\n```python\nimport json, random\n\ndef llm(prompt: str, temperature: float = 0.8) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Call your model provider and return text. Use temperature ~0.8 to write, 0 to check.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\nVENDORS = [\"Acme Tooling\", \"Northwind Traders\", \"Blue Fern Studio\", \"Kappa Logistics\"]\nPERSONAS = [\"terse accounts-payable clerk\", \"chatty small-business owner\", \"automated billing system\"]\n\ndef sample_record(rng):                          # the LABEL comes first, so it is correct by construction\n    return {\"vendor\": rng.choice(VENDORS), \"invoice_no\": f\"INV-{rng.randint(1000, 99999)}\",\n            \"total\": round(rng.uniform(20, 5000), 2), \"currency\": rng.choice([\"USD\", \"EUR\"]),\n            \"due_date\": f\"2026-{rng.randint(1, 12):02d}-{rng.randint(1, 28):02d}\"}\n\ndef make_example(rng):\n    rec = sample_record(rng)\n    email = llm(f\"You are a {rng.choice(PERSONAS)}. Write a realistic email of 60-150 words that mentions \"\n                f\"exactly these facts in your own words and order, plus one unrelated detail: \"\n                f\"{json.dumps(rec)}. Do not output JSON.\")\n    # round-trip verification: an independent extraction pass must recover the record exactly\n    back = llm(\"Extract vendor, invoice_no, total (number), currency and due_date (YYYY-MM-DD) as JSON \"\n               f\"from this email:\\n{email}\", temperature=0)\n    try:\n        ok = json.loads(back) == rec\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        ok = False\n    return {\"input\": email, \"output\": rec, \"verified\": ok,\n            \"provenance\": {\"generator\": \"<model id>\", \"prompt\": \"invoice-v3\", \"persona_pool\": \"v1\"}}\n\nrng = random.Random(7)\nexamples = [make_example(rng) for _ in range(1000)]\nkept = [e for e in examples if e[\"verified\"]]\nprint(f\"kept {len(kept)}/{len(examples)}\")\n# Next: near-deduplicate the emails, balance vendors and currencies, hold out a slice for evaluation,\n# and have a human read 50 kept and 50 rejected examples before trusting the pass rate.\n```\n\n### Execution verifier for generated code\nPure Python, runnable. Keeps only candidates whose unit tests pass in a separate process with a timeout.\n`subprocess` isolation is not a security sandbox; run untrusted generations in a locked-down container.\n\n```python\nimport subprocess, sys, tempfile, os\n\ndef passes_tests(candidate_code: str, tests: str, timeout_s: float = 5.0) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Run generated code plus its unit tests in a separate process; keep only clean exits.\"\"\"\n    with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(\"w\", suffix=\".py\", delete=False) as f:\n        f.write(candidate_code + \"\\n\\n\" + tests)\n        path = f.name\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-I\", path], capture_output=True, timeout=timeout_s)\n        return r.returncode == 0\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        return False                                   # infinite loops count as failures\n    finally:\n        os.unlink(path)\n\ntests = \"assert slugify('Hello World') == 'hello-world'\\nassert slugify('  A  b ') == 'a-b'\"\ngood = \"def slugify(s):\\n    return '-'.join(s.lower().split())\"\nbad = \"def slugify(s):\\n    return s.lower().replace(' ', '-')\"\nprint(passes_tests(good, tests), passes_tests(bad, tests))   # True False\n```\n\n### A diversity gate: distinct-n across a batch\nPure Python. A batch that collapsed onto one template scores far lower than a varied one; track this\nnumber per batch and per dataset version.\n\n```python\nimport re\n\ndef distinct_n(texts, n=2):\n    \"\"\"Unique n-grams / total n-grams across a batch: falls as a generator collapses onto templates.\"\"\"\n    grams = [tuple(t[i:i + n]) for t in (re.findall(r\"\\w+\", x.lower()) for x in texts)\n             for i in range(len(t) - n + 1)]\n    return len(set(grams)) / max(1, len(grams))\n\ncollapsed = [\"Dear team, I hope this email finds you well. Invoice INV-1001 is due soon.\"] * 3 + \\\n            [\"Dear team, I hope this email finds you well. Invoice INV-2002 is due soon.\"]\nvaried = [\"hey - acme here, 1001 still unpaid?? due friday\",\n          \"Per our records, invoice INV-2002 (EUR 310.00) remains outstanding.\",\n          \"Reminder: Kappa Logistics bill #3003 falls due on 2026-04-12.\",\n          \"Hi Sam, quick one: can you confirm you got invoice 4004 for the shoot?\"]\nprint(f\"collapsed distinct-2 = {distinct_n(collapsed):.2f}\")   # 0.29\nprint(f\"varied    distinct-2 = {distinct_n(varied):.2f}\")      # 1.00\n```","patterns":["**Construction-first for structured tasks.** For extraction, classification and tool calls, sample the label programmatically and generate only the surface text, with a round-trip extraction at temperature 0. Monitor round-trip pass rate per type, and read rejects to catch a checker that discards hard-but-valid examples.","**Per-row provenance in the dataset schema.** Every row carries generator id and version, prompt template version, seed, sampling settings and verifier verdicts, so a bad behaviour traces to one batch and you regenerate that batch, not everything.","**Batch gates on verification and diversity.** Reject a batch whose pass rate or distinct-n falls outside the range of previous accepted batches; also track length and topic distributions per version.","**Judge calibration before scale.** Before an LLM judge filters a full run, score a few hundred human-labelled items and measure agreement; recalibrate whenever the judge model or rubric changes.","**Real data as the anchor, walled-off evals.** Accumulate real curated data across versions and add synthetic data only for gaps found by error analysis. Keep evaluation sets from any generator prompt, and decontaminate synthetic sets against them."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Seeds, personas, real artefacts\"] --> SP[\"Specify: task, schema, diversity axes\"]\n    SP --> G[\"Generate several samples per prompt\"]\n    G --> V[\"Verify: execution, construction, consistency, judge, humans\"]\n    V -->|\"fails\"| RJ[(\"Rejects, read by hand\")]\n    V -->|\"passes\"| DD[\"Deduplicate and balance, check distinct-n\"]\n    DD --> MX[\"Mix with accumulated real data\"]\n    MX --> T[\"Train\"]\n    T --> E[\"Evaluate on REAL held-out data\"]\n    E --> F[\"Inspect failures\"]\n    F -->|\"adjust the specification\"| SP","caption":"The generate-verify loop: verification decides what survives, real held-out data decides whether it\nworked, and failures flow back into the specification rather than into more generation."},"interview":[{"q":"Temperature alone does not give a diverse synthetic set. What do you do instead?","a":"- **Seeds with a novelty filter:** Self-Instruct sampled tasks from a seed pool and rejected candidates\n  whose ROUGE-L overlap with existing tasks was too high, so each round pushed outward.\n- **Evolution and personas:** Evol-Instruct rewrites prompts along explicit axes (add a constraint,\n  deepen, mutate topic); conditioning on who is asking spreads vocabulary, intent and style.\n- **Ground in real artefacts:** generating questions from real documents, records or code keeps inputs on\n  the real distribution, then dedup by n-gram and embedding similarity."},{"q":"Your model scores very well on a synthetic eval but disappoints in production. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check eval provenance:** if the eval came from the same generator and prompt as training, the score\n  measures template-matching; rebuild it from real held-out data.\n- **Check diversity:** a low distinct-n ratio or repeated names and openings means the model learned a\n  narrow template that real inputs do not follow.\n- **Check style and verifier drift:** inherited generator style, or a filter that kept only easy cases,\n  show up as fine accuracy on easy inputs and misses on the hardest real ones."},{"q":"Can a student trained on synthetic data beat its generator? When would you not use synthetic data?","a":"- **Only with outside information:** test results or ground truth from a verifier let self-improvement\n  loops exceed the generator; without one, expect a slightly worse copy.\n- **Avoid leaning on it** when real curated data is abundant: use it to anchor the set and generate only\n  for gaps found by error analysis.\n- **Also avoid** when the generator's terms or licence forbid training on its outputs."},{"q":"You want an LLM judge to filter 500,000 generated examples. How do you decide whether to trust it?","a":"- **Calibrate first:** score a few hundred human-labelled items and measure agreement before the judge\n  filters the full run.\n- **Know its biases:** length, position and self-preference bias can skew what survives, so inspect\n  rejects as well as keeps.\n- **Recalibrate on change:** whenever the judge model or rubric changes, rerun the agreement check, and\n  spend human effort calibrating verifiers rather than labelling everything."},{"q":"Design the verifier for a synthetic code-generation dataset. What do you monitor?","a":"- **Execution:** run each candidate with its unit tests in a separate process with a timeout, counting\n  infinite loops as failures, and keep only clean exits.\n- **Isolation:** `subprocess` is not a security sandbox, so run untrusted generations in a locked-down\n  container.\n- **Monitor:** pass rate and distinct-n per batch against previous accepted batches, plus per-row\n  provenance so a bad batch can be regenerated alone."}],"tradeoffs":["**Coverage vs drift.** Cheap, fast, controllable coverage of rare cases, against a distribution that drifts from real usage. Once real traffic exists, curated real data should anchor the set and synthetic data fill gaps, not the reverse.","**Bounded by the generator.** The student is bounded by the generator on the same task, unless verification adds outside information (test results, ground truth); then self-improvement loops become possible. Without such a verifier, expect a slightly worse copy.","**Verifier choice.** Executable verifiers are exact and cheap but exist only for some domains; LLM judges cover everything with known biases; humans are accurate and slow. Calibrate the fast verifiers against a human-labelled sample.","**Breadth vs realism.** Personas and evolution widen coverage but produce some unrealistic examples, so you trade a realism check for the breadth.","**Privacy vs realism.** Synthetic stand-ins unblock work on private data, but a generator fine-tuned on that data can leak it; a purely programmatic generator is safe and less realistic."],"pitfalls":["**No verification step.** The student learns the generator's errors as truth. Symptom: confidently wrong in exactly the generator's places.","**Letting a model-based verifier define \"correct\" without auditing rejects.** A round-trip or judge filter keeps what the checker already handles, discarding hard-but-valid examples and drifting toward easy cases. Symptom: high pass rate, little gain on the hardest real inputs. Read the rejects and rescue valid ones with a stronger checker or a human.","**Low diversity.** The same four names, opening sentence and structure. Symptom: a low distinct-n-gram ratio and a model that handles the template but fails real inputs.","**Evaluating on data from the same generator and prompt as training.** Symptom: an excellent eval score and a production rollout that does not match.","**Replacing real data across versions.** Symptom: vocabulary and variance shrink version to version, which is collapse in progress.","**Inheriting the generator's style.** Overly polite, markdown-heavy, verbose. Symptom: the customer says outputs do not sound like their house style despite fine accuracy.","**No per-example provenance.** Nobody can say which batch, prompt or generator version taught a bad behaviour, so the fix becomes \"regenerate everything\".","**Ignoring terms of service or licence until launch review.** Symptom: a legal block on a model that already works."],"when":"- **Use when:** real labelled data is scarce, private or missing rare cases; you need behaviours nobody\n  writes down (reasoning traces, preference pairs, tool-call sequences); you are bootstrapping before\n  traffic; and above all when you have a verifier that tells good examples from bad.\n- **Lean on real curated data when:** it is abundant; use synthetic data to fill gaps found by error\n  analysis.\n- **Measure:** verification pass rate per batch, diversity across versions, the eval delta on *real*\n  held-out data, and agreement between fast verifiers and a human-labelled sample.\n- **Day to day:** a generation pipeline with provenance on every row.\n- **Interviews:** how you would prevent collapse, and why you would generate the label before the input.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the generate–verify–deduplicate pipeline with per-row provenance, calibrates the judge against human labels, and gates every batch on verification pass rate and diversity metrics.","architect":"Decides which generators are permitted (licence and terms), how synthetic and real data are mixed and versioned, and which evaluation sets are walled off from any generator.","fde":"Bootstraps a customer's model from thirty labelled examples by construction-first generation over their real document types, with SMEs reviewing samples and verifying the round-trip check."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a model that extracts fields from forty internal form types, but can supply only\nthirty labelled examples. How would you build a training set, and how would you know it is trustworthy?","a":"I would not ask the model to invent forms. I would take the forty real templates and the field\nschema, write a program that samples valid records for each type, and use a model to render each record\ninto realistic filled forms in several personas and styles, varying layout noise. The label is the sampled\nrecord, so it is correct by construction; a separate extraction pass must recover it exactly, and only\nround-trip successes survive. I would near-deduplicate, balance across form types, and keep the thirty\nhuman-labelled examples strictly as evaluation, never training or few-shot prompts. Thirty examples\ncannot cover forty types, though, so I would ask the SMEs to label a handful more for each uncovered type,\nor report those types as unmeasured rather than averaging them away. Trust comes from three measurements: the\nround-trip pass rate per form type, SME review of a stratified sample of kept and rejected items, and the\neval score on the thirty real examples, with any gap between synthetic and real scores treated as the\npipeline's error bar, not noise."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most determines the quality of a synthetic training set?","options":["The size of the generator model","The sampling temperature","The strength of the verification step that decides which generated examples survive","The number of examples generated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Generation is cheap and errors are the norm; a verifier that catches wrong or duplicated examples is what separates a dataset that improves a model from one that teaches the generator's mistakes."},{"q":"Why generate the label first and the input second in a construction-first pipeline?","options":["Because inputs are longer and more expensive to generate","Because the label is then correct by construction, and a round-trip check can verify the input faithfully contains it","Because models cannot produce structured output reliably","Because it avoids needing any deduplication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sampling the structured answer programmatically removes the generator from the label entirely; the model only writes the surface text, and an independent extraction pass confirms nothing was dropped or changed."},{"q":"Which practice best protects against model collapse across successive training rounds?","options":["Raising the sampling temperature each round","Using a larger generator each round","Keeping real data in the mix, verifying outputs, and tracking diversity across dataset versions","Training for fewer epochs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Collapse comes from recursively copying a narrowing distribution with compounding errors. Anchoring on real data, filtering errors and measuring diversity attack each part of that mechanism directly."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/data-labeling","sources":["Cohen, 1960 — A Coefficient of Agreement for Nominal Scales","Landis & Koch, 1977 — The Measurement of Observer Agreement for Categorical Data","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback"],"why":"- **Everything downstream needs targets.** Supervised fine-tuning, reward models, and the small\n  classifiers that route requests or enforce guardrails all learn to reproduce the mapping in the labeled\n  set, noise included. The model does not learn \"the task\".\n- **Noise has a direction.** If 15% of labels are wrong in a consistent direction, the model learns that\n  direction. If they are wrong at random, achievable accuracy drops and more data is needed to reach any\n  given level.\n- **Small sets do not average errors away.** Pretraining averages any single document away across\n  billions of tokens. A fine-tuning set of 2,000 examples has no such averaging, so one annotator's habit\n  of filing every ambiguous ticket under \"billing\" becomes model behaviour.\n- **A one-line instruction to whoever is free fails three ways.** Real tasks are underspecified (\"is\n  this answer helpful?\" means different things to different people), so labels encode the annotator. One\n  labeler per item cannot measure noise, so an 80% validation plateau is unattributable. And rare,\n  borderline items are exactly where untrained labelers disagree most.\n- **Labeling as a discipline fixes this.** The target becomes defined (guidelines with worked edge\n  cases), measurable (agreement on overlapping items) and improvable (adjudicated disagreements feed\n  back into the guidelines).","how":"### What a label is in LLM work\n| Label type | What it is | Cost and use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Categorical** | intent, topic, toxicity, routing class | cheapest per item; feeds classifiers and guardrails |\n| **Span or structured** | entities, citations, JSON fields | needs a schema and tooling that enforces it |\n| **Demonstrations** | a complete target response, written or edited by an expert | SFT data; most expensive, because the annotator produces rather than judges |\n| **Preferences** | a prompt plus two candidates: which is better, optionally why | feeds reward models, RLHF and DPO |\n| **Rubric scores** | 1–5 on named criteria | convenient; hardest to keep consistent unless every level has anchor examples |\n\n- **Why preferences dominate RLHF data.** Comparing is easier and more consistent than writing, and a\n  comparison stays informative even when both candidates are flawed. That is why InstructGPT-style\n  pipelines collect far more comparisons than demonstrations.\n\n### The loop\n1. **Define the task on paper.** Label schema, decision rules, and 20–30 worked examples that include\n   the boundary cases and an explicit \"if unsure, do X\" rule. Ambiguity left here gets resolved\n   differently by every annotator later.\n2. **Pilot.** 100–200 items, each labeled independently by two or three people.\n3. **Measure agreement.** Use chance-corrected agreement (formula and bands below), bootstrap it, and\n   read the confusion matrix.\n4. **Adjudicate and rewrite.** Every disagreement is a guideline gap or an annotator error. Fix the\n   guideline, re-brief, re-pilot until agreement is acceptable.\n5. **Produce with QA.** Single-label most items, but keep 5–10% overlap to track agreement, seed gold\n   items with known answers to catch drift and inattention, and route anything flagged to adjudication.\n6. **Version and freeze.** Store guideline version, annotator id and timestamp on every record. A\n   guideline change mid-project silently splits the dataset into two tasks.\n\n### Measuring agreement\nFor two raters on categorical labels use Cohen's kappa:\n\n```\nκ = (p_o − p_e) / (1 − p_e)\np_o = observed fraction of items the raters agree on\np_e = agreement expected by chance from each rater's label frequencies\n```\n\n- **Why correct for chance.** Two raters who both say \"not spam\" 95% of the time agree about 90% of the\n  time by luck alone.\n- **More raters or other scales.** Fleiss' kappa extends this to many raters; Krippendorff's alpha also\n  handles missing labels and ordinal scales.\n- **Landis–Koch bands (the common reading):**\n\n| κ | Reading |\n|---|---|\n| below 0 | poor |\n| 0–0.20 | slight |\n| 0.21–0.40 | fair |\n| 0.41–0.60 | moderate |\n| 0.61–0.80 | substantial |\n| above 0.80 | almost perfect |\n\n- **The bands are a convention, not a test.** Set the acceptable bar by the stakes of the task.\n- **Pilot kappa is noisy.** Kappa from a 150-item pilot is itself noisy; bootstrap the items for an\n  interval before declaring victory.\n- **One number hides where disagreement lives.** Also read the rater-by-rater confusion matrix to see\n  which pairs of classes the guideline fails to separate.\n\n### Who labels, and how much a model should help\n- **Domain experts** are the only option for specialised demonstrations.\n- **Crowd or vendor workforces** scale categorical tasks once guidelines are solid.\n- **Model-assisted labeling** (an LLM proposes a label or drafts a response, a human verifies) cuts cost\n  sharply but introduces anchoring. People accept plausible suggestions, so confident model errors pass\n  through, and a model later trained on that data is partly trained on its own output.\n- **Keep overlap and gold subsets blind** to the suggestion, so you can measure the acceptance bias.\n- **Active learning** chooses which items to label next: those where the current model is uncertain or\n  annotators disagreed, because labels on items the model already handles add little.\n\n### Finding label errors after the fact\nAgreement measures noise on the overlap; it does not tell you *which* single-labeled items are wrong.\n1. **Train with k-fold cross-validation** so every item gets a prediction from a model that never saw it.\n2. **Rank items** where the out-of-fold model confidently disagrees with the given label. Confident\n   learning formalises this by estimating, per class, the threshold above which a disagreement is likely\n   a label error.\n3. **Review the top of the list by hand** rather than auto-relabeling, because the model's confident\n   disagreements also include genuinely hard cases it gets wrong.\n4. **Treat an LLM labeler as one more rater.** Compute its agreement with adjudicated human labels on the\n   pilot, and only then decide where to trust it.\n\n### Why agreement rather than accuracy\n- **There is no external truth.** The labels are the truth you are constructing.\n- **Agreement is the observable proxy** for whether the task is defined well enough to be learnable.\n- **It sets a ceiling.** A model cannot be meaningfully evaluated above the level at which humans agree\n  with each other.","example":"### Pilot round: kappa, adjudication and SFT records\nTwo annotators label support tickets; kappa is computed by hand, disagreements are listed for\nadjudication, and the adjudicated result is written as SFT records with provenance. On this data it\nprints kappa ≈ 0.48, \"moderate\" on the Landis–Koch scale, which means the guidelines are not finished.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport json\n\npilot = [  # (item_id, text, label_from_annotator_a, label_from_annotator_b)\n    (\"t1\", \"Card charged twice this month\", \"billing\", \"billing\"),\n    (\"t2\", \"App crashes when I open invoices\", \"bug\", \"billing\"),\n    (\"t3\", \"How do I export my data?\", \"howto\", \"howto\"),\n    (\"t4\", \"Refund still not received after 10 days\", \"billing\", \"billing\"),\n    (\"t5\", \"Login page blank on Safari\", \"bug\", \"bug\"),\n    (\"t6\", \"Can I pay yearly instead of monthly?\", \"billing\", \"howto\"),\n]\n\ndef cohen_kappa(a, b):\n    n = len(a)\n    p_o = sum(x == y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / n\n    ca, cb = Counter(a), Counter(b)\n    p_e = sum((ca[k] / n) * (cb[k] / n) for k in set(ca) | set(cb))\n    return (p_o - p_e) / (1 - p_e)\n\na = [r[2] for r in pilot]; b = [r[3] for r in pilot]\nprint(f\"kappa = {cohen_kappa(a, b):.2f}\")          # 0.48 on this pilot\nfor item_id, text, la, lb in pilot:\n    if la != lb:\n        print(f\"ADJUDICATE {item_id}: {text!r}  A={la}  B={lb}\")\n\n# After adjudication the agreed label is the target. Keep provenance on every record.\ngold = {\"t2\": \"bug\", \"t6\": \"billing\"}\nwith open(\"tickets_sft.jsonl\", \"w\") as f:\n    for item_id, text, la, lb in pilot:\n        label = gold.get(item_id, la)\n        record = {\n            \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Classify this support ticket: {text}\"},\n                         {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": label}],\n            \"meta\": {\"guideline\": \"v3\", \"annotators\": [\"a\", \"b\"], \"adjudicated\": item_id in gold},\n        }\n        f.write(json.dumps(record) + \"\\n\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: 96% agreement, kappa 0.31\nTwo raters label 1,000 tickets for a rare \"fraud\" class; each marks 30 items as fraud, but they agree on\nonly 10 of them.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Both \"not fraud\" / both \"fraud\" | 950 / 10 |\n| Disagreements | 40 (20 each way) |\n| Raw agreement p_o | 960 / 1,000 = 0.96 |\n| Chance agreement p_e | 0.03 × 0.03 + 0.97 × 0.97 = 0.9418 |\n| κ | (0.96 − 0.9418) / (1 − 0.9418) ≈ 0.31, \"fair\" |\n\n- **Reading it.** Every disagreement sits on the rare class. The guideline does not define \"fraud\"\n  well enough; the workforce is not careless.\n- **Fix.** Add worked fraud and not-fraud boundary cases to the guideline, then re-pilot with the rare\n  class oversampled.\n\n### Ranking likely label errors with out-of-fold predictions\nA sketch with scikit-learn: every item is scored by a model that never trained on it, then the items\nwhose given label gets the lowest predicted probability go to a human review queue.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_predict\n\n# X: features (e.g. text embeddings), y: annotator labels encoded as integers 0..K-1\n# (so column k of probs is class k; give every class at least as many items as folds)\nprobs = cross_val_predict(LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000), X, y, cv=5, method=\"predict_proba\")\ngiven_label_prob = probs[np.arange(len(y)), y]      # model's belief in the label it was given\nsuspects = np.argsort(given_label_prob)[:200]       # lowest-confidence labels first\n# Send `suspects` to adjudication; do not overwrite labels automatically.\n```","patterns":["**Overlap plus seeded gold.** During production labeling, give 5–10% of items to two annotators and mix in gold items with known answers. Monitor kappa on the overlap per week and the gold pass rate per annotator; a falling pass rate flags drift or inattention before the batch is accepted.","**Provenance on every record.** Store guideline version, annotator id, timestamp and an `adjudicated` flag in each record's metadata (as in the pilot example). When a batch turns out bad, filter it out by annotator or guideline version instead of discarding the dataset.","**Blind subset for model-assisted labeling.** When an LLM pre-fills labels, route a slice of items to annotators without the suggestion. Compare agreement and label distribution between the blind and assisted slices; a large gap is acceptance bias, not quality.","**Out-of-fold label audit.** After the first model, score every item with cross-validated predictions (as in the scikit-learn sketch, or a confident-learning library such as cleanlab) and hand-review the lowest-confidence labels. Track the fraction of reviewed items that are actually relabeled.","**Stratified, uncertainty-driven queues.** Fill the labeling queue by class quotas and by model uncertainty or past disagreement, not uniformly from production. Monitor per-class item counts and recall on the rare class you built the model for.","**Redact before export.** Strip PII from customer text and sign a data-processing agreement before anything reaches a vendor. Monitor redaction coverage with a sampled audit of exported items."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    G[\"Write guideline: schema, rules, 20-30 worked examples\"] --> P[\"Pilot: 100-200 items, 2-3 raters each\"]\n    P --> K[\"Measure kappa, bootstrap it, read confusion matrix\"]\n    K --> Q{\"Agreement acceptable for the stakes?\"}\n    Q -->|\"no\"| A[\"Adjudicate disagreements\"]\n    A -->|\"revise guideline, re-brief\"| G\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| PR[\"Production: 5-10% overlap plus seeded gold\"]\n    PR --> V[(\"Versioned records: guideline, annotator, timestamp\")]\n    PR -->|\"flagged items, gold failures\"| A\n    V --> AU[\"Out-of-fold audit of likely label errors\"]\n    AU -->|\"hand review\"| A","caption":"Follow the loop: guidelines are not finished until a pilot's agreement is acceptable, and production\nlabeling keeps feeding overlap, gold and audit results back into the guideline."},"interview":[{"q":"Cohen's kappa, Fleiss' kappa or Krippendorff's alpha: which do you use, and when?","a":"- **Cohen's kappa:** exactly two raters on nominal labels, every item labeled by both. It is\n  κ = (p_o − p_e) / (1 − p_e), observed agreement corrected for chance agreement given each rater's own\n  label frequencies, which matters because skewed labels inflate raw agreement.\n- **Fleiss' kappa:** three or more raters on nominal labels, the usual choice for a multi-rater pilot.\n- **Krippendorff's alpha:** when some items are missing labels, or the scale is ordinal (rubric 1–5),\n  because it can count a 4-vs-5 disagreement as smaller than a 1-vs-5 one.\n- **Whichever you pick:** bootstrap an interval on a small pilot and read the confusion matrix, because\n  one number hides which classes the guideline fails to separate."},{"q":"Your classifier plateaus at 80% validation accuracy. How do you tell whether the labels or the model are the limit?","a":"- **Measure label noise first:** have two or three people independently relabel a few hundred validation\n  items and compute agreement. If humans agree only about as often as the model is right, the ceiling is\n  the task definition, not the model.\n- **Locate the disagreement:** read the confusion matrix to find which class pairs the guideline fails to\n  separate, then add worked boundary cases for them.\n- **Audit single-labeled items:** rank out-of-fold predictions that confidently contradict the given label\n  and hand-review the top of the list before relabeling anything."},{"q":"When would you not use an LLM to pre-fill labels?","a":"- **For the evaluation set:** keep it human-only and blind to suggestions, because anchoring inflates\n  agreement with the model and the eval score with it.\n- **When you cannot measure acceptance bias:** without a blind subset you cannot tell agreement with each\n  other from agreement with the suggestion, so confident model errors pass through unnoticed.\n- **When the next model trains on it:** that model is partly trained on its predecessor's output, so its\n  errors compound rather than average out."},{"q":"Should you collect demonstrations or preference pairs for an assistant in your domain?","a":"- **Demonstrations** teach the format and house style directly through SFT, but they are the most\n  expensive label because the annotator produces rather than judges, and they need domain experts.\n- **Preferences** are cheaper and more consistent per item and still informative when both candidates are\n  flawed, but each carries less information, so you need many more plus a reward model or DPO.\n- **Typical answer:** a smaller expert demonstration set to establish behaviour, then preference\n  comparisons at scale to refine it, the shape InstructGPT-style pipelines use."},{"q":"How do you keep label quality from drifting over a multi-week production labeling run?","a":"- **Ongoing overlap:** 5–10% of items double-labeled, with kappa tracked per week.\n- **Seeded gold items:** known answers mixed into the queue, with a pass rate tracked per annotator; a\n  falling rate flags drift or inattention before a batch is accepted.\n- **Versioned guidelines:** store guideline version and annotator id on every record, and relabel or split\n  by version when the guideline changes, so the model does not learn a blend of two definitions."}],"tradeoffs":["**Expert quality vs cost and throughput.** Domain experts give the highest label quality and are mandatory for specialised demonstrations, but cost and throughput push most categorical work to trained crowds once agreement is stable.","**Overlap vs budget.** Overlapping annotators multiplies cost, but it is the only way to measure label noise. Reduce overlap to 5–10% only after the pilot shows agreement has stabilised.","**Model assistance vs anchoring.** It cuts cost and time sharply but anchors humans to the model's errors; the saving is real only if a blind subset shows acceptance is not inflating agreement.","**Detailed guidelines vs drift.** They raise consistency but freeze a definition. When production inputs drift, agreement on new items falls and the guideline must be reopened rather than defended.","**Preferences vs demonstrations.** Preference labels are cheaper per item and tolerate imperfect candidates, but each carries less information, so you need many more and a reward model or DPO to use them."],"pitfalls":["**One annotator per item, no overlap.** Symptom: a validation plateau nobody can attribute to the model or the labels. Fix: overlap in the pilot and 5–10% in production.","**Same model-assisted pipeline for the eval set.** Symptom: inflated scores that collapse on a human-only audit. Fix: label the evaluation set human-only, blind to suggestions.","**Changing guidelines mid-project without relabeling.** Symptom: agreement with gold items drifts over time and the model learns a blend of two definitions. Fix: relabel earlier items or split by version.","**Uniform sampling from production.** Rare classes get a handful of examples. Symptom: high overall accuracy with near-zero recall on the class you built the model for. Fix: stratify.","**Reading low kappa on a skewed task as careless raters.** With a 97% majority class, kappa can be low even at very high raw agreement. Symptom: the confusion matrix shows all disagreement on the rare class, a guideline problem for that class, not a workforce problem. Fix: add boundary cases for that class and re-pilot with it oversampled.","**Rubric scores without anchor examples per level.** Symptom: one annotator's \"4\" is another's \"2\", hidden because most items cluster in the middle. Fix: anchor every score level.","**Raw customer text to a vendor without PII redaction and a data-processing agreement.** Symptom: a compliance incident rather than a model problem. Fix: redact and contract first."],"when":"- **Use when:** the behaviour you need is not in the base model or cannot be prompted reliably, and you\n  will measure the result: a routing classifier, a reward model for your domain, SFT demonstrations in a\n  house style, or a golden evaluation set.\n- **Check first:** whether a few hundred high-quality examples plus a strong prompt gets you there. In\n  LLM fine-tuning, quality and coverage beat volume; a smaller set of clean, diverse demonstrations\n  routinely outperforms a much larger noisy one.\n- **Measure:** kappa on the pilot, the gold-item pass rate during production, and the model's accuracy\n  on a human-only held-out set.\n- **Interviews:** explain why chance-corrected agreement matters, how adjudication improves guidelines,\n  and what model-assisted labeling does to the data it produces.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the labeling export and import, computes agreement in the pipeline, stores guideline version and annotator ids on each record, and keeps the gold subset blind to model suggestions.","architect":"Decides what gets labeled and by whom, where labeled data lives and how it is versioned, and which tasks justify expert annotators versus crowd or model-assisted labeling.","fde":"Runs the pilot with the customer's own domain experts, turns their disagreements into guidelines, and sets the expectation that labeling quality, not model choice, is usually the bottleneck."},"check":{"q":"A vendor offers to label 20,000 historical support tickets into eight routing classes in a week,\none annotator per ticket. How would you structure the engagement so that the result is trustworthy?","a":"Start with a written guideline and a pilot of about 200 tickets labeled by three annotators\nindependently, compute Fleiss' kappa or Krippendorff's alpha, and adjudicate every disagreement into\nguideline revisions before releasing the full batch. Insist on 5–10% ongoing overlap and a seeded gold\nset so drift and inattention are measurable during the week. Stratify the sample so the rare classes get\nenough items, redact PII before export, and label a separate human-only evaluation set that never sees\nany model suggestion. Finally, record guideline version and annotator id on every record so later\nanalysis can isolate a bad batch instead of discarding the whole dataset."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why report Cohen's kappa instead of the raw percentage of items two annotators agree on?","options":["Kappa is easier to compute for more than two annotators","Raw agreement is inflated when labels are skewed, because raters agree by chance on the common class; kappa subtracts that chance agreement","Kappa measures accuracy against the ground truth, which raw agreement cannot","Raw agreement cannot be computed for categorical labels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With a 95% \"not spam\" base rate, two raters who ignore the text would still agree about 90% of the time. Kappa removes the expected-by-chance share, so it reflects agreement on the decisions that actually required judgement. Neither metric measures accuracy, since there is no external truth."},{"q":"What is the main risk of having an LLM pre-fill labels for human review?","options":["It makes the labels incompatible with the fine-tuning format","Human annotators become slower because they must read two things","Annotators tend to accept plausible suggestions, so the model's confident errors pass through and the next model is partly trained on its own output","The model's labels are always worse than human labels, so pre-filling never helps"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pre-filling genuinely cuts cost, which is why it is common. The problem is anchoring: agreement looks high because humans agree with the suggestion, not with each other. Keeping a blind subset lets you measure how much acceptance bias the suggestions introduce."},{"q":"Why do RLHF-style pipelines collect many more preference comparisons than written demonstrations?","options":["Preference labels are the only kind a transformer can learn from","Demonstrations cannot be stored in the chat format used for training","Comparing two responses is faster and more consistent across annotators than writing a good response, and it still gives signal when both candidates are imperfect","Preference comparisons carry more information per item than a demonstration"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each comparison carries less information than a full demonstration, which is why many are needed. They win on cost and consistency: judging is easier than producing, and a reward model can aggregate thousands of noisy comparisons into a usable signal."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","sources":["Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, 2009 — The Elements of Statistical Learning","Kohavi, 1995 — A Study of Cross-Validation and Bootstrap for Accuracy Estimation and Model Selection","Official docs: scikit-learn model_selection"],"why":"- **Training loss says nothing about new data.** Training minimises loss on the examples it sees; a\n  model that memorised every row would score perfectly. Held-out data is the only instrument that\n  measures generalisation.\n- **One held-out set stops being enough once you decide with it.** Try twenty learning-rate and epoch\n  combinations and keep the best validation score. Each score is true quality plus noise, so the maximum\n  of twenty picks the luckiest noise too, and the winner overstates production.\n- **Selection bias needs a second, untouched set.** Statisticians call it selection bias; practitioners\n  call it overfitting the validation set. The fix is the same: a held-out set no decision ever touched,\n  read once.\n- **\"Shuffle the rows and cut 80/20\" fails quietly.** Rows are rarely independent: four turns of one\n  support conversation, ten chunks of one PDF, fifty events from one user, last month's and this month's\n  tickets. Train on some, test on the rest, and the model is rewarded for recognising the source.\n- **The split level is the claim.** A split is only meaningful when made at the level on which you\n  expect the model to generalise; otherwise the number has no relationship to a genuinely new case.","how":"### Three roles, not three ratios\n| Set | Role | Read how often |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Train** | receives gradient steps; nothing else is fit to it | every step |\n| **Validation** (dev) | every choice made by looking at a number: hyperparameters, early stopping, checkpoint selection, decision thresholds, prompt and template variants | as often as you like, which is exactly why its final score is biased upward |\n| **Test** | the one-shot estimate | once; read, change, read again and it is a second validation set |\n\n- **When the test set is consumed,** disciplined teams carve a fresh one out of newly arrived data.\n\n### How big each set should be\nRatios such as 80/10/10 are inherited from the small-data era. What matters is the absolute size of the\nheld-out sets, because the standard error of a measured accuracy *p* on *n* examples is about\n`sqrt(p·(1−p)/n)`.\n\n| n (at p = 0.9) | Standard error |\n|---|---|\n| 100 | ±3 points |\n| 200 (a 10% slice of 2,000 fine-tuning examples) | about ±2 points |\n| 1,000 | ±0.95 points |\n| 10,000 | ±0.3 points |\n\n- **Small test sets cannot separate close models.** A 1,000-example test set cannot separate two models\n  one point apart, however large the training set is. The 200-row slice cannot reliably separate two\n  models two points apart.\n- **Huge datasets need tiny fractions.** With ten million rows a 1% test set is 100,000 rows, far more\n  than needed.\n- **Rule:** choose held-out sizes from the effect you need to detect, then give everything else to\n  training.\n\n### Comparing two models\n- **Unpaired is noisier.** If each model were scored on an independent sample, the noise on the\n  *difference* would be about √2 times the single-model error.\n- **Paired is tighter.** Score both on the same items: most items are answered identically, and only the\n  items where they disagree carry information about which is better.\n- **Use a paired test** on those discordant items (McNemar's test, or a paired bootstrap over items)\n  rather than eyeballing two error bars. This is one more reason to keep the test set fixed and shared\n  across every candidate.\n\n### Ways to cut\n1. **Random.** Only valid when rows are independent and identically distributed, which is rarer than it\n   looks.\n2. **Stratified.** Preserve class or category proportions in every split. Essential when a class is rare:\n   a random 10% slice of 50 positives can easily contain two of them, and the metric on that class\n   becomes a coin flip.\n3. **Grouped.** Assign whole groups (conversation, user, document, source repository) to a single split.\n   Hashing the group id into a bucket makes the assignment deterministic and stable as new data arrives.\n4. **Temporal.** Train on the past, validate on the recent past, test on the newest period. Mandatory\n   when the data changes over time and the deployed model will serve the future, which covers most\n   production systems; it also exposes drift early.\n5. **Cross-validation.** *k* folds rotate the validation role, giving *k* estimates at *k* times the\n   training cost. Worth it when data is small and training is cheap (classical models, small heads),\n   rarely for LLM fine-tunes.\n\n### LLM-specific rules\n- **Deduplicate before splitting,** including near-duplicates such as one template with different\n  fill-ins; otherwise validation is partly a copy of training and overfitting becomes invisible.\n- **Check held-out sets for contamination** against any public benchmark you report and against data\n  your base-model vendor may have pretrained on; a contaminated test set measures memory, not skill.\n- **Split before fitting anything on data:** tokenizer training, normalisation statistics, target\n  encodings, embedding-based filters. Fitting on the union leaks the held-out distribution into the\n  pipeline.\n- **Keep two instruments.** Validation loss on the dev set is token-level, cheap and continuous; the task\n  metric on the test set is what the customer sees. Keep both.","example":"### Deterministic grouped 80/10/10 split\nA split for a multi-turn support dataset. Hashing the conversation id means every turn of a\nconversation lands in the same split, the assignment never changes when rows are added, and the result\nis identical on every machine.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef bucket(key: str, buckets: int = 100) -> int:\n    # Stable across processes and machines; Python's built-in hash() is salted per run.\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % buckets\n\ndef assign(example: dict) -> str:\n    # Split on the unit of generalisation: all turns of one conversation travel together.\n    b = bucket(example[\"conversation_id\"])\n    return \"test\" if b < 10 else \"val\" if b < 20 else \"train\"   # 10 / 10 / 80\n\nrows = [{\"conversation_id\": f\"conv-{i // 4}\", \"turn\": i % 4,\n         \"label\": \"billing\" if i % 7 == 0 else \"other\"} for i in range(4000)]\nsplits = {s: [r for r in rows if assign(r) == s] for s in (\"train\", \"val\", \"test\")}\n\nfor name, rs in splits.items():\n    convs = {r[\"conversation_id\"] for r in rs}\n    print(name, len(rs), \"rows\", len(convs), \"conversations\", Counter(r[\"label\"] for r in rs))\n\n# Leakage check: no conversation may appear in two splits.\nids = {s: {r[\"conversation_id\"] for r in rs} for s, rs in splits.items()}\nassert not (ids[\"train\"] & ids[\"val\"]) and not (ids[\"train\"] & ids[\"test\"]) and not (ids[\"val\"] & ids[\"test\"])\n\n# A row that arrives next month keeps the split its conversation already has.\nassert assign({\"conversation_id\": \"conv-17\"}) == assign({\"conversation_id\": \"conv-17\"})\n```\n\n- **The `Counter` line is the stratification check.** If the `billing` share differs noticeably between\n  splits, either enlarge the held-out sets or stratify within hash buckets.\n- **No groups?** scikit-learn's `train_test_split(..., stratify=y)` does the stratified cut directly.\n\n### Worked comparison: is a 1.5-point win real?\nModel A scores 91.0% and model B 89.5% on the same 1,000 test items. The paired view counts only the\nitems where they disagree.\n\n| Outcome per item | Count |\n|---|---|\n| both right | 870 |\n| only A right | 40 |\n| only B right | 25 |\n| both wrong | 65 |\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb, sqrt\n\ndef mcnemar_exact(b, c):\n    # b: items only model A got right, c: items only model B got right\n    n, k = b + c, min(b, c)\n    tail = sum(comb(n, i) for i in range(k + 1)) / 2 ** n\n    return min(1.0, 2 * tail)                       # two-sided exact binomial test\n\nprint(f\"single-model SE ~ {sqrt(0.9 * 0.1 / 1000):.4f}\")        # 0.0095\nprint(f\"McNemar exact p = {mcnemar_exact(40, 25):.3f}\")          # 0.082\n```\n\n- **Reading it.** Only the 65 discordant items carry evidence, and 40 vs 25 gives p ≈ 0.08: suggestive,\n  not conclusive at the usual 0.05 bar.\n- **Decision.** Do not ship on this alone; grow the test set or add fresh items, keeping it paired.","patterns":["**Hash-keyed split in the data pipeline.** Assign split by hashing the group key (conversation, customer, document) as in the first example, and store the split name on each row. Run the leakage assertion in CI on every dataset build; monitor per-split row and group counts for unexpected jumps.","**Library splitters for grouped and temporal cuts.** In scikit-learn, `GroupShuffleSplit` or `GroupKFold` keep groups together, `StratifiedGroupKFold` also balances classes, and `TimeSeriesSplit` gives forward-in-time folds. Monitor the class mix per fold with the `Counter` check.","**Versioned, frozen evaluation sets.** Store validation and test sets as immutable, versioned files (content hash in the name) and record which version every reported score used. Monitor how many times the test set has been read; retire it after the project's final comparison.","**Rolling temporal test set.** For drifting data, rebuild the test set from the newest period each cycle (for example the latest month) and keep the previous one as a regression set. Monitor the gap between the old and new test scores as a drift signal.","**Paired evaluation by default.** Score every candidate on the same frozen items and report a paired bootstrap interval or McNemar p-value next to the headline difference. Monitor discordant-item counts, because they set how small a difference you can detect."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[(\"Raw labeled data\")] --> DD[\"Deduplicate, incl. near-duplicates\"]\n    DD --> Q1{\"Do rows share a source?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"yes\"| GR[\"Key: conversation, user or document id\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| RK[\"Key: row id\"]\n    GR --> Q2{\"Will the model serve the future?\"}\n    RK --> Q2\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| TM[\"Temporal: past to train, newest to test\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| HB[\"Hash key to buckets, stratify rare classes\"]\n    TM --> S[\"Train / Val / Test\"]\n    HB --> S\n    S -->|\"gradient steps\"| TR[\"Train\"]\n    S -->|\"every decision, read often\"| VA[\"Validation\"]\n    S -->|\"read once, paired comparison\"| TE[\"Test\"]","caption":"Read it top to bottom: clean and choose the cut before anything is fit, then note that only the test set\nis read exactly once."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do you need both a validation set and a test set?","a":"- **Validation absorbs decisions.** Hyperparameters, early stopping, checkpoint and threshold choices are\n  all made by looking at it, so the winning score includes the luckiest noise and is biased upward.\n- **Test is the unbiased estimate** only if no decision ever touched it, which means reading it once.\n  Read, change and read again, and it has become a second validation set."},{"q":"How would you split a dataset of RAG question-answer pairs generated from 500 internal documents?","a":"- **Unit of generalisation is the document.** Many questions come from the same chunk or PDF, so a row\n  split rewards recognising the source text.\n- **Cut:** deduplicate near-duplicate questions, then hash the document id into train, validation and\n  test buckets so every question from one document lands in the same split.\n- **Check:** assert no document id appears in two splits, and keep the class or topic mix comparable with\n  a `Counter` per split; add a temporal cut if new documents keep arriving."},{"q":"When is k-fold cross-validation not worth it?","a":"- **When one training run is expensive,** as with LLM fine-tunes: *k* folds cost *k* times the training\n  compute for one estimate.\n- **When a single split's variance is already small** relative to the differences you need to detect,\n  for example with large held-out sets.\n- **It pays** for small classical models or small heads where data is scarce and training is cheap."},{"q":"Model B beats model A by 1 point on a 1,000-item test set. Do you ship it?","a":"- **Not on that number alone.** At about 90% accuracy the single-model standard error is\n  `sqrt(p·(1−p)/n)` ≈ 0.95 points, so a 1-point gap sits inside the noise.\n- **Run a paired test** on the same items: McNemar's test or a paired bootstrap over the discordant\n  items, which carry all the evidence.\n- **If it stays inconclusive,** grow the test set with fresh items, keeping the comparison paired, rather\n  than re-reading the same set until something looks significant."},{"q":"How do you size held-out sets for a 2,000-example fine-tuning project?","a":"- **Start from the effect size, not the ratio.** A 200-row slice gives about ±2 points at 90% accuracy, so\n  it cannot reliably separate models two points apart.\n- **Decide what difference matters,** pick the held-out size whose standard error resolves it, and give\n  every remaining row to training.\n- **Use paired comparisons** on a fixed shared test set, because pairing tightens the error bar on the\n  difference for the same number of items."}],"tradeoffs":["**Bigger held-out sets vs training data.** Larger validation and test sets give tighter estimates but take rows from training; the balance flips toward training when the dataset is a small fine-tuning set and a ±2-point error bar is acceptable.","**Honest vs flattering numbers.** Grouped and temporal splits report lower numbers than a random split, and the lower number is the honest one; the random number was high precisely because it leaked.","**Cross-validation vs cost.** It multiplies training cost by *k*; it pays only when the variance of a single split is large relative to the differences you need to detect.","**Hash stability vs re-stratification.** A hash-based split is reproducible and stable as data grows, but cannot be re-stratified after the fact; rare classes must be checked or stratified within buckets.","**One-shot test vs learning.** Reading the test set once gives an unbiased estimate but teaches you nothing for the next iteration; rotating in fresh test sets costs labelling effort but keeps honesty over a long project."],"pitfalls":["**Splitting by row when rows share a source.** Symptom: validation accuracy far above production and a sharp drop the first time a new customer, document or user arrives. Fix: grouped split.","**Fitting preprocessing before splitting** (scalers, tokenizers, vocabularies, filters). Symptom: a small, persistent optimistic bias that vanishes when the pipeline is rebuilt on train only. Fix: split first, fit every preprocessing step on train only.","**Reusing the test set across iterations.** Symptom: a test score that keeps climbing while production does not move, because the test set has quietly become a validation set. Fix: carve a fresh test set from newly arrived data.","**Random splits with a rare class.** Symptom: the minority-class metric jumping between 0% and 100% across seeds. Fix: stratify.","**Re-splitting with a new seed on every run.** Symptom: incomparable experiments and \"improvements\" that are nothing but split noise. Fix: a fixed, hashed split.","**Temporal splits with whole-window features** (aggregates, embeddings fit on all periods). Symptom: a time-split test score that is still too good to be true. Fix: compute features only from data before each split's cutoff.","**Validation drawn from the same synthetic template as training.** Symptom: near-perfect validation alongside poor human evaluation on real prompts. Fix: deduplicate templates across splits and hold out real prompts."],"when":"Every supervised project needs the three roles; the decision is how to cut.\n- **Grouped split:** the moment any two rows share an origin.\n- **Temporal split:** whenever data arrives over time and the model will serve the future.\n- **Sizing:** size held-out sets from the standard-error formula against the smallest difference you\n  need to detect.\n- **Cross-validation:** for small classical models, not for LLM fine-tunes where one run is expensive.\n- **Audit first when a model looks too good:** who made the split, on what key, and was anything fit\n  before it.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you split this data\" really asks \"what is the unit of generalisation and how\n  would you stop leakage\"; a strong answer names the key, the sizes and the one-shot test discipline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the deterministic split function, the leakage assertion and the stratification check into the data pipeline so every run uses the same held-out sets.","architect":"Owns the policy that evaluation sets are versioned, group- or time-keyed, sized to the decision they support and refreshed when the test set has been consumed by iteration.","fde":"Builds the held-out set with the customer's SMEs from their most recent data and defends a lower, honest number against the inflated one produced by a row-level random split."},"check":{"q":"You fine-tune a ticket classifier on 40,000 tickets from 3,000 customers with a random 80/10/10\nsplit and get 94% validation accuracy, but the first new customer onboarded sees 78%. What happened, how\nwould you re-split, and how large should the held-out sets be?","a":"The random split put tickets from the same customer on both sides, so the model learned\ncustomer-specific signals (product names, signatures, phrasing) and the 94% measured recognition, not\nclassification. Re-split by hashing the customer id so each customer is entirely in one set, and\nconsider a temporal cut as well if ticket mix drifts. The honest number will fall toward 78%, which is\nthe number that matters. Sizing: 4,000 held-out tickets would give a standard error of about 0.5 points\nat 90% accuracy if tickets were independent, but tickets from one customer are correlated, so the\neffective sample lies somewhere between the ~300 held-out customers and the 4,000 tickets; expect wider\nerror bars (a customer-level bootstrap measures them) and stratify by category so rare ticket types\nappear in every split. Finally, keep the test set untouched until the final comparison, and make that\ncomparison paired on the same customers so the error bar on the difference is as tight as the data allows."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why keep a separate test set when a validation set already measures held-out performance?","options":["The validation set is too small to be statistically meaningful","Choosing the best of many configurations on validation biases its score upward, so an untouched set is needed for an unbiased estimate","Validation data is used for gradient updates after the first epoch","Test sets are required to compute the loss function correctly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every selection made on validation picks the configuration whose noise was luckiest as well as the best model, so the winning validation score overstates generalisation. Validation data is never used for gradient updates, and size is a separate concern."},{"q":"You are predicting next month's churn from per-user event logs. Which split is appropriate?","options":["A random 80/10/10 split over events","A stratified split over events by churn label","A temporal split: build features and train on earlier months, evaluate on a later month","Ten-fold cross-validation over events"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model will be applied to a future month, so evaluation must come after all training data; any event-level random split puts a user's later events (and their churn outcome) into training, and stratification does not fix that. If the model must also serve users never seen, hold out whole users too."},{"q":"Roughly how precisely does a 1,000-example test set measure accuracy near 90%?","options":["About ±0.1 percentage points","About ±1 percentage point","About ±5 percentage points","About ±10 percentage points"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard error is sqrt(0.9 × 0.1 / 1000) ≈ 0.0095, so differences smaller than about one point are inside the noise; a tenfold larger set is needed for ±0.3, which is why absolute size matters more than the split ratio."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/epochs","sources":["Muennighoff et al., 2023 — Scaling Data-Constrained Language Models","Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, 2016 — Deep Learning","Official docs: Hugging Face Trainer (num_train_epochs, max_steps)"],"why":"- **Minibatches consume data piecewise.** Gradient descent updates on minibatches of *B* examples, so\n  after `ceil(N / B)` steps every one of the *N* training examples has contributed once. \"Every example\n  seen once\" is the natural unit for reshuffling, evaluating and stopping.\n- **One pass is not enough on small sets.** With a small learning rate each example nudges the weights\n  only slightly, so for a few thousand rows one pass leaves the model far from the best fit. Repeating\n  passes extracts more gradient signal from data you cannot get more of.\n- **Repetition eventually fits the particulars.** After the shared pattern is learned, only label noise,\n  phrasing and field order are left to fit. The epoch count is a knob on how much to trust these exact\n  examples versus the model's prior.\n- **Naive stopping rules fail.** \"Until training loss stops falling\" never stops, because loss on repeated\n  data falls all the way to memorisation. \"Always one epoch\" leaves small sets underfit; \"always ten\"\n  overfits most. The honest control is the validation curve: train while it improves, stop when it turns.\n- **Pretraining is the opposite regime.** The corpus is so large relative to compute that the run ends\n  before one pass, and unique tokens are worth more than repeats. A data-constrained scaling study found\n  repeating data for up to roughly four passes costs little compared with fresh data.","how":"### Accounting\nWith *N* examples, micro-batch size *B* and gradient accumulation over *A* micro-batches:\n\n```\nmicro_batches_per_epoch = ceil(N / B)\noptimizer_steps_per_epoch = micro_batches_per_epoch / A\ntotal_optimizer_steps = epochs × optimizer_steps_per_epoch\n```\n\n- **Schedulers run on optimizer steps,** so changing the epoch count silently changes the decay horizon.\n  A cosine schedule over 375 steps reaches a given fraction of peak LR at a different point than one\n  over 1,250 steps.\n- **Hugging Face default.** The `Trainer` derives `max_steps` from `num_train_epochs` (default 3) unless\n  `max_steps` is set explicitly, in which case it wins.\n- **So different epoch counts are different schedules,** not \"the same run for longer\".\n\n### Reshuffling every epoch\n- **Order sets batch composition.** A fixed order makes the gradient noise identical in every pass, and\n  the same examples are always freshest at the end of the epoch when you evaluate.\n- **Reseed the sampler per epoch** (`seed + epoch`): different batch compositions each pass, while the\n  run stays reproducible and resumable.\n- **Resume exactly.** A checkpoint that records the epoch and the number of batches consumed can\n  regenerate the exact remaining order.\n\n### Where evaluation and checkpoints go\n| Epoch length | Evaluate | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| ~125 steps | at each epoch boundary | every example has been seen an equal number of times |\n| hours long | every *n* steps | the model can overfit or diverge mid-epoch, and an end-of-epoch check would miss the best checkpoint |\n| a handful of steps (200 fine-tuning examples) | per epoch | it is the only granularity that is not noise |\n\n### Why the number matters so much in fine-tuning\n- **Each pass moves away from the prior.** A pretrained model is a strong prior; each pass over a small\n  instruction set moves it toward the specifics of those rows.\n- **Illustrative arithmetic.** 1,000 examples at batch 8 give 125 steps per epoch; three epochs are 375\n  steps, and a 3% warmup is about eleven steps (frameworks round 11.25 differently).\n- **Common practice** for supervised fine-tuning is a small number of epochs, rising only for tiny sets\n  where each epoch is a few steps.\n- **The real control** is total steps × learning rate judged against the validation curve, not a magic\n  integer.\n\n### Reading the curve across epochs\n1. **The staircase.** Once the model starts memorising, training loss is roughly flat within an epoch and\n   then drops abruptly at the start of the next one.\n2. **Why it drops.** The model now sees examples it has already seen, and predicts them better than the\n   unseen remainder of the first pass.\n3. **The diagnosis.** A staircase on training loss with flat or rising validation loss is direct\n   evidence that further epochs buy memorisation, not skill.\n4. **The decision.** Early stopping with patience: evaluate at a fixed cadence, keep the best checkpoint,\n   stop after *p* evaluations without improvement. The configured epoch count becomes an upper bound,\n   not a commitment.\n\n### What \"an example\" means with packing\n- **Packing** puts several short examples into one fixed-length sequence to avoid padding waste.\n- **An epoch is still one pass over the examples,** but steps per epoch follow from total tokens divided\n  by tokens per batch, not from `N / B`.\n- **Same rows, different steps.** Many short examples and a few long ones can share a row count but\n  differ widely in step count, so compute steps from tokens when packing is on.\n\n### Pretraining counts tokens\n- **No meaningful epoch.** A corpus of trillions of tokens with a budget covering a fraction of it has its\n  schedule, warmup and evaluation cadence defined in tokens or steps.\n- **Reuse is a choice.** Partial reuse of data is made under data constraints, not a default.\n\n### Which unit to compare on\n- **Same dataset:** compare by epochs.\n- **Different dataset sizes:** compare by optimizer steps or tokens seen. \"Two epochs on 10,000\n  examples\" and \"two epochs on 1,000\" differ by 10× in updates.","example":"### A training loop with explicit accounting\nEpochs, micro-batches, accumulation, a per-epoch reseeded sampler, and a linear schedule defined over\noptimizer steps. The printed step counts show why the schedule length depends on the epoch count.\n\n```python\nimport math, torch\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader, TensorDataset\n\nN, B, EPOCHS, ACCUM = 1000, 8, 3, 4\nx = torch.randn(N, 16); y = (x.sum(1) > 0).float()\nxv = torch.randn(200, 16); yv = (xv.sum(1) > 0).float()            # held-out\nmodel = torch.nn.Sequential(torch.nn.Linear(16, 32), torch.nn.ReLU(), torch.nn.Linear(32, 1))\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\nloss_fn = torch.nn.functional.binary_cross_entropy_with_logits\n\nmicro_per_epoch = math.ceil(N / B)                                 # 125\ntotal_steps = math.ceil(EPOCHS * micro_per_epoch / ACCUM)          # 94 optimizer steps, incl. final flush\nsched = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.LambdaLR(opt, lambda s: max(0.0, 1 - s / total_steps))\n\nmicro, step = 0, 0\nfor epoch in range(EPOCHS):\n    g = torch.Generator().manual_seed(1234 + epoch)                # new order every epoch, reproducible\n    loader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(x, y), batch_size=B, shuffle=True, generator=g)\n    model.train()\n    for xb, yb in loader:\n        (loss_fn(model(xb).squeeze(1), yb) / ACCUM).backward()\n        micro += 1\n        if micro % ACCUM == 0:                                     # one update per ACCUM micro-batches\n            opt.step(); sched.step(); opt.zero_grad(); step += 1\n    model.eval()\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        val = loss_fn(model(xv).squeeze(1), yv).item()\n    print(f\"epoch {epoch+1}: micro-batches={micro} optimizer steps={step} \"\n          f\"lr={sched.get_last_lr()[0]:.2e} val_loss={val:.3f}\")\n\n# 375 micro-batches is not divisible by 4: three gradients are still pending. Flush them.\nif micro % ACCUM:\n    opt.step(); opt.zero_grad(); step += 1\n```\n\nThe leftover micro-batches are the kind of accounting detail epochs hide:\n- **Without the final flush,** three batches of data contribute nothing.\n- **If the schedule is sized to 93 steps instead of 94,** the flush runs at a learning rate of zero and\n  still contributes nothing.\n- **With accumulation that does not divide an epoch,** gradients from the last batches of one epoch land\n  in the first update of the next.\n\n### Worked scenario: converting configs to optimizer steps\nBefore judging a config, turn it into steps. Packing assumes 2,048-token sequences.\n\n| Config | Steps per epoch | Total steps |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1,000 examples, batch 8, no accumulation, 3 epochs | 125 | 375 (3% warmup ≈ 11) |\n| same, accumulation 4 | 125 micro-batches → 31.25 updates | 94 with the final flush |\n| 10,000 examples, batch 8, 2 epochs | 1,250 | 2,500 |\n| 10,000 examples × ~200 tokens, packed, batch 8, 2 epochs | 2,000,000 tokens ÷ 2,048 ≈ 977 sequences → 123 | 246 |\n\n- **Reading it.** The packed run has the same rows as the unpacked 10,000-example run but about a tenth\n  of its steps, so its schedule and warmup must be sized on 246 steps, not 2,500.\n\n### Production snippet: epochs as an upper bound\nHugging Face `Trainer` with step-based evaluation and early stopping, so the epoch count caps the run\nrather than fixing it.\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import Trainer, TrainingArguments, EarlyStoppingCallback\n\nargs = TrainingArguments(\n    output_dir=\"out\",\n    num_train_epochs=5,                 # upper bound; early stopping decides\n    eval_strategy=\"steps\",              # named evaluation_strategy in older releases\n    eval_steps=50,\n    save_strategy=\"steps\",\n    save_steps=50,                      # must line up with eval for load_best_model_at_end\n    load_best_model_at_end=True,\n    metric_for_best_model=\"eval_loss\",\n    greater_is_better=False,\n    save_total_limit=3,\n)\ntrainer = Trainer(model=model, args=args, train_dataset=train_ds, eval_dataset=val_ds,\n                  callbacks=[EarlyStoppingCallback(early_stopping_patience=3)])\ntrainer.train()\n```","patterns":["**Epoch cap plus early stopping.** For any fine-tune, set the epoch count as a ceiling and attach early stopping with patience (for example `EarlyStoppingCallback(early_stopping_patience=3)` with `load_best_model_at_end=True`). Monitor validation loss and the task metric per evaluation, and the step at which the best checkpoint was taken.","**Step-based evaluation for long epochs.** When an epoch takes hours, evaluate and save every fixed number of steps (`eval_strategy=\"steps\"`, matching `save_steps`). Monitor whether the best checkpoint falls mid-epoch; if it often does, epoch-boundary evaluation would have lost it.","**Log steps and tokens, not just epochs.** Record optimizer steps, tokens seen and the current learning rate in the experiment tracker for every run. Compare runs across dataset sizes on those axes, and re-derive warmup and decay length whenever the epoch count or packing changes.","**Resumable, reseeded sampler.** Seed the sampler with `seed + epoch` and save the epoch and batches consumed in each checkpoint; the Hugging Face `Trainer` skips already-consumed batches on resume unless `ignore_data_skip` is set. Monitor that a resumed run's loss curve matches an uninterrupted one.","**Staircase alert.** Flag runs where training loss drops sharply at an epoch boundary while validation loss is flat or rising. Monitor the train-validation gap per epoch and stop or cut epochs when it widens."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Start epoch e\"] --> R[\"Reseed sampler with seed + e\"]\n    R --> B[\"Micro-batches: ceil(N / B) per epoch\"]\n    B -->|\"every A micro-batches\"| O[\"Optimizer step, scheduler step\"]\n    O --> E{\"Eval point reached?\"}\n    E -->|\"no\"| D{\"Epoch finished?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| V[\"Validation loss and task metric\"]\n    V --> I{\"Improved on best?\"}\n    I -->|\"yes\"| C[(\"Save best checkpoint\")]\n    I -->|\"no, patience used up\"| X[\"Stop, load best checkpoint\"]\n    I -->|\"no, patience left\"| D\n    C --> D\n    D -->|\"no\"| B\n    D -->|\"yes\"| N{\"Epoch cap reached?\"}\n    N -->|\"no\"| S\n    N -->|\"yes\"| X","caption":"One fine-tuning run with early stopping: each epoch reshuffles, updates and evaluates, and the epoch\ncount only caps the loop while the validation curve decides when it ends."},"interview":[{"q":"What is an epoch, and why does fine-tuning use epochs while pretraining uses tokens?","a":"- **Definition:** one full pass of the optimiser over the training set, `ceil(N / B)` micro-batches, the\n  natural unit for reshuffling, evaluating and stopping.\n- **Fine-tuning:** a small fixed set must be seen several times to extract enough gradient signal, so\n  epochs are a meaningful count.\n- **Pretraining:** the corpus is larger than the compute budget, so the run ends before one pass and\n  schedules, warmup and evaluation are defined in tokens or steps."},{"q":"Your training loss drops sharply at each epoch boundary. What is happening and what do you do?","a":"- **The staircase signals memorisation starting.** The model now sees examples it has already seen and\n  predicts them better than the unseen remainder of the first pass.\n- **Confirm with validation:** if validation loss is flat or rising across the same boundaries, further\n  epochs buy memorisation, not skill.\n- **Act:** keep the best checkpoint, stop with patience, or reduce epochs or learning rate for the next run."},{"q":"You change a run from 3 to 6 epochs and keep everything else. What else changed?","a":"- **The schedule did.** Schedulers run on optimizer steps, so total steps double and warmup and decay\n  stretch over a different horizon; the learning rate at step 300 is no longer the same.\n- **So the runs are not comparable** as \"the same run for longer\". Compare best checkpoints on\n  validation, and re-derive warmup and decay length explicitly."},{"q":"How many optimizer steps does a packed run take, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Compute from tokens:** with packing, steps per epoch are total tokens divided by tokens per batch, not\n  `N / B`.\n- **Example:** 10,000 examples of about 200 tokens packed into 2,048-token sequences at batch 8 give about\n  977 sequences, 123 steps per epoch, so two epochs are 246 steps rather than 2,500.\n- **Why it matters:** warmup, decay and evaluation cadence sized for 2,500 steps would be badly wrong."},{"q":"When would you not evaluate at epoch boundaries?","a":"- **When an epoch takes hours.** The model can overfit or diverge mid-epoch, and the best checkpoint can\n  sit between two end-of-epoch evaluations and never be saved.\n- **Instead evaluate every *n* steps** and save at the same cadence so the best can be loaded at the end,\n  paying extra evaluation compute for it."}],"tradeoffs":["**More epochs vs memorisation.** They lower training loss and improve the fit on small sets, but each extra pass fits more of the rows' particulars; the balance flips where the validation curve turns upward.","**Repeated vs new data.** Repeating data is free while new data costs money, but unique tokens are worth more per token, so repetition is a fallback for data-constrained regimes, not a substitute for collection.","**Per-epoch vs step-based evaluation.** Per-epoch is cheap and conventional but coarse for long epochs; step-based evaluation finds the best checkpoint at the cost of extra evaluation compute.","**Dropping the last partial batch.** It keeps tensor shapes uniform but leaves a few examples unseen each pass; irrelevant at scale, measurable with a few hundred examples."],"pitfalls":["**Copying a fixed epoch count from a tutorial.** Symptom: held-out loss rising through the second half of the run while training loss keeps falling. Fix: early stopping on the validation curve.","**Changing epochs without noticing the LR schedule changed.** Symptom: runs that are \"longer\" but incomparable, with different LR at the same step. Fix: log the LR per step and re-derive warmup and decay for the new step count.","**Accumulation that does not divide the epoch.** Symptom: an optimizer step count that does not match the expected arithmetic, and gradients leaking across epoch boundaries. Fix: size the schedule with `ceil` and flush pending gradients at the end.","**Comparing runs by epoch when dataset sizes differ.** Symptom: a \"two-epoch\" run on ten times the data credited to the data rather than to ten times the updates. Fix: compare on optimizer steps or tokens.","**Resuming without the sampler position.** Symptom: some examples seen twice and others never in that epoch, and a resumed run that no longer matches an uninterrupted one. Fix: checkpoint the epoch and batches consumed.","**Reading the staircase as \"learning more\".** The symptom that shows it is memorisation is validation loss flat or rising across the same epoch boundaries. Fix: stop early and keep the best checkpoint.","**Evaluating only at epoch boundaries on a large dataset.** Symptom: the best checkpoint sits between two evaluations and is never saved. Fix: step-based evaluation and saving."],"when":"- **Think in epochs when:** the dataset is fixed and small enough to be seen several times: supervised\n  fine-tuning, reward-model training, classifier heads. Start low, evaluate at least once per epoch, and\n  save a checkpoint at each evaluation so the best can be recovered.\n- **Let the curve decide:** the validation curve, not a convention, decides when to stop.\n- **Think in steps or tokens when:** data streams or is far larger than the budget: pretraining and\n  continued pretraining.\n- **Reading someone else's config:** convert epochs into optimizer steps before judging it, and check\n  whether the schedule length was tied to that number.\n- **Interviews:** derive steps from examples, batch size and accumulation, and explain why pretraining\n  uses tokens while fine-tuning uses epochs; that is the expected baseline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets `num_train_epochs` or `max_steps`, wires per-epoch reseeding and evaluation, and checks that accumulation divides the epoch so step counts match the plan.","architect":"Defines the training budget in steps or tokens and the evaluation cadence, so epoch counts across teams and datasets translate into comparable compute and comparable schedules.","fde":"Explains to a customer with 800 labelled examples why the fine-tune is run for a few epochs with validation checks rather than \"until the loss is zero\", and what the curve they are shown means."},"check":{"q":"A colleague reports that the fine-tune run with five epochs reached a lower loss than the run with\ntwo, so five epochs is better. What would you ask to see before agreeing?","a":"First, which loss: training loss on repeated data always falls with more epochs, so only the\nheld-out loss or the task metric can support the claim. Second, the validation curve across the whole run,\nto see whether held-out performance peaked earlier and the five-epoch endpoint is already past the turn.\nThird, the schedule: with step-based decay the five-epoch run spent its learning rate differently, so the\ncomparison mixes epochs with schedule shape; comparing the best checkpoint of each run is fairer than\ncomparing endpoints. Finally, a check on the task metric and on general capability, because a lower token\nloss after many passes often comes with memorised phrasing and brittleness on paraphrased inputs."},"quiz":[{"q":"4,000 examples, micro-batch 16, gradient accumulation 2, three epochs. How many optimizer steps?","options":["750","375","250","1,500"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each epoch has 4000 / 16 = 250 micro-batches, and accumulation over two makes 125 optimizer steps per epoch; three epochs give 375. The tempting 750 forgets accumulation, which halves the update count."},{"q":"Why is LLM pretraining usually described in tokens rather than epochs?","options":["Tokenisation makes examples variable-length so epochs cannot be counted","The corpus is larger than the compute budget can cover, so the run ends before one full pass and unique data is preferred over repeats","Optimizers require a token budget to compute the learning rate","Epochs only apply to classification tasks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A pass over trillions of tokens rarely completes, so the schedule and evaluation cadence are set in tokens or steps. Variable-length examples are easily counted; the issue is scale, not tokenisation."},{"q":"What goes wrong if the training set is presented in the same order every epoch?","options":["The learning rate schedule resets at every epoch boundary","Gradient accumulation stops working across epochs","Batch compositions and gradient noise repeat exactly, and the same examples are always freshest at evaluation time","The validation loss can no longer be computed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reseeding the sampler each epoch gives different batch groupings and decorrelated noise between passes while staying reproducible; a fixed order biases what the model has just seen when it is evaluated."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/learning-rate","sources":["Smith, 2017 — Cyclical Learning Rates for Training Neural Networks","Goyal et al., 2017 — Accurate, Large Minibatch SGD: Training ImageNet in 1 Hour","Loshchilov & Hutter, 2019 — Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"- **A gradient is local.** Gradient descent is `θ ← θ − η·∇L(θ)`. The gradient says which direction is\n  downhill here, not how far downhill continues; how far is governed by curvature.\n- **There is a hard stability bound.** For a one-dimensional quadratic `L = ½λθ²` the update multiplies\n  θ by `(1 − ηλ)`, so it converges only when `η < 2/λ`. Above that, each step overshoots the minimum by\n  more than it approached it and the loss grows without bound.\n- **One η must serve every direction.** A real loss surface has many curvatures; a single η must be small\n  enough for the sharpest while still progressing along the flat ones. That is why it must be tuned, and\n  why adaptive optimisers, which rescale each coordinate, exist. Even with them, the global η sets how far\n  every weight can move.\n- **\"Small to be safe\" wastes the budget.** Distance travelled is roughly steps × step size, so a rate\n  ten times too small ends with the loss of a much shorter run. It also tends to generalise worse, an\n  empirical regularity whose explanation is still debated.\n- **\"Large to go fast\" breaks things.** Loss spikes, NaNs, and pretrained features destroyed in the first\n  hundred steps. So the learning rate is a trajectory, not a constant: warmup, peak and decay, each\n  answering a different failure.","how":"### What η means under SGD versus Adam\n| Optimiser | Update | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| SGD | `Δθ = −η·g` | step scales with gradient magnitude, which differs across layers by orders of magnitude, so a good rate is specific to the architecture and even the initialisation |\n| Adam / AdamW | `Δθ ≈ −η·m̂ / (√v̂ + ε)` | m̂ and v̂ are running estimates of the gradient's mean and squared magnitude; the division normalises scale away |\n\n- **Adam steps are about η per parameter** for a consistently signed gradient, regardless of the raw\n  gradient size, and smaller when the gradient flips sign.\n- **So transformer rates cluster in a narrow band.** The Llama 2 report trained its 7B and 13B models\n  with a peak of 3e-4 and the 34B and 70B with 1.5e-4, with cosine decay to 10% of peak.\n- **Weight decay rides on η.** In PyTorch's AdamW and most framework implementations the weight-decay\n  step is multiplied by η (`θ ← θ·(1 − η·λ)`), so raising the learning rate strengthens regularisation at\n  the same time.\n\n### Warmup: why the first steps are special\nRuns start with η near zero and ramp linearly to the peak: over thousands of steps in pretraining, or a\nfew percent of the steps in fine-tuning. Two reasons:\n1. **Adam's statistics are immature.** The second-moment estimate is built from very few samples at the\n   start, so normalisation is unreliable and a full-size step can land anywhere.\n2. **Early gradients are large and uninformative.** In a full fine-tune, a freshly attached, randomly\n   initialised head sends large, essentially random gradients into the pretrained body, which would tear\n   through pretrained features at full rate.\n\nWarmup lets the statistics and the representation settle before committing to large moves. Depth on the\nmechanism lives in `ml-fundamentals/warmup`.\n\n### Decay: why the last steps are special\n- **A fixed rate hits a noise floor.** Minibatch gradients are noisy, so parameters jitter inside a\n  region whose size grows with η, and the loss plateaus above the minimum.\n- **Shrinking η late** (cosine or linear decay to a fraction of peak) lets the parameters settle into the\n  basin.\n- **Constant-then-decay.** Some recent recipes hold the rate constant and decay only in a final phase, so\n  a run can be extended without replaying the schedule.\n- Schedule shapes are covered in `ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers`.\n\n### Scaling with batch size\n- **Larger batches tolerate larger steps,** because averaging more samples reduces gradient noise.\n- **SGD:** the linear scaling rule (multiply η by *k* when the batch grows by *k*, with warmup) is well\n  established.\n- **Adam:** the right exponent is contested; square-root scaling is often recommended.\n- **Either way,** a rate copied from a run with a different batch size is a different hyperparameter.\n\n### Fine-tuning rates\n| Setting | Typical peak (starting point, not a law) | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Full fine-tune of an LLM | low 1e-5 range | every weight moves, and large moves erase pretrained knowledge (see catastrophic forgetting) |\n| LoRA and other adapters | around ten times higher | the trainable set is small and its contribution starts at zero (LoRA's B matrix is zero-initialised), so the update must grow from nothing within the run |\n\n- **The exact ratio is empirical.**\n- **LoRA's α/r interacts with η.** LoRA's output is multiplied by a scaling factor α/r, so changing the\n  rank or α changes the effective step on the model's outputs at the same nominal η. That is one reason\n  a LoRA rate rarely transfers between configurations unchanged.\n\n### One rate or several\n- **Discriminative (layer-wise) rates** give a freshly initialised head a higher rate than the pretrained\n  body, because the head starts from nothing while the body only needs nudging. Optimisers take this as\n  separate parameter groups.\n- **Width-aware parameterisations such as μP** rescale initialisation and per-layer rates so the best\n  rate found on a small model transfers to a wider one. That is how some labs tune large runs without\n  sweeping at full size.\n\n### Finding the rate\n1. **Range test.** Increase η exponentially over a short run and plot loss. The usable region ends just\n   before the loss explodes; the best peak sits somewhat below that edge.\n2. **Coarse grid.** ×3 spacing (1e-5, 3e-5, 1e-4) resolves most fine-tuning questions.\n3. **Read the diagnostics:**\n\n| Symptom | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| spikes or NaN | too high |\n| slow, monotone descent that ends above the baseline | too low |\n| gradient clipping firing on most steps | the rate is leaning on the clip rather than being right |","example":"### The stability bound and Adam's normalisation\nThe bound is easiest to see on the quadratic, and Adam's normalisation by feeding it two gradients that\ndiffer by 1000× and watching the parameter move by the same amount.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef gd(lr, curvature=4.0, theta=1.0, steps=10):\n    # Loss L = 0.5 * curvature * theta**2, gradient = curvature * theta\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        theta = theta - lr * curvature * theta          # = theta * (1 - lr * curvature)\n    return theta\n\nfor lr in (0.05, 0.1, 0.45, 0.5, 0.6):\n    print(f\"lr={lr:<5} factor={1 - lr * 4:+.2f}  theta after 10 steps={gd(lr):+.4f}\")\n# lr=0.05  factor=+0.80  0.1074   slow, monotone\n# lr=0.1   factor=+0.60  0.0060   fast, monotone\n# lr=0.45  factor=-0.80  0.1074   oscillates, still converges\n# lr=0.5   factor=-1.00  1.0000   bounces forever: exactly the 2/curvature limit\n# lr=0.6   factor=-1.40  28.925   diverges\n\n# Adam normalises the gradient scale away, so the first step is ~lr whatever the gradient size.\nw = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.tensor([1.0]))\nopt = torch.optim.Adam([w], lr=1e-3)\nfor scale in (1.0, 1000.0):\n    w.data.fill_(1.0); opt.state.clear()\n    w.grad = torch.tensor([scale])\n    opt.step()\n    print(f\"gradient={scale:>6}: moved by {1.0 - w.item():.6f}\")   # both ~0.001\n```\n\nIn a Hugging Face fine-tune the same ideas appear as `learning_rate=2e-5`, `warmup_ratio=0.03` and\n`lr_scheduler_type=\"cosine\"`; the peak, the ramp and the decay are three settings of one trajectory.\n\n### A learning-rate range test in plain Python\nMinibatch SGD on a one-weight regression, with η ramped exponentially from 1e-4 to 10 and the run stopped\nwhen the smoothed loss explodes. The loss curvature here is λ = 2·E[x²] ≈ 0.67, so theory puts the edge\nnear 2/λ = 3.\n\n```python\nimport math, random\n\nrandom.seed(0)\ndata = [(x, 3 * x + random.gauss(0, 0.1)) for x in (random.uniform(-1, 1) for _ in range(2000))]\n\ndef range_test(lr_min=1e-4, lr_max=10.0, steps=200, batch=16):\n    w, best, smooth, history = 0.0, float(\"inf\"), None, []\n    for i in range(steps):\n        lr = lr_min * (lr_max / lr_min) ** (i / (steps - 1))       # exponential ramp\n        xb = random.sample(data, batch)\n        grad = sum(2 * (w * x - y) * x for x, y in xb) / batch      # d/dw of mean squared error\n        loss = sum((w * x - y) ** 2 for x, y in xb) / batch\n        w -= lr * grad\n        smooth = loss if smooth is None else 0.7 * smooth + 0.3 * loss\n        history.append((lr, smooth))\n        best = min(best, smooth)\n        if smooth > 4 * best or math.isnan(smooth):                 # loss has exploded: stop\n            break\n    return history\n\nedge = range_test()[-1][0]\nprint(f\"loss exploded near lr={edge:.2f}; try a peak around {edge / 10:.2f}\")   # conservative: ~10x below\n# The edge lands between about 3 and 6 depending on the seed (smoothing lags the blow-up).\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: warmup copied from a pretraining config\nA team fine-tunes on 2,400 examples at batch 16 for 2 epochs and copies a fixed warmup of 500 steps.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Steps per epoch | 2,400 ÷ 16 = 150 |\n| Total steps | 300 |\n| Copied warmup | 500 steps: the peak is never reached |\n| `warmup_ratio=0.03` instead | 9 steps, then cosine decay over the remaining 291 |\n\n- **Symptom.** The run \"learns almost nothing\", which looks like a too-low rate.\n- **Fix.** Express warmup as a ratio of total steps for fine-tunes, and recompute it when epochs or\n  dataset size change.","patterns":["**Record the trajectory as one unit.** In every training config and experiment record, store peak `learning_rate`, warmup (`warmup_ratio` or steps), `lr_scheduler_type`, effective batch size and optimiser together. Log the actual learning rate per step (the Hugging Face `Trainer` logs it) and check it matches the plan.","**Short ×3 grid before the full run.** For a new task or parameterisation, run 1e-5, 3e-5 and 1e-4 (or ten times higher for LoRA) for a fraction of the budget and pick on the validation metric. Monitor for spikes at the top of the grid and a flat curve at the bottom.","**Gradient-norm and clip-rate dashboard.** Keep clipping on (`max_grad_norm=1.0` is the Hugging Face default) but chart the pre-clip gradient norm and the fraction of steps clipped. A clip rate near 100% means the rate is too high, not that clipping is working.","**Separate parameter groups for head and body.** When a new head is attached, give it a higher rate: `torch.optim.AdamW([{\"params\": body_params, \"lr\": 1e-5}, {\"params\": head_params, \"lr\": 1e-4}])`. Monitor per-group update norms so the body is not drifting more than intended.","**Spike rollback.** On a loss spike or NaN in a long run, restore the last good checkpoint and resume with a lower peak or longer warmup rather than pushing through. Monitor loss spikes per thousand steps and alert on the first NaN.","**Re-tune on any structural change.** Treat a new batch size, optimiser, LoRA rank or α, or model width as invalidating the old rate; rerun the grid. Track the rate alongside these settings so drift in one is visible."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P{\"What is trained?\"} -->|\"full fine-tune\"| F[\"Start in the low 1e-5 range\"]\n    P -->|\"LoRA or adapters\"| L[\"Start about 10x higher\"]\n    F --> G[\"Range test or x3 grid around the start\"]\n    L --> G\n    G --> T[\"Trajectory: warmup few percent, peak, cosine or linear decay\"]\n    T --> R[\"Short run: loss, gradient norm, clip rate\"]\n    R --> D{\"What does the curve show?\"}\n    D -->|\"spikes or NaN\"| H[\"Lower peak or lengthen warmup\"]\n    D -->|\"slow descent above baseline\"| U[\"Raise peak\"]\n    D -->|\"clip fires on most steps\"| H\n    H --> R\n    U --> R\n    D -->|\"stable, validation improving\"| OK[\"Record peak, warmup, schedule, batch size\"]","caption":"The path to a usable rate: pick a starting band by parameterisation, probe it, then read the curves and\nmove the peak or warmup until neither spikes nor a flat plateau appears."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is the learning rate usually the most sensitive hyperparameter?","a":"- **A gradient gives direction, not distance.** How far downhill continues is set by curvature, and the\n  update `θ ← θ − η·∇L(θ)` only converges on a quadratic when `η < 2/λ`.\n- **One η must serve every direction:** small enough for the sharpest curvature, large enough to progress\n  along flat ones. Too large diverges, too small spends the budget crawling, so small changes in η move\n  the outcome more than most other settings."},{"q":"Why do we warm up the learning rate, and what goes wrong if you copy a warmup from pretraining?","a":"- **Warmup exists because** Adam's second-moment estimate is built from very few samples at the start,\n  and early gradients (for example from a new random head) are large and uninformative.\n- **Copied fixed warmup:** a 500-step warmup on a 300-step fine-tune never reaches the peak, so the run\n  learns almost nothing and looks like a too-low rate.\n- **Fix:** express warmup as a ratio of total steps (for example `warmup_ratio=0.03`) and recompute it when\n  epochs or dataset size change."},{"q":"You double the batch size. Should you change the learning rate?","a":"- **Probably, because larger batches reduce gradient noise and tolerate larger steps.**\n- **SGD:** the linear scaling rule (multiply η by *k* with warmup) is well established.\n- **Adam:** the right exponent is contested, and square-root scaling is often recommended. Either way, treat\n  the old rate as a different hyperparameter and re-run a short grid."},{"q":"Your long run shows a loss spike followed by NaN at step 40,000. What do you do?","a":"- **Do not push through.** Restore the last good checkpoint and resume with a lower peak or a longer\n  warmup.\n- **Diagnose with the gradient norm** and clip rate around the spike: a norm that climbs before the spike,\n  or clipping firing on most steps, says the rate is too high for that phase.\n- **Monitor afterwards:** loss spikes per thousand steps and an alert on the first NaN."},{"q":"Why does AdamW weight decay change when you change the learning rate?","a":"- **In PyTorch's AdamW and most framework implementations** the decay step is multiplied by η:\n  `θ ← θ·(1 − η·λ)`.\n- **So raising η also strengthens regularisation,** and weights can shrink faster than intended, producing\n  underfitting that looks like a data problem.\n- **Practical rule:** when sweeping η, keep the product in mind and re-check λ if the best rate moves a lot."}],"tradeoffs":["**Higher peak vs stability.** A higher peak reaches a lower loss within a fixed budget and often generalises better, but instability grows with it; the balance flips as soon as loss spikes appear, which happens more readily at larger model scale.","**Longer warmup vs lost learning.** It protects against early divergence and feature damage but delays learning; in a short fine-tune of a few hundred steps, a long warmup can consume a large share of the run.","**Decay to near zero vs extensibility.** It gives the best final weights but a finished run cannot be extended; a constant phase with a late decay keeps that option at the cost of a slightly less settled endpoint.","**Low fine-tuning rate vs underfitting.** It preserves pretrained knowledge and reduces forgetting but may underfit the new task; the balance shifts toward a higher rate when the task is far from the pretraining distribution."],"pitfalls":["**Copying a rate across optimisers, batch sizes or parameterisations** (SGD versus AdamW, full fine-tune versus LoRA). Symptom: a run that diverges immediately or one that barely moves in the budget. Fix: re-run a short grid for the new setup.","**Changing step count while the schedule is defined in steps** (epochs, dataset size). Symptom: two \"identical\" configs with different effective rates at the same step and incomparable results. Fix: define warmup as a ratio and log the per-step rate.","**Letting gradient clipping hide an excessive rate.** Symptom: the clip triggers on almost every step while the loss creeps rather than drops. Fix: lower the peak until clipping is occasional.","**Skipping or truncating warmup with Adam.** Symptom: divergence or a permanent loss plateau in the first few hundred steps that no later schedule repairs. Fix: restore a warmup of a few percent.","**Raising the rate without noticing AdamW's decay scales with it.** Symptom: weights shrinking faster than intended and underfitting that looks like a data problem. Fix: re-check weight decay when the best rate moves.","**Reporting \"learning rate 2e-5\" with no warmup, schedule or batch size.** Symptom: nobody, including you, can reproduce the run. Fix: record the whole trajectory with the batch size."],"when":"Every training run sets a learning rate; the decision is how to choose it and its trajectory.\n- **New fine-tuning task:** start from a common rate for your parameterisation, run a short range test or\n  a ×3 grid, and read the loss curve for spikes (too high) or a flat, early plateau (too low).\n- **Pair and record:** a peak with a warmup of a few percent of the steps and a decay that fits the run\n  length; record all three with the batch size.\n- **Measure:** the gradient norm alongside the loss. A healthy run has a stable norm, not one rescued by\n  clipping.\n- **Interviews:** derive the 2/λ bound, explain why Adam makes rates transferable across layers, and say\n  why fine-tuning rates are lower than pretraining rates; that chain distinguishes understanding from a\n  remembered number.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the peak rate, warmup ratio and schedule in the training config, runs the range test or grid, and reads loss and gradient-norm curves to diagnose a rate that is too high or too low.","architect":"Sets the policy that learning rate, warmup, schedule and batch size are recorded together for every run so results are reproducible and comparable across teams and model sizes.","fde":"When a customer's fine-tune on their data diverges or regresses on general ability, the learning rate and its warmup are the first settings to inspect and the easiest to explain."},"check":{"q":"Your full fine-tune at a peak rate of 2e-5 learns the task but regresses on general instruction\nfollowing; your LoRA run at the same 2e-5 learns almost nothing. Explain both outcomes and what you would\nchange.","a":"The full fine-tune moves every weight by about the learning rate per step under AdamW, so even a\nmodest rate accumulated over hundreds of steps shifts the whole network away from the pretrained solution;\nthe regression is forgetting, and the fixes are a lower rate, fewer steps, early stopping on a general\nevaluation, or mixing general data into the set. The LoRA update is the product B·A scaled by α/r, and B\nstarts at zero, so the change to the model begins at exactly zero and can only grow as fast as B's\nentries move, about η per step under Adam. At 2e-5 over a few hundred steps those entries stay tiny and\nthe product barely affects the outputs; adapter training commonly uses rates around ten times higher,\nand the right value also depends on the chosen α and rank. The change is to tune the rate per parameterisation, run a\nquick grid at ×3 spacing for each, and watch both the task metric and the general evaluation rather than\ntraining loss alone."},"quiz":[{"q":"For the loss L = ½λθ² with λ = 4, which learning rate makes plain gradient descent diverge?","options":["0.1","0.25","0.45","0.6"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The update multiplies θ by (1 − ηλ), which must stay inside (−1, 1), so η must be below 2/λ = 0.5. At 0.45 the iterate oscillates but shrinks; at 0.6 the factor is −1.4 and the loss grows every step."},{"q":"Why do Adam learning rates transfer across layers and architectures better than SGD rates?","options":["Adam ignores the gradient and moves every weight by exactly η","Adam divides by a running estimate of gradient magnitude, so the step is roughly η per parameter regardless of raw gradient scale","Adam automatically performs a learning-rate range test during warmup","Adam applies weight decay, which cancels differences in gradient scale"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Normalisation by the second-moment estimate removes the layer-to-layer scale differences that make SGD rates architecture-specific. Adam does not ignore gradients; sign changes and noise still shrink its steps."},{"q":"A bf16 fine-tune shows loss spikes and then NaN within the first 50 steps. Which learning-rate issue is most likely?","options":["The rate is too low, so the loss cannot leave its plateau","The peak rate is too high or the warmup too short, so early unreliable Adam steps are taken at full size","The decay schedule ended too early","Weight decay was set to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Early steps combine the largest gradients with the least reliable second-moment estimates; a full-size step there overshoots sharply curved directions. Decay is irrelevant that early, and a too-low rate produces slowness, not spikes."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/overfitting","sources":["Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, 2009 — The Elements of Statistical Learning","Srivastava et al., 2014 — Dropout: A Simple Way to Prevent Neural Networks from Overfitting","Nakkiran et al., 2019 — Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models and More Data Hurt","Belkin et al., 2019 — Reconciling Modern Machine-Learning Practice and the Classical Bias-Variance Trade-off"],"why":"- **The objective cannot tell rule from rows.** Training asks only \"lower the loss on these examples\".\n  A model with enough capacity can learn the rule or learn the examples; both reach low training loss,\n  only the rule reaches low loss on unseen examples.\n- **Capacity makes memorising possible.** A network with far more parameters than examples can memorise\n  anything.\n- **Memorising is often easier.** Every dataset carries in-set-only signals: an annotator who filed\n  ambiguous tickets under \"billing\", a template whose phrasing differs by class, an id or timestamp that\n  sorts with the answer. Gradient descent has no reason to prefer the rule, and once it is learned, the\n  shortcuts and noise are the only loss left.\n- **Both naive fixes fail.** \"Train until the loss stops falling\" is the recipe for overfitting, because\n  on repeated data training loss falls to zero. \"Use a smaller model\" throws away the capacity that makes\n  the model good at the rule.\n- **What you want:** enough capacity to learn the pattern and a procedure that stops, or is held back,\n  before it learns the noise. Measuring that needs unseen data, which is why overfitting and the\n  train/validation/test split are inseparable.","how":"### The generalisation gap\n1. **Early:** *L_train* (loss on training data) and *L_val* (loss on fresh data from the same\n   distribution) fall together, because the model learns shared structure.\n2. **Later:** *L_train* keeps falling while *L_val* flattens. The difference is the generalisation gap,\n   and it grows with every step that fits something specific to the training rows.\n3. **What widens it:** the ratio of effective capacity to effective data. More trainable parameters, more\n   steps, higher learning rate and less diverse data all widen the gap.\n4. **The noise floor.** Label noise sets a floor on achievable *L_val*; a model that goes below it on\n   training data has memorised the noise.\n\n### How it looks in LLM fine-tuning\n- **Held-out loss turns up after one or two passes** while training loss keeps dropping, often with a\n  staircase: an abrupt training-loss drop at each epoch boundary, because the model now predicts rows it\n  has already seen.\n- **Verbatim reproduction.** The model reproduces training completions, including names, numbers and\n  boilerplate, and can be prompted to regurgitate training records: a privacy incident as well as a\n  modelling failure.\n- **Style collapse.** Outputs converge on one style or length regardless of the prompt, because the\n  fine-tuning set had one.\n- **Paraphrase brittleness.** Accuracy is high on prompts phrased like the training data and collapses on\n  paraphrases.\n- **Loss and metric can disagree.** It is common for held-out loss to rise while the task metric is flat\n  or improving, because loss penalises every token including stylistic ones. Pick checkpoints on the\n  metric you ship, with loss as the early-warning signal.\n\n### Levers, and why each one works\n| Lever | How | Why it works |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Data** | more examples, deduplication, cleaner labels | memorisation becomes harder than the rule; duplicates stop being seen several times per epoch; cleaner labels lower the noise floor |\n| **Steps** | fewer epochs, lower learning rate, early stopping | limits how far the model travels from the pretrained prior, stopping after shared structure and before particulars |\n| **Capacity** | parameter-efficient fine-tuning (low-rank adapters, a few trainable layers); a smaller model, more bluntly | constrains the update to a small subspace |\n| **Regularisation** | weight decay, dropout, label smoothing, mixing in general-purpose data | penalises large weights, forces redundancy, stops probabilities being driven to exactly one, dilutes row-specific shortcuts |\n\n- **Data is the only lever here that raises the ceiling** as well as closing the gap. A stronger base\n  model can raise the ceiling too, but that is a model choice, not an overfitting remedy.\n- Regularisation details are in `ml-fundamentals/dropout-regularization`.\n\n### Diagnosing with a learning curve\n1. **Retrain on 25%, 50% and 100%** of the training set and plot the best validation score against data\n   size.\n2. **Steep improvement and a narrowing gap:** the model is variance-limited; more (diverse) data is the\n   lever worth paying for.\n3. **Flat validation with train and validation close:** more of the same data will not help; the limit\n   is label noise, task definition or model capacity.\n4. **Why it matters:** this is the bias-variance decomposition turned into an experiment. It replaces an\n   argument about whether to label another 5,000 rows with a chart.\n\n### What overfitting is not\n| Condition | Signature | Implication |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Underfitting** | both losses high and close together | model lacks capacity or steps |\n| **Distribution shift** | gap between validation and production, not training and validation | more training on the same data will not help |\n| **Contamination or leakage** | held-out data duplicates training data | makes overfitting invisible rather than curing it |\n| **Catastrophic forgetting** | losing capabilities the fine-tuning set never measured | often accompanies overfitting, but diagnosed on a different evaluation |\n\n### A caveat on the classic picture\n- **The U-curve is not the whole story.** The textbook curve (error falls, then rises with capacity)\n  does not fully describe modern networks.\n- **Double descent.** Heavily over-parameterised models can fit training data exactly and still\n  generalise well, and test error can fall again past the interpolation point.\n- **Practical consequence:** do not rely on a rule of thumb about model size; rely on the held-out curve\n  for the model and data in front of you.","example":"### Worked scenario: ten epochs on 2,000 support replies\nIllustrative numbers. A team fine-tunes a 7B instruct model on 2,000 support replies for ten epochs at a\nmodest learning rate and evaluates after each epoch:\n\n| epoch | train loss | val loss | task metric (human-rated usefulness, %) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1.42 | 1.38 | 71 |\n| 2 | 1.10 | 1.21 | 78 |\n| 3 | 0.95 | 1.18 | 80 |\n| 4 | 0.82 | 1.19 | 80 |\n| 5 | 0.70 | 1.25 | 78 |\n| 8 | 0.41 | 1.52 | 69 |\n| 10 | 0.28 | 1.71 | 63 |\n\n- **Training loss falls monotonically** all the way.\n- **Validation loss bottoms at epoch 3,** and the task metric plateaus across epochs 3 and 4 before\n  falling.\n- **By epoch 10** the model answers training-set questions word for word and gives a boilerplate reply\n  to anything else.\n- **The verdict:** the right checkpoint is epoch 3 or 4, and the run should have stopped around epoch 6.\n\n### Early-stopping monitor\nThe rule from the scenario as code: stop after three evaluations without improvement and reload the best\ncheckpoint.\n\n```python\nclass EarlyStopping:\n    \"\"\"Stop when the validation metric has not improved by min_delta for `patience` evaluations.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, patience=3, min_delta=0.005, mode=\"min\"):\n        self.patience, self.min_delta = patience, min_delta\n        self.sign = 1 if mode == \"min\" else -1\n        self.best, self.best_step, self.bad_evals = None, None, 0\n\n    def update(self, value, step) -> bool:\n        improved = self.best is None or self.sign * (self.best - value) > self.min_delta\n        if improved:\n            self.best, self.best_step, self.bad_evals = value, step, 0\n        else:\n            self.bad_evals += 1\n        return self.bad_evals >= self.patience      # True -> stop and reload best_step's checkpoint\n\nstop = EarlyStopping(patience=3)\nfor epoch, val_loss in enumerate([1.38, 1.21, 1.18, 1.19, 1.25, 1.33, 1.44, 1.52, 1.61, 1.71], start=1):\n    if stop.update(val_loss, step=epoch):\n        print(f\"stop at epoch {epoch}; best was epoch {stop.best_step} (val {stop.best})\")\n        break\n# -> stop at epoch 6; best was epoch 3 (val 1.18)\n```\n\n### Verbatim-overlap check before release\nFlags held-out generations that copy a long word span from any training target, the memorisation\nsymptom that loss curves do not show. A flag is a prompt for review, not proof of harm.\n\n```python\ndef ngrams(text, n):\n    words = text.lower().split()\n    return {tuple(words[i:i + n]) for i in range(len(words) - n + 1)}\n\ndef verbatim_rate(generations, train_targets, n=8):\n    \"\"\"Share of generations that copy at least one n-word span from any training target.\"\"\"\n    seen = set().union(*(ngrams(t, n) for t in train_targets))\n    flagged = [g for g in generations if ngrams(g, n) & seen]\n    return len(flagged) / len(generations), flagged\n\ntrain_targets = [\n    \"Thanks for reaching out. I have refunded the duplicate charge to your card ending 4417 today.\",\n    \"Please clear your browser cache and sign in again; the invoice page should then load normally.\",\n]\ngenerations = [  # model outputs on held-out prompts\n    \"Thanks for reaching out. I have refunded the duplicate charge to your card ending 4417 today.\",\n    \"You can export your data from Settings, then Account, then Download archive.\",\n    \"Please clear your browser cache and sign in again; that usually fixes a blank login page.\",\n]\nrate, flagged = verbatim_rate(generations, train_targets)\nprint(f\"verbatim rate = {rate:.0%}\")          # 67%: two of three held-out answers copy training text\nfor g in flagged:\n    print(\"COPIED:\", g[:60])\n```","patterns":["**Best-checkpoint selection on the shipped metric.** Evaluate at a fixed cadence, save at each evaluation and reload the best (in Hugging Face, `load_best_model_at_end=True` with `metric_for_best_model` set to your task metric). Monitor the epoch or step of the best checkpoint; if it is always the first, cut epochs or the learning rate.","**Deduplicate before training and across splits.** Remove exact duplicates by hashing normalised text and near-duplicates with a similarity method such as MinHash, then check that no validation row has a near-copy in training. Monitor the duplicate rate per data release.","**Verbatim-overlap gate.** Run an n-gram overlap check (as above) between held-out generations and training targets as part of release evaluation, with extra scrutiny on records containing personal data. Monitor the flagged share per model version and block release on any PII copy.","**Learning curve before buying labels.** Before commissioning another labeling batch, retrain on 25%, 50% and 100% of the data and plot validation against size. Fund more data only if the curve is still rising steeply.","**Paraphrase and general-capability evals.** Keep a held-out set of paraphrased prompts and a small general-skills set alongside the task set. Monitor the gap between original-phrasing and paraphrase accuracy, which widens as the model memorises surface form."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Train, validation and production scores\"] --> L{\"Validation set leak-free and deduplicated?\"}\n    L -->|\"no\"| FX[\"Re-split by group, dedupe across splits, re-measure\"]\n    FX --> S\n    L -->|\"yes\"| A{\"Both losses high and close?\"}\n    A -->|\"yes\"| U[\"Underfitting: more capacity or steps\"]\n    A -->|\"no\"| B{\"Train falling, validation rising?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| O[\"Overfitting\"]\n    B -->|\"no\"| C{\"Validation fine, production low?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| DS[\"Distribution shift: fresh data, temporal split\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| OK[\"Healthy: keep watching the gap\"]\n    O --> LC[\"Learning curve on 25, 50, 100 percent of data\"]\n    LC -->|\"still rising steeply\"| MD[\"More diverse data\"]\n    LC -->|\"flat\"| ST[\"Fewer steps, lower LR, PEFT, regularise\"]","caption":"A diagnostic path: compare the three scores first, because the gap that is large tells you whether the\nproblem is underfitting, overfitting or something that only looks like it."},"interview":[{"q":"What is overfitting, and why can a model with low training loss still be bad?","a":"- **Definition:** fitting the particulars of the training rows (label noise, phrasing, in-set-only\n  shortcuts) instead of the transferable pattern, so training loss falls while held-out loss stalls and\n  then rises.\n- **Why low training loss proves little:** the objective cannot tell rule from rows. Both reach low\n  training loss; only the rule reaches low loss on unseen examples, which is why held-out data is required."},{"q":"Validation loss rises after epoch 2 but your task metric keeps improving until epoch 4. Which checkpoint do you ship?","a":"- **Usually the one that is best on the metric you ship,** because token loss penalises every token,\n  including stylistic ones that do not affect usefulness.\n- **Treat the rising loss as an early warning:** check paraphrase accuracy and verbatim overlap on the\n  later checkpoint, since memorised phrasing often shows up there first.\n- **Set it up so this is automatic:** `metric_for_best_model` set to the task metric with best-checkpoint\n  loading."},{"q":"Your team wants to label another 5,000 rows to fix overfitting. How do you decide if it is worth it?","a":"- **Run a learning curve:** retrain on 25%, 50% and 100% of the current data and plot the best validation\n  score against size.\n- **Steep improvement and a narrowing gap** means the model is variance-limited, and more diverse data pays.\n- **Flat validation with train and validation close** means more of the same data will not help; the limit\n  is label noise, task definition or model capacity."},{"q":"When would you not reach for more regularisation?","a":"- **When the gap is between validation and production,** not training and validation: that is\n  distribution shift, which needs fresh data and a temporal validation set.\n- **When both losses are high and close:** that is underfitting, and regularisation adds more bias.\n- **When the validation set leaks:** fix the split first, because leakage makes overfitting invisible\n  rather than curing it."},{"q":"How would you check that a fine-tuned support model is not regurgitating customer data?","a":"- **Run a verbatim-overlap check:** compare held-out generations against training targets for long shared\n  n-gram spans (8 words in the example) and review every flag.\n- **Probe with prefixes** of training records that contain personal data and see whether the model\n  completes names, addresses or account numbers.\n- **Prevent it:** fewer epochs, deduplication, PII redaction before training, and a release gate that\n  blocks any PII copy."}],"tradeoffs":["**Less variance vs more bias.** Regularisation and fewer steps reduce variance but add bias; pushed too far they produce underfitting, so the balance flips when training and validation loss both sit high together.","**Early stopping vs validation quality.** It is nearly free but only as good as the validation set; with a leaky or tiny validation set it stops at the wrong place, and the real cost moves to building that set properly.","**PEFT vs learning genuinely new tasks.** Parameter-efficient fine-tuning limits how much the model can memorise, but equally how much of a new task it can learn; for tasks far from pretraining, full fine-tuning with more data wins.","**More data vs cost.** Among the levers, more data is the only one that improves the achievable ceiling, but it costs labelling time, and synthetic data that shares one template can add rows while adding little diversity."],"pitfalls":["**Judging a run by training loss.** Symptom: a proud \"loss went to 0.1\" followed by a model that cannot handle a rephrased question. Fix: judge on held-out loss and the task metric.","**A validation set that near-duplicates training rows.** Symptom: validation loss tracking training loss downward forever, with overfitting surfacing only in production. Fix: deduplicate across splits.","**Stopping on the wrong signal.** Stopping on token loss when the task metric matters, or the reverse. Symptom: a checkpoint one or two epochs away from the best one on the metric customers see. Fix: select on the shipped metric, watch loss as the early warning.","**Mistaking distribution shift for overfitting.** Adding regularisation does not help. Symptom: validation numbers stay fine while production keeps degrading. Fix: fresh production data and a temporal validation set.","**Tuning dozens of hyperparameters on one validation set.** Symptom: a validation score that improves with every experiment and a test score that does not, because you have overfit the validation set. Fix: read the untouched test set once, and refresh it when consumed.","**Fine-tuning on personal-data records for many epochs.** Symptom: the model regurgitates names, addresses or account numbers verbatim when prompted with a prefix. Fix: redact PII before training, fewer epochs, and a verbatim-overlap release gate."],"when":"Overfitting is not something you choose; every sufficiently long run does it, so the decision is how to\ndetect and bound it.\n- **Always:** train with a held-out set that respects the unit of generalisation, evaluate at least once\n  per epoch, save a checkpoint at each evaluation and keep the best one.\n- **Start small:** few epochs and a low learning rate; lengthen only if validation is still improving.\n- **Reach for data first:** more or cleaner data before regularisation knobs, and a smaller trainable\n  footprint when data is scarce. A learning curve over data fractions tells you whether more data pays.\n- **Measure:** the train-validation gap at the chosen checkpoint, the task metric on held-out data, and a\n  verbatim-overlap check between generations and training targets.\n- **Day to day:** the gap between training and validation curves is the first chart in any run review.\n- **Interviews:** explain the gap, the levers, and why the classic U-curve is only a guide for modern\n  networks.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires held-out evaluation, early stopping and best-checkpoint loading into the training job and reads the train/validation gap before believing any loss number.","architect":"Sets the policy that every fine-tuned model ships with a validation curve, a held-out task metric and a data-deduplication step, so overfit models are caught before deployment.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a model trained longer on their 500 examples got worse, and why the cure is more diverse examples and an honest evaluation set rather than more epochs."},"check":{"q":"A fine-tuned classifier shows 99% training accuracy, 97% validation accuracy, and 81% accuracy\nin production two weeks after launch. Is this overfitting, and what would you check before changing the\ntraining recipe?","a":"The small train-validation gap argues against classic overfitting; the big validation-production\ngap points elsewhere. Check first whether the validation set shares sources with training (same users,\ndocuments or templates), since leakage would hide overfitting and make 97% meaningless; a grouped\nre-split answers that. Then compare the production input distribution with the training data: new\ncustomers, new product names or a changed upstream pipeline mean distribution shift, which more\nregularisation will not fix and which calls for fresh data and a temporal validation set. Only if a\nleak-free validation set also drops would I treat it as overfitting and reduce epochs, add data or\nconstrain the trainable parameters."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which pattern in the training curves most clearly indicates overfitting?","options":["Training and validation loss both high and close together","Training loss falling steadily while validation loss has turned upward","Validation loss falling faster than training loss","Both losses falling together throughout the run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A widening gap with validation loss rising means the model is fitting particulars of the training rows that do not transfer. Both losses high and close is underfitting, and both falling together is healthy."},{"q":"A 2,000-example fine-tune overfits after two epochs. Which single change most directly raises the achievable quality rather than just closing the gap?","options":["Training for more epochs with a lower learning rate","Raising the LoRA rank so the adapter has more trainable capacity","Adding more deduplicated, diverse training examples","Increasing weight decay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"More diverse data makes the rule cheaper to learn than the rows and lowers the noise floor, improving the ceiling. Weight decay and a lower rate only trade variance for bias, and a higher LoRA rank adds trainable capacity without adding information, which makes memorising the 2,000 rows easier, not harder."},{"q":"The validation set contains near-duplicates of training examples. What happens to overfitting detection?","options":["Overfitting is detected earlier because duplicates are easier to fit","Nothing changes as long as the test set is clean","Validation loss tracks training loss downward, so overfitting becomes invisible until production","The model cannot overfit because duplicates act as regularisation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memorised rows score well on their near-copies, so the gap that reveals overfitting never appears. A clean test set would show the truth, but only once, at the end, after every decision was already made."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","sources":["McCloskey & Cohen, 1989 — Catastrophic Interference in Connectionist Networks: The Sequential Learning Problem","Kirkpatrick et al., 2017 — Overcoming Catastrophic Forgetting in Neural Networks","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training Language Models to Follow Instructions with Human Feedback","Qi et al., 2023 — Fine-tuning Aligned Language Models Compromises Safety, Even When Users Do Not Intend To!"],"why":"- **One shared set of weights.** There is no region for \"French\", one for \"SQL\" and one for \"refusing\n  dangerous requests\"; the same matrices participate in all of them.\n- **The new gradient has no term for old tasks.** Training on a new dataset computes the gradient of the\n  new loss alone, so any weight the new task wants to move will move, however important it was to\n  something the loss cannot see.\n- **Scale does not remove it.** It was named in the connectionist literature in the late 1980s as the\n  sequential learning problem. Some studies find larger models forget less per step, others the opposite,\n  so treat model size as no protection. A small dataset repeated for several epochs at a healthy learning\n  rate drags the whole network toward those rows.\n- **For LLMs, what you forget is the product.** InstructGPT reported an \"alignment tax\": RLHF models\n  regressed on public NLP benchmarks, and the authors mixed pretraining gradients back in to reduce it.\n  Later work showed that fine-tuning an aligned chat model on benign task data can erode refusals, even\n  when nobody intended that.\n- **\"My task evaluation went up, ship it\"** is exactly how these regressions reach production: the metric\n  that would have caught them was never run.","how":"### The mechanism in one line\n- **Setup.** Let θ* be the weights after pretraining and alignment. Fine-tuning minimises *L_new(θ)*,\n  moving θ along directions that reduce *L_new*. Where those directions also raise *L_old*, the old loss\n  rises and nothing in the update notices.\n- **Curvature picture.** Directions in which *L_old* is sharply curved matter to the old tasks (the\n  Fisher information approximates this curvature). Near the old optimum, where the old gradient is\n  roughly zero, a displacement δ along such a direction costs roughly `½·F·δ²` of old loss.\n- **So forgetting depends on three things:**\n\n| Factor | Controlled by |\n|---|---|\n| how far you move | steps × learning rate |\n| in which directions | what the data asks for |\n| how sharp the old loss is there | the old capabilities themselves |\n\n### Mitigation families\n1. **Replay (rehearsal).** Mix data representing the old behaviour into the fine-tuning set so the loss\n   has a term for it. InstructGPT's PPO-ptx variant is this idea with pretraining data. In practice teams\n   mix a share of general instruction, chat-formatted and safety examples into a task fine-tune, tuning\n   the proportion on the regression suite. Limit: you need representative data, and for a closed base\n   model you only have proxies.\n2. **Constrain the move.** A lower learning rate, fewer epochs and early stopping judged on the general\n   suite, not only the task, keep the model near θ*. Explicit distance penalties:\n   - elastic weight consolidation weights each parameter's displacement by its Fisher importance,\n     `λ/2 · Σ_i F_i (θ_i − θ*_i)²`;\n   - L2-SP uses a plain quadratic;\n   - RLHF and DPO carry a KL term to a reference policy, the same role at the level of outputs.\n\n   At LLM scale the learning-rate and epoch controls plus the KL term are what is actually used;\n   Fisher-weighted penalties are rare.\n3. **Isolate the update.** LoRA and other adapters freeze the base weights and train a small set of new\n   parameters. The base is untouched by construction, the adapter can be removed or swapped per task, and\n   the low rank limits how far outputs move.\n   - **Reduces, does not prevent.** Behaviour still changes wherever the adapter is active, and a\n     high-rank adapter trained hard forgets too.\n   - **The trade runs both ways.** Comparisons of LoRA against full fine-tuning on code and maths found\n     LoRA forgets less of the source domain and also learns less of the target: the constraint that\n     protects old skills caps new ones.\n4. **Average back.** Interpolating the fine-tuned weights with θ* (weight-space ensembling, model\n   merging) recovers much of the old behaviour while keeping most of the task gain. Choose the mixing\n   coefficient on the two evaluations; see `training-adaptation/model-merging`.\n5. **Measure it.** None of the above is a guarantee. Run a fixed general-capability suite (instruction\n   following, a reasoning subset, format and chat-template adherence, refusal behaviour on a harmful\n   prompt set) before and after every fine-tune, and block release on regression beyond a tolerance.\n\n### What usually gets forgotten first\nBehaviours rare in the fine-tuning data and sharply defined in the original. Each is invisible to a\ntask-only evaluation and obvious to a user.\n\n| Training on | Tends to lose |\n|---|---|\n| raw completions | the chat template and system-prompt handling |\n| compliant answers | refusals |\n| one language | multilingual ability |\n| prose | tool-call formatting |\n\n### Continued pretraining forgets more\n- **Bigger displacement.** Continued pretraining on a domain corpus (legal text, a new language, a\n  codebase) runs far more tokens than a task fine-tune, usually full-parameter, so it moves further from\n  θ* and forgets more.\n- **The common recipe.** Re-warm the learning rate to a peak below the original pretraining peak, decay\n  it again, and keep a share of general pretraining-style text in the mix as replay.\n- **The shift is the point.** Moving the model's distribution is the goal; the question is how much\n  general ability you will trade, which the regression suite answers.\n\n### Sequential fine-tunes compound\n- **Chaining forgets per hop.** Fine-tuning v2 from v1 on the second batch, then v3 from v2, forgets a\n  little of each earlier batch at every hop.\n- **Safer:** fine-tune from the base model on the union of all data each time, which also makes the run\n  reproducible.","example":"### Replay mix plus a regression gate\nA team fine-tunes an instruct model on 5,000 legal-clause extraction examples.\n- **Attempt 1** (full fine-tune, three epochs, task data only) lifts the extraction metric, but the\n  general evaluation shows lower instruction following and a drop in refusal rate on harmful prompts.\n- **Attempt 2** makes general instruction data a quarter of the training mix, shuffled through every part\n  of the run, trains LoRA adapters for two epochs at an ordinary adapter rate, and gates on the suite.\n- The helpers (`load_jsonl`, `run_suite`, `finetune`) stand in for the team's pipeline functions.\n\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(0)\n\ntask = load_jsonl(\"legal_clauses.jsonl\")            # 5,000 new-task examples\ngeneral = load_jsonl(\"general_chat_sample.jsonl\")   # open instruction/chat data: the replay buffer\n\nREPLAY = 0.25                                       # general data as a share of the mixed set\nn_replay = int(len(task) * REPLAY / (1 - REPLAY))   # 1,666 rows so general = 25% of the total\nmixed = task + random.sample(general, n_replay)\nrandom.shuffle(mixed)                               # interleave; never train the sources in blocks\n\nSUITE = [\"ifeval_subset\", \"gsm8k_subset\", \"chat_template_adherence\", \"refusal_rate_harmful\"]\n\ndef gate(before: dict, after: dict, max_drop: float = 0.02):\n    regressions = {k: (before[k], after[k]) for k in before if after[k] < before[k] - max_drop}\n    assert not regressions, f\"general capability regressed: {regressions}\"\n\nbefore = run_suite(base_model, SUITE)\nmodel = finetune(base_model, mixed, lr=1e-4, epochs=2, lora_r=16, eval_every_steps=100)\nafter = run_suite(model, SUITE)\ngate(before, after)                                 # fails the release if any metric drops > 2 points\n```\n\n- **Starting points, not constants.** The replay share, the rank and the two-point tolerance are tuned on\n  the suite.\n- **The key design choice:** the suite runs before training too. A drop can only be recognised against\n  the base model's own number.\n\n### Averaging back toward the base\nWhen a strong task checkpoint has already regressed, interpolate its weights with the base and pick the\nmixing coefficient on both evaluations. This works for a full fine-tune of the same architecture; the two\nstate dicts must share keys and shapes.\n\n- **For a LoRA adapter,** scale the adapter's contribution by alpha instead: interpolating the merged\n  weights toward the base gives base plus alpha times the adapter delta.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef interpolate(base_sd, ft_sd, alpha):\n    \"\"\"alpha=0 -> base model, alpha=1 -> fine-tuned model.\"\"\"\n    return {k: (1 - alpha) * base_sd[k] + alpha * ft_sd[k] if base_sd[k].is_floating_point() else ft_sd[k]\n            for k in base_sd}\n\nbase_sd = torch.load(\"base.pt\", map_location=\"cpu\")\nft_sd = torch.load(\"finetuned.pt\", map_location=\"cpu\")\nfor alpha in (0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0):\n    model.load_state_dict(interpolate(base_sd, ft_sd, alpha))\n    print(alpha, run_suite(model, SUITE), run_task_eval(model))   # pick alpha on both\n```\n\nIllustrative sweep (numbers are for shape only, not a benchmark):\n\n| alpha | task metric | general suite (avg) | refusal rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.0 (base) | 52 | 71 | 0.96 |\n| 0.5 | 78 | 69 | 0.95 |\n| 0.75 | 82 | 66 | 0.88 |\n| 1.0 (fine-tuned) | 84 | 60 | 0.79 |\n\n- **Reading it.** If the gate allows a 2-point drop, alpha = 0.5 passes (general −2, refusal −1) and keeps\n  26 of the 32 points of task gain; 0.75 and 1.0 fail. Use it as a stopgap while a proper replay run is\n  prepared.","patterns":["**Before/after regression gate in CI.** Fix a general suite (instruction following, a reasoning subset, chat-template adherence, refusal rate on harmful prompts), record the base model's numbers, and fail promotion when any metric drops beyond a tolerance (the example uses 2 points as a start). Monitor the per-metric deltas for every release.","**Replay share in the data mix.** Add general instruction, chat and safety examples to every task fine-tune, shuffled through the whole run, starting around a quarter of the mix and tuning on the suite. Monitor the general-suite score against the task gain as you vary the share.","**Train in the serving chat template.** Format all training rows, replay included, with the same template the model is served with (for Hugging Face tokenizers, `apply_chat_template`), including system prompts. Monitor a template-adherence and system-prompt-following eval.","**Retrain from base on the union.** For recurring releases, fine-tune from the base model on all versioned data each time instead of chaining from the last release. Monitor scores on each earlier batch's held-out set so erosion is visible.","**Interpolation as a fallback.** When a good task checkpoint regresses, sweep the interpolation coefficient toward the base (for example 0.25, 0.5, 0.75) and ship the largest one that passes the gate. Monitor both the task metric and the gate at each coefficient.","**Continued-pretraining recipe.** For domain continued pretraining, re-warm the learning rate to a peak below the original pretraining peak, decay it again, and keep general text as replay. Monitor general benchmark scores at each checkpoint, not only domain perplexity."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Base model, fixed weights\"] --> S0[\"Run general suite: baseline numbers\"]\n    T[(\"Task data\")] --> M[\"Mixed set: task plus about 25 percent replay, shuffled\"]\n    R[(\"General, chat-template and safety data\")] --> M\n    M --> F[\"Fine-tune from base: LoRA or low LR, few epochs\"]\n    B --> F\n    F --> E[\"Evaluate task metric and general suite at each checkpoint\"]\n    E --> G{\"Any suite metric dropped beyond tolerance?\"}\n    S0 -.->|\"compare against baseline\"| G\n    G -->|\"no\"| SH[\"Promote\"]\n    G -->|\"yes, task checkpoint strong\"| I[\"Interpolate toward base, pick alpha on both evals\"]\n    I --> G\n    G -->|\"yes, still failing\"| RR[\"Raise replay share, lower LR or epochs, re-run from base\"]\n    RR --> F","caption":"A guarded fine-tune: the general suite is measured on the base before training, replay and a bounded\nupdate keep old behaviour in the loss, and the gate decides between shipping, interpolating back or\nre-running."},"interview":[{"q":"What causes catastrophic forgetting, and why does a bigger model not solve it?","a":"- **Cause:** every capability shares one set of weights, and the fine-tuning gradient has no term for the\n  old tasks, so any weight the new loss wants to move will move, however important it was elsewhere.\n- **Scale is no protection:** some studies find larger models forget less per step and others the\n  opposite. A small dataset repeated for several epochs at a healthy rate still drags the network toward\n  those rows."},{"q":"How would you design the evaluation for a fine-tune so forgetting cannot reach production?","a":"- **Fix a general suite first:** instruction following, a reasoning subset, chat-template adherence and\n  refusal rate on a harmful prompt set.\n- **Run it on the base model before training,** because a drop can only be recognised against the base's\n  own numbers, then at every checkpoint alongside the task metric.\n- **Gate promotion** on a per-metric tolerance (2 points is a reasonable starting value) and record the\n  deltas for every release."},{"q":"Does training LoRA adapters instead of the full model prevent forgetting?","a":"- **It reduces it, it does not prevent it.** The base weights are frozen and the adapter is removable, but\n  behaviour still changes wherever the adapter is active.\n- **High rank plus many epochs forgets too,** and shows regressions similar to a full fine-tune.\n- **The trade runs both ways:** comparisons on code and maths found LoRA forgets less of the source domain\n  and also learns less of the target."},{"q":"When would you pick weight interpolation over a replay re-run?","a":"- **Interpolation is the quick fallback:** when a strong task checkpoint already exists and has regressed,\n  sweep alpha (0.25, 0.5, 0.75) toward the base and ship the largest value that passes the gate.\n- **Replay is the proper fix,** because it attacks the cause by giving the loss a term for the old\n  behaviour; plan it as the next run.\n- **Interpolation needs matching weights:** a full fine-tune of the same architecture, with state dicts\n  that share keys and shapes. For a LoRA adapter, scaling its contribution by alpha does the same job."},{"q":"A customer chains monthly fine-tunes from last month's model. What breaks and what do you recommend?","a":"- **What breaks:** each hop forgets a little of every earlier batch, so first-month capabilities erode\n  quietly release by release, and no run is reproducible from scratch.\n- **Recommendation:** fine-tune from the base model on the union of all versioned data each release, and\n  monitor scores on each earlier batch's held-out set so any remaining erosion is visible."}],"tradeoffs":["**Replay vs cost and coverage.** It costs training tokens and needs data that represents the old behaviour; for a closed base model, open instruction sets are a proxy that protects general skills but not the vendor's specifics.","**Small moves vs underfitting.** A lower learning rate and fewer steps reduce forgetting but may underfit; the balance tips toward more movement when the task is far from pretraining and old capabilities matter less.","**Adapters vs deep changes.** Adapters bound the damage and keep the base swappable, but a task that needs deep changes may need full fine-tuning with heavier replay instead.","**Pull toward the reference vs task gain.** A KL penalty or weight-space pull keeps behaviour close but caps the reward or task gain; raising the penalty trades task quality for retention.","**Fisher penalties vs simplicity.** They are principled but need an extra pass to estimate importance, and are rarely worth it at LLM scale when replay and early stopping achieve the same retention."],"pitfalls":["**Evaluating only on the task.** Symptom: a model that aces the fine-tuning benchmark and fails users on anything adjacent, discovered after release. Fix: a general suite before and after.","**Training on raw completions without the chat template.** Symptom: the model ignores system prompts or produces malformed turns after the fine-tune. Fix: format every row with the serving template.","**Fine-tuning on compliant answers only.** Symptom: a measurable rise in compliance with harmful requests even though the data was benign. Fix: include safety examples and a refusal eval.","**Chaining fine-tunes v1 → v2 → v3.** Symptom: capabilities from the first batch quietly erode with each release. Fix: retrain from base on the union.","**Treating LoRA as a guarantee.** Symptom: a high-rank adapter trained for many epochs shows the same regressions as a full fine-tune. Fix: gate adapter runs on the same suite as full fine-tunes.","**Diagnosing forgetting as overfitting.** Adding regularisation does not help. Symptom: the task metric drops while the general metric does not recover, because the old data is still absent from the loss. Fix: add replay so the loss has a term for the old behaviour."],"when":"Treat forgetting as a risk to manage on every fine-tune, including small adapter runs.\n- **Before training:** fix the general suite and record the base model's numbers.\n- **During training:** evaluate on both task and suite at the same cadence.\n- **After training:** gate on the suite.\n- **Order of mitigations:** replay first, because it attacks the cause; then a lower learning rate, fewer\n  epochs and adapters; then weight averaging when a good task checkpoint regressed and you want most of\n  it back cheaply. Skip heavier machinery unless the suite shows it is needed.\n- **Day to day:** \"what did we lose\" is as routine a question as \"what did we gain\" in a fine-tune\n  review.\n- **Interviews:** explain the mechanism (no loss term for the old tasks) and the ordered set of\n  mitigations; that is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the data mix with a replay share, runs the general suite before and after, and implements the regression gate that blocks a fine-tune from being promoted.","architect":"Owns the standing general-capability and safety suite every fine-tuned model must pass, and the rule that fine-tunes start from the base on the union of data rather than chaining.","fde":"Shows a customer the before/after suite results alongside the task gain, so the decision to deploy weighs what the model lost in their environment and not only what it learned."},"check":{"q":"A customer fine-tuned a chat model on 8,000 of their internal Q&A pairs and reports it now answers\ntheir questions well but \"feels dumber\", occasionally ignores the system prompt and answers questions it\npreviously refused. Explain what happened and lay out a recovery plan.","a":"The fine-tune optimised only the Q&A loss, so weights supporting general instruction following,\nsystem-prompt handling and refusal behaviour were overwritten; the internal data likely lacked system\nprompts and refusals entirely, so those behaviours had no gradient keeping them in place. Recovery:\nre-run from the base model, not from the damaged checkpoint; format the data with the chat template and\nsystem prompts; mix in a share of general instruction data plus a safety set so the loss has a term for\nthe behaviours to keep; train adapters at a lower rate for fewer epochs with evaluation on a general suite\nat every checkpoint; and gate deployment on that suite. If a strong task checkpoint already exists,\ninterpolating it toward the base can recover much of the lost behaviour quickly while the proper run is\nprepared."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the root cause of catastrophic forgetting during fine-tuning?","options":["The model's parameter budget is full, so new knowledge must evict old knowledge","The fine-tuning loss has no term for the old tasks, so gradients freely move weights those tasks depended on","Adam's momentum carries the model past the optimum of the new task","The tokenizer vocabulary is changed by the new data"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shared weights are updated to reduce only the new loss; capacity is rarely the constraint, and neither momentum nor tokenisation explains why old capabilities degrade while new ones improve."},{"q":"Which mitigation requires access to data representative of the capabilities you want to keep?","options":["Lowering the learning rate","Training a low-rank adapter instead of the full model","Replay, mixing general data into the fine-tuning set","Early stopping on the task metric"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replay works by giving the loss a term for the old behaviour, which needs examples of it; the other options constrain the update without needing old data, and early stopping on the task metric alone does not detect forgetting at all."},{"q":"A team ships monthly fine-tunes, each trained from the previous month's model on that month's new data. What should change?","options":["Increase the learning rate so each month's data is learned faster","Train each release from the base model on the union of all months' data, with a general regression suite","Drop the evaluation suite because the task data is growing","Use a larger rank for the adapters each month"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chained fine-tunes forget a little of every earlier batch per hop; starting from the base on all data removes the compounding, makes runs reproducible, and the suite catches whatever is still lost."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/checkpointing","sources":["Mohan et al., 2021 — CheckFreq: Frequent, Fine-Grained DNN Checkpointing","Rajbhandari et al., 2020 — ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Official docs: PyTorch torch.save / Distributed Checkpoint; Hugging Face Trainer"],"why":"- **Long runs on failing hardware.** A single-GPU fine-tune runs for hours on a spot instance that can be\n  reclaimed at any minute; a pretraining run occupies thousands of accelerators for weeks, where hardware\n  faults are routine. The Llama 3 report counts hundreds of job interruptions in a 54-day pretraining\n  window, most of them unexpected.\n- **Without checkpoints, cost explodes.** Every failure restarts from zero. With independent failures the\n  expected time to finish grows exponentially in run length over mean time between failures (roughly\n  `MTBF·(e^(L/MTBF) − 1)`), so a run many times longer than the MTBF effectively never finishes.\n- **With checkpoints, cost is bounded.** Saving every *T* minutes, a failure costs on average *T/2*\n  minutes of recomputation plus the restart time.\n- **Model selection and rollback.** The best model is usually not the last, because validation peaks and\n  then overfitting sets in, so the peak's weights exist only if saved. A misbehaving deployed model needs\n  a known-good predecessor, and a result nobody can reconstruct is not a result.\n- **Weights at the end, or per epoch, fails all three.** It cannot resume correctly (optimizer state is\n  gone), misses the peak, and leaves nothing to roll back to.","how":"### What a resumable checkpoint contains\nResuming means continuing the exact same trajectory, so everything that influences the next step must be\nsaved:\n\n| Component | Why it is needed |\n|---|---|\n| **Model weights** (training precision, plus fp32 master weights under mixed precision) | the parameters themselves |\n| **Optimizer state** (AdamW: two fp32 moments per parameter) | fresh moments after resume cause a loss spike, because Adam's first steps after a reset are badly scaled |\n| **Scheduler state and step counter** | the learning rate continues instead of restarting warmup |\n| **Mixed-precision loss-scaler state**, where used | keeps the scale where it was |\n| **RNG states** (Python, NumPy, PyTorch CPU and CUDA) and **data-loader position** (epoch, batches consumed, sampler seed) | dropout masks and example order match an uninterrupted run; no example skipped or seen twice |\n| **Config and tokenizer** | the artefact loads without the training code |\n\n- **Size arithmetic.** In mixed precision a checkpoint holds about 14 bytes per parameter: 2 for\n  bf16/fp16 weights plus 12 for the fp32 master copy and the two moments.\n- **ZeRO's often-quoted 16 bytes** also counts the 2-byte gradients, which live only in memory.\n- **So a 7B model's resumable state is roughly 100 GB,** most of it fp32 optimizer state.\n\n### Frequency\n- **The basic trade.** Expected loss from a failure is half the interval; each save costs write time\n  during which GPUs may idle.\n- **Example.** Saving 100 GB over a 1 GB/s link is about 100 seconds; saving every 30 minutes costs about\n  5% of throughput in exchange for losing at most 30 minutes.\n- **Asynchronous checkpointing** snapshots the state to host memory (a stall of seconds or less per GPU,\n  bounded by device-to-host bandwidth) and writes to storage in the background while training continues.\n- **Incremental or differential schemes** save only what changed; they pay off mainly when much of the\n  state is static, such as large sparse embedding tables.\n\n### The Young–Daly interval\nIf a save stalls training for *C* seconds and failures arrive with mean time *M* between them, overhead\nper unit time is roughly `C/T` (saving) plus `T/(2M)` (expected recomputation). Minimising the sum gives:\n\n```\nT_opt ≈ sqrt(2 · C · M)\n```\n\n| Save stall C | M | T_opt | Total overhead |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 100 s (synchronous) | 10,800 s (a failure every three hours) | ≈ 1,470 s, about 25 minutes | roughly 14% |\n| 5 s (asynchronous snapshot) | 10,800 s | about 5.5 minutes | near 3% |\n\n- **Cheaper saves buy both** more frequent saves and less waste.\n- **The square root** says save cost matters as much as failure rate.\n- **Caveats.** The formula ignores restart time and assumes memoryless failures. Treat it as the start,\n  then save more often near the end of a run, where losing work costs the most.\n\n### Distributed layout\n- **Data parallelism:** every rank holds the same state and one rank writes it.\n- **ZeRO or FSDP:** optimizer state and weights are sharded, so each rank writes its own shard in\n  parallel. Fast, but it ties the checkpoint to that world size and sharding scheme.\n- **Resuming on a different GPU count** requires a resharding step; PyTorch Distributed Checkpoint and\n  DeepSpeed provide consolidation utilities.\n- **Keep a consolidated, weights-only export** alongside the sharded training state for evaluation and\n  serving.\n\n### Atomicity and retention\n1. **Temp path, then rename.** A crash mid-write never leaves a truncated file masquerading as the latest\n   checkpoint.\n2. **Manifest with a hash** catches silent corruption.\n3. **Flush before rename** on a local or network filesystem, or a power loss can leave the new name\n   pointing at incomplete data.\n4. **Object stores have no atomic directory rename.** Upload every shard first and write a small manifest\n   or \"latest\" pointer last; readers trust only checkpoints whose manifest exists.\n5. **Retention:** keep the last *k* for resume, the best on validation for selection, and periodic ones\n   (every *n* steps or every epoch) for analysis. Delete the rest, because 100 GB per save fills any disk\n   quickly.\n\n### Selection, averaging and formats\n- **Record the validation metric with each checkpoint,** so \"best\" is a lookup, not a re-evaluation.\n- **Average the last several checkpoints.** A cheap, old trick that often improves the final model; the\n  original Transformer paper averaged the last 5 (base) or 20 (big) checkpoints.\n- **Export as `safetensors`, not pickled `torch.save` files.** A pickle can execute arbitrary code on\n  load, which is why `torch.load` defaults to `weights_only=True` since PyTorch 2.6 and why weights from\n  untrusted sources should never be unpickled.\n- **Adapters need their base.** The checkpoint is the adapter weights plus an exact reference (name and\n  hash) to the base model they were trained on; without it the adapter is unusable.","example":"### Save and load the full state in PyTorch\nA compact pair for a single-process loop: full state, atomic rename, keep-last-*k* retention, and RNG\nrestoration. The Hugging Face `Trainer` does the same through `save_steps`, `save_total_limit`,\n`load_best_model_at_end`, `metric_for_best_model` and `resume_from_checkpoint`.\n\n```python\nimport os, random, numpy as np, torch\n\ndef save_checkpoint(dirpath, step, model, opt, sched, scaler, sampler_state, keep_last=3):\n    state = {\"step\": step, \"model\": model.state_dict(), \"optimizer\": opt.state_dict(),\n             \"scheduler\": sched.state_dict(), \"scaler\": scaler.state_dict() if scaler else None,\n             \"sampler\": sampler_state,                                   # epoch, batches consumed, seed\n             \"rng\": {\"torch\": torch.get_rng_state(), \"cuda\": torch.cuda.get_rng_state_all(),\n                     \"python\": random.getstate(), \"numpy\": np.random.get_state()}}\n    tmp, final = f\"{dirpath}/ckpt-{step}.pt.tmp\", f\"{dirpath}/ckpt-{step}.pt\"\n    torch.save(state, tmp)\n    os.replace(tmp, final)             # atomic rename: a crash mid-write never leaves a half file\n    saved = sorted((int(f.split(\"-\")[1].split(\".\")[0]), f) for f in os.listdir(dirpath) if f.endswith(\".pt\"))\n    for _, old in saved[:-keep_last]:  # retention: keep the newest k; keep \"best\" elsewhere\n        os.remove(f\"{dirpath}/{old}\")\n\ndef load_checkpoint(path, model, opt, sched, scaler):\n    state = torch.load(path, map_location=\"cpu\", weights_only=False)  # trusted file only: it is a pickle\n    model.load_state_dict(state[\"model\"]); opt.load_state_dict(state[\"optimizer\"])\n    sched.load_state_dict(state[\"scheduler\"])\n    if scaler and state[\"scaler\"]:\n        scaler.load_state_dict(state[\"scaler\"])\n    torch.set_rng_state(state[\"rng\"][\"torch\"]); torch.cuda.set_rng_state_all(state[\"rng\"][\"cuda\"])\n    random.setstate(state[\"rng\"][\"python\"]); np.random.set_state(state[\"rng\"][\"numpy\"])\n    return state[\"step\"], state[\"sampler\"]          # caller fast-forwards the data loader from these\n\n# In the loop:  if step % SAVE_EVERY == 0: save_checkpoint(CKPT_DIR, step, model, opt, sched, scaler,\n#                   {\"epoch\": epoch, \"consumed\": batches_this_epoch, \"seed\": 1234 + epoch})\n# On startup:   step, sampler = load_checkpoint(latest, model, opt, sched, scaler); skip `consumed` batches.\n```\n\n- **The part most hand-written loops forget:** the caller restores data order by reseeding the sampler\n  with the saved epoch seed and skipping the consumed batches.\n- **What the sketch leaves out: the flush.** `os.replace` makes the rename atomic but not durable against\n  power loss. On a local or network disk, save through an open file and call `f.flush()` and\n  `os.fsync(f.fileno())` before the rename.\n- **Retention here is keep-last-*k* only.** A best-on-validation checkpoint must be copied to a separate\n  name, or this loop will eventually delete it.\n\n### Choosing the save interval\nA runnable Young–Daly calculator. The third scenario is a spot-GPU fine-tune with a three-minute\nsynchronous write, preempted about every four hours.\n\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\ndef young_daly(save_stall_s, mtbf_s):\n    t = sqrt(2 * save_stall_s * mtbf_s)                  # optimal interval between saves\n    overhead = save_stall_s / t + t / (2 * mtbf_s)        # saving + expected recomputation\n    return t, overhead\n\nscenarios = {                                             # (stall per save, mean time between failures)\n    \"sync save, failure every 3 h\": (100, 10_800),\n    \"async snapshot, failure every 3 h\": (5, 10_800),\n    \"spot GPUs: 3-min sync save, preempted every 4 h\": (180, 14_400),\n}\nfor name, (c, m) in scenarios.items():\n    t, ov = young_daly(c, m)\n    print(f\"{name:48s} save every {t / 60:5.1f} min  overhead ~{ov:.0%}\")\n# sync save, failure every 3 h          save every  24.5 min  overhead ~14%\n# async snapshot, failure every 3 h     save every   5.5 min  overhead ~3%\n# spot GPUs: 3-min sync save, ...       save every  37.9 min  overhead ~16%\n```\n\n- **Reading it.** Saving once per three-hour epoch on the spot fleet would lose about 1.5 hours per\n  preemption on average; cutting the stall is worth more than tuning the interval.","patterns":["**Trainer-managed checkpoints for fine-tunes.** In the Hugging Face `Trainer`, set `save_steps` from the interval calculation, `save_total_limit` for retention, and `load_best_model_at_end=True` with `metric_for_best_model` so the best checkpoint is retained. Resume with `trainer.train(resume_from_checkpoint=True)`. Monitor save time per checkpoint and free disk.","**Asynchronous sharded saves at scale.** For FSDP or ZeRO jobs, write per-rank shards with PyTorch Distributed Checkpoint or DeepSpeed, using an asynchronous save so the GPU stall is only the copy to host memory. Monitor stall seconds per save, host-memory headroom, and background-write failures, which otherwise fail silently.","**Manifest-last on object storage.** Upload all shards, then write a manifest listing files and hashes, then update a \"latest\" pointer. The resume logic reads only checkpoints with a valid manifest. Monitor for orphaned shard directories without a manifest.","**Two artefacts per milestone.** Keep the full sharded training state for resume and a consolidated, weights-only `safetensors` export for evaluation and serving, registered with step, validation metric, data version and (for adapters) the base model's name and hash.","**Kill-and-resume drill.** Before a long run, kill the job on purpose mid-epoch and check that the resumed loss curve overlays the uninterrupted one. Monitor recovery time after the simulated preemption."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant T as Training job\n    participant H as Host memory\n    participant S as Durable storage\n    T->>H: Snapshot weights, optimizer, scheduler, RNG, data position\n    Note over T: GPU stall is only this copy\n    T->>T: Continue training\n    H->>S: Write shards to temp paths in the background\n    H->>S: Write manifest with hashes, then update latest pointer\n    S->>S: Retention keeps last k, best on validation, periodic\n    Note over T: Preemption or hardware fault\n    T->>S: Read latest pointer, verify manifest hashes\n    S-->>T: Full training state\n    T->>T: Restore RNG, reseed sampler, skip consumed batches, resume","caption":"One save and one resume: the GPU stalls only for the snapshot, the manifest is written last so a\nhalf-written checkpoint is never trusted, and resume restores every piece of state, not just weights."},"interview":[{"q":"What must a resumable training checkpoint contain, and why is a weights file not enough?","a":"- **Everything that influences the next step:** model weights (plus fp32 master weights under mixed\n  precision), optimizer moments, scheduler state and step counter, loss-scaler state, RNG states and\n  data-loader position, plus config and tokenizer.\n- **Why weights alone fail:** fresh Adam moments make the first steps badly scaled, the scheduler\n  restarts warmup, and the sampler loses the epoch's order, so the run never rejoins its original curve."},{"q":"How big is the checkpoint for a 7B full fine-tune, and how often would you save it?","a":"- **Size:** about 14 bytes per parameter in mixed precision (2 for bf16 weights plus 12 for the fp32\n  master copy and two moments), so roughly 100 GB.\n- **Interval:** Young–Daly, `T_opt ≈ sqrt(2 · C · M)`. A 100 s synchronous save with a failure every three\n  hours gives about 25 minutes and roughly 14% overhead.\n- **Better lever:** an asynchronous snapshot with a 5 s stall cuts the interval to about 5.5 minutes and\n  overhead to near 3%."},{"q":"A crash during a save left your latest checkpoint truncated. How do you design so this cannot happen?","a":"- **Local or network filesystem:** write to a temp path, flush, then atomically rename, so the final name\n  only ever points at a complete file.\n- **Object stores have no atomic directory rename:** upload every shard, then write a manifest with hashes,\n  then update a \"latest\" pointer. Resume trusts only checkpoints with a valid manifest.\n- **Monitor** for orphaned shard directories and failed background writes."},{"q":"When would you not keep sharded checkpoints as your only artefact?","a":"- **When anything other than the same training job must read them.** Sharded ZeRO or FSDP state is tied\n  to a world size and sharding scheme, and resuming on a different GPU count needs a resharding step.\n- **So keep two artefacts:** the full sharded state for resume and a consolidated weights-only\n  `safetensors` export for evaluation, serving and the model registry."},{"q":"Why should you avoid loading `.pt` checkpoints from an untrusted source?","a":"- **They are pickles:** a pickle can execute arbitrary code on load.\n- **That is why** `torch.load` defaults to `weights_only=True` since PyTorch 2.6, and why weights from\n  untrusted sources should be exported and loaded as `safetensors`.\n- **Only your own trusted training state** should be loaded with `weights_only=False`, as in the resume\n  example."}],"tradeoffs":["**Frequent saves vs write time and storage.** They bound the work lost to a failure; the balance moves toward more frequent saves as failure rates rise, and toward fewer as the save becomes slow relative to the interval.","**Sharded vs consolidated.** Sharded checkpoints write in parallel and avoid gathering 100 GB onto one rank, but tie the artefact to a world size and need a resharding step to resume elsewhere.","**Weights-only vs full state.** Weights-only exports are small and serve directly but cannot resume training; full state resumes exactly but is several times larger, so most teams keep both.","**Many checkpoints vs storage bill.** Keeping many enables rollback, averaging and post-hoc analysis, but at 100 GB each storage becomes the constraint, which is what retention policies trade against.","**Asynchronous vs complexity.** It hides write latency but needs host-memory headroom for a full copy of the state and adds a failure mode in which the background write itself fails silently."],"pitfalls":["**Saving only weights and resuming with a fresh optimizer.** Symptom: a loss spike immediately after resume and a resumed run that never rejoins the original curve. Fix: save optimizer, scheduler and scaler state in every checkpoint.","**Not saving the data-loader position.** Symptom: examples seen twice or never in the resumed epoch, and a loss curve after resume that differs from an uninterrupted run. Fix: save epoch, batches consumed and sampler seed.","**Writing checkpoints in place without a temporary file and rename.** Symptom: a truncated latest checkpoint after a crash and no resumable state at all. Fix: temp path, flush, rename (or manifest last).","**Letting the checkpoint directory fill the disk.** Symptom: the run dies during a save, often taking the latest good checkpoint with it. Fix: a retention policy and a free-disk alert.","**A retention rule that deletes the best checkpoint because it is old.** Symptom: a final model worse than one that existed an hour earlier. The Hugging Face `Trainer` retains the best checkpoint when `load_best_model_at_end` is set; hand-written loops must do the same explicitly.","**Saving adapters without the base model's exact version.** Symptom: an adapter that loads without error onto a different base and produces garbage. Fix: store the base model's name and hash with the adapter.","**Loading pickled checkpoints from untrusted sources.** Symptom: arbitrary code running on load. Fix: `safetensors`, and `weights_only=True`."],"when":"- **Always checkpoint** any training run longer than a few minutes; keep the best checkpoint by\n  validation metric for every run whose output will be deployed.\n- **Decide:** the interval from failure rate and save cost, the format from how the run is parallelised\n  and who consumes the artefact, and retention from storage and the need for rollback.\n- **Measure:** save time as a share of step time, and recovery time after a simulated preemption.\n- **Day to day** it is a configuration task in the training framework; in a platform role it becomes\n  storage layout, model registry integration and promotion rules.\n- **Interviews:** list what a resumable checkpoint must contain and explain why weights alone are\n  insufficient.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures save interval, retention and best-checkpoint selection in the trainer, tests a resume after a kill, and exports weights-only `safetensors` for evaluation and serving.","architect":"Owns checkpoint storage layout, sharding strategy, the registry that records which checkpoint became which deployed model, and the rollback path to a previous one.","fde":"On a customer's cluster, verifies resume works under their preemption policy and that checkpoints and adapter references are stored where the customer's governance requires."},"check":{"q":"A full fine-tune of a 7B model runs on spot GPUs that are preempted about every four hours. The job\nsaves `model.state_dict()` once per epoch (every three hours) with a three-minute synchronous write. After\na preemption at step 3,200 the resumed loss jumps from 0.9 to 1.6 and never rejoins the original curve.\nDiagnose both problems and redesign the scheme.","a":"Two separate failures. Correctness: a weights-only checkpoint restarts AdamW with empty moments,\nso the first steps are badly scaled; the scheduler restarts warmup and resets its decay horizon; and the\nsampler restarts, so the epoch's order is lost. The checkpoint must also hold both Adam moments and fp32\nmaster weights, scheduler state and global step, scaler state if used, RNG states and the data position,\nroughly 100 GB in total, written atomically with a manifest. Frequency: with failures every four hours,\na three-hour interval loses about 1.5 hours per preemption on average, and often the run is killed before\nits first save. Young–Daly gives sqrt(2 × 180 s × 14,400 s) ≈ 2,280 s, about 38 minutes, at roughly 16%\ntotal overhead; moving to an asynchronous snapshot with a stall of ten seconds or so brings the interval\nunder ten minutes and the overhead to a few percent. Finally, test the design by killing the job on\npurpose and checking that the resumed loss curve overlays the uninterrupted one."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does resuming from a weights-only checkpoint typically produce a loss spike?","options":["The weights are saved in lower precision and lose information","The optimizer's moment estimates and the scheduler step are reset, so the first updates are badly scaled and warmup restarts","The data loader starts from the first example of the dataset again","Gradient checkpointing must be re-enabled after a resume"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adam's steps depend on accumulated moments and the schedule position; fresh moments and a restarted warmup change the step size abruptly. Data order matters for reproducibility but does not by itself cause a spike."},{"q":"Which practice prevents a crash during a save from destroying the latest usable checkpoint?","options":["Saving checkpoints more frequently","Using sharded checkpoints across ranks","Writing to a temporary file and atomically renaming it on completion","Compressing the checkpoint before writing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A crash mid-write leaves a truncated file; writing to a temporary path and renaming means the final name only ever points at a complete file. Frequency and sharding change cost, not atomicity."},{"q":"How is checkpointing related to gradient checkpointing?","options":["Gradient checkpointing saves gradients to disk between steps","They are the same feature enabled by one flag in most trainers","They are unrelated: checkpointing persists training state, gradient checkpointing recomputes activations to save memory","Gradient checkpointing is the sharded form of checkpointing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The shared name is historical. One is about durability and resume; the other trades compute for activation memory during backward and writes nothing to storage."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","sources":["Chen et al., 2016 — Training Deep Nets with Sublinear Memory Cost","Korthikanti et al., 2022 — Reducing Activation Recomputation in Large Transformer Models","Official docs: PyTorch torch.utils.checkpoint"],"why":"- **Training memory has four parts.** Weights, gradients, optimizer state and activations. The first\n  three are a fixed number of bytes per parameter (about 16 with AdamW in mixed precision), and PEFT and\n  quantization attack them.\n- **Activations scale with the workload, not the model.** They grow with batch × sequence length ×\n  hidden size × layers, and they exist only because backprop needs them: for `y = xW` the weight gradient\n  is `xᵀ · dL/dy`, so `x` must be kept; GELU needs its input; softmax needs its output.\n- **All of them are live at once.** Autograd holds every intermediate tensor until backward consumes it,\n  and backward runs last-layer-first, so at the end of the forward pass *all* of them are alive. At long\n  sequence lengths they outweigh the weights.\n- **The naive alternatives fail here.** Gradient accumulation shrinks the micro-batch, but you are already\n  at b = 1. Shortening the sequence changes the task. A bigger GPU is a procurement ladder, not an\n  engineering answer.\n- **What it buys.** It trades the one resource GPUs have in abundance, compute, for the one they lack,\n  memory.","how":"### How big activations are\nKorthikanti et al. estimate one transformer layer's activations in bf16, without FlashAttention, at\nroughly `s·b·h·(34 + 5·a·s/h)` bytes (s = sequence, b = batch, h = hidden, a = heads).\n\n| 7B-class model (h = 4096, a = 32, 32 layers), s = 4096, b = 1 | Size |\n|---|---|\n| Linear term 34·s·b·h | about 570 MB per layer |\n| Attention-score term 5·a·s²·b | about 2.7 GB per layer |\n| Whole model, eager attention | ~105 GB |\n| Whole model, FlashAttention (no s² term, score matrix never materialised) | ~18 GB, doubling at 8k |\n\n- **Consequence:** a QLoRA run whose weights plus adapter plus optimizer state fit in 6 GB still cannot\n  fit on a 24 GB card at 8k context, because of activations alone (all figures illustrative, from the\n  formula above).\n- **Caveat on the constant:** 34 was derived for a GPT-style block with a 4h-wide GELU MLP and dropout. A\n  Llama-style SwiGLU MLP or a dropout-free recipe changes it somewhat, so use the formula for orders of\n  magnitude, not for a precise budget.\n\n### The observation\n- A block's internal activations are a deterministic function of its input (dropout aside, which is\n  handled by replaying the RNG state).\n- If you still have the input, you can rebuild the internals whenever you need them, so you only need\n  them during that block's own backward step, not across the whole forward pass.\n\n### The algorithm\n1. **Partition** the network into segments. In transformers the common choice is one decoder block per\n   segment, because block boundaries are where the activation tensor is smallest (just `s·b·h`).\n2. **Forward:** run each segment without recording the autograd graph. Keep only the segment's input\n   tensor; let its internal activations be freed.\n3. **Backward:** when the gradient reaches segment *k*'s output, re-run segment *k*'s forward from its\n   stored input with graph recording on, backpropagate through that local graph, free it, and move on to\n   segment *k − 1*.\n\n### Memory and compute cost\n- **Peak activation memory** becomes (all segment inputs) + (one segment's full internals).\n- **The √n result:** with n layers and segments of √n layers each, both terms are O(√n), the \"sublinear\n  memory\" result of Chen et al.\n- **Per-block in a transformer:** n block inputs, each `s·b·h·2` bytes, plus one block's internals. In the\n  7B example at 4k: 32 × 33.5 MB ≈ 1.1 GB of inputs plus ~570 MB for the live block, down from ~18 GB.\n- **Compute:** each segment's forward runs twice. Forward is roughly a third of a step's FLOPs (backward\n  is about twice forward), so the worst case is about a third more compute.\n- **Wall clock:** the commonly quoted slowdown is 20–30% (illustrative; it depends on how much of the step\n  is communication or data loading rather than math).\n\n### Selective recomputation\n- **Not every activation costs the same.** Korthikanti et al. point out that the attention scores and\n  their softmax/dropout outputs are the entire s² term yet cheap to recompute. Recomputing *only* those\n  gives most of the memory benefit for a fraction of the overhead.\n- **FlashAttention does this implicitly:** it saves the per-row log-sum-exp and rebuilds score tiles in\n  backward.\n- **The two compose.** Per-block checkpointing cuts *both* terms from \"n layers' worth\" to \"one live\n  layer's worth\"; FlashAttention then removes the s² term from that one live layer too.\n- **Why the second step matters:** the live block's eager-attention score tensors are `5·a·s²·b` bytes,\n  about 2.7 GB for one 7B layer at 4k and ~10.7 GB at 8k. Without FlashAttention that single block sets a\n  memory floor no amount of checkpointing lowers.\n\n### Framework mechanics and the reentrant trap\nPyTorch exposes `torch.utils.checkpoint.checkpoint(fn, *args, use_reentrant=False)`, with two\nimplementations:\n\n| Mode | How it works | Behaviour with frozen inputs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reentrant (older) | Runs the segment under `no_grad`, re-enters autograd in backward | If **no input requires grad**, the segment is never recorded and its parameters silently get no gradient |\n| Non-reentrant (recommended) | Uses saved-tensor hooks | Handles inputs without grad correctly |\n\n- **The LoRA case:** the embedding output does not require grad because the embeddings are frozen. PEFT\n  recipes work around reentrant mode with `model.enable_input_require_grads()`.\n- **In Transformers:**\n  `model.gradient_checkpointing_enable(gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={\"use_reentrant\": False})` or\n  `TrainingArguments(gradient_checkpointing=True)`.\n- **KV cache off:** Transformers forces `use_cache=False` when checkpointing is on, because the cache\n  generation builds is pointless during training and conflicts with recomputation.\n\n### Where it sits among the other levers\n| Lever | What it cuts |\n|---|---|\n| Gradient accumulation | the b in `s·b·h` |\n| Sequence or context parallelism | the s, split across GPUs |\n| FlashAttention | the s² term |\n| CPU offloading of activations | moves them across PCIe, usually slower than recomputing |\n| Gradient checkpointing | the \"× layers\" factor |\n\n- **With FSDP:** checkpoint per block *inside* each FSDP unit, so recomputation does not trigger extra\n  parameter all-gathers.","example":"### Residual MLP stack with and without checkpointing\nWork the budget out before running it:\n- **Parameters:** each block has 8·d² ≈ 33.5M parameters at d = 2048, so 24 blocks are ~805M: ~3.2 GB of\n  fp32 weights and, by the end of backward, ~3.2 GB of gradients.\n- **Without checkpointing:** autograd keeps per block the `[4096, 2048]` input (for the first Linear's\n  weight gradient), the `[4096, 8192]` pre-GELU tensor (for GELU's derivative) and the `[4096, 8192]` GELU\n  output (for the second Linear).\n- **That totals** 9 × 4096 × 2048 × 4 bytes ≈ 0.3 GB per block, ~7.2 GB for 24 blocks, all alive at the\n  start of backward.\n- **With checkpointing:** only the 24 block inputs survive (~0.8 GB), and one block's 0.3 GB is rebuilt\n  at a time.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn\nfrom torch.utils.checkpoint import checkpoint\n\nclass Block(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.ff = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(d, 4 * d), nn.GELU(), nn.Linear(4 * d, d))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return x + self.ff(x)\n\nclass Net(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d, n, ckpt):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.blocks = nn.ModuleList(Block(d) for _ in range(n))\n        self.ckpt = ckpt\n    def forward(self, x):\n        for b in self.blocks:\n            x = checkpoint(b, x, use_reentrant=False) if self.ckpt else b(x)\n        return x\n\ndef peak_gb(ckpt, d=2048, n=24, tokens=4096):\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache(); torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\n    net = Net(d, n, ckpt).cuda()\n    x = torch.randn(tokens, d, device=\"cuda\", requires_grad=True)\n    net(x).sum().backward()\n    return torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 2**30\n\nfor flag in (False, True):\n    print(f\"checkpointing={flag}: peak {peak_gb(flag):.1f} GB\")\n# Rough expectation: ~10-11 GB without (weights + all activations) vs ~7 GB with (weights +\n# gradients + block inputs + one live block). Double `tokens` and the unchecked peak grows by ~7 GB\n# while the checkpointed one grows by about 1 GB: activations scale with tokens, weights do not.\n# Hugging Face equivalent: model.gradient_checkpointing_enable(\n#     gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={\"use_reentrant\": False})\n```\n\n### Activation budget calculator for a 7B fine-tune\nPure Python, no GPU: plug the Korthikanti estimate into a planning script before you book hardware.\n\n```python\ndef act_gb_per_layer(s, b=1, h=4096, a=32, flash=False):\n    linear = 34 * s * b * h                    # bytes, bf16, Korthikanti et al.\n    scores = 0 if flash else 5 * a * s * s * b # the s^2 attention-score term\n    return (linear + scores) / 1e9\n\ndef budget(s, layers=32, h=4096, a=32, flash=True, ckpt=False):\n    per_layer = act_gb_per_layer(s, h=h, a=a, flash=flash)\n    if not ckpt:\n        return per_layer * layers               # every layer's activations live at once\n    inputs = layers * s * h * 2 / 1e9           # one bf16 block input per layer\n    return inputs + per_layer                   # plus one live block during recompute\n\nfor s in (4096, 8192):\n    print(f\"s={s}: eager {budget(s, flash=False):.0f} GB | flash {budget(s):.1f} GB\"\n          f\" | flash+ckpt {budget(s, ckpt=True):.1f} GB | eager+ckpt {budget(s, flash=False, ckpt=True):.1f} GB\")\n# s=4096: eager 104 GB | flash 18.3 GB | flash+ckpt 1.6 GB | eager+ckpt 4.3 GB\n# s=8192: eager 380 GB | flash 36.5 GB | flash+ckpt 3.3 GB | eager+ckpt 14.0 GB\n```\n\nReading the output for a QLoRA run on a 24 GB card (~4 GB of 4-bit weights):\n\n| Setup at 8k | Activations (estimate) | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| Eager attention, no checkpointing | ~380 GB | hopeless |\n| FlashAttention, no checkpointing | ~36 GB | still OOM |\n| Eager attention + checkpointing | ~14 GB, of which ~10.7 GB is one block's scores | marginal; backward also needs gradient buffers for those score tensors |\n| FlashAttention + checkpointing | ~3.3 GB | fits, with room to raise the micro-batch |","patterns":["**Checkpointing plus fused attention as the long-context default.** For any 4k+ fine-tune on one GPU, set `TrainingArguments(gradient_checkpointing=True, gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={\"use_reentrant\": False})` and load the model with `attn_implementation=\"sdpa\"` (or `\"flash_attention_2\"`). Monitor peak memory per step and tokens per second.","**Re-tune the micro-batch after enabling it.** Raise `per_device_train_batch_size` and lower `gradient_accumulation_steps` so the effective batch is unchanged, until memory is nearly full. Compare tokens per second and GPU utilisation against the uncheckpointed baseline, not seconds per step.","**Guard LoRA/QLoRA runs against silent zero gradients.** Use non-reentrant mode, or PEFT's `prepare_model_for_kbit_training(model, use_gradient_checkpointing=True)`, which also enables input gradients. After the first step, assert every trainable parameter has a non-`None` gradient and that the logged gradient norm is non-zero.","**Wrap blocks inside FSDP units.** With FSDP, apply checkpointing per decoder block (for example PyTorch's `apply_activation_checkpointing` with a `check_fn` matching the block class), so recompute reuses the already-gathered parameters. Monitor step time and all-gather volume for unexpected growth.","**Selective recompute at pretraining scale.** In Megatron-LM, prefer `--recompute-granularity selective` (recompute attention internals only) and fall back to `full` only when memory still does not fit. Monitor model FLOPs utilisation, since full recompute costs noticeably more.","**Measure before and after in a smoke test.** Run one step each way, log `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()`, and keep the numbers next to the config so regressions show up when sequence length or model changes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Block k input\"] --> F[\"Forward block k, no graph\"]\n    F --> S[(\"Stored: block inputs only\")]\n    F --> N[\"Next block, then loss\"]\n    N --> G[\"Gradient reaches block k output\"]\n    G --> R[\"Re-run block k forward from input\"]\n    S -.->|\"read input k\"| R\n    R --> B[\"Backward through local graph\"]\n    B --> FR[\"Free block k internals\"]\n    FR -->|\"move to block k minus 1\"| G","caption":"Forward keeps only each block's input; backward re-runs one block at a time from that input, so only\none block's internals are ever alive."},"interview":[{"q":"What is gradient checkpointing, and why does it save memory?","a":"- **Definition:** activation recomputation. The forward pass keeps only each segment's (usually each\n  block's) input and frees the internals; backward re-runs that segment's forward to rebuild them.\n- **Why it saves memory:** autograd otherwise keeps every layer's activations alive until backward, so\n  peak memory scales with layers. Checkpointing turns that into \"all block inputs plus one live block\".\n- **Not to be confused with** saving model state to disk, which is a separate setting."},{"q":"A LoRA run with checkpointing on shows a flat loss. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check gradients first:** after one step, list trainable parameters whose `.grad` is `None` or whose\n  gradient norm is zero. All adapter gradients missing points at the reentrant trap.\n- **Cause:** reentrant mode only records a segment when an input requires grad, and frozen embeddings\n  produce inputs that do not.\n- **Fix:** pass `use_reentrant=False`, or call `model.enable_input_require_grads()`, then rerun the\n  one-step assertion before the full run."},{"q":"When would you turn gradient checkpointing off?","a":"- **When memory is not the constraint:** small models or short sequences where the GPU is compute-bound,\n  because the extra forward pass (roughly a third more compute in the worst case) is then pure loss.\n- **When the batch already saturates the GPU:** the usual win, a bigger micro-batch, is gone, so the\n  20–30% step-time cost (illustrative) is not repaid.\n- **Prefer selective recompute** at pretraining scale, recomputing only attention internals, when full\n  recompute costs too much model FLOPs utilisation."},{"q":"Peak memory dropped sharply but the run still OOMs at 8k. What is going on?","a":"- **The live block sets a floor.** With eager attention, the block being recomputed still materialises\n  its `5·a·s²·b` score tensors, about 10.7 GB for one 7B layer at 8k.\n- **Checkpointing cannot remove that,** because it only cuts the \"× layers\" factor. Switch to\n  FlashAttention or SDPA so the score matrix is never materialised.\n- **Then verify** with `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()` before and after."},{"q":"Steps got 25% slower after enabling it. Is that a regression?","a":"- **Not necessarily.** Seconds per step is the wrong metric; tokens per second is the right one.\n- **Spend the freed memory:** raise `per_device_train_batch_size` and lower\n  `gradient_accumulation_steps` to keep the effective batch, because a GPU at micro-batch 1 is badly\n  under-utilised.\n- **Compare against the baseline** in tokens per second and GPU utilisation; throughput often rises\n  despite the recompute."}],"tradeoffs":["**Compute vs memory.** You pay roughly one extra forward pass per step (20–30% wall-clock, illustrative) for a large cut in activation memory. If you are compute-bound with memory to spare, it is pure loss and should be off.","**Slower steps vs higher throughput.** The freed memory usually lets you raise the micro-batch, and a GPU at micro-batch 1 is badly under-utilised, so tokens/second often goes *up* overall despite the recompute. This flips once the batch already saturates the GPU.","**Per-block vs coarser segments.** Per-block is simple and matches FSDP wrapping. Coarser segments store fewer boundary inputs but hold a larger live segment during recompute (total recompute is one extra forward either way); the √n split that balances the two is rarely worth the engineering.","**Non-reentrant vs reentrant.** Non-reentrant handles frozen inputs and nested checkpoints correctly. Reentrant can have slightly lower overhead in some stacks but fails silently in the LoRA case, so the default should be non-reentrant."],"pitfalls":["**Confusing it with model checkpointing (saving state to disk).** Symptom: someone enables \"checkpointing\" for resumability, loses a multi-day run, and discovers nothing was ever written. Fix: configure save checkpoints separately.","**Reentrant mode with frozen embeddings in a LoRA run.** Symptom: a flat loss curve and adapter gradients that are `None`. Fix: `enable_input_require_grads()` or `use_reentrant=False`.","**Still OOM at long context after enabling it.** Symptom: the peak fell a lot but stalls at a floor of several GB per live block, because eager attention materialises that block's s² score tensors (~10 GB for one 7B layer at 8k) during recompute. Fix: only FlashAttention/SDPA removes them.","**Switching it off because \"steps got slower\".** Symptom: lower throughput after reverting. Fix: raise the micro-batch to recover utilisation; the right metric is tokens per second, not seconds per step.","**Side effects inside a checkpointed function run twice.** Counters, logging, in-place updates and custom RNG not covered by `preserve_rng_state` repeat. Symptom: doubled metrics or a gradient mismatch versus the uncheckpointed run. Fix: keep checkpointed functions pure."],"when":"- **Use when:** activation memory is the thing that does not fit. Diagnose it by computing the `s·b·h·34`\n  estimate or by comparing `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()` with and without the flag.\n- **Default on:** for any fine-tune at 4k+ context on a single GPU; it is what makes QLoRA on a 24 GB\n  card at 8k realistic.\n- **Avoid when:** small models and short sequences, where the GPU is compute-bound and memory is\n  plentiful.\n- **Always:** pair it with FlashAttention or SDPA, re-tune the micro-batch after enabling it, and judge\n  the result by tokens/second.\n- **Interviews:** separate per-parameter memory (weights, gradients, optimizer) from activation memory,\n  state what checkpointing trades, explain the √n argument, and name the reentrant trap.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Flips the flag in `TrainingArguments`, Axolotl or Unsloth, pairs it with FlashAttention, raises the micro-batch until memory is full again, and checks tokens/second against the baseline.","architect":"Sets the sequence-length-versus-GPU-tier policy knowing that checkpointing lets a 24 GB card handle an 8k QLoRA, so the capacity plan is not \"buy 80 GB cards\" by default.","fde":"Fits a customer's long-document fine-tune onto the fixed GPU quota in their VPC instead of waiting on a procurement cycle, and explains the 20–30% step-time cost up front."},"check":{"q":"A team runs QLoRA on a 7B model at 8k context on a 24 GB GPU. The 4-bit weights are ~4 GB and the\nadapter plus optimizer state is tiny, yet they OOM at micro-batch 1. Explain what is using the memory and\nwhat you would change, with rough numbers.","a":"The rest is activations. With h = 4096 and 32 layers, the linear term 34·s·b·h at s = 8192 is about\n1.1 GB per layer, roughly 36 GB across the model even with FlashAttention; without it the attention-score\nterm 5·a·s²·b adds about 10 GB per layer. So first turn on FlashAttention or SDPA to delete the s² term,\nthen enable gradient checkpointing in non-reentrant mode (or call `enable_input_require_grads()`), which\ncuts stored activations to one block input per layer (~67 MB each, ~2 GB total) plus a single live block\n(~1.1 GB). That fits with room to raise the micro-batch, at roughly a 20–30% per-step cost. Verify with\n`max_memory_allocated()` before and after, and judge the change by tokens per second."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does gradient checkpointing trade, and for what?","options":["Disk space for GPU memory, by paging activations to NVMe","Extra forward compute for activation memory, by recomputing block internals during backward","Numerical precision for memory, by storing activations in 8-bit","Communication bandwidth for memory, by sharding activations across GPUs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Checkpointing keeps only block inputs and re-runs each block's forward during the backward pass. Nothing leaves the GPU and nothing changes precision; the price is roughly one additional forward pass of compute."},{"q":"A LoRA run with reentrant checkpointing shows a flat loss and `None` adapter gradients. Why?","options":["LoRA adapters cannot be placed inside a checkpointed segment","4-bit base weights cannot be recomputed during the backward pass","No segment input requires grad because embeddings are frozen, so autograd never records the segment","The RNG state is not restored, so dropout masks differ between the two forward passes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In reentrant mode the segment is only attached to the graph through inputs that require grad. Frozen embeddings produce inputs that do not, so the adapters inside get no gradient. `enable_input_require_grads()` or `use_reentrant=False` fixes it."},{"q":"A 7B QLoRA run at 8k context enables per-block checkpointing; peak memory drops sharply but the run still OOMs on a 24 GB card during backward. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Optimizer state dominates and checkpointing does not touch it","The one live block being recomputed still materialises its s² attention-score tensors because FlashAttention/SDPA is off","Too many gradient-accumulation steps are keeping old activations alive","The checkpoint files on disk are too large for the page cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Per-block checkpointing shrinks stored activations to the block inputs plus one live block, but inside that live block eager attention still builds the full `5·a·s²·b` score tensors, about 10 GB for a single 7B layer at 8k, so the floor is set by one block and only a kernel that never materialises the matrix removes it. Optimizer state in QLoRA is tiny, and accumulation steps do not keep activations alive between micro-batches."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","sources":["Lewis et al., 2020 — Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","Ovadia et al., 2023 — Fine-Tuning or Retrieval? Comparing Knowledge Injection in LLMs","Zhang et al., 2024 — RAFT: Adapting Language Model to Domain Specific RAG"],"why":"- **The same requests arrive every week.** \"Make the model know our stuff\", \"make it sound like us\",\n  \"make it stop doing X\". The reflex answer is \"fine-tune it\", because training feels like the real\n  engineering. That reflex is expensive and usually wrong for the first request.\n- **Fine-tuning on documents does not reliably inject them.** The loss is next-token prediction over a\n  handful of phrasings, nothing like the massive repetition and paraphrase of pretraining. Ovadia et al.\n  found retrieval consistently beat unsupervised fine-tuning on new facts; fine-tuning improved only when\n  each fact was shown in many paraphrases, so the problem is exposure and phrasing, not effort.\n- **Even knowledge that sticks is frozen.** It is fixed at training time, cannot be scoped per tenant or\n  deleted, carries no citation, and must be redone when the base model is upgraded.\n- **\"Just prompt harder\" has ceilings too.** Prompts grow to thousands of tokens, costing money and\n  latency on every call; few-shot examples cannot pin a precise output distribution; some behaviours\n  (strict schema compliance in a small model, a domain's tone, a tool-calling idiom) never stabilise by\n  instruction alone.\n- **RAG fixes only one gap, and adds a failure mode.** It helps when the model is *missing* information\n  that exists in retrievable text, not when it has the information and still does the wrong thing, and\n  retrieval quality becomes a new risk. Without a framework, teams spend two weeks on a LoRA that a\n  200-token system prompt edit would have fixed, or build retrieval to fix formatting.","how":"### Three levers, three different objects\n| Lever | What it changes | Good at | Weak at | Cost shape |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Prompt (system prompt, few-shot, structured output) | The input of this request | Instructions, format, small behaviour shifts, per-deployment config | Precise distributions, very long rule sets, small-model compliance | Tokens × requests, partly offset by prompt caching |\n| RAG | The input, with retrieved evidence | Facts, freshness, per-user/tenant scoping, citations, deletion | Behaviour the model already gets wrong with the facts in hand | Index, embeddings, retrieval quality, ops |\n| Fine-tune (SFT/DPO, usually LoRA) | The weights | Default style, format, task skill, tool idiom, compressing a long prompt, distilling into a smaller model | Facts that change, facts needing attribution, anything still being redesigned | Data, GPU time, evals, re-doing on base-model upgrades, forgetting risk |\n\n### The decision procedure\n1. **Name the gap with failing examples.** Collect 50–200 real failures and sort them:\n   - *knowledge gap:* the model lacked information;\n   - *behaviour gap:* it had the information and still answered wrongly, in the wrong form or tone;\n   - *economics gap:* it was right but too slow or expensive.\n   Most requests contain two gaps tangled together, and the lever differs for each.\n2. **Knowledge gap → RAG.** Or long context, if the corpus is small, static and the token cost is\n   acceptable. Do not fine-tune to inject the content. If the model handles retrieved passages badly\n   (ignores them, cannot say \"not in the documents\"), that is a behaviour gap: fine-tune it to *use*\n   retrieved context (the RAFT idea), still keeping the facts in the index.\n3. **Behaviour gap → prompt first.** System prompt, a few curated examples, structured-output mode. Build\n   the eval set from the failures and score it. If the score plateaus below target, or the prompt needed\n   to reach it dominates per-request cost, move to a LoRA SFT on the same reviewed examples. Use DPO when\n   failures are preference-shaped (two answers, one better) rather than right/wrong.\n4. **Economics gap → fine-tune.** Distil the big model's outputs into a smaller model, or train the\n   few-shot block into the weights so the prompt shrinks; verify on the same eval set that quality held.\n5. **Combine.** The usual production end state is RAG for facts, a fine-tuned or well-prompted model for\n   behaviour, and a prompt for per-deployment configuration. The levers are not exclusive; the mistake is\n   using the wrong one for a given gap.\n\n### Why fine-tuning is weak at facts\n- **SFT shifts probability mass toward the sequences it sees.** A fact seen a few times in one phrasing\n  does not become reliably retrievable under other phrasings, which question answering requires.\n- **RAG puts the fact in the context window,** where attention can copy it directly, so no memorisation\n  is needed.\n- **Fine-tuned \"knowledge\" fails silently,** because the model produces plausible details in the style of\n  the training data rather than refusing.\n\n### The economics, as arithmetic\nThe cost case for fine-tuning is a break-even, not a feeling:\n\n```\nmonthly saving   = tokens removed per request × requests per month × price per input token\n                   − (any extra per-token or hosting cost of serving the tuned model)\ncost per cycle   = data review + training runs + eval runs, repeated at every base-model upgrade\nfine-tune pays   when monthly saving × months between upgrades > cost per cycle\n```\n\n- **The second term of the saving is the one people forget.** Some hosted fine-tuning products price\n  tuned models above the base model per token.\n- **Self-hosting has its own cost.** A tuned model needs its own GPUs unless the serving stack can load\n  many LoRA adapters onto one shared base (multi-adapter serving).\n- **Prompt caching shrinks the first term,** because a cached prefix is already billed at a discount.\n- **Run the numbers** with your vendor's current prices before arguing either way.\n\n### Reversibility and the upgrade tax\n| Lever | Change and rollback | On a new base model |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prompt | One deploy, instant rollback | Transfers largely unchanged |\n| RAG index | Incremental updates, supports deletion | Transfers to any model |\n| Fine-tune | New artifact to re-evaluate; roll back by swapping adapters | Re-train for every release; with proprietary APIs, may lock you to one vendor's fine-tuning product |\n\nThat tax is why \"prompt until proven insufficient\" is the default ordering, not a statement that\nfine-tuning is bad.","example":"### Support assistant: sorting the gaps\nA support assistant for a SaaS product (all numbers illustrative), running 2 million requests a month\nwith a 5,000-token prompt of instructions and twelve examples.\n\n| Complaint | Gap | Lever |\n|---|---|---|\n| Does not know the product docs, which change weekly | Knowledge | RAG over the docs, weekly re-indexing, so new pages are answerable the day they ship and retired pages stop being cited |\n| Should answer in brand voice | Behaviour | Tighten the system prompt, then measure |\n| Must return a fixed JSON object | Behaviour | Structured-output mode, then measure on 200 reviewed cases |\n\n- **Pick the right metric.** Structured output guarantees the JSON parses and matches the schema, not\n  that the right fields carry the right content. So the metric is field-level correctness (right\n  `category` enum, `escalate` set when policy requires it).\n- **The prompt plateaus.** It reaches 97% field-level correctness and a tone score of 4.1/5 on a rubric,\n  against targets of 99.5% and 4.5.\n- **So fine-tune.** A LoRA SFT on 1,500 reviewed answers reaches 99.7% and 4.6, and the twelve examples\n  leave the prompt, which shrinks to 800 tokens.\n- **Division of labour.** Knowledge stayed in the index; the weights only learned behaviour; the system\n  prompt still carries per-customer configuration.\n\n### The economics of that fine-tune\n| Quantity | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prompt tokens per request | 5,000 | 800 |\n| Input tokens per month (× 2M requests) | 10 billion | 1.6 billion |\n| Reduction | | about 6× fewer, an 8.4-billion-token saving |\n\n- **Caching discounts the win.** With prompt caching the cached share is already discounted, so the\n  realised saving is smaller but still material.\n- **Plug in the serving price.** If the tuned model costs more per token, part of that 8.4-billion-token\n  saving is handed straight back.\n- **Count the cycle cost.** A few GPU-hours per iteration plus the eval harness, repeated at each\n  base-model upgrade.\n\n### Break-even calculator\nThe formula above as runnable Python. The prices are placeholders; replace them with your vendor's\ncurrent list prices before deciding anything.\n\n```python\ndef monthly_saving(tokens_removed, requests, price_per_mtok,\n                   cached_share=0.0, cached_price_ratio=1.0, extra_serving=0.0):\n    # Removed tokens that would have been cached were already billed at a discount,\n    # so they save less than full price.\n    blended = price_per_mtok * ((1 - cached_share) + cached_share * cached_price_ratio)\n    return tokens_removed * requests * blended / 1e6 - extra_serving\n\ndef pays_off(saving, months_between_upgrades, cost_per_cycle):\n    return saving * months_between_upgrades > cost_per_cycle\n\n# Placeholder prices: replace with your vendor's current list prices.\nbase = dict(tokens_removed=5000 - 800, requests=2_000_000, price_per_mtok=3.00)\ncycle, months = 15_000, 6          # review + training + evals per cycle; months per base model\n\nfor label, kw in [\n    (\"no caching, same price\",         dict()),\n    (\"80% cached at 10% price\",        dict(cached_share=0.8, cached_price_ratio=0.1)),\n    (\"cached + tuned model +$6k/month\", dict(cached_share=0.8, cached_price_ratio=0.1, extra_serving=6000)),\n]:\n    s = monthly_saving(**base, **kw)\n    print(f\"{label:34s} saving ${s:>9,.0f}/mo  pays off: {pays_off(s, months, cycle)}\")\n# no caching, same price             saving $   25,200/mo  pays off: True\n# 80% cached at 10% price            saving $    7,056/mo  pays off: True\n# cached + tuned model +$6k/month    saving $    1,056/mo  pays off: False\n```\n\nThe same 8.4 billion removed tokens go from an easy win to a loss once caching and a tuned-model\nserving premium are counted, which is why the second term matters.","patterns":["**Eval-gated lever ladder.** When any \"know / sound like / stop doing\" request arrives, build one golden set from the labelled failures and score every rung (prompt, RAG, LoRA) on it. Block promotion in CI unless the new rung beats the previous one on the target metric. Monitor score per rung and regressions on unrelated tasks.","**Facts in the index, behaviour in the weights.** Re-index on every document change (weekly at minimum for the support case) and delete retired pages from the index, never from weights. Monitor retrieval recall on the golden set, citation rate and \"not in the documents\" answers.","**Cache before you train.** Put stable instructions, tool definitions and few-shot examples first and byte-identical so provider prompt caching applies (Anthropic's `cache_control` breakpoints, OpenAI's automatic prefix caching). Monitor cached-token share in the usage fields, then redo the break-even.","**Multi-adapter serving for many tunes.** When several teams or tenants each need a LoRA, serve them on one shared base, for example vLLM started with `--enable-lora` and `--max-loras`, choosing the adapter per request. Monitor adapter load latency and per-adapter error rates.","**Budget the upgrade tax up front.** Version the training data, adapter and eval harness together. On each base-model release, first rerun the prompt-only baseline: if the new base closes the gap, retire the adapter. Track months between upgrades as an input to the break-even."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"50 to 200 real failures\"] --> C{\"Which gap?\"}\n    C -->|\"knowledge\"| R[\"RAG or long context\"]\n    R --> U{\"Uses passages well?\"}\n    U -->|\"no\"| RF[\"Fine-tune to use context\"]\n    C -->|\"behaviour\"| P[\"Prompt plus eval set\"]\n    P --> PL{\"Plateau or prompt too costly?\"}\n    PL -->|\"yes\"| L[\"LoRA SFT or DPO\"]\n    C -->|\"economics\"| D[\"Distil or shrink prompt\"]\n    D --> E[\"Same eval set, quality held?\"]\n    L --> E\n    RF --> E\n    PL -->|\"no\"| S[\"Ship the prompt\"]","caption":"Start from labelled failures, route each gap to its lever, and only climb to fine-tuning when the eval\nset plateaus on the cheaper rung."},"interview":[{"q":"Why doesn't fine-tuning on a company's documents make the model know them?","a":"- **Next-token training sees few phrasings.** SFT shifts probability toward the exact sequences it saw,\n  which does not build the paraphrase-invariant recall that question answering needs.\n- **Evidence:** Ovadia et al. found retrieval consistently beat unsupervised fine-tuning for new facts,\n  and fine-tuning helped only with many paraphrases per fact.\n- **And it fails silently:** the model invents plausible details in the training data's style instead of\n  refusing, with no citation and no way to delete a retired fact."},{"q":"The retriever returns the right passage but answers still ignore it. What do you do?","a":"- **Classify it correctly:** retrieval works, so this is a behaviour gap, not a knowledge gap. More\n  retrieval tuning will not help.\n- **Prompt first:** instruct the model to answer only from the passages and to say \"not in the\n  documents\" otherwise, then score faithfulness on the golden set.\n- **If that plateaus:** fine-tune the model to use retrieved context (the RAFT idea) while the facts stay\n  in the index."},{"q":"The prompt has plateaued below target. When would you still not fine-tune?","a":"- **When the task is still changing,** because every redesign means new data, a new run and a new eval.\n- **When the base model upgrades often,** because the upgrade tax repeats each release and a newer base\n  may close the gap on its own.\n- **When you cannot evaluate,** because without a golden set you cannot tell a better adapter from a\n  regression on other tasks."},{"q":"2M requests a month carry a 5,000-token prompt. Is fine-tuning it down to 800 tokens worth it?","a":"- **Token arithmetic:** 4,200 tokens removed × 2M requests = 8.4 billion input tokens a month, from 10\n  billion to 1.6 billion.\n- **Then subtract:** the cached share was already discounted, and a tuned model may cost more per token\n  or need its own GPUs.\n- **Compare with the cycle cost:** review, training and evals, multiplied by how often you re-train. It\n  pays only if monthly saving × months between upgrades exceeds that."},{"q":"A legal team wants answers from internal policies, scoped by department, with citations. Design it.","a":"- **RAG is the core,** because the requirements (freshness, per-department access control, citations,\n  deletion) are exactly what retrieval gives and weights cannot.\n- **Enforce scoping at retrieval time** with metadata filters per user's department, so restricted text\n  never reaches the prompt.\n- **Behaviour via prompt:** a system prompt requiring citations and refusals outside the documents, scored\n  on a golden set; fine-tune only if that set plateaus."}],"tradeoffs":["**Prompting: free to try vs paid on every request.** It rolls back instantly, but every request pays for the prompt and quality plateaus. It flips to fine-tuning when a multi-thousand-token prompt runs at high volume or evals stall below target.","**RAG: freshness vs owning retrieval.** It gives freshness, citations, access control and deletion, but you now own a retrieval system whose recall becomes the dominant failure mode. It flips to plain long context when the corpus is small and rarely changes, and it never flips to fine-tuning for facts.","**Fine-tuning: behaviour and cost vs upkeep.** It gives behaviour you cannot prompt into existence and a cheaper per-request path, but costs data, evals, forgetting risk and re-training at every base-model release. It flips back to prompting when the task is still changing or the model is upgraded often.","**RAG plus a fine-tuned model: strongest vs most moving parts.** It is the strongest configuration but doubles the moving parts, so it only pays off when the team can run the same eval set across both layers after every change."],"pitfalls":["**Fine-tuning to inject facts.** Symptom: confident, plausible details that match the training data's style but not its content, and no way to answer questions about last week's changes. Fix: move the facts to retrieval.","**Skipping the eval set.** Symptom: \"the fine-tuned model feels better\" with no number attached, and regressions on other tasks discovered by users rather than by the team. Fix: build the set from real failures before the first change.","**Building RAG for a behaviour gap.** Symptom: retrieval returns the right passage while the model still answers in the wrong format or ignores the passage. Fix: treat it as behaviour (prompt, then fine-tune to use context).","**Fine-tuning before the prompt has been seriously tried.** Symptom: a two-week training project that a short system-prompt edit would have resolved. Fix: prompt until the eval plateaus.","**Ignoring the upgrade tax.** Symptom: a fine-tuned model blocks a migration to a stronger base model for months because nobody budgeted to re-train and re-evaluate. Fix: budget it in the break-even.","**Treating long context as free RAG.** Symptom: per-request cost blows up and lost-in-the-middle errors appear on the documents that matter most. Fix: retrieve, or keep long context for small static corpora."],"when":"- **Use the procedure:** every time someone asks for a model to \"know\", \"sound like\" or \"stop doing\"\n  something. Classify failures, then prompt, then RAG for knowledge, then LoRA for behaviour or cost, and\n  measure the same eval set at every rung.\n- **Reach for fine-tuning when:** the behaviour is stable and the prompt has plateaued or become the\n  dominant per-request cost; a smaller model must match a larger one on a narrow task; or the output\n  distribution must be precise.\n- **Avoid fine-tuning when:** the task is still being designed, facts must be fresh or attributable, or\n  you cannot afford to re-train at the next base-model release.\n- **Interviews:** the expected answer is never \"fine-tune\" or \"RAG\" alone; it is the gap classification,\n  the ordering, and the measurement loop that justifies each step.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval set from real failures first, ships the prompt, then the retrieval layer, then a LoRA, scoring the same set at every rung and owning the prompt-token budget.","architect":"Owns the lever policy: which knowledge must stay in retrieval for freshness, access control or compliance, which behaviours may move into weights, and the plan for base-model upgrades.","fde":"Translates the customer's \"it doesn't know our business\" into the knowledge/behaviour split in week one, and declines the request to fine-tune on the company wiki with the reasoning to back it."},"check":{"q":"A customer says: \"The model doesn't know our product catalogue and keeps answering in the wrong\ntone, so let's fine-tune it on our docs.\" How do you respond, and what plan do you propose?","a":"Separate the two gaps. The catalogue is knowledge that changes, must be scoped and must be\nattributable, so it belongs in retrieval: fine-tuning on the docs will not make SKUs reliably recallable,\ncannot remove discontinued items, and gives no citations. Tone is behaviour: tighten the system prompt\nwith a few curated examples first and score it on a rubric over 100–200 real conversations. If that\nplateaus below target, train a LoRA on reviewed answers for tone only, keeping facts in the index. Later,\nif the model mishandles retrieved passages, fine-tune it to use context rather than to contain it.\nPropose the measurement plan up front so each step is justified by a number, not a feeling."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which situation is RAG the right lever for?","options":["The model has the facts but formats answers inconsistently","The model lacks information that exists in documents and changes over time","The model is accurate but too slow and expensive at current volume","The model refuses too many legitimate requests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"RAG supplies missing, changing, attributable information at request time. Formatting and refusal are behaviour gaps for prompting or fine-tuning; cost is an economics gap for distillation or prompt compression."},{"q":"Why is supervised fine-tuning a poor way to inject facts?","options":["Fine-tuning cannot modify attention-layer weights, only the output head","Next-token training on a few phrasings does not make facts reliably retrievable, and it offers no freshness or citation","LoRA's low rank is too small to store any factual content","Fine-tuning only changes the tokenizer vocabulary, not the model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SFT shifts probabilities toward seen sequences; it does not build the robust, paraphrase-invariant recall that pretraining's massive repetition produces. Retrieval puts the fact in context, where attention can copy it."},{"q":"When does fine-tuning beat prompting on cost?","options":["Always, because fine-tuned models are inherently smaller","When the task definition changes every week","When a long few-shot prompt at high volume dominates per-request cost and the behaviour is stable","When the base model is upgraded every month"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Training the examples into the weights removes thousands of tokens from every request, which pays off at volume; frequent task or base-model changes mean re-training repeatedly, which erases that saving."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/tooling","sources":["Official docs: TRL (Hugging Face)","Official docs: Axolotl","Official docs: Unsloth","Hu et al., 2021 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models"],"why":"- **The hard part was never the loop.** Before these tools every fine-tune was a bespoke script, and the\n  work was the dozen details around it:\n  - render chat data through the exact template the model expects;\n  - mask the loss so the model learns assistant turns, not the user's questions;\n  - pack sequences without letting examples attend to each other;\n  - inject LoRA into the right projection matrices;\n  - mixed precision, gradient checkpointing, a 4-bit base with paged optimizers, checkpoint and resume,\n    logging. Each is a few lines and easy to get subtly wrong.\n- **Preference training adds a second layer.** DPO needs log-probabilities from a reference model on\n  exactly the right tokens; GRPO needs a generation loop, reward functions and group-relative advantages.\n- **The mistakes do not crash.** Loss on prompt tokens gives a model that parrots questions. A template\n  mismatch between training and serving gives a model that looked fine in the notebook and misbehaves in\n  vLLM. A padded-token bug in DPO gives a model slightly worse for reasons nobody can diagnose. Teams that\n  write the loop from scratch reproduce these bugs one at a time, over weeks.\n- **What the tools buy.** They encode the known-good recipe, so the only variables left are the ones that\n  should vary: data, hyperparameters and evaluation.\n- **Where a hand-written loop still fits.** It is the right way to *learn* what happens, and the escape\n  hatch for a genuinely new loss, but not the way to ship a fine-tune.","how":"### At a glance\n| Tool | Layer | You write | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| TRL | Trainers on top of `transformers` and PEFT | Python | Control, custom losses, newest methods |\n| Axolotl | Config over Transformers/TRL/PEFT/`accelerate` | YAML | Reproducible runs, dataset formats, multi-GPU |\n| Unsloth | Kernels under a TRL trainer | Two wrapper calls, then TRL | Fast, light LoRA/QLoRA on one GPU |\n\n### TRL: trainers as the primitive\n- **Where it sits.** TRL sits on top of `transformers` and PEFT. Unsloth hands its patched model to TRL\n  trainers. Axolotl uses TRL for its preference and RL trainers; its SFT path subclasses the Transformers\n  `Trainer` directly, with its own packing and loss masking.\n- **`SFTTrainer` with `SFTConfig`.** Accepts a dataset of `messages` (conversational) or plain `text`,\n  renders it with the tokenizer's chat template, optionally packs sequences, applies LoRA through a\n  `peft_config`, and runs the standard `Trainer` loop.\n- **`DPOTrainer`.** Takes `prompt`/`chosen`/`rejected` rows, computes the log-probability ratio of each\n  answer against a reference model, and minimises `−log σ(β · (Δ_chosen − Δ_rejected))`. With a LoRA\n  policy the reference is the base model with the adapter disabled, so no second copy is held in memory.\n- **`GRPOTrainer`.** Samples several completions per prompt (optionally through vLLM), scores them with\n  plain Python reward functions, and normalises rewards within each group to form advantages.\n- **`RewardTrainer` and `PPOTrainer`.** Cover the classic RLHF path.\n- **Why choose it.** You write Python and every piece is overridable; that is the point. TRL is the layer\n  for people who need control or a custom loss.\n\n### Axolotl: the run as a config file\n- **One YAML document describes the run:** base model, adapter type (full, LoRA, QLoRA), LoRA rank and\n  targets, datasets with their format type (`alpaca`, `sharegpt`, `chat_template`, and others), sequence\n  length, sample packing, micro-batch and accumulation, learning rate and scheduler, precision,\n  FlashAttention, gradient checkpointing, DeepSpeed or FSDP settings, and experiment tracking.\n- **What it does with it:** tokenises and packs the data once and caches it, launches through\n  `accelerate` so a one-GPU test and an eight-GPU run use the same file, and can merge the adapter\n  afterwards.\n- **Why it exists:** the dataset format matrix is a genuine pain, and a config file in git is a\n  reproducible experiment; a Python script with twenty keyword arguments is not.\n\n```yaml\nbase_model: Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\nadapter: qlora\nload_in_4bit: true\nlora_r: 16\nlora_alpha: 32\nlora_target_linear: true\ndatasets:\n  - path: data/support_chats.jsonl\n    type: chat_template\nsequence_len: 4096\nsample_packing: true\nmicro_batch_size: 2\ngradient_accumulation_steps: 8\nnum_epochs: 2\nlearning_rate: 2e-4\nlr_scheduler: cosine\nbf16: auto\nflash_attention: true\ngradient_checkpointing: true\noutput_dir: ./out/support-qlora\n```\n\nRun it with the Axolotl CLI (`axolotl train config.yml`; older releases use\n`accelerate launch -m axolotl.cli.train config.yml`).\n\n### Unsloth: the kernels\n- **What it replaces.** The attention, MLP and LoRA forward and backward passes of supported model\n  families, with hand-written Triton kernels and manual autograd.\n- **Why that is faster.** Fewer intermediate tensors are materialised, more operations are fused, and the\n  cross-entropy over the vocabulary is computed in chunks instead of as one huge logits tensor.\n- **Its own checkpointing variant.** It offloads block inputs to CPU asynchronously\n  (`use_gradient_checkpointing=\"unsloth\"`).\n- **A two-call API.** `FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained(name, max_seq_length=..., load_in_4bit=True)` then\n  `FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model(model, r=16, target_modules=[...], lora_alpha=16, ...)`; then hand the\n  model to a TRL trainer as usual.\n- **The claimed gain.** The project's own figures are roughly 2× faster training and substantially lower\n  VRAM than stock Transformers plus PEFT on the same GPU. Treat those as vendor claims and benchmark on\n  your model.\n- **Sharp edges.** Only patched architectures are accelerated, and the open-source release has\n  historically been single-GPU focused, so check the current docs before planning a multi-GPU run around\n  it.\n- **Exports.** Merged 16-bit weights, bare adapters, or GGUF for llama.cpp.\n\n### How they compose\n- **TRL is the base.**\n- **Axolotl configures** Transformers/TRL/PEFT/`accelerate` from YAML.\n- **Unsloth swaps in faster kernels** underneath a TRL trainer.\n- **Same mental model elsewhere.** Similar config-driven frameworks exist (LLaMA-Factory, torchtune).","example":"### LoRA SFT with TRL\nA LoRA SFT on reviewed support conversations. The rows carry a `messages` list, so the trainer renders\nthem with the model's chat template. The adapter is saved on its own and merged into the bf16 base later\nonly if the serving stack needs a single checkpoint. The commented lines show the Unsloth swap-in.\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=\"bfloat16\")\n\n# rows: {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": ...}, {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": ...}]}\nds = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files=\"support_chats.jsonl\", split=\"train\")\n\npeft_cfg = LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05, task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n                      target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\",\n                                      \"gate_proj\", \"up_proj\", \"down_proj\"])\n\nargs = SFTConfig(\n    output_dir=\"out/support-lora\",\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4, gradient_accumulation_steps=4,   # effective batch 16\n    learning_rate=2e-4, num_train_epochs=2, lr_scheduler_type=\"cosine\", warmup_ratio=0.03,\n    bf16=True, gradient_checkpointing=True, packing=True,\n    max_length=2048,                     # older TRL releases call this max_seq_length\n    assistant_only_loss=True,            # needs a chat template that marks assistant turns;\n    logging_steps=10, save_strategy=\"epoch\",   # otherwise use prompt/completion rows\n)\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(model=model, args=args, train_dataset=ds,\n                     processing_class=tok,   # tokenizer= in older TRL releases\n                     peft_config=peft_cfg)\ntrainer.train()\ntrainer.save_model()                     # writes the adapter only\n\n# Unsloth variant: from unsloth import FastLanguageModel\n# model, tok = FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained(name, max_seq_length=2048, load_in_4bit=True)\n# model = FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model(model, r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=[...])\n# then build SFTTrainer(model=model, ...) with peft_config=None.\n```\n\nWhat the trainer does with one row is the part worth verifying by hand:\n1. **Render.** The `messages` list goes through Qwen's ChatML template, in which each turn becomes\n   `<|im_start|>{role}\\n{content}<|im_end|>`, then is tokenised.\n2. **Mask.** With `assistant_only_loss`, every system and user token gets label −100, so only the\n   assistant span contributes to the loss.\n3. **Pack.** Rendered rows are concatenated into 2,048-token sequences. With a padding-free /\n   position-id-aware attention path each row restarts its positions, so it cannot attend to its neighbour.\n\n### Counting steps before you launch\nIllustrative numbers for the run above:\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Data | 3,000 chats averaging 500 tokens = 1.5M tokens |\n| Tokens per optimizer step | 16 × 2,048 ≈ 32.8k |\n| Steps per epoch | ~46 |\n| Steps for two epochs | ~92 |\n| `warmup_ratio=0.03` | about three steps |\n| `save_strategy=\"epoch\"` | two checkpoints |\n\n### Pre-flight: print what the loss actually sees\nDecode one packed batch and print its labels before the first real run. It takes a minute and catches\ntemplate and masking bugs that the loss curve never shows. Run it on the `trainer` built above, before\n`trainer.train()`.\n\n```python\nbatch = next(iter(trainer.get_train_dataloader()))\nids, labels = batch[\"input_ids\"][0], batch[\"labels\"][0]\ntrained = labels != -100\n\nprint(f\"{trained.float().mean():.0%} of tokens carry loss\")   # far below 100% for chat data\nprint(\"TRAINED ON:\\n\", tok.decode(ids[trained])[:600])        # should read as assistant answers only\nprint(\"IGNORED:\\n\", tok.decode(ids[~trained])[:600])          # system and user turns, template tokens\n# If TRAINED ON contains user questions, masking is off: check the chat template or use\n# prompt/completion rows. If it contains two unrelated chats glued together, check packing.\n```","patterns":["**Pin the training stack per run.** TRL and Transformers rename arguments across releases, so lock exact versions of `trl`, `transformers`, `peft` and `accelerate` in a lockfile or container image tagged with the run. Monitor deprecation warnings in the training log as an early signal before upgrading.","**One committed config per experiment.** Keep the Axolotl YAML in git next to a hash of the dataset file, and run `axolotl preprocess config.yml` once so tokenisation and packing are cached and inspectable. Track run name, commit and data hash in the experiment tracker.","**Template parity test in CI.** Render the same conversation with `tok.apply_chat_template(...)` in the training image and the serving image and assert identical token ids. Monitor production outputs for stray special tokens such as a literal `<|im_end|>`.","**Pre-flight label check on every new dataset.** Run the batch-decoding snippet above and record the share of tokens carrying loss. A sudden jump toward 100% means masking broke.","**Benchmark before standardising on Unsloth.** Run about 50 steps of the same config with stock PEFT and with Unsloth, and compare tokens per second and `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()`. Adopt it only for architectures where the gain shows up.","**Evaluate the artifact you ship.** If serving needs one checkpoint, merge into the bf16 base (PEFT's `merge_and_unload()`), then re-run the held-out eval on the merged model, not just the adapter."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"messages rows in JSONL\"] --> T[\"Render with chat template\"]\n    T --> M[\"Label non-assistant tokens -100\"]\n    M --> P[\"Pack into 2,048-token rows\"]\n    A[\"Axolotl YAML config\"] -->|\"configures\"| TR[\"TRL trainer: SFT, DPO, GRPO\"]\n    U[\"Unsloth patched model\"] -->|\"hands model to\"| TR\n    P --> TR\n    TR --> AD[(\"LoRA adapter\")]\n    AD --> MG[\"Merge into bf16 base or GGUF\"]\n    MG --> S[\"Serving stack, same template\"]","caption":"One conversation row flows through the same steps whichever tool drives it; Axolotl configures the\ntrainer, Unsloth patches the model underneath it."},"interview":[{"q":"What do TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth actually save you compared with a hand-written loop?","a":"- **Not the loop itself,** which is short. They save the details around it: chat-template rendering, loss\n  masking, packing, LoRA injection, precision, checkpointing and resume.\n- **Why that matters:** those bugs do not crash. They produce a model that parrots questions or misbehaves\n  only after deployment, and a team finds them one at a time over weeks.\n- **What stays yours:** data quality, hyperparameters and evaluation."},{"q":"A fine-tuned model is fine in the notebook but emits stray end tokens in vLLM. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect a template mismatch first.** Render one conversation with the training tokenizer's chat\n  template and with the serving stack's, and diff the token ids.\n- **Check the stop tokens:** the serving config must treat the template's end-of-turn token (for ChatML,\n  `<|im_end|>`) as a stop sequence.\n- **Fix at the source:** ship the tokenizer and template with the adapter, and add a parity test so it\n  cannot drift again."},{"q":"When would you not use Unsloth or Axolotl?","a":"- **Not Unsloth** when the model architecture is not patched, or the job needs several GPUs, because the\n  speedup disappears and the open-source release has historically been single-GPU focused.\n- **Not Axolotl** when the experiment needs a custom loss or unusual data flow, because you end up reading\n  and patching its source; drop to TRL and write Python.\n- **Not either** when the goal is learning the mechanics; write the loop once by hand."},{"q":"10,000 chats averaging 800 tokens, 4,096-token packing, effective batch 16. How many steps per epoch?","a":"- **Tokens:** 10,000 × 800 = 8M tokens.\n- **Per optimizer step:** 16 × 4,096 = 65,536 tokens, so about 122 steps per epoch.\n- **Why compute it:** it sets warmup (a ratio of 0.03 of a one-epoch run is about four steps), how many checkpoints a\n  per-epoch save writes, and whether logging every 10 steps gives a readable loss curve."},{"q":"How does DPO with a LoRA policy avoid holding two copies of the model?","a":"- **DPO needs a reference model** to compute each answer's log-probability ratio.\n- **With LoRA, the reference is the base with the adapter disabled,** so TRL computes reference\n  log-probabilities from the same weights, and no second copy is held in memory.\n- **The trade-off:** extra forward passes per batch instead of extra memory."}],"tradeoffs":["**TRL: control vs plumbing.** Full control and the newest algorithms first, but you own the plumbing around the trainer (data formatting, launch, multi-GPU config). It flips toward Axolotl once a team runs many similar jobs.","**Axolotl: reproducibility vs flexibility.** Reproducible configs and painless multi-GPU, but a custom loss or unusual data flow means reading its source. It flips back to raw TRL when the experiment is not one of its supported shapes.","**Unsloth: speed vs coverage.** Real speed and memory savings on one GPU, but only for patched architectures and with single-GPU focus historically. It flips to Axolotl or plain FSDP when the job needs several GPUs.","**Convenience vs verification.** Every layer of convenience hides a decision: the lower you go, the more you must verify yourself; the higher you go, the more you must trust defaults you did not read."],"pitfalls":["**Chat-template mismatch between training and serving.** Symptom: a model that behaves in the training notebook but misbehaves or emits stray special tokens once deployed in vLLM or Ollama. Fix: one template source and a parity test.","**Computing the loss over prompt tokens.** Symptom: suspiciously low training loss alongside a model that generates user-style questions or echoes instructions. Fix: assistant-only or completion-only loss, verified by decoding labels.","**Packing without per-example attention masking.** Symptom: answers that drift into the topic of a neighbouring training example, which is context bleed across the pack. Fix: a padding-free, position-id-aware attention path.","**Pinning nothing.** TRL and Transformers rename arguments across releases (`tokenizer` → `processing_class`, `max_seq_length` → `max_length`). Symptom: a job that ran last month fails on an unknown keyword. Fix: pin versions.","**Merging a QLoRA adapter into the 4-bit base instead of the bf16 base.** Symptom: a merged model that scores worse than the adapter-plus-base it came from. Even a bf16 merge is slightly off, because the adapter was trained against dequantised 4-bit weights, so re-run the eval on the merged artifact.","**Trusting Unsloth's speedup without checking the architecture list.** Symptom: an error at load or a run that is no faster than stock Transformers. Fix: check the supported list and benchmark."],"when":"- **Reach for TRL when:** you need a trainer class in your own Python, a custom reward or loss, or the\n  newest method the day it lands.\n- **Reach for Axolotl when:** runs must be reproducible across people and machines, you juggle several\n  dataset formats, or the job spans several GPUs.\n- **Reach for Unsloth when:** the job is a LoRA or QLoRA on one GPU and the model family is supported,\n  because the saving in time and VRAM is large enough to change what fits.\n- **Measure, whatever you pick:** tokens per second, peak memory, eval score on a held-out set, and an\n  end-to-end check through the serving stack's chat template.\n- **Interviews:** place the three on a control-versus-convenience axis and name the failure modes the\n  tools exist to prevent.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the TRL or Axolotl config, verifies loss masking and the chat template on one example by hand, and benchmarks Unsloth against stock PEFT before committing to it.","architect":"Chooses the standard stack for the organisation so runs are reproducible and auditable, and decides where custom TRL code is allowed versus config-only training.","fde":"Gets a customer's first LoRA running on the GPU they already have, often Unsloth on one card, and leaves behind an Axolotl config the customer's team can re-run without you."},"check":{"q":"You have 3,000 reviewed support chats and a single 24 GB GPU. A colleague proposes writing a\ncustom PyTorch training loop. Which tool would you pick, why, and what decisions would still be yours?","a":"A QLoRA SFT through TRL's `SFTTrainer`, loaded with Unsloth if the model family is supported,\nbecause that fits a 7B-class model on 24 GB and removes the known bugs a hand-written loop reinvents:\ntemplate rendering, loss masking, packing, adapter injection and checkpointing. Axolotl would be the\npick if the run must be reproducible for others or move to several GPUs later. What remains yours: data\nquality and de-duplication, the chat template matching the serving stack, confirming the loss covers only\nassistant turns, LoRA rank, learning rate and epochs, a held-out eval set, and whether to serve the\nadapter or merge it into the bf16 base."},"quiz":[{"q":"How do TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth relate to each other?","options":["They are three competing training loops that cannot be combined","TRL provides the trainers; Axolotl configures them from YAML; Unsloth accelerates the kernels beneath them","Axolotl is the base library and TRL is its command-line wrapper","Unsloth replaces TRL entirely with its own trainer classes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TRL is the primitive layer. Axolotl orchestrates Transformers, TRL, PEFT and accelerate from a config file, and Unsloth patches the model's forward and backward passes, then hands the model to a TRL trainer."},{"q":"What is the actual mechanism behind Unsloth's speed and memory savings?","options":["It silently trains a smaller distilled model in place of the one you loaded","It skips the backward pass through all frozen base-model layers","Hand-written fused Triton kernels and manual backward passes that cut intermediate tensors and memory traffic","It performs all arithmetic in 4-bit integers instead of bf16"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The base weights may be stored in 4-bit, but compute still happens in 16-bit; the gain comes from fused kernels, chunked loss computation and fewer materialised intermediates, not from changing the model."},{"q":"Which symptom most strongly suggests the loss was not masked to assistant tokens?","options":["Out-of-memory errors in the first training step","A NaN loss after the warmup phase","Training loss that looks unusually low while the model generates user-style questions","Loss that never decreases from its initial value"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Averaging the loss over the easy-to-predict user turns pulls the number down, and the model learns to continue conversations in the user's voice because that is what it was trained to predict."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/distributed-training","sources":["Rajbhandari et al., 2020 — ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","Shoeybi et al., 2019 — Megatron-LM: Training Multi-Billion Parameter Language Models Using Model Parallelism","Huang et al., 2019 — GPipe: Efficient Training of Giant Neural Networks using Pipeline Parallelism","Zhao et al., 2023 — PyTorch FSDP: Experiences on Scaling Fully Sharded Data Parallel"],"why":"- **Two separate limits: memory and time.** Either one alone forces training across GPUs.\n- **Memory: 16 bytes per parameter.** Mixed-precision Adam keeps a bf16 weight (2 bytes), a bf16 gradient\n  (2), an fp32 master weight (4) and two fp32 Adam moments (8), the accounting the ZeRO paper uses. A 7B\n  model is ~112 GB of state before a single activation; a 70B model is ~1.1 TB. Even a LoRA fine-tune of\n  70B needs its 140 GB of bf16 base weights to live somewhere.\n- **Time: about 6 × parameters × tokens FLOPs.** A 7B model on one trillion tokens is about 4.2 × 10²²\n  FLOPs. At an illustrative sustained 400 TFLOP/s on one GPU (an H100's dense bf16 peak is about\n  989 TFLOP/s; 40% utilisation is a realistic ballpark) that is ~10⁸ seconds, over three years.\n  Pretraining is a thousands-of-GPUs problem by arithmetic, not ambition.\n- **Each naive fix solves one limit only.** Plain data parallelism (replicate on N GPUs, split the batch)\n  divides time by nearly N but every replica still holds the full 112 GB. Naive model parallelism (layers\n  on different GPUs) divides memory but runs one GPU at a time while the others idle.\n- **The design space:** ways to split both state and work so GPUs stay busy and the communication they\n  need fits the links between them.","how":"### The four schemes at a glance\n| Scheme | What is split | What it communicates | Link it needs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Data parallel (DDP) | The batch | Gradient all-reduce once per step | Modest; overlaps with backward |\n| ZeRO / FSDP | Optimizer state, gradients, parameters | Per-layer all-gather and reduce-scatter | Good; prefer intra-node for gathers |\n| Tensor parallel | Each matrix | Activation all-reduces inside every layer | NVLink-class, within a node |\n| Pipeline parallel | Contiguous layers | Stage-boundary activations per micro-batch | Tolerates slower inter-node links |\n\n### Data parallelism (DDP)\n- **Mechanism.** Every GPU holds a full replica and processes a different micro-batch. After backward,\n  gradients are averaged with an all-reduce.\n- **Volume.** In a ring all-reduce each GPU sends and receives about `2·(N−1)/N` times the gradient size,\n  roughly two gradient-sized transfers per step regardless of N.\n- **Overlap.** DDP buckets gradients and reduces early buckets while later layers are still in backward,\n  hiding most of the cost.\n- **Scaling.** Throughput scales with N; memory per GPU does not change.\n- **Global batch** becomes N × micro-batch × accumulation steps, which is why learning rate and warmup\n  must be re-tuned when the GPU count changes.\n\n### Sharded data parallelism: ZeRO and FSDP\nThe waste in DDP is that all N replicas store identical optimizer state and gradients. ZeRO partitions\nthem across the data-parallel group and gathers only what each step needs. Per GPU, with Ψ parameters\nand N ranks:\n\n```\nDDP            16Ψ\nZeRO stage 1   2Ψ + 2Ψ + 12Ψ/N     optimizer state sharded\nZeRO stage 2   2Ψ + 14Ψ/N          gradients sharded too\nZeRO stage 3   16Ψ/N               parameters sharded too\n```\n\n| 7B on 8 GPUs, before activations | Per GPU |\n|---|---|\n| DDP | 112 GB |\n| ZeRO stage 1 | ~38.5 GB |\n| ZeRO stage 2 | ~26 GB |\n| ZeRO stage 3 | 14 GB |\n\n- **What stage 3 pays.** It all-gathers each layer's parameters just before use in forward and again in\n  backward, then reduce-scatters its gradients: about 1.5× DDP's communication volume, spread through the\n  step and overlapped by prefetching the next layer.\n- **PyTorch FSDP is the native implementation.** `SHARD_GRAD_OP` is roughly stage 2, `FULL_SHARD` is\n  stage 3, and `HYBRID_SHARD` shards within a node and replicates across nodes, so the frequent\n  all-gathers stay on fast intra-node links.\n- **The wrapping unit sets peak memory** (usually one transformer block), because one unit is held\n  unsharded at a time.\n- **When it still does not fit.** DeepSpeed adds ZeRO-Offload (optimizer state and gradients to CPU RAM)\n  and ZeRO-Infinity (NVMe), at a large bandwidth cost.\n\n### Tensor parallelism\n- **When.** A single layer is too large, or per-GPU activation memory and latency must fall without idle\n  time: split the matrices themselves (Megatron-LM).\n- **MLP split.** The first linear is split by columns, so each GPU computes its slice and applies GELU\n  independently. The second is split by rows, so each GPU produces a partial sum, combined with one\n  all-reduce. Attention splits heads across GPUs the same way.\n- **Cost.** Two all-reduces per layer in forward and two in backward, each moving an `s·b·h` activation.\n- **So** it is bandwidth- and latency-hungry, and is kept inside a node over NVLink-class links,\n  typically at degree 2–8.\n\n### Pipeline parallelism\n1. **Assign** contiguous groups of layers to stages on different GPUs.\n2. **Split** the batch into m micro-batches that stream through, so stages work concurrently (GPipe).\n3. **Accept the bubble,** the idle fraction, roughly `(p−1)/(m+p−1)` for p stages.\n\n| Four stages | Bubble |\n|---|---|\n| Sixteen micro-batches | about 16% idle |\n| Four micro-batches | about 43% idle |\n\n- **1F1B schedule.** Interleaves forward and backward so each stage holds at most about p micro-batches'\n  activations instead of all m. It does not shrink the bubble by itself.\n- **Interleaved variant (Megatron).** Gives each GPU several non-contiguous chunks of layers, which divides\n  the bubble by the number of chunks at the price of more stage-to-stage messages.\n- **Why it tolerates slower links:** only stage-boundary activations cross GPUs, once per micro-batch.\n- **Its costs:** the bubble, stage imbalance, and the engineering of partitioning a model.\n\n### 3D parallelism and the choice procedure\n- **Composition.** Tensor parallelism inside a node, pipeline across nodes, data or sharded-data\n  parallelism over the remaining replicas: world size = TP × PP × DP.\n- **Long contexts** add sequence or context parallelism, which splits the sequence dimension (ring\n  attention).\n\nThe decision order for most teams:\n1. **If the state fits with FSDP or ZeRO-3** across the available GPUs, use only that; it covers nearly\n   all fine-tuning.\n2. **If a layer or its activations still do not fit, or step latency matters,** add tensor parallelism\n   within the node.\n3. **If the job spans many nodes with slow interconnect,** add pipeline parallelism rather than stretching\n   tensor parallelism across the network.\n\n### What the frameworks provide\n| Framework | Provides |\n|---|---|\n| PyTorch | DDP, FSDP (and the newer `fully_shard` FSDP2 API), the `torchrun` launcher, the NCCL backend |\n| DeepSpeed | ZeRO stages 1–3, offloading and a pipeline engine behind a JSON config; plugs into the Hugging Face Trainer and Accelerate |\n| Megatron-LM/Megatron-Core | Tensor, pipeline and sequence parallelism for pretraining |\n| Accelerate, Axolotl | FSDP or DeepSpeed as configuration, so the training script does not change |\n\nA ZeRO-3 run with optimizer offload is a few lines:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"bf16\": {\"enabled\": true},\n  \"zero_optimization\": {\n    \"stage\": 3,\n    \"overlap_comm\": true,\n    \"offload_optimizer\": {\"device\": \"cpu\"},\n    \"stage3_gather_16bit_weights_on_model_save\": true\n  },\n  \"train_micro_batch_size_per_gpu\": 1,\n  \"gradient_accumulation_steps\": 16\n}\n```\n\n- **FSDP vs DeepSpeed at stage 3.** FSDP `FULL_SHARD` and ZeRO-3 shard the same things and communicate the\n  same way, so the choice is mostly about the stack.\n- **FSDP** is native PyTorch and tracks its newest features.\n- **DeepSpeed** has the more mature CPU/NVMe offload and a single config that people already know how to\n  read.","example":"### FSDP full fine-tune of a 7B model on eight GPUs\nA minimal full-parameter fine-tune with FSDP in `FULL_SHARD` mode (the ZeRO-3 equivalent), wrapping per\ndecoder block and checkpointing activations per block. The model is loaded in bf16 to keep the example\nshort; production runs usually keep fp32 master weights through the mixed-precision policy. Launch with\n`torchrun --nproc_per_node=8 train.py`.\n\n```python\nimport os, functools, torch, torch.distributed as dist\nfrom torch.distributed.fsdp import FullyShardedDataParallel as FSDP, MixedPrecision, ShardingStrategy\nfrom torch.distributed.fsdp.wrap import transformer_auto_wrap_policy\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nfrom transformers.models.qwen2.modeling_qwen2 import Qwen2DecoderLayer\n\ndist.init_process_group(\"nccl\")\nrank = int(os.environ[\"LOCAL_RANK\"]); torch.cuda.set_device(rank)\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16)\nmodel.gradient_checkpointing_enable(gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={\"use_reentrant\": False})\n\nmodel = FSDP(\n    model,\n    auto_wrap_policy=functools.partial(transformer_auto_wrap_policy,\n                                      transformer_layer_cls={Qwen2DecoderLayer}),\n    sharding_strategy=ShardingStrategy.FULL_SHARD,      # params, grads, optimizer state all sharded\n    mixed_precision=MixedPrecision(param_dtype=torch.bfloat16, reduce_dtype=torch.bfloat16),\n    device_id=rank, use_orig_params=True,\n)\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-5)\n\nbatch = tok([\"Replace me with a sharded DataLoader.\"] * 4, return_tensors=\"pt\", padding=True).to(rank)\nlabels = batch[\"input_ids\"].clone(); labels[batch[\"attention_mask\"] == 0] = -100\nfor step in range(10):\n    loss = model(**batch, labels=labels).loss\n    loss.backward(); opt.step(); opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    if rank == 0: print(step, round(loss.item(), 4))\ndist.destroy_process_group()\n# Per-GPU budget, roughly: Qwen2.5-7B is ~7.6B params. With everything in bf16 (weights, grads,\n# two AdamW moments = 8 bytes/param) that is ~61 GB of state / 8 ranks ≈ 7.6 GB per GPU; with fp32\n# master weights and moments it is the 16-byte budget, ~15 GB per GPU. On top sit one gathered block\n# (~233M params ≈ 0.47 GB in bf16, two with prefetch), checkpointed block inputs and one live block's\n# activations. Note every rank first loads the full 15 GB on CPU: 8 × 15 GB of host RAM.\n```\n\n### Planning calculator: memory, bubble and calendar time\nPure Python, no GPU. It reproduces the lesson's numbers so you can plug in your own model and cluster.\n\n```python\nGB = 1e9\n\ndef state_gb(params, n, scheme):\n    \"\"\"Per-GPU training state in GB, Adam in mixed precision (16 bytes/param), before activations.\"\"\"\n    p = params\n    per = {\n        \"DDP\":    16 * p,\n        \"ZeRO-1\": 2 * p + 2 * p + 12 * p / n,   # optimizer state sharded\n        \"ZeRO-2\": 2 * p + 14 * p / n,           # gradients sharded too\n        \"ZeRO-3\": 16 * p / n,                   # parameters sharded too\n    }[scheme]\n    return per / GB\n\ndef bubble(p_stages, m_micro):\n    return (p_stages - 1) / (m_micro + p_stages - 1)\n\ndef train_years(params, tokens, flops_per_gpu, gpus=1):\n    return 6 * params * tokens / (flops_per_gpu * gpus) / (3600 * 24 * 365)\n\nfor s in (\"DDP\", \"ZeRO-1\", \"ZeRO-2\", \"ZeRO-3\"):\n    print(f\"7B on 8 GPUs, {s:6s}: {state_gb(7e9, 8, s):6.1f} GB\")\nprint(f\"70B ZeRO-3: 8 GPUs {state_gb(70e9, 8, 'ZeRO-3'):.0f} GB, 16 GPUs {state_gb(70e9, 16, 'ZeRO-3'):.0f} GB\")\nprint(f\"bubble p=4: m=16 {bubble(4, 16):.0%}, m=4 {bubble(4, 4):.0%}\")\nprint(f\"7B x 1T tokens at 400 TFLOP/s: {train_years(7e9, 1e12, 400e12):.1f} years on 1 GPU, \"\n      f\"{train_years(7e9, 1e12, 400e12, gpus=1024) * 365:.1f} days on 1,024\")\n# 7B on 8 GPUs, DDP   :  112.0 GB\n# 7B on 8 GPUs, ZeRO-1:   38.5 GB\n# 7B on 8 GPUs, ZeRO-2:   26.2 GB\n# 7B on 8 GPUs, ZeRO-3:   14.0 GB\n# 70B ZeRO-3: 8 GPUs 140 GB, 16 GPUs 70 GB\n# bubble p=4: m=16 16%, m=4 43%\n# 7B x 1T tokens at 400 TFLOP/s: 3.3 years on 1 GPU, 1.2 days on 1,024\n```\n\nThe 1,024-GPU figure assumes perfect scaling; real runs lose some of it to communication, which is why\nscaling efficiency is measured, not assumed.\n\n### Worked scenario: a multi-node run that scaled badly\nA team moved a ZeRO-3 fine-tune from one node of eight GPUs to four nodes (illustrative).\n\n| Observation | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tokens per second per GPU fell sharply | Every per-layer all-gather now crossed the slower inter-node network | Hybrid sharding: shard within the node, replicate across nodes |\n| Loss curve no longer matched the one-node run | Global batch grew 4× with the same learning rate and warmup | Re-tune learning rate and warmup for the new global batch |\n| Resume from the old checkpoint failed | Sharded checkpoint was tied to world size 8 | A resharding distributed checkpoint format, or consolidate on save |","patterns":["**Hybrid sharding across nodes.** When a sharded job spans several nodes, use FSDP's `ShardingStrategy.HYBRID_SHARD` so all-gathers stay on intra-node links and only gradient reduction crosses the network. Monitor step time split between compute and communication, and model FLOPs utilisation.","**Load once, shard, broadcast.** For 70B-class models, build the model on the meta device and load real weights on rank 0 only, then broadcast (FSDP's `sync_module_states=True`; Accelerate exposes this as CPU-RAM-efficient loading). Monitor host RAM during initialisation, which otherwise peaks at ranks × model size.","**Resharding checkpoints.** Save with PyTorch distributed checkpointing (`torch.distributed.checkpoint`), which can load into a different world size, or consolidate with DeepSpeed's `zero_to_fp32.py` script. Test a resume on a different GPU count before the run matters.","**Scaling-efficiency gate.** Before a long run, measure tokens per second per GPU at one node and at the target size. Re-tune learning rate and warmup whenever the global batch changes, and compare loss curves against the smaller run for the first few hundred steps.","**Make hangs diagnosable.** Set a finite collective timeout in `dist.init_process_group(timeout=...)`, run with `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO` while debugging, and use `drop_last=True` on the sampler so ranks see equal batch counts. Alert on a step-time heartbeat, not GPU utilisation, because a hung collective can still show 100%.","**Keep tensor parallelism inside the node.** In Megatron-LM, set `--tensor-model-parallel-size` no larger than GPUs per node and use `--pipeline-model-parallel-size` to span nodes. Monitor MFU; a drop to single digits signals TP traffic on a slow link."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    J[\"Training job\"] --> L{\"Can LoRA or QLoRA avoid it?\"}\n    L -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Single GPU or plain DDP\"]\n    L -->|\"no\"| F{\"State fits with FSDP or ZeRO-3?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes\"| FS[\"FSDP or ZeRO-3 only\"]\n    F -->|\"no, or latency matters\"| T[\"Add tensor parallel inside node\"]\n    T --> N{\"Many nodes, slow links?\"}\n    N -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Add pipeline across nodes\"]\n    N -->|\"no\"| D[\"Sharded DP over replicas\"]\n    P --> D\n    D --> W[\"World size = TP x PP x DP\"]","caption":"The choice procedure as a decision path: try not to need it, then shard, then split matrices inside the\nnode, then split layers across nodes."},"interview":[{"q":"What do the ZeRO stages shard, and what does stage 3 cost?","a":"- **Stage 1** shards optimizer state, **stage 2** adds gradients, **stage 3** adds parameters, so per-GPU\n  state falls from 16Ψ to 16Ψ/N; for 7B on 8 GPUs, 112 GB down to 14 GB.\n- **Why it works:** in DDP all N replicas store identical state, so sharding removes pure duplication.\n- **The cost:** stage 3 all-gathers each layer's parameters in forward and backward and reduce-scatters\n  gradients, about 1.5× DDP's volume, hidden by prefetching when the interconnect is fast enough."},{"q":"A multi-GPU run hangs at 100% GPU utilisation with no progress. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect a mismatched collective:** one rank skipped or reordered an all-reduce or all-gather, often\n  because ranks got different numbers of batches.\n- **Confirm:** set a finite process-group timeout and `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO`, then find which rank is waiting\n  on which collective.\n- **Fix:** `drop_last` on the sampler, a join context for uneven inputs, or an explicit barrier, and alert\n  on step-time heartbeats rather than utilisation."},{"q":"When would you choose pipeline parallelism over tensor parallelism?","a":"- **When the split must cross nodes,** because tensor parallelism all-reduces activations in every layer\n  and needs NVLink-class links, while pipeline parallelism only sends stage-boundary activations.\n- **Not when the global batch is small,** because the bubble `(p−1)/(m+p−1)` is about 43% with four stages\n  and four micro-batches.\n- **Usual answer:** both, tensor parallelism inside the node and pipeline across nodes."},{"q":"Why can't you pretrain a 7B model on one trillion tokens with a single GPU?","a":"- **Compute:** 6 × 7B × 1T ≈ 4.2 × 10²² FLOPs.\n- **Throughput:** at an illustrative sustained 400 TFLOP/s that is ~10⁸ seconds, over three years.\n- **So** it needs on the order of a thousand GPUs for a run of days, and memory (112 GB of state) rules out\n  one GPU anyway."},{"q":"You moved from 8 to 32 GPUs and the loss curve changed. What happened?","a":"- **The global batch changed:** N × micro-batch × accumulation grew 4×, while learning rate and warmup were\n  tuned for the smaller batch.\n- **Fix:** re-tune learning rate and warmup, or lower accumulation steps to keep the global batch\n  constant.\n- **Then check scaling efficiency:** tokens per second per GPU against the 8-GPU run, to confirm the\n  extra GPUs are doing useful work."}],"tradeoffs":["**DDP: simplest vs full state on every GPU.** The simplest and most compute-efficient scheme, but every GPU holds the full state. It flips to FSDP or ZeRO the moment weights plus optimizer state exceed one card, or batch is memory-limited.","**ZeRO-3/FSDP: memory ÷ N vs gathers on the critical path.** It divides memory by N but adds all-gathers on the critical path and is sensitive to the interconnect; across slow inter-node links use hybrid sharding so gathers stay inside the node.","**Tensor parallelism: no bubble vs bandwidth every layer.** It cuts per-GPU weight and activation memory with no bubble, but needs NVLink-class bandwidth every layer. It flips to pipeline parallelism as soon as the split crosses a node boundary.","**Pipeline parallelism: light communication vs the bubble.** The bubble only shrinks when micro-batches far outnumber stages; it flips against you when the global batch is small or stages are unbalanced.","**Offload: fits the impossible vs speed.** CPU or NVMe offload fits otherwise impossible jobs on few GPUs at a large speed cost; it pays only when wall-clock is cheap and GPUs are not."],"pitfalls":["**Scaling GPUs without re-tuning for the larger global batch.** Symptom: a loss curve that no longer matches the single-GPU run, usually because learning rate and warmup were left unchanged. Fix: re-tune both for the new global batch.","**Materialising the full model on every rank before sharding.** Symptom: host-RAM or GPU OOM during initialisation of a 70B model. Fix: meta-device init and syncing weights from rank 0.","**Wrapping the whole model as one FSDP unit.** Symptom: OOM at the first all-gather despite `FULL_SHARD`, because the entire parameter set is gathered at once instead of one block. Fix: wrap per decoder block.","**Uneven data across ranks so one rank skips a collective.** Symptom: a run that hangs at 100% GPU utilisation with no progress. Fix: `drop_last`, a join context or an explicit barrier.","**Sharded checkpoints tied to one world size.** Symptom: resume fails after changing the GPU count. Fix: distributed checkpoint formats that reshard, or consolidating on save.","**Tensor parallelism across GPUs connected only by PCIe or Ethernet.** Symptom: single-digit model FLOPs utilisation while the links sit saturated. Fix: keep TP within an NVLink domain."],"when":"- **Application roles:** you need the vocabulary and arithmetic, not the practice: enough to read a\n  DeepSpeed or FSDP config, to know why a 70B full fine-tune needs more than one 80 GB node, and to\n  recognise that the cheapest \"distributed\" option is usually to not need it (LoRA, QLoRA, a smaller base).\n- **Training roles decide:** the ZeRO stage or sharding strategy, the FSDP wrap unit, whether to add tensor\n  parallelism inside the node, the global batch and learning-rate schedule, and checkpoint format.\n- **Measure:** tokens per second per GPU against the single-GPU baseline (scaling efficiency) and model\n  FLOPs utilisation.\n- **Interviews:** say what each scheme communicates, how often, and which link it needs; and derive the\n  16-bytes-per-parameter memory budget on the spot.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the sharding strategy and wrap policy, launches with `torchrun` or Accelerate, and checks scaling efficiency and MFU before trusting that eight GPUs are doing eight GPUs' worth of work.","architect":"Owns the training topology and cost model: node interconnect, GPU count, whether full fine-tuning is justified over PEFT at all, and the `6 × params × tokens` estimate behind every schedule.","fde":"Rarely runs this, but reads a customer's DeepSpeed or FSDP config to explain why their eight-GPU ZeRO-3 fine-tune will not reproduce on the four-GPU staging box they want to use."},"check":{"q":"A team wants a full-parameter SFT of a 70B model on one node of eight 80 GB GPUs. Does it fit, and\nwhat are their options if not? Show the arithmetic.","a":"With Adam in mixed precision the state is 16 bytes per parameter, so 70B × 16 ≈ 1.12 TB. ZeRO-3 or\nFSDP across eight GPUs leaves 140 GB per GPU before activations, which exceeds 80 GB, so it does not fit.\nOptions: offload the 12 bytes-per-parameter of fp32 master weights and Adam moments to CPU (about 840 GB\nof host RAM, and much slower); add a second node so sixteen GPUs hold 70 GB each, which is tight and\nneeds gradient checkpointing, a small micro-batch and hybrid sharding to keep all-gathers intra-node; or\nchange the problem to LoRA on a bf16 base (140 GB of weights sharded to ~17.5 GB per GPU plus a tiny\noptimizer state) or QLoRA. The arithmetic decides, not the framework."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which parallelism scheme communicates activations in every layer and therefore needs NVLink-class bandwidth?","options":["Data parallelism, which all-reduces gradients once per step","Tensor parallelism, which all-reduces partial results inside every layer","Pipeline parallelism, which sends activations only at stage boundaries","ZeRO stage 1, which shards only the optimizer state"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Splitting a matrix across GPUs means each layer's output must be combined before the next layer runs, so the traffic is per layer and latency-sensitive; the other schemes communicate far less often."},{"q":"A 7B model trained with Adam in mixed precision across 8 GPUs under ZeRO stage 3 needs roughly how much state per GPU, before activations?","options":["About 112 GB, the same as data parallelism","About 56 GB, because only the optimizer state is split","About 14 GB, since all 16 bytes per parameter are sharded across the 8 ranks","About 1.75 GB, since only the bf16 weights are sharded"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stage 3 shards weights, gradients and optimizer state, so per-GPU memory is 16Ψ/N = 112 GB / 8. The 1.75 GB figure counts only the bf16 weights and forgets the other 14 bytes per parameter."},{"q":"In a four-stage pipeline, what most directly reduces the idle bubble?","options":["Using fewer micro-batches so each stage finishes sooner","Using more micro-batches per step, since the bubble fraction is about (p−1)/(m+p−1)","Adding more pipeline stages to spread the work","Enabling gradient checkpointing inside each stage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bubble is the time stages wait for work to arrive; more micro-batches in flight keep every stage busy for a larger share of the step, while more stages or checkpointing make the bubble worse or leave it alone."}]}]});
